<!-- emerald document, schema 65 -->
<!-- emerald: decision.format.md -->
---
format: decision
title: Decision
language: 24
---

One decision the project made, as a row: what was undecided, what was decided, when, and whether it still stands. A decision log kept as prose grows until nobody reads it end to end, and then the same question gets decided again, differently, by somebody who did not know it had been answered. A row is found by asking — `search:"caching"`, `match:{state:live}` — rather than by reading.

**A decision others already rely on is not changed in place.** When it stops being true, a later decision supersedes it: the new row names the old one in `supersedes`, and the old one's `state` becomes `amended` or `superseded`. That is why there is no field to overwrite, and why the history stays readable. Correcting the wording of a decision the same day it was written is fine; changing what it says once others have acted on it is not.

`question` and `answer` are the two halves every decision has. Kept apart, a session can be handed the answer alone and act on it, and go and read the question when the answer is not enough.

`ref` is the name people use for the decision — `D-12` — and is not the row's `id`, which every table already has and which a link resolves through.

A decision makes no claim: its rules are "not empty" and "this link resolves", which the columns already enforce.

| column | type | points at | unit | per | required | unique | values | min | max | note |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| ref | text |  |  |  | yes | yes |  |  |  | The decision's own name, as the project writes it — `D-12`. Not the row id. |
| date | date |  |  |  | yes | no |  |  |  | The day it was decided. |
| milestone | text |  |  |  | yes | no |  |  |  | The milestone it was decided in — `M2`. Text rather than a link, so a decision can name a milestone this document does not hold. |
| scope | text |  |  |  | yes | no |  |  |  | Which part of the project it governs — `api`, `web`, `docs`, or whatever the project's own parts are called. Split by what the decision governs, not by the milestone it came out of, so a filter on it shows one part's rules and nothing else's. |
| title | text |  |  |  | yes | no |  |  |  | The decision in one line, as a sentence — `Orders are kept for seven years`. |
| question | text |  |  |  | yes | no |  |  |  | What was undecided before this — the thing a later session would otherwise decide differently by accident. |
| answer | text |  |  |  | yes | no |  |  |  | What was decided. Enough on its own that a session need not read the argument to act correctly. |
| supersedes | link | decision |  |  | no | no |  |  |  | The earlier decision this one replaces or amends — `D-7`. Empty for a decision that changes nothing before it. |
| state | enum |  |  |  | yes | no | live, amended, superseded |  |  | Whether this is still the decision of record. `amended`: a later decision changed part of it and the rest still stands. `superseded`: a later decision replaced it. A decision others rely on is never rewritten, so this column is how to ask. |

## rows

### D-001
date: 2026-08-09
milestone: M0
scope: engine
title: Kotlin Multiplatform — SUPERSEDED by D-019
question: What platform is the app built on?
answer: SUPERSEDED by D-019. The original decision was Compose Multiplatform, Android first and iOS later. The app became a desktop-first TypeScript/React web app, later wrapped in an Electron shell (D-115). Retained for the record only.
state: superseded

### D-002
date: 2026-08-09
milestone: M0
scope: engine
title: SQLDelight — SUPERSEDED by D-020
question: What is the storage binding?
answer: SUPERSEDED by D-020. The original decision was SQLDelight over Room KMP. The SQLite half survived; only the Kotlin binding went, replaced by sql.js (SQLite in WebAssembly). Retained for the record only.
state: superseded

### D-003
date: 2026-08-09
milestone: M0
scope: engine
title: Money as integer minor units
question: How is money represented?
answer: As a signed integer of minor units (agorot) plus a currency code. No floating point in the money path, ever. Floating-point money produces off-by-one-agora errors that surface in aggregates, where they are hardest to trace, and destroy trust. Percentage allocation uses basis points and largest-remainder distribution. The rule has no exception for a schema the user wrote: a declared `money` column compiles to two columns (minor units + currency), and a price that needs more precision carries its own scale (D-204). Converting to a decimal for export inserts the point into the digits and never divides (D-119).
state: live

### D-005
date: 2026-08-09
milestone: M0
scope: engine
title: User-defined column mapping — SUPERSEDED then RESTATED
question: Are statement importers driven by shipped provider profiles?
answer: SUPERSEDED the same day by D-013. The original plan was importers driven by shipped ProviderProfile data. It was replaced by the user owning the column mapping, with the app only guessing. Retained for the record only.
state: superseded

### D-010
date: 2026-08-09
milestone: M0
scope: engine
title: Everything inferred goes to a confirmation queue
question: How does anything the app infers reach the user's data?
answer: Originally: categories, fixed expenses, debt payments and AI suggestions all arrive unconfirmed, through one shared accept/edit/dismiss review pattern, because consistency is what makes automation trustworthy. A user who knows nothing happens without their say-so will let the app do more. AMENDED: the principle, nothing written without a human action, survives. The per-item queue did not. D-092 found that a queue you empty by hand hands the automation's value back, so the plugin confirms a whole batch in the conversation (dry run, then commit). D-140 made a reading one surface with ONE accept and the doubts above it, never forty confirmations.
state: amended

### D-012
date: 2026-08-09
milestone: M0
scope: engine
title: AI is optional, opt-in, bring-your-own-key
question: Is AI on by default, and who pays for it?
answer: SUPERSEDED by D-124: there is no key and no remote path at all. No AI inside the app reaches the network; the local model runtime (loopback only, main process) and the Claude Code plugin are what remain. What survives from this entry: AI is off by default, and every AI capability has a manual equivalent that is fully sufficient, so the app is whole with the feature off.
state: superseded

### D-013
date: 2026-08-09
milestone: M0
scope: engine
title: The user maps the columns; the app only guesses
question: How does the app read a statement file from a provider nobody wrote a parser for?
answer: Parsing is driven by an ImportMapping the USER creates and owns. The app reads the file, guesses the header row and column roles (from header aliases and value-shape inference), shows a live parse preview, and asks the user to confirm or correct. The mapping is saved and matched to future files by header signature. Shipped per-provider parsers break silently when exports change and cover only providers someone coded. A user-owned mapping works on day one and turns a format change into a 30-second repair. Detection-first with the mapper as a fallback was rejected: the fallback gets no testing, and a wrong automatic detection is worse than a prompt. Shipped alias tables are hints that may be wrong without breaking anything. Because correctness depends on the mapping, the live preview and the parsed-total-versus-printed-total check are load-bearing. 'Add a provider' disappears as a development task. D-132 generalised this: a model may propose the mapping, a person confirms it, and code executes it.
supersedes: D-005
state: live

### D-017
date: 2026-08-09
milestone: M0
scope: engine
title: Black-on-white minimal UI with functional motion only
question: What does the app look like, and what is colour for?
answer: Near-monochrome: typography and whitespace instead of colour and chrome, tabular figures everywhere, and animation used only to explain a change. The content is dense numeric data, so removing colour makes the numbers legible and leaves colour free to MEAN something. Outflows are plain ink, not red; red is reserved for problems. Category colours are the one licensed exception, muted, for chart legibility. Dark mode is built from tokens from day one. The full spec is docs/11. AMENDED: D-030 allowed motion that smooths a change and one soft shadow for floating surfaces. D-125 made emerald the accent for what you can act on, never on a figure. D-154 let the primary button look raised. D-167 made numbers count only when they change in front of you.
state: amended

### D-018
date: 2026-08-09
milestone: M0
scope: engine
title: File import is the architecture, not a stopgap
question: Will the app connect directly to banks?
answer: No, never. File import is permanent. Direct account access in Israel needs a Financial Information Service licence or a paid dependency on a licensed aggregator, and either contradicts the local-first, no-backend, zero-cost design. So no backend, no subscription and no credentials are ever handed over; that is the positioning, not a limitation. Import quality is the whole product experience, not a temporary phase, so the mapper deserves real investment. Any future 'real connections' is a company-scale licensing decision, not a feature. The same logic generalises to the engine: a document or a conversation is the intake, never a live feed.
state: live

### D-019
date: 2026-08-09
milestone: M0
scope: engine
title: Desktop web app, not a mobile app
question: Is this a mobile app?
answer: No. A desktop-first web app in TypeScript, React and Vite, with no Android, no iOS and no KMP (supersedes D-001). The highest-value screens (column mapper, import review, bulk categorise, splitter, deposit table) are wide-data tasks a phone is wrong for. Usage is monthly, not daily, which removes the value of notifications and widgets. Web also escapes the store maintenance treadmill, which matters for a project touched in bursts over years. SheetJS reads spreadsheets and charts are hand-rolled SVG. Moving to native later is a rewrite, accepted knowingly. AMENDED by D-115: it ships as an Electron desktop shell around the same dist/, which also remains a static site.
supersedes: D-001
state: amended

### D-020
date: 2026-08-09
milestone: M0
scope: engine
title: SQLite in the browser via sql.js, database as a file
question: What is the store, and what is the saved artifact?
answer: SQLite compiled to WebAssembly (sql.js), held in memory and saved as a file that is a real SQLite database (supersedes D-002). It keeps every schema, query and aggregation decision in SQL. The saved file backs up, syncs through any folder and opens in any SQLite tool, which suits an app whose pitch is data ownership. IndexedDB and plain JSON with JS aggregation were rejected, the latter because it makes statistics ad hoc. Migrations are numbered .sql files run on load inside a transaction, and are immutable once shipped. A file written by a newer schema must REFUSE to open in an older build rather than corrupt itself. D-246 later let a document be stored as text or SQLite bytes, chosen per file; either way it is a real SQLite database when open.
supersedes: D-002
state: live

### D-021
date: 2026-08-09
milestone: M0
scope: engine
title: Document model — your data is a file you own
question: Where does the user's data live?
answer: In a document: you open a file, work, and save it. There is no invisible browser-managed database as the source of truth. It was first forced (IndexedDB is blocked on file://) and is also right on its own: for an app replacing a spreadsheet, a portable file is a better artifact than a store that can be cleared or lost with a profile. Storage is one interface with several adapters, selected by feature detection, not a build flag. Save discipline is designed for: a dirty indicator, Ctrl/Cmd+S, a close guard, and a localStorage crash snapshot. The app never renames a file the user named (D-156). AMENDED by D-115 and D-133: the constraint is gone (the shell could store state anywhere) and document-based is now a CHOICE. Making the app stateful because it finally could is the specific mistake to avoid. Machine preferences may live on the machine (D-135); the user's data never does.
state: amended

### D-022
date: 2026-08-09
milestone: M0
scope: engine
title: IBM Plex Sans + IBM Plex Sans Hebrew, embedded
question: What typeface, and where is it loaded from?
answer: IBM Plex Sans with IBM Plex Sans Hebrew: one family across both scripts, self-hosted as subsetted woff2. It is the only pairing meeting all four requirements: a real Hebrew companion (names from documents are Hebrew on nearly every row), proper tabular figures, the Open Font License for unambiguous embedding, and a neutral voice that does not compete with the data. No Google Fonts link and no CDN: the app must work with no network at all, and the shell now blocks any external request anyway (D-124, D-133). Subset to Latin, Hebrew and punctuation. The UI uses two weights, 400 and 500 (D-030).
state: live

### D-023
date: 2026-08-09
milestone: M0
scope: engine
title: Date-only values are calendar parts, not `Date` + date-fns
question: How are date-only values represented?
answer: As plain calendar-part records, LocalDate and YearMonth in core/time.ts ({year, month, day}), with their own validation and comparison. No `Date` object is constructed anywhere in the date path. A Date carries a timezone whether or not the value has one. The first real statement drifted about 40 seconds on every date, because SheetJS converts serials through local time and Asia/Jerusalem's 1899 offset is +02:20:54, and the first symptom of that is an off-by-one day at a month boundary, moving a transaction into the wrong period. Parts also make invalid dates unrepresentable: tryLocalDate(2026, 2, 30) is undefined where new Date silently gives 2 March. date-fns is still fine for interval arithmetic, with an explicit conversion at that boundary. Stored dates are strings (YYYY-MM-DD), never epochs. Spreadsheet date cells are read from the serial via SheetJS's own format parser, keeping the timezone out of import.
state: live

### D-024
date: 2026-08-09
milestone: M0
scope: engine
title: The sql.js wasm is compiled into the bundle, not fetched
question: How does sql.js get its WebAssembly binary without a fetch?
answer: A small Vite plugin (`wasm-as-bytes`) inlines the binary at build time and hands it to emscripten as wasmBinary, so sql.js never fetches it. It was first needed because a single file opened from disk had no second file and no network. It survived that build's retirement (D-133) because it also lets Vitest run the database layer in Node with no setup (a ?url import makes emscripten in Node abort) and keeps dist-plugin/server.mjs self-contained. Any future wasm dependency uses the same ?bytes import. The shell's CSP needs 'wasm-unsafe-eval' for it to compile (D-126).
state: live

### D-025
date: 2026-08-09
milestone: M0
scope: engine
title: A CSV has no cell types, so none are invented
question: How are CSV cells typed?
answer: They are not: CSV is read with SheetJS's `raw` option, so every cell arrives as text. Type and date-order inference happen later, in the mapper, over a whole COLUMN at a time. Only real spreadsheet formats carry cell types read as authoritative. SheetJS's own guess is month-first, so a day-first `02-07-2026` silently became 7 February: a wrong date in a right-looking format. The value in a CSV is genuinely text, and claiming a type fabricates information the file does not contain. Column-level inference is strictly better, since one day over 12 settles the order for the whole column, which one cell never can. Overriding a wrong guess afterwards is rejected: the evidence is already discarded. Preserve the types a file has; a CSV has none.
state: live

### D-026
date: 2026-08-09
milestone: M0
scope: engine
title: The step ladder is retired; the roadmap is the plan
question: How is work planned?
answer: The roadmap is the plan of record, and work is planned in units against a milestone, not as numbered steps sized for evenings (four steps were done in one afternoon, and their boundaries caused rewrites). Three disciplines carry over and are not optional: real data from the first commit; an explicit out-of-scope list per unit; and ending every unit green, committed, with the affected notes updated in the same commit. Every serious defect so far was found where output was LOOKED AT, not by a test, which is where 'real documents find the bugs' and the show-the-author rule come from. Each unit writes its own acceptance criteria before it starts. Later refinements: one unit per session (session cost), the closing pass (D-131), and planned units live as rows in docs/memory.emd with a brief (M22).
state: live

### D-027
date: 2026-08-10
milestone: M1
scope: engine
title: A file is recognised by an aligned header signature, not a stored row
question: How is a statement file recognised as a format already mapped?
answer: By a header signature: the header row's normalised cells, in order, versioned and delimited. On import, EVERY row in the top twenty is signed and compared with every saved mapping, so recognition does not depend on the guesser or on a stored header-row index (a preamble line added is not a new format). Signatures are compared by longest-common-subsequence ALIGNMENT, not position by position. An inserted column is then one change rather than fourteen, the highlight of changed columns is truthful, and the alignment gives the index map so saved roles move with their columns. A close match needs at least 70% of the wider header unchanged AND at least 3 columns in common, because a share alone lies on narrow files (two unrelated 3-column CSVs share 67%). Not hashed, since a hash cannot measure closeness. Ordered, not a set, because roles are indices. Not keyed on sheet name. A signature from an older normalisation is unreadable rather than wrongly matched, so nothing migrates. D-139 applied the same idea to label-value documents, as a set of labels.
state: live

### D-028
date: 2026-08-10
milestone: M1
scope: engine
title: The staging area holds a shaped draft, not a blob
question: What does the import staging area store?
answer: A shaped draft: staged_row mirrors the ledger table as real, nullable columns, with raw_cells the only JSON. The review screen filters and counts by status over thousands of rows, and a blob makes every such question a JS pass. Real columns make commit a straight copy and let the staged row carry the ledger's sign/direction CHECK, catching a sign bug one hop earlier. EVERY row is staged (parsed, failed and skipped), because the rows most worth a human's attention are the failures. Everything starts PENDING, even what the app is sure of, because nothing is auto-anything. 'Accept all new' keeps the ordinary case to one action, and is scoped to NEW because accepting a duplicate would be a button that silently does nothing. The suggested and chosen categories are separate columns, because user-overrides-win is only enforceable if a manual choice stays distinguishable from a guess forever. Staged rows are retained after commit for rollback and resume; if they grow, the answer is a retention rule the user controls.
state: live

### D-030
date: 2026-08-10
milestone: M1
scope: engine
title: A real theme toggle, and motion that smooths rather than sits still
question: How does the visual system become real, and how much motion is allowed?
answer: The type ramp, spacing, radius, elevation and colour are @theme tokens. A single Button (primary/secondary/ghost) and a light/dark/system ThemeToggle are the shared vocabulary, built before the screens multiplied, because retrofitting touches every screen. Motion is allowed when it smooths a change: colour crossfades on theme change, views fading up on arrival, a 1px press settle. It is never a load animation, which is a flash of the wrong thing. prefers-reduced-motion removes all motion and keeps the colour. `system` is the default theme, because the honest default is the machine's. One soft shadow exists, --shadow-pop, for floating surfaces only. No component library and no CSS-in-JS runtime. useTheme applies the palette before React mounts, from main.tsx, and crossfades ride on a .theme-ready class added one frame after mount. Later: D-125 added the accent, D-154 the primary button's lift, and D-167 counting figures.
state: live

### D-031
date: 2026-08-10
milestone: M1
scope: engine
title: Upsert-on-signature survives the mappings screen, and duplicate is its exception
question: Once mappings can be listed and deleted, should saving still upsert on the header signature?
answer: Yes, on a better argument. The matcher resolves a file to ONE mapping, so two rows with one signature confuse the matcher, not the user. Saving from the mapper says 'this is how this format is read', and one row already says it; an insert would fork that sentence on every correction. Duplicate is the deliberate exception, because the user asked out loud for a copy. It keeps the signature (without it the copy is unusable), ties break by most recently used, and both rows are marked, because an ambiguity the user created is one they should see. Delete is soft, with no in-use guard; it explains its cost (the next import falls back to guessing) rather than refusing. A UNIQUE constraint was rejected: it turns a visible choice into a failed write. The screen edits no column roles and has no 'new mapping' button: a role is a claim about a column and checking it needs the file, so re-import is the re-map. A shared label or style on its third copy is promoted to one home. D-139 later chose a version chain instead for templates, because a read document must keep the map that read it.
state: live

### D-060
date: 2026-08-14
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: A class is derived, a settlement is a group, and the last-4 was being eaten
question: How does bank import classify rows and find card settlements without double counting?
answer: Pure domain first (domain/bankImport.ts, model/banking.ts). SEPARATE_DEBIT_CREDIT becomes a real convention with debit and credit roles (no migration: amount_convention had no CHECK; the guard was VALID_CONVENTIONS in TypeScript); the convention is authoritative and the column decides the sign, never a sign printed in a debit column. RowClass is never stored, because the right answer changes when a card statement lands; only the user's answers are stored (CardSettlementLink.userDecision, Account.excludeSettlementsFromStats, a nullable column on account). The bank-to-card link is a table, since userDecision is not derivable. A settlement is a group per card per month, summed, so a split charge reconciles and two months compare against two statements. The amount identifies the billing period, within a zero-to-one-month window (pinned by a test); agreement is what makes confidence HIGH. normalizeMerchant strips trailing digits, so the last-4 is read from merchantRaw; BankRow carries both. bankSpendingBasis hands out a total, with undecided settlements outside it. Hebrew construct forms (עמלת) are in the keyword table. חובה/debit move to the debit role; תאריך ערך is chargeDate; an unreadable filled balance is a row error.
state: live

### D-061
date: 2026-08-14
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: The answer is a column, the link is a table, and a sync never overwrites either
question: How are card-settlement links and the user's answers stored?
answer: Migration 015: account.exclude_settlements_from_stats, nullable with three states (null = not yet asked; a NOT NULL default would re-ask someone who answered keep), and card_settlement_link. What is stored is what cannot be derived: the user's decision and the confidence shown when they gave it; card, period and confidence are otherwise a cache syncSettlementLinks may overwrite. syncSettlementLinks never writes user_decision; setSettlementDecision (through applyDecision) is the only writer and sets the bank row's is_excluded_from_stats in the same transaction — a cross-table invariant with one door, no trigger. A link created already carrying a remembered EXCLUDED sets the flag too. PENDING is a stored state so the dashboard can count waiting settlements. One link per row of a group. Rows skipped in review are not linked. mergeAccounts moves links, soft-deletes those of deduplicated rows, and adopts answers the survivor lacked. The unique index on bank_transaction_id is partial on deleted_at IS NULL. Schema 15. Rejected: storing the last-4 on the link, one transaction per link (SQLite will not nest BEGIN).
state: live

### D-062
date: 2026-08-14
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: The rescan runs after every commit, and the test is the property
question: When are card settlements re-derived, and how is order-independence tested?
answer: rescanSettlements runs after every commit, card or bank, unconditionally — a condition is a case someone has to spot — and outside the commit's transaction, so a failed scan never takes a successful import down (it can simply run again). It is idempotent, pinned by a test. The test is the property: import bank-first in one document and card-first in another and assert equal link sets. Lookback is thirteen months from the newest bank row in the document, never a clock, so a document rescans identically any day. Recorded gap: bankSpendingBasis keeps an undecided settlement out, but the ledger counts it until an EXCLUDED answer sets isExcludedFromStats; reconciling silently would exclude money on the user's behalf, so pendingSettlementCount lets the app say how many are waiting. CommitResult gains settlementsLinked and settlementsUnmatched. A ledger row carries no running balance, so verifyRunningBalance belongs to the parse only.
state: live

### D-063
date: 2026-08-14
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: The balance check is stored as one JSON value, and it blocks with a way past
question: How is a bank statement's running-balance check stored and enforced?
answer: Migration 016: import_session.balance_check, one JSON column, decoded defensively (a mangled value reads as 'no check recorded', failing open, the state of every card import). The check runs once at stage time, because by review the sheet and its row order are gone. One column, not eight, because VERIFIED and DIVERGED share no fields and only one strip reads it. commitSession refuses when the check diverged and takes an explicit ignoreBalanceCheck, offered by the review screen behind a deliberate tick that states the cost — the check had never met a real Israeli bank export, so an unoverridable block could be confidently wrong. The refusal lives in commitSession so every caller gets it. The panel names the row, both figures, the gap and which end was read as oldest, and says the fix is usually re-mapping a column and re-importing. A verified chain earns one clause in the summary; no balance column says nothing. Rejected: storing the check on the account or mapping.
state: live

### D-064
date: 2026-08-14
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: The settlement prompt is a queue on the account screen, not a strip on the review screen
question: Where does the user answer whether card settlements count as spending?
answer: A confirmation queue on the Accounts screen, linked from the import-finished notice — not the review screen, which would miss the direction where a card statement imported today turns an old bank row into a settlement (D-062). The review screen keeps the balance check. Each prompt states a checkable claim ('matches 3 transactions totalling ₪4,210.55 in 2026-07'), grouped one card one month in the repository (listSettlementSummaries). The per-card control is a select of three — ask me / never counts / counts — and it moves the money: decideForCard answers every settlement of the card, and PENDING puts them back and un-excludes them. decideAllForCard (the queue's bulk answers, which also set the card memory) does not overwrite answers given elsewhere; a single group's answer does not set card memory. An unmatched charge (card with no statement) is shown, re-derived, and cannot be answered. D-160 later added a Home attention item and a notice carrying the count, keeping the queue here.
state: live

### D-065
date: 2026-08-14
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: The labeling queue was already built, and what it was missing was "skip"
question: Does F06's labeling queue need its own screen?
answer: No: it is the bulk categorize screen (#/categorize, D-047), which already groups by merchantNormalized with counts, totals, suggestions and the rule offer. 'Mark as internal transfer' is not an action either, since Internal Transfer is a seeded category in the picker. What was missing was skip: an app_setting key labeling.skipped, a Skip per row, 'Skip all N', and a Skipped section to restore. Skip is a fact about the queue, not the rows — nothing is written to any transaction, they stay in every total, and filtering happens after listMerchantGroups so other callers still see the merchant. The timestamp is kept and skipAll does not restamp. The skipped count is in the screen header, not a nav badge. Deferred then: promoting a name to a fixed expense or a debt payment (later built by D-191).
state: live

### D-066
date: 2026-08-14
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: A deposit is never a gain, and the guarantee is a parameter
question: How does the pension/fund domain guarantee a deposit never shows as a return?
answer: By signature: periodReturn takes a snapshot pair and a ContributionBasis, with no overload taking two balances; UNKNOWN contributions yield CONTRIBUTIONS_UNKNOWN carrying dates and no amounts. NONE_EXPECTED is separate, so a dormant fund's whole change is its return. 'Not enough data' is a union variant (NEEDS_TWO_POINTS) in reconcileDeposits, fundReturns and effectiveFee. Payslip-to-fund comparison shifts salary months by a configurable one-month lag and compares cumulative totals over the longest reported run. There is no linkedDeductionTypeIds: the link lives on deduction_type.linked_fund_account_id. Fees are integer basis points (0.5% = 50). Fee benchmarks ship in src/data/reference/fundFeeReference.ts with asOf and source, overrides in an app_setting, and the domain is given references rather than importing them. Contributions are derived (ExpectedContribution has no id); the one stored answer is fund_balance_snapshot.deposit_gap_acknowledged, which changes only the verdict. Severance is always its own field (componentSplit, longTermSavings) with the remainder named unattributed. monthsBetween moves to core/time.ts. Shipped fee caps were unverified against a primary source.
state: live

### D-067
date: 2026-08-14
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: Two tables, no contribution table, and the foreign key stays unwritten
question: What does migration 017 store for funds, and is the deduction-type foreign key written?
answer: fund_account and fund_balance_snapshot (schema 17), read by fundRepository. No contribution table: expected contributions are derived by expectedContributions over payslips, and a SQL copy would be a second answer. The foreign key on deduction_type.linked_fund_account_id stays unwritten: adding it means rebuilding a table paycheck_line points into, and the app never sets PRAGMA foreign_keys, so every REFERENCES is documentation. deleteFund instead clears every type naming the fund and soft-deletes its snapshots. One reading per fund per date (partial unique index), refused by name first. validateSnapshot keeps the other invariants in the domain. Component amounts are nullable (missing is not zero) and deposits may be negative (a severance withdrawal). fundDepositCheck composes the reconciliation's inputs so a caller cannot mismatch them; deductionTypesForFund asks the side that stores the link.
state: live

### D-068
date: 2026-08-14
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: Wealth is a route until it has a second screen, and the benchmark is shown while it is typed
question: Where do the funds screen and reading form live, and how are fees and missing readings shown?
answer: At #/wealth as a plain route, since a sub-nav of one is furniture (D-054). The fee benchmark is shown under the field while the fee is typed, not red, since an above-cap fee is usually a typo or an old policy. No reading is not zero: the total is '—' when no fund has a reading and names how many are missing otherwise. Staleness is a domain function, snapshotFreshness, four states with six- and twelve-month thresholds. Readings are an expandable list with edit and delete on the card until a detail screen exists. fundDepositChecks reconciles all funds in one pass. The top nav gains a fifth item. The fund manager list was already seeded in migration 006. Superseded in part by D-072, which made /wealth a section shell, and D-071, which moved readings to the fund detail.
state: amended

### D-069
date: 2026-08-14
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: The user can name the card a merchant pays, and a stale link now goes away
question: What can the user do when a card settlement is not detected, and what happens when that is taken back?
answer: A settlement alias: normalized merchant → card account, an app_setting key, read by readSettlementPlan and set from the ledger's detail panel. Keyed on merchantNormalized so the rows answered about and the rows covered are the same set. The alias makes a row a settlement; it does not decide whether settlements count — that is asked in the panel with the same calls as the accounts queue. A user-stated link is MEDIUM confidence, raised to HIGH by an agreeing statement total; isUserStated is a separate field. An alias never overrides the outflow guard (a refund from the card company settles nothing). syncSettlementLinks now drops links whose row is no longer a settlement, scoped to rows the scan read; it clears only an exclusion the link caused (userDecision EXCLUDED), never a hand exclusion. SyncResult gains dropped. The per-row checkbox stays, described as a one-off. Archived cards resolve but cannot be chosen.
state: live

### D-070
date: 2026-08-14
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: An imported file is not always a card, and an account's kind is editable
question: How is an imported file's account kind decided, and can it be corrected?
answer: StageBar hard-coded CREDIT_CARD, so a bank statement landed on a card account, was never scanned, and its card charges double counted silently — found by driving the app, not by tests, which always built the right kind. Now the mapper guesses: a parse with a balance column is a bank account, anything else a card, and the user can change it before staging (D-013: the app guesses, the user decides). The control appears only when an account is about to be created, not for a file bound to an existing account by a saved mapping. An existing account's kind is editable on the accounts screen, and changing it rescans settlements in the same write. resolveAccountForImport already took a kind; only its caller was wrong.
state: live

### D-071
date: 2026-08-14
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: The verdict is a sentence and the table is its working
question: What does the fund detail screen show, and where does it get it?
answer: A fund detail screen (a child route of the fund, not a sibling, so the shell still waited; D-072 later moved it to #/wealth/funds/:fundId). Everything comes from one fundAnalysis call — reconciliation, return series, per-period table, effective fee — so the lag and pairing cannot differ. depositPeriods is evidence for the cumulative verdict, and a test asserts its rows sum to the headline. No payslips is not ₪0.00: ContributionTotal.paycheckCount lets the table print a dash. FEE_DISCREPANCY_BASIS_POINTS is 10, since quarterly rounding moved 0.50% to 0.51%. The chart has two rows: the balance line, and bars splitting movement into contributions and growth; a window with unknown contributions draws no bars. The readings list moves off the funds card; the card's name links to the fund. fundAnalysis takes currency from the fund's own readings.
state: live

### D-072
date: 2026-08-15
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: The section gets a sub-nav, the projection gets a home, and a slider may not disagree with its own number
question: How do the fee-impact and cross-fund deposit screens land, and how does the fee projection behave?
answer: #/wealth/fees and #/wealth/deposits are the first real siblings, so /wealth becomes a section shell; #/wealth redirects to funds and the fund detail moves to #/wealth/funds/:fundId with a redirect. The fee screen is a set of sliders over feeDragIllustration's assumptions (balance, contribution, horizon, fees, returns), each prefilled from the document with its source; a fund with no fee starts at the typical fee and says so. Three kinds of zero each get their own sentence, and the range is printed signed. A range input snaps its own value, so rounding lives in the change handler and slider ceilings come from the document, never the live value. The deposit-check screen computes nothing (fundDepositChecks, the third caller) and lists each fund it cannot check with the reason; acknowledgement writes to the closing snapshot. projectBalance moves to domain/projection.ts taking plain basis points; applyBasisPoints to core/percent.ts. recentContributionRate is twelve months scaled by months that carry a payslip. Returns clamped to 20%.
supersedes: D-068
state: live

### D-073
date: 2026-08-15
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: The link is a column on the row that stores it, offered where the money is still yours
question: How does a user link a payslip deduction to a fund?
answer: The deduction-types screen gains a Pays into column writing linked_fund_account_id, which the whole deposit reconciliation turns on. It is offered only where the Saving flag is ticked (pension, study fund, severance — never income tax), and a link that exists always shows itself whatever the flags say. Archived funds are listed and marked, so a real link never renders as 'Not linked'. The row carries an 'open' link to the fund once linked, to check the effect. F13 became usable end to end without SQL. deleteFund clears these links (D-067). Amended by D-077, which made the column one select across fund or saving goal, and D-085, which adds a debt for LOAN_REPAYMENT types.
state: amended

### D-074
date: 2026-08-15
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: A balance is a signature, liquidity belongs to the place, and a rate says whose it is
question: How does the savings domain keep balances honest, and where do liquidity and expected return live?
answer: No function in domain/savings.ts takes a balance: every entry point takes deposits and sums them privately, and SavingGoal has no balance field. Liquidity belongs to the place; emergencyFund works out the liquid balance itself; an unrecognized place counts as locked, so liquid + locked = total. An emergency goal's ordinary progress is measured against liquid too (progressBalance). The emergency target is a SavingGoal with a multiplier in integer basis points of a month (45,000 = 4.5) and both bases (fixed-expense floor, total spending) are always computed. Expected return is one nullable field binding the rate to CONTRACTUAL or ASSUMED, and scenario adjustments move only ASSUMED rates. A place with money and no rate is projected flat and named (placesWithoutRate). Withdrawals are negative deposits with a required reason. Migration 018's shape, including depositSourceKey's unique index. payrollSavingDeposits derives payroll deposits from linkedSavingGoalId. F13 balances count as locked savings via liquiditySplit. Amended by D-175: rate, basis and liquidity moved from the place to a holding.
state: amended

### D-075
date: 2026-08-15
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: Three tables with no balance in them, and a line id does not survive an edit
question: How are savings stored, and how is a payroll deposit kept from duplicating on edit?
answer: Migration 018 (schema 18): holding_place, saving_goal, saving_deposit, with no balance column and no SUM in savingsRepository. A payroll deposit is keyed on paycheckId:goalId, one per goal per payslip summing its lines, because updatePaycheck rewrites lines and a line id does not survive an edit. Deleting a goal or place with deposits refuses and names the alternatives (close it, moveDeposits) — a deposit whose place vanished would count as locked. Transfers are written and deleted as a pair under one key. Rate and basis are written as a pair by one function; a lone rate reads as ASSUMED. monthlyExpenses gives the fixed floor from fixedExpenseBasis and the comfortable figure as the mean of the last three complete months, divided by months that have transactions. syncPayrollDeposits also removes deposits no longer claimed. No foreign keys (D-067); listDeposits is not capped; a payroll deposit with no default place is reported, not guessed.
state: live

### D-076
date: 2026-08-15
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: Savings leads the section, deposits are a view, and three identical columns are worse than one
question: How are F07's savings screens laid out?
answer: Two tabs, #/wealth/savings and #/wealth/places (the money map), with goal detail at #/wealth/savings/:goalId; savings leads the sub-nav. The deposits list is a view of the savings tab behind a toggle. The movement form has modes (in, out, moved between places) rather than a typed minus; a move writes both legs and deleting one deletes both. When every rate is contractual or unrecorded, the projection collapses to a single Worth column with a sentence, and the cautious/middling/hopeful range returns only when an assumed rate is involved. The emergency panel leads with the chosen basis and prices the other in a sentence; the goal editor prices both as the slider moves. ProgressRing draws no ring for a goal without a target and never wraps past 100%. Later reshaped by D-157, which reduced Wealth to three children.
state: live

### D-077
date: 2026-08-15
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: One destination per line, and a plan that proposes a deposit
question: How can a payslip line or a split plan feed a saving goal?
answer: The Pays into select offers a fund or a saving goal in one control; picking either clears the other in the same write, so one line cannot feed two destinations and double count. Linking runs syncPayrollDeposits in the same mutation, and it also runs after every paycheck save and delete. The splitter offers SAVING_GOAL buckets, and applying a plan produces SAVING_DEPOSIT suggestions; accepting one records a deposit, and the panel says 'record' and counts targets and deposits separately. Plan deposits are keyed on plan, month and bucket, not goal, and dated the first of the planned month. A goal with no default place is refused by name. Suggestion gains a period. Hazard recorded for M6: the savings-rate formula must exclude FROM_PAYCHECK_DEDUCTION deposits or double count.
supersedes: D-073
state: live

### D-078
date: 2026-08-15
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: The salary is the duplicate, and the answer is a month
question: How does the app stop a salary deposit in the bank double counting the payslip?
answer: matchSalaries in domain/bankImport.ts, salaryRepository, and a confirmation queue on the paychecks screen; no migration. The payslip is authoritative and the bank row is the duplicate that gets excluded. A deposit is compared against payslips of its own month and the month before, and an exact match wins over a near one whichever month. Deposits are grouped by the month they landed and summed, so a split salary works; one payslip is claimed by one group only. A disagreement shows both figures and the difference and stays listed after the double-count question is answered. The answer is an app_setting keyed by month, and it sets transaction.is_excluded_from_stats; taking it back restores the rows. Nothing is excluded automatically and a mismatch blocks nothing. It reuses listBankRowsForScan and readSettlementPlan so a row is classified once.
state: live

### D-079
date: 2026-08-15
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: The picker offers four groups, and two of them do not make accounts
question: What does onboarding's institution picker create for investment platforms and pension funds?
answer: Four groups: cards and banks create an Account (where statements land); an investment platform creates a HoldingPlace; a pension or study fund manager creates a FundAccount. The step's copy says what each becomes, and the footer counts records across all three. A fund starts as PENSION and is corrected on the funds screen. A bank does not create a checking HoldingPlace. Selection is derived per kind, a place matched by institution name. Unticking removes what ticking created and is refused when there is history. Closed M5's last line; whether savings goals and pension balances join the checklist was left to F12 (later done by D-166).
state: live

### D-080
date: 2026-08-15
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: The provider's own heading is a rule you can write, behind the editor and not on one click
question: How does a user write a rule from the provider's category?
answer: The transaction detail panel offers 'Create a rule from the provider's category' on rows that have one, pre-filling a PROVIDER_CATEGORY draft and handing it to the rules screen through D-041's router state. The pattern is the string as printed, via matchable, never normalizeMerchant (which would strip trailing digits that are part of a category's name); a test asserts the round trip. It stays out of the one-click correction offer, which still writes EXACT_MERCHANT only, because a provider category claims rows the user has never seen and belongs behind the editor. A caption names what it covers in the provider's words (through Bidi), not a count. Requested.
state: live

### D-081
date: 2026-08-15
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: Money that was already there, and money the market made, are in the balance and are not saving
question: How are an opening balance and investment growth recorded in a savings goal?
answer: saving_deposit.source_kind gains OPENING_BALANCE and GROWTH (migration 019, a table rebuild to widen the CHECK; schema 19). Both count in the balance and are excluded from every figure measuring what the user set aside, through one predicate, isContribution — an aggregate cannot read a MANUAL deposit's note. GoalProgress splits openingBalance + contributed + withdrawn + growth = balance, tested as an identity (deposited renamed contributed). GROWTH may be negative and is exempt from the withdrawal reason; the form has 'It grew' and 'It fell' modes. The goal detail's 'paid in by month' chart excludes the opening balance; the deposits list prints '· ₪X paid in' when it differs from the net. M6's statistics have two exclusions on the deposit term. Nothing fetches a price.
state: live

### D-082
date: 2026-08-15
milestone: M5
scope: ledger
title: A projected balance nobody can decompose is a number taken on trust
question: What is a fee-impact projection made of?
answer: FeeDragScenario gains paidIn, grossGrowth and feesTaken, printed under each scenario, as an exact identity in minor units: paidIn + grossGrowth − feesTaken = atCurrentFees. feesTaken is measured — the projection with both fees at zero minus the projection at today's fees — never inferred from rate × balance × years, which understates it because fees are taken from money that would have compounded (₪71,950 vs a naive ₪37,500 on the fixture). projectBalance runs three times per scenario. F07's savings projection already reports contributed and growth the same way.
state: live

### D-083
date: 2026-08-15
milestone: M6
scope: ledger
title: A debt has no balance, a payment states its interest, and the payroll link already exists
question: What shape does the debt arithmetic take before any table or screen exists?
answer: src/domain/debt.ts, pure and tested, first. A payment stores its amount and its interest only; principal is the remainder and principalOf is the one function that knows it — outstanding counts down by principal, and reaching for amount would overstate progress by the interest. All-principal is interest zero. PAYROLL_DEDUCTION is not a tracking kind: the link lives on deduction_type.linked_debt_id (migration 012), and the Pays into select gains a debt as a third destination beside fund and goal (D-077's one-question shape). The remaining four tracking kinds are one discriminated union. debtObligationBasis excludes three limbs, each returned named: payroll-deducted loans (already out of net, D-053), card installments (already in spending), and standing orders also recorded as fixed expenses (already in fixedExpenseBasis, found by comparing refs). A row two debts both claim is an ambiguity for review, never two payments, which is why the four mechanisms run in one pass. containsMerchant is shared with the CONTAINS rule kind. projectPayoff takes the start month rather than reading a clock; one payment yields NEEDS_TWO_PAYMENTS. docs/04 drops principalPortion and interestRatePct.
state: live

### D-084
date: 2026-08-22
milestone: M6
scope: ledger
title: A dismissal removes a claimant, not a payment
question: How are debts stored and discovered, and how does a dismissed match behave?
answer: Migration 020 (schema 20): debt and debt_payment with no balance and no principal column, and no SUM in the repository — the domain adds rows up. One currency per debt and per payment, so mixed currencies cannot be stored; the tracking kind/ref pair is a CHECK (NONE has no ref, every other kind has one). Discovery runs on every import commit and every paycheck save. Payroll payments are upserted (derived from a slip still in the document, so an edit moves them and an unlinked type takes them away); discovered payments are appended and stand alone, surfaced as orphaned if the source row disappears. A dismissal removes that debt as a claimant inside the discovery pass (DiscoveryInput.dismissedKeys), so a row also matched by another debt becomes an ordinary payment for it instead of an unanswerable ambiguity. Dismissals are one app_setting keyed by paymentSourceKey; ambiguities are dismissed per row (AMBIGUOUS:<id>). unresolvedAmbiguities subtracts answers found in the payment table. commitImport runs syncDiscoveredPayments after the settlement rescan, so order does not matter. deleteDebt refuses while there is history. No foreign keys (D-067).
state: live

### D-085
date: 2026-08-22
milestone: M6
scope: ledger
title: One question per line, and the kind decides which one
question: How are F08's debt screens laid out, and when does Pays into offer a debt?
answer: Five screens: #/wealth/debts, debt detail, the editor, the payment form and the review queue, with Debts second in Wealth beside savings. The review queue is a toggle beside a count on the debts screen, not a tab — and accepting items changes only marks, never a figure, since unconfirmed payments already count. The Pays into select offers a fund or goal when the type counts as saving and a debt when kind is LOAN_REPAYMENT: two gates for two questions. One destination per line, all three columns written on every pick. The editor computes debtWarnings from its live draft (other debts, fixed expenses), not from a prop captured when it opened. balanceOverTime is drawn as a step and steps by principal. debtsSummary computes every row's balance and projection in one call, including installmentCommitment for slices still to come. Every 'cannot say' from projectPayoff becomes a sentence. The monthly obligation prints what it left out and why. Linking a deduction type to a debt runs syncDiscoveredPayments in the same write.
state: live

### D-086
date: 2026-08-22
milestone: M6
scope: ledger
title: Promoting the purchase is the confirmation
question: How is a card installment group promoted to a debt?
answer: installmentCandidates and promoteInstallmentGroup, with a threshold and declines. The offer sits above the debts on the debts screen; the import notice carries only a count and a link (as for settlements, D-064), because a one-time offer after import gets dismissed by accident. Slices already charged are written confirmed, since the user just said yes to the group; a slice charged later is a new reading and goes to the review queue. The principal says whose number it is: STATED (the statement's total) or ESTIMATED (slice × count), shown before agreeing. No expected end date is stored; projectPayoff reads the rate the slices arrive at. A promoted debt tracks CARD_INSTALLMENT_GROUP, so debtObligationBasis's alreadyInSpending limb applies at once. A decline and the offers switch are separate answers, in one app_setting with the threshold; the threshold is read back through the control's floor (two payments is not a split purchase). installmentCommitment is unchanged: it counts future slices whether or not promoted. F08 complete.
state: live

### D-087
date: 2026-08-22
milestone: M6
scope: engine
title: Zero and unknown are different, and only the caller knows which
question: How do statistics avoid a chart that lies without any number being wrong?
answer: Aggregation happens in SQL and arithmetic happens in the domain. Four shapes hold. ZERO AND UNKNOWN ARE DIFFERENT: a year is twelve `Summary | undefined` slots and a trend point's value may be undefined, so a period nobody imported is a gap, never a claim that spending stopped. A PARTIAL PERIOD IS NOT AN AVERAGE: isComplete travels with every observation, and no averaging function takes a flag to include one. Saving is not spending. A COMPARISON THAT CANNOT BE MADE IS A VARIANT, because 'unchanged' and 'there is no last year' are opposite claims and a zero delta says the first. When two sources could both count one thing, split it into two fields and add them in exactly one place. Only the layer that knows the answer decides it: a chart receiving an empty list cannot tell 'nothing' from 'no data', so the report layer, which knows whether the period exists, says so. A figure computed anywhere else quietly lies. Totals are derived from the same rows the table prints, never a second query. Windows are over periods that exist. A trend line refuses one point (NEEDS_TWO_POINTS), and a rolling window with a hole is refused. No materialised table without profiling.
state: live

### D-088
date: 2026-08-22
milestone: M6
scope: ledger
title: A rolled-up total has to drill into a rolled-up ledger
question: How does the monthly summary work, and how does a rolled-up category drill into the ledger?
answer: The monthly summary with a hand-rolled donut (24pt stroke, 2pt gaps) and a cumulative spend curve with last month ghosted, every figure from one monthlyReport call so nothing can disagree. Category colour is stored as a token name, never a hex (two themes); a category with none gets a stable slot hashed from its id, never its position. Uncategorized is a grey wedge, a row, and inside the total. Drilling a rolled-up parent must open the ledger rolled up too: TransactionFilter.includeSubcategories, off by default and cleared when a category is picked by hand. Category colour variables are plain :root rules for both themes, because Tailwind v4 tree-shakes @theme variables it never sees in a class name. The projection is a figure under the curve, with no verdict or colour. Amended: D-105 added the colour picker, D-161 resolves automatic colours per taxonomy level, and D-157 removed Insights as a section (the month is Home).
state: amended

### D-089
date: 2026-08-22
milestone: M6
scope: engine
title: A gap in a bar chart has to be drawn as nothing
question: How does a chart draw a period with no data, and how is 'which rows count' kept written once?
answer: A missing period is drawn as NOTHING. Twelve zero-height bars and a genuine gap look identical and mean opposite things, and the table row says 'nothing imported' instead of printing zeroes. A period still running is a third state: drawn dimmed, included in totals but not in averages or best-and-worst. A block about a different source (payslips) is its own call carrying how many records it read, and says so in a sentence when there are none, instead of showing ₪0. One query serves a month and a year by taking a date prefix (an ISO date is a prefix of itself), so the definition of 'spending' is written once. An average divides by the periods the document holds, not by twelve, and says so. Every row drills into a ledger view whose figures match (D-088). Series are tints of ink, not hues; the category palette is the one exception. Tailwind v4 emits only @theme variables seen in a class name, so anything read at runtime via var() must be in a plain :root rule or already used as a class. A section with a second sibling becomes a sub-nav, and the old route forwards rather than breaking.
state: live

### D-090
date: 2026-08-22
milestone: M6
scope: engine
title: An absent figure has to say why it is absent
question: When a figure is withheld for a good reason, what does the screen show?
answer: The reason, where the number would have been. A rolling total refused because every window has a hole simply vanished, and silence is indistinguishable from a chart that failed to render. It now says 'no rolling 12-month total, every window is missing a month'. The app's habit everywhere: a basis names what it could not subtract, and a balance lists the payments in another currency. A window is FILLED IN, not taken from the data: every period in the span is a point even when empty, or 'the last two years' silently compresses to the nine months that exist. The caption says '11 of 24 months have data'. Inference is drawn as inference: the trend line is dashed ink-tertiary beside the solid line of what happened, and refused under two points. A percentage whose denominator is a LIMIT says so ('of these fifteen'). A second series on its own scale is honest only when the quantities are not comparable, and the caption says which is which. A leaderboard carries how many distinct periods each entry appears in, so one big event does not outrank a habit. Queries share one `spendable(alias)` definition, never a copy.
state: live

### D-091
date: 2026-08-23
milestone: M6
scope: engine
title: A detail has to add up to the figure above it
question: When a screen breaks a headline figure into parts, what must hold?
answer: The parts reconstruct the whole, or the detail is worse than none: it makes the reader distrust both numbers. Build parts from ONE grouping of ONE scan, using the same predicate the total uses, and take the remainder by subtraction rather than a second count that has to keep agreeing. An item belongs to exactly one bucket, and the precedence is written in one place (the more specific statement, a merchant pattern, settles before a category), read by both the query's CASE and the domain. Classify what actually happened, not what was declared: a yearly premium counts in the month it lands. Something never in the ledger (a payroll deduction) is on neither side. Absences are kept distinct ('no charge arrived', 'not one of its months', 'nothing can check it'); only one is a question, and a bimonthly bill in its off month is NOT_DUE, not missing. A percentage's denominator is the figure printed on the same screen. A share is its own division, never 100 minus the other, because the screen doing arithmetic is what the layer split stops. Expensive checks are gated on the condition that makes them possible, if the gate changes no answer.
state: live

### D-092
date: 2026-08-23
milestone: M7
scope: engine
title: A review queue you have to empty by hand is the plugin handed back
question: Should the MCP plugin only propose changes for the app to confirm, or read and write directly?
answer: It reads and writes directly, and lives in this repo as a third build output. 'Propose, don't dispose' fails its first real use: four hundred categorised rows ending as four hundred rows to confirm on a screen saves nothing. The confirmation MOVES rather than disappearing. A mutating tool dry-runs by default, the user answers in the conversation, and committing is a second call. Two things protect what the queue did: a timestamped .bak before any mutating batch, and provenance kept as a label (categorySource 'AI' now means 'how the row got here', not 'awaiting a human'). The plugin writes THROUGH the app's repository functions, so user overrides still win. Rules and imports keep their own gates: a rule is shown its matching rows first, and an import that does not reconcile is refused exactly as in the app. With two writers on one file, the app gained an mtime check and offers to reload. The plugin never migrates (trap 1). Tool names are an API from the first stranger's conversation. There is no SQL string in src/plugin/, which is the mechanical test for 'a wrapper, not a fork'. Later, D-114 found the prose gate holds only in Claude Code, and D-124 removed the in-app remote AI (M8).
supersedes: D-010
state: live

### D-093
date: 2026-08-23
milestone: M7
scope: engine
title: The plugin wraps, and the one thing it could not borrow was a read-only question
question: How is the plugin built so that it wraps the app rather than forking it, and how does it ask a document its schema without changing it?
answer: src/plugin/ contains no SQL, enforced by a test, and reads only through repository functions. The one missing piece, reading the schema version without the migrator creating schema_version, was added BESIDE migrate in data/db/database.ts (readSchemaVersion, openDatabaseWithoutMigrating), never in the plugin. A plugin with its own way to open a document is the first step of a fork. The long name is the guard: it reads as a warning against reaching for openDatabase by habit. The gate refuses in both directions with different advice: an older document is fixed by opening it in the app, a newer one by rebuilding the plugin. Use @modelcontextprotocol/sdk's low-level Server (not McpServer, so no zod and the schemas are literal JSON), never hand-rolled JSON-RPC. The build is an SSR build like Vitest, with sql.js and the SDK external, so dist-plugin/server.mjs is the one output resolving node_modules. A refused document is a successful tool call carrying the sentence, not a protocol error. The tool is `document_info`, not `open_document`: nothing is held open between calls. The zero-network test proves its spy is live by catching a real call.
state: live

### D-094
date: 2026-08-23
milestone: M7
scope: engine
title: Read-only is a mode SQLite enforces, not a promise the plugin makes
question: How are the plugin's read tools guaranteed read-only and consistent with the screens?
answer: PRAGMA query_only on every open, so a read tool cannot write by any route; writing is a line somebody adds on purpose (setQueryOnly(db, false)). The gate is structural: every tool goes through one readDocument(path, read), which opens, gates, reads and closes in a finally, so no tool can skip the schema check. Every shaped tool calls the same single function its screen calls, so a figure in conversation and on screen is the same arithmetic. A capped list is totalled with the UNCAPPED summary, and every list tool caps and says so. Nothing prettifies a user's string (no bidi marks, trimming or case change): a merchant name must round-trip byte-exact back into a write tool. Only table-format escaping is allowed. The no-SQL rule bans statement shapes everywhere and strips comments, because a rule that punishes explaining yourself gets deleted. The plugin has its own today(), duplicated rather than importing React-land or putting a clock in core/. No structuredContent: the consumer is a conversation. AMENDED by D-095: the general `query` tool built here was removed one commit later.
state: amended

### D-095
date: 2026-08-23
milestone: M7
scope: engine
title: A shaped tool is a capability; a general query is all of them
question: Should the plugin offer a general query tool?
answer: No. The plugin has no general query tool, and no tool takes SQL or any query language from its caller. Every capability is a named, shaped tool. Three reasons, each stronger than durability. SURFACE: the tool list is what the plugin exposes, and a general query silently means 'and everything else' (it reached all 29 tables where nine tools reached about eleven, including every payslip line). GATING, the decisive one: any capability ever gated is reconstructible with a SELECT, so a general query makes every future gate unenforceable, and removing it later breaks someone's conversation while not shipping it costs nothing. TRUST: the caller is an LLM reading text from statement files. It costs the document's owner nothing, because the document is plain SQLite that sqlite3 opens; the escape hatch exists outside the trust boundary. A question no tool answers is a request for a new shaped tool, never a reason to reopen the hatch. A flag-gated query is rejected: a gate a flag can flip is not a gate. The no-SQL test has no exemptions and asserts the query tool has not come back.
supersedes: D-094
state: live

### D-096
date: 2026-08-23
milestone: M7
scope: engine
title: The safest write in the plugin is the one that makes a file that does not exist
question: Where should the plugin's write path be introduced first, and how does conversational onboarding work?
answer: With create_document and onboarding, before the general write tools. Creating a file needs none of the batch safeguards (nothing to back up, no rows to label), but it exercises the whole write path (query_only off, export, atomic rename) on a file where a bug costs nothing. Prove the hardest thing where failure is free. create_document refuses an existing path, with no overwrite. The flow drives the pure domain step machine and never reimplements it; progress lives in app_setting, so a half-onboarded document resumes in either front end. Elicitation (Claude Code renders forms) is an enhancement, never a requirement: every tool takes its answers as ordinary arguments, and a cancel, decline or absent capability falls back to conversation. A cancel with nothing attached is the everyday case. NAMING RULE: reads are noun phrases (month_summary), writes are verb phrases (create_document), so a transcript says what happened and readOnlyHint is not the only signal. A step whose capability is not built yet is skipped, never faked.
state: live

### D-097
date: 2026-08-23
milestone: M7
scope: engine
title: The second front end is what found the rule in the wrong layer
question: What did building conversational onboarding reveal, and how are its writes made safe without a backup?
answer: A second front end reveals the seam: what a ticked institution produces lived as private functions in a React screen. The plugin could neither import React nor reimplement it, so it moved to the repository and both call it. A rule by the 'rules, not layout' test belongs below the UI. NO_ONBOARDING stands in for a backup: a document holding work is never in onboarding, so every write lands on content created minutes ago, and all writing tools CHECK it rather than trusting the caller (a mistyped path opens someone else's file). create_document uses the `wx` open flag, not check-then-write, because the gap between them is where a document gets overwritten; there is no force flag. The write is atomic from the first write: temp file beside the target, renamed over it, exported only if the mutation returned, with the temp unlinked on failure. ToolContext.elicit exists only when the client declared it, and a thrown elicitation folds into cancel, so the optional thing is never load-bearing. The naming rule is asserted: noun reads, verb writes, and the hint matches.
state: live

### D-098
date: 2026-08-23
milestone: M7
scope: engine
title: A preview you cannot write from, a copy you can find, a label you cannot lose
question: What exactly do the dry run, the backup and provenance guarantee for a batch write?
answer: mutatingCall (plan, describe, apply) is the shape every mutating tool reuses; there is no second write path. The dry run goes through readDocument under query_only, so a preview that tried to write fails loudly. Order: gate, then copy, then write. The backup is taken after the schema gate passes, so a refused document leaves no meaningless .bak. A failed backup REFUSES the write. The plan is RECOMPUTED at commit inside its own transaction, never carried over, because the document can change between calls; a difference from the preview is reported as news. .bak policy: beside the document, sortable UTC timestamp, last five kept, pruned by mtime, and the tool says where it went. What a write refuses is most of what it is. A MANUAL assignment is never overwritten, and a RULE row is left to its rule; both are counted and named in the diff ('34 rows you categorised by hand were skipped'). AI is a label, not a lock. Provenance has its own repository function and does not fake human-override rows, which would feed learning its own output. Dry-run-by-default is for batches whose size you cannot see from the call; a write touching exactly the ids passed commits directly.
state: amended

### D-099
date: 2026-08-23
milestone: M7
scope: engine
title: A rule keeps firing, so a dry run is not enough of a gate
question: A rule keeps firing on every future import. Is a dry-run default enough of a gate for creating one?
answer: No. A default is exactly what an LLM steps past with commit: true on the first call. create_rule's commit requires a CONFIRMATION TOKEN only the preview can produce, derived from the rule's shape AND the matching row ids. So there is no path to a written rule that skipped the evidence, and the token goes stale the moment the match set changes. The token check runs in its own read pass before mutatingCall, so a refused commit leaves no .bak. A caller-synthesisable confirmation (echoing a count) is rejected, because it does not bind to which rows. The tool applies the rule itself, so rows read RULE with the rule named, not AI; provenance answers 'why is this here'. inTransaction now NESTS: the outermost call opens a transaction and inner calls take savepoints, so an inner block rolls back alone and the whole commits as a unit. This fixed a latent app bug rather than reimplementing commitReapply, and it never detects an open transaction by catching an error message. The preview names older rules that outrank the new one. Driving the built server caught an empty rule name no test saw: drive it, don't only test it.
state: live

### D-100
date: 2026-08-23
milestone: M7
scope: engine
title: The importer runs in Node, so ingestion was the tool set, not the parser
question: How does a statement get imported from a conversation, and does the plugin's import behave exactly like the app's?
answer: Nothing in src/plugin/ parses a statement: the app's importer (readWorkbook, mapping match and guess, natural keys, printed total, stage, commitSession) runs in Node unchanged, and idempotence, rules, settlement and debt discovery came along for free. Two tools, because 'the user maps the columns' is a rule (D-013): statement_preview shows the header, columns and the guess, and import_statement takes the answer. A saved mapping matched by header signature skips the question, and save_mapping_as makes one. The staging area is not used as a queue: the confirmation already happened in the conversation, so stage, accept and commit are one call in one transaction. Duplicates stay unaccepted, so re-importing is a no-op. The printed-total check is STRICTER than the app, deliberately: the app lets a person looking at the table commit a mismatch, but in a conversation nobody is looking, so the plugin refuses by default (ignore_printed_total overrides). Callers in different positions may diverge, and that is recorded, not smoothed. The refusal runs before the backup in its own read pass. A supplied mapping's amount convention is read off its roles. Never guess a mapping and import without asking.
state: live

### D-101
date: 2026-08-23
milestone: M7
scope: engine
title: The way to reach everyone is the app's own AI, not somebody else's client
question: Is the MCP plugin the route to non-technical users, or should the app have its own AI behind a proxy and subscription?
answer: SUPERSEDED (deferred by D-102, removed by D-124): there is no proxy, no subscription and no remote AI. What survives is the measurement: a local MCP server reaches Claude Desktop and Claude Code only, never web or mobile, so the plugin is for people who will install something. M7's tools are the tool layer, already shaped like API tool use, and the dry run, backup, provenance and MANUAL protection are the safeguards any front end needs. Also still true: a general query tool would route around any gate (D-095). The proposal was a proxy-backed in-app AI split by payload: intake sends only what the user handed over, and conversation sends the ledger, with two consents. Bring-your-own-key and the single-file build were kept free of it. Its rejected alternatives remain rejected: never ship an API key in a client, and never a hosted service that stores users' financial data.
state: superseded

### D-102
date: 2026-08-23
milestone: M7
scope: engine
title: Deferred: the in-app AI product is a bigger product, and RiseUp's
question: Should D-101's proxy-and-subscription AI product be adopted?
answer: No, deferred, and later removed outright by D-124. Two reasons, neither about the design. It is a bigger product: a proxy is a backend (auth, billing, metering, uptime, custody of a promise about other people's financial data), which is a company, arriving before the app has a user other than its author. And it aims at ground RiseUp holds with a team and bank connections, while what this project has that they cannot offer is data in a file you own, on your machine, with no account. Kept from the exercise: local MCP reaches only Claude Desktop and Claude Code; the importer is market-agnostic by construction (no per-provider parsers, rulepacks per market); and an unchecked claim about free plans did not survive checking the docs. Analysis is archived, not deleted, so it is not re-derived badly. The trigger to revisit was enough users wanting their finances on a phone for a backend to pay for itself.
supersedes: D-101
state: superseded

### D-103
date: 2026-08-23
milestone: M7
scope: engine
title: A save is a claim about a file you last saw minutes ago
question: The app writes the whole document back on save. How does it avoid silently undoing another writer's changes?
answer: It checks the file's mark (mtime + size, never a hash, since hashing makes focus cost as much as opening) before every save and on every window focus. On a moved mark it REFUSES to save and offers reload, save a copy, or overwrite deliberately. Save is where the loss is prevented; focus stops an hour of typing into a stale copy. Any second writer is the hazard, not just the plugin: two tabs, a sync client, a backup restore. The UI does not pretend a choice is safe: with unsaved work on both sides the only lossless answer is a copy, so it leads, and the others say what they discard. Never choose whose work to throw away. `unwatched` is a fourth state, not a reassuring 'in sync': a new document, a crash-snapshot restore and the download adapter have nothing to compare, and saving is never blocked there. Only openFile, save, saveAs and reloadFromDisk record a mark; openBytes clears it. The policy is a pure function (compareToDisk). save({overwrite:true}) is the only way past it. No merging, since there is one file and you own it. D-127 later added a real directory watcher in the shell; the focus check stays as a second trigger.
state: live

### D-104
date: 2026-08-23
milestone: M7
scope: engine
title: A backlog that hands back the same forty names is a conversation with no memory
question: How does the categorisation backlog stop handing a conversation the same names it already went through?
answer: A memo: note_merchant_suggestions records, per normalised merchant, SUGGESTED, DECLINED (do not offer that category again) or NO_SUGGESTION. The backlog shows it as a column and hides nothing (report the evidence, do not act on it). It lives in one app_setting key with no migration: nothing references a note, and no migration means no schema gate for existing documents. It is capped at 500, oldest dropped first. Assignment clears the note, because the question is answered. The provider's own category is shown for the rows in view (several, most common first, never collapsed into false certainty), scoped by the caller's filter because it is a fact about those rows. The tool writes immediately with no dry run and no .bak: those guard batches of money rows, and a safeguard that costs more than what it protects gets routed around. It still goes through writeDocument, with no second write path. Driving the built server found two bugs tests could not see. SheetJS is CJS, so under Node's ESM interop `XLSX.SSF` is undefined (ssfFrom reads either shape). And document_info printed no account ids while three tools said to get ids there.
state: live

### D-105
date: 2026-08-24
milestone: M6
scope: ledger
title: A swatch grid rather than a colour wheel, because the palette knows about the second theme
question: How does a user choose a category's colour and icon?
answer: One popover on the Categories screen and one writer, setCategoryAppearance, writing colour and icon together (one decision, one mutation, one crash-cache snapshot); undefined clears either. Colour is the palette's eight --color-category-* token names plus 'automatic', never a hex input: a hex is a claim about one theme, and the palette carries a lifted dark variant of every hue (D-088). 'Automatic' is a swatch showing the hashed slot the category actually lands on, not an empty state. Legality lives with the palette: the repository stores any string, ui/charts/categoryColor.ts falls back to the hashed slot for unknown names. System categories may be coloured (renaming them is what refuses). The icon is sixteen suggestions plus a one-emoji field (firstGrapheme in core/text.ts, because an emoji is not one code point); in CategoryPicker an icon replaces the colour dot, and a hollow dot means nothing chosen, distinct from Uncategorized grey. No migration. Amended by D-161: 'automatic' now resolves per taxonomy level rather than by the bare hash.
state: amended

### D-106
date: 2026-08-24
milestone: M6
scope: engine
title: Nothing measured it is not the same as it measured nothing, and neither is a verdict
question: How is a plan compared with what actually happened when some lines cannot be measured at all?
answer: The domain does the subtraction and, more importantly, the bookkeeping about what is COMPARABLE; the repository takes the measurements. A line nothing could confirm or deny (a FREE bucket) is absent from both totals, so the two headline figures are sums over the same set of lines, which is the only way subtracting them means anything. It still appears in the table, so the comparison does not disagree with the plan above it. A MEASURED zero is kept, because it is the most interesting answer (D-087: zero and unknown differ, and only the caller knows which). A period with no ledger data takes the ledger-backed bases out and says so, rather than showing zeros; hand-recorded figures still count. The verdict is a word and a number, never green or red, because OVER is opposite news on groceries and on savings, and the app gives no advice. There is no tolerance band: ON_PLAN means equal to the agora, and the difference column says how close. The figures come from the SAME function the report screen uses, never a query that ought to agree with it. monthStart/monthEnd moved to core/time.ts on their third private copy, because every copy of daysInMonth is another place February can be wrong.
state: live

### D-107
date: 2026-08-24
milestone: M6
scope: ledger
title: A net worth of what the document knows, which is not everything you own
question: What does the net worth figure count, and how is its history drawn?
answer: domain/netWorth.ts (netWorthAt, netWorthOver) and a screen at #/wealth/worth. The figure is savings minus debts and says so at the top; it never guesses a current-account balance, car or home (bank balances would double count deposits held in a CHECKING place). gaps names everything the document could have counted and could not — a fund with no reading is a gap, never zero. Two figures: assets − liabilities, and 'if you settled up today' = liquid − liabilities (liquiditySplit, D-074); the difference is the locked money. Negative is not clamped. The 24-month line calls netWorthAt per month at the month's last day, so the chart's end equals the headline by construction. A fund counts from its first reading, using the latest snapshot on or before each month, never interpolated. isClosed is ignored by history (no date on it); debts opened after the date are excluded. The report loads closed goals, archived funds and places, and closed debts. netWorthRepository owns no table, deliberately. TrendChart gains a zero baseline drawn only when a series goes negative. No migration.
state: live

### D-108
date: 2026-08-24
milestone: M6
scope: ledger
title: Home is the root, it computes nothing, and it is ordered by consequence rather than by count
question: What is at #/, and how does Home decide what to show?
answer: #/ is Home; the ledger moves to #/transactions, and every drill-through names /transactions explicitly (a root that ignored navigation state would have 'worked' and shown the wrong thing). Unknown hashes and leaving onboarding land on Home. Home computes nothing: every figure is monthlyReport or netWorthReport, the same calls the linked screens make. The attention list is ranked by consequence in a pure, tested function (domain/home.ts): unfiled transactions first however few, since they make every other total wrong. Nothing with a count of zero appears; an empty month gets a sentence, not zeros. The getting-started checklist moves off the global banner onto Home; checklistDismissed is still honoured and ChecklistCard is deleted. homeRepository owns no table. Closed M6. Amended twice: D-157 rebuilt the nav (Insights folded into Home as periods, Paychecks top level) and D-160 ranked pending settlements above unfiled rows.
state: amended

### D-109
date: 2026-08-24
milestone: M7
scope: engine
title: A question no tool answered, answered the way D-095 said to: a twenty-first tool
question: A conversation could not add a category. What does D-095's rule require, and what did answering it find?
answer: A new shaped tool, create_category, never a reopened query hatch. The rule held the first time it cost something: one file and seven tests. An additive write (creates, changes nothing) needs no dry run and no .bak: there is no prior state to hold, and the undo is archiving. An archived twin is RESTORED rather than duplicated, because a second 'Groceries' splits history silently. A live twin creates nothing and returns its id, so the tool is idempotent under an LLM's retry. The subcategory-inherits-parent's-kind rule lived in a React form and moved into createCategory. A rule enforced by one caller is a rule only until there is a second caller, so every rule in ui/ is a question about which front end it applies to. Shared prose names the task, never one front end's gesture ('drop the file in this window' is wrong in a conversation): the prose is the interface. Do not repeat a count in prose: the tool count was wrong in five places, so the test pins the names, not the number.
state: live

### D-110
date: 2026-08-24
milestone: M7
scope: engine
title: The round trip was the bill, the gate was in the wrong place, and nothing said what the helper was for
question: What did the first real session with the plugin show about cost, gating and guidance?
answer: Three findings. (1) THE BILL IS ROUND TRIPS, NOT OUTPUT: every tool call is a model turn re-sending the whole transcript, so tools take LISTS (one preview, one confirmation, one .bak per batch). Onboarding went from 8 calls to 4. Entries apply in order against the live document, so a parent and its child go in one call. The old single shape is REFUSED with the sentence that rewrites it, never kept beside the list, because a surface with two shapes cannot be kept honest. (2) An invariant that justified skipping a backup had been written as a refusal. The state now decides HOW a write is protected (writeWithBackup takes a .bak outside onboarding) rather than WHETHER it happens. The dangerous argument stays gated by state (keep_ids archives everything else, so it is refused after the first run). Ungating is not removing the reasoning. (3) Nothing said what the helper is FOR: the MCP `instructions` string (rendered by Claude Code, sent once, cached) carries the goal, working practices, vocabulary and a 'Not here' list. A test asserts the capability: every tool it names exists, and every screen it calls app-only has no matching tool, so the day that changes it goes red. Tool results return the new state; never tell a caller to re-read it.
state: live

### D-111
date: 2026-08-24
milestone: M7
scope: ledger
title: The detector had been built for four milestones and the conversation could not ask it anything
question: How does a conversation reach F09's fixed-expense detector?
answer: Three plugin tools — fixed_expense_candidates, track_fixed_expenses, dismiss_fixed_expense_candidates — wrapping detectFixedExpenses, confirmCandidate and the dismissal set, nothing new below the plugin. The tell was D-109's: a read tool with no matching write. A candidate is never stored, so tools name a merchant, not an id, and the batch is re-detected at commit time (a merchant tracked or dismissed since the preview gets an honest 'already tracked'). Two verbs, because confirming writes a standing commitment and wants the dry run and .bak, while dismissing writes one app_setting key and writes immediately. A window cannot see a cadence longer than itself, so empty and short-window answers say so in words — what a screen shows structurally, a tool must say. The plugin writes only MERCHANT_PATTERN and MANUAL_ONLY; PAYCHECK_DEDUCTION and CATEGORY stay app-only. A merchant the detector did not offer is accepted with expected_amount and lands as USER_DEFINED. server.ts stops counting its tools in prose.
state: live

### D-112
date: 2026-08-24
milestone: M7
scope: ledger
title: The payslip and the fund report are one question, and neither half was reachable
question: How does a conversation read and write payslips and fund reports?
answer: Four tools — paychecks, record_paychecks, funds, record_fund_readings — over paycheckRepository and fundRepository, no schema change, built together because F13's point (deducted on the slip vs received by the fund) lives in the join; the answer states the unaccounted amount in a sentence. paychecks returns the deduction-type vocabulary every time (a round trip is the bill, D-110). The app-only 'Pays into' link (linkedFundAccountId, D-073) is named in three places — paychecks, record_paychecks, funds — because without it a fund reports no payslip data forever. record_fund_readings groups entries by fund name: attach to an existing fund or create it once per batch, said loudly in the dry run. Nothing edits or deletes; an entry that names an existing fund and also describes it is refused. A non-zero reconciliation delta is written with the difference named, never refused. A second slip for a month is allowed and flagged. Fees are integer basis points. Readings are REPORT_ENTRY; an absent figure means 'the report did not say', a zero is a claim. The write reports where the deposit check stands afterwards.
state: live

### D-113
date: 2026-08-24
milestone: M7
scope: ledger
title: The two things no file contains: what you are saving for and what you owe
question: How does a conversation record savings goals, deposits, debts and debt payments?
answer: Four tools — create_saving_goals, record_deposits, create_debts, record_debt_payments — over savingsRepository and debtRepository, no schema change; the savings and debts reads already existed. These record what the user knows rather than what a file says. Out of scope: the debt-payment review queue and installment-group promotion, because those answer what the app discovered, not what the user said. A holding place is an object needing kind and is_liquid, never inferred. MODE_SOURCE moved into domain/depositEntry.ts. The plan threads its own effects within a batch (a place created then deposited into; a withdrawal after an opening balance). record_deposits and record_debt_payments refuse unknown names rather than creating, since a typo would split a balance (the opposite of record_fund_readings). An emergency goal takes a months multiplier or a target, never both, in basis points. A withdrawal must say why and a LOSS must not (D-081). CARD_INSTALLMENT_GROUP tracking is refused; merchant or category tracking is told discovery lands unconfirmed in an app-only queue.
state: live

### D-114
date: 2026-08-24
milestone: M9
scope: engine
title: The write gate is prose, and prose holds only in Claude Code
question: Does the plugin's surface hold up when the conversation is driven by a local model rather than Claude?
answer: Measured with scripts/local-host.mjs (committed, so it is re-run rather than re-derived). Five findings. (1) The tool list has a hardware cost: 32 schemas plus instructions was ~13.8k tokens a turn, which pushed an 8B model off an 8GB GPU (170s a turn). Five tools took it to 37s. MCP gives the server no say, because subsetting is the client's act. (2) Reading is reliable and deciding is not: a payslip was transcribed to the agora, while every failure was a judgement. Draw the hybrid line at transcription vs judgement, not sensitive vs not. (3) The dry-run/commit gate is prose, and prose gave way under 'record it'. It replaced the app's review screen, so write safety is a property of the client's discipline, and the server cannot tell which client it has. A server-side gate is rejected because the server is stateless per call. (4) A tool printed amounts where the privacy rule says it must not; reads disclose. (5) Nothing said imported text is data, not instruction, and now the instructions do. Merchant identity is a rulepack problem: models 4x larger made the same errors on Israeli brands.
state: live

### D-115
date: 2026-08-24
milestone: M9
scope: engine
title: The document-based app gets to open its own documents: a desktop shell
question: Should the app become a desktop application, and what must not change when it does?
answer: Yes: an Electron shell, with the static dist/ kept. There are two reasons. The load-bearing one: the app was document-based because IndexedDB is blocked on file://, and everything awkward (polling mtime on focus, whole-file writes, no double-click to open) came from that. The second is that D-114 showed a local model works only when the host cuts the tool list, preloads vocabulary and enforces the gate in code, which a host we own can do and a browser tab or Claude Code cannot. THE RULE THIS LEAVES: document-based stops being a constraint and becomes a choice. Once the shell could store state anywhere, making the app stateful 'because it finally could' is the specific mistake to avoid. The file the user owns is the product. What was given up: the emailable single HTML file, code signing (the app ships unsigned, D-124), and per-platform builds. The size budget and no-CDN rule must now be kept deliberately. Tauri was rejected because Electron reuses the Node plugin server and 150MB is nothing beside a model. An in-browser WebGPU model was rejected. Money, domain purity, bidi, hash routing and no real statements in the repo do not move.
supersedes: D-021
state: live

### D-116
date: 2026-08-24
milestone: M6.5
scope: ledger
title: M8 is deleted, and M6.5 loses the three lines that were not hardening
question: Should M8 (in-app cloud AI) survive, and what belongs in M6.5?
answer: M8 is removed and deferred with a named trigger: someone who cannot run a local model, will not install the plugin, and wants this anyway. Its three capabilities are already covered by the plugin talking to Claude and by M11 against a local provider; what remained (key storage, consent, payload preview, call log, cost cap) exists only for the app calling a cloud API, whose justification died with D-115. F11 stops being a milestone and becomes the privacy specification any remote provider must meet. M6.5 drops the 60k performance pass (measure, don't schedule — it rides with the migration fixture), soft-delete (undo, not durability) and rule/mapping export (no consumer; the file is plain SQLite); CSV export stays. M6.5 is four lines: migration tests, CSV export, growing il-core.json, and D-114's two plugin items. The empty/loading/error audit moves to M9. Order: M6.5 → M9 → M10 → M11. Amended by D-124, which removed remote AI outright, so F11's role as a standing constraint is moot.
state: amended

### D-117
date: 2026-08-25
milestone: M9
scope: engine
title: The app is called Emerald, the pitch is the tool rather than the ledger, and green stays a signal
question: What is the app called, how is it pitched, and does its colour enter the interface?
answer: Emerald. The pitch leads with what the app refuses to do: no account, no server, no network call, a file you own. It does not lead with features. The honest difference from Obsidian is said in the pitch: it is a SQLite file, which sqlite3 opens and whose schema is documented, so the data outlives the project. The name is crowded (Emerald Chat, Emerald24 in money transfer, and more), which is a discoverability price, not a legal problem. Never expect a clean domain. `emerald` is the id everywhere (package, app, MCP server), and disambiguation lives in the server's instructions header. The rename was one unit at the head of M9, before the file association, because after an OS registers an extension a rename is a migration. AMENDED: D-125 made emerald the interface accent after all, keeping green-as-signal by separating the two greens by role (`accent` marks what you can act on, `positive` reports what the app found, and accent never colours a figure). D-156 replaced `.emerald` with `.emd`.
state: amended

### D-118
date: 2026-08-25
milestone: M6.5
scope: engine
title: Ten years of data measured, no Web Worker, and the one read that was loading the ledger into JS
question: Is 60k rows fast enough for sql.js on the main thread, or does the app need a Web Worker?
answer: Measured, not asserted: no Web Worker, and sql.js stays on the main thread. src/data/scale.test.ts builds ten years (120 months x 500 rows) and times every read a screen makes. Count is 6ms, the full summary 39ms, the monthly report about 390ms, the dashboard about 414ms, save/reopen 5-6ms, a 60k import about 3.2s, and the document about 26MB. The trigger for a Worker is now a failing test, not a feeling: any read over one second fails the build. Ceilings are loose on purpose, since a tight timing assertion flakes and encodes one machine; the thing worth catching is an order of magnitude. Import and save are excluded as explicit waits. The measurement found the real bug that justified it: the dashboard's debt summary read every outflow into a JS object per row to find installment slices. Pushing the filter into SQL (installmentSlicesOnly) took it from 613ms to 45ms. The rule it enforces: statistics are SQL aggregations, never rows loaded into JS. Add an option rather than narrowing a shared read other callers depend on. Time a screen by part, since a regression lives in a part. A fixture that exists for volume is generated; one that exists for values is hand-written.
state: live

### D-119
date: 2026-08-25
milestone: M6.5
scope: engine
title: Two CSVs, a byte-order mark, and a merchant name that is a formula
question: What does CSV export produce, and what has to be in the file for it to be correct and safe?
answer: Two files with a named reader and no more: the ledger as filtered, and the monthly summary. The .emd is already SQLite that sqlite3 opens, so portability was never missing; a spreadsheet you can sort was. It is uncapped (limit -1), because a total over the first 500 rows is a smaller number, not a truer one, and the button says which it takes. Three easy-to-omit things. A UTF-8 BOM, or Excel on Hebrew Windows guesses CP1255 and the file is unreadable to its one reader while the numbers survive, which is the kind of bug that ships. Plain decimal amounts via toDecimalString in core/money.ts, which inserts the point into the minor-unit digits and never divides by 100 (formatMoney is for people, this is for parsers). An empty cell for an unknown rate, never zero. The formula guard: imported text is data, so a field starting = + - @ tab or CR gets an apostrophe prefix, EXCEPT plain numbers, or every outflow becomes text and Amount stops summing. Guard before quoting. The guard is a tested domain function, so the next export cannot forget it.
state: live

### D-120
date: 2026-08-25
milestone: M6.5
scope: engine
title: The rulepack learns the three brands, the backlog stops printing spending, and a statement is not an instruction
question: How are D-114's three findings answered: the brands models misfile, a read that discloses spending, and imported text reaching a model unlabelled?
answer: Merchant identity is a rulepack problem, and a line of JSON beats a GPU: il-core.json went to 180 rules, with the three brands pinned by tests. A short Latin brand needs a word-boundary REGEX or stays out; CONTAINS 'ACE' matches SPACE, and REGEX outranks CONTAINS (D-037), so a false positive would beat a correct rule. Rules normalise both sides (trailing digits are stripped), so write patterns in the compared form. In JSON, "\b" is backspace; a test now rejects any control character in a pattern. The backlog tool stops printing amounts by default (include_amounts to ask): the totals still sort it, but no amount helps decide a category. Disclose only what the question needs. Minimum payload is a claim about categorisation, not every read. A statement is not an instruction: the server's `instructions` say imported text (merchants, labels, notes, file names) is data. It lives in the instructions, rendered once, not in every tool description (D-110: shared text belongs where it is paid for once). This is the same principle as D-119's CSV guard.
state: live

### D-121
date: 2026-08-25
milestone: M6.5
scope: ledger
title: The plugin's rule writer was asking the wrong question about what rules exist
question: Why did the plugin's create_rule re-apply miss rows, and how is it fixed?
answer: create_rule now reads the rule set with loadRules, not listRules, in both the preview's shadowing check and the committed re-apply — ruleSetRepository's stated invariant is that loadRules is the one answer to 'what rules are there'. Using listRules left out every built-in, so the plugin's re-apply silently skipped rows a built-in should claim (routine, since the pack grows between releases) and the shadowing check could not see 180 rules. Nothing was mis-filed, since planReapply never clears a category. The message counts the new rule's own rows apart from rows other rules claimed in the same pass ('usually a built-in that had never been run over these rows'), because taking credit for another rule's work is a provenance bug. Rejected: scoping the re-apply to the new rule's matches — the app re-applies the whole set, and the fix is to tell the truth about what moved. A test pins it and fails against listRules. Any listRules read in src/plugin/ is the same bug.
state: live

### D-122
date: 2026-08-25
milestone: M9
scope: engine
title: A model is handed context when it is chosen, and the Israeli parts are found rather than assumed
question: When does a local model get the context it needs, and is the claim that nothing Israeli is hard-coded true?
answer: Two additions to the plan. First, context is injected when a model is CHOSEN: that is the one moment its window, tool budget and priming are all known, and a bad fit can be told to a person rather than found as a slow wrong answer. The candidates were the deduction vocabulary, the category tree, the document's scale, and the rulepack as examples. The rulepack is applied deterministically at import and no model has ever seen it; more rules make a model's job smaller, not the model better. D-137 later found context is per task, not per model. Second, the locale claim is three-quarters true. Locale data sits in code: card-settlement patterns in domain/bankImport.ts, issuer aliases, printed-total vocabulary, the ש"ח and installment patterns, and a branch suffix stripped in core/text.ts. Israeli concepts sit in the schema's CHECK lists (fund_type, deduction kinds). Bilingual UI glosses are not a defect. That audit became M12's scope. One plain bug went on the open list: six screens hard-coded ILS instead of documentCurrency(db). Never correct a claim in prose while the code stays put.
state: live

### D-123
date: 2026-08-25
milestone: M9
scope: engine
title: The rename lands, and the mark is the one green that is not a signal
question: How is the rename to Emerald executed, and what must it not break?
answer: In one commit, before the file association, so the rename is a find-and-replace rather than a migration. Every string, the package, the MCP server id (`emerald`), the page title and the crash-snapshot key changed; the format did not, so there was no schema change and renaming a file is the upgrade. src/ui/emerald.svg is the mark and the one source for the header, tab icon, installer and document icon; they are cut from it, never redrawn. It is an <img>, not inline SVG, so it cannot inherit currentColor and drift toward a signal. Past decision entries and docs/archive were NOT renamed: the record says what was true when it was written, and entries are superseded, never edited. The checkout directory stays financial-companion/, because renaming it breaks absolute paths people already wrote. The tab icon was attached from JS because of the single-file build (retired by D-133). AMENDED: D-125 put emerald in the palette as the accent, and D-156 changed `.emerald` to `.emd`.
state: amended

### D-124
date: 2026-08-25
milestone: M9
scope: engine
title: The app is free, the download is unsigned, and nothing inside it reaches the network
question: What is the business model, and may any AI inside the app reach the network?
answer: Emerald is free: no subscription, tier, licence key or trial. Optional support is a README link, never in the app, and it unlocks nothing. The shell ships for macOS and Windows, unsigned and distributed by hand; the download page says what the Gatekeeper/SmartScreen warning is. Signing is the cost to take on when the audience stops being people the maintainer can talk to, which is a trigger. No AI inside the app reaches the network, ever: the remote path is deleted, not deferred. The local model (M10/M11) and the Claude Code plugin survive. The price and the architecture are the same decision: a subscription needs a backend, metering and custody of other people's data, and a paid local-model tier needs a licence server. Separating them is how a local-first app grows a backend by accident. F11/F15/F16 were archived, not deleted, and F11's four portable rules moved to M11: off by default, the app whole without it, nothing written without a human action, and a batch is one accept. The roadmap distinguishes 'Removed, not deferred' from 'Later'. The one network exception since is D-178's opt-in price fetch, from the main process, never the page.
supersedes: D-102
state: amended

### D-125
date: 2026-08-25
milestone: M9
scope: engine
title: Emerald becomes the accent, and the two greens are separated by role rather than hue
question: Can the mark's green be the interface accent without destroying `positive` as the signal that the money arrived?
answer: Yes, by role rather than hue. `--color-accent` is #07805F light and #34D399 dark; light is deepened because the bright gem gives 2.56:1 with white text, and the deeper one gives 4.93:1. Its variants are accent-ink, accent-hover and accent-soft, and --color-action follows it. THE RULE: `accent` marks what you can act on (filled button, link, focus ring, nav underline, chosen card, selected row) and `positive` reports what the app found. ENFORCEABLE GUARD: accent never colours a number. Every figure is ink, positive or negative, even when the figure is a link. The objection was measured: in light the two greens are 1.08:1 in luminance, effectively the same colour. So they must never appear in the same form, and colour is never the only carrier. An emerald status pill or a positive-tinted surface breaks it. Charts stay ink, because a chart is the app reporting. The dirty dot stays ink. If the pair ever confuses a real reader, `positive` moves and `accent` does not. Tailwind v4 only emits theme vars it sees used, so verify computed colours in both themes. This reverses D-117 and D-123.
supersedes: D-117
state: live

### D-126
date: 2026-08-25
milestone: M9
scope: engine
title: The shell opens a window, and the app it opens has not changed a line
question: How does the Electron shell load the app, and what does the first unit enforce?
answer: The renderer is served from `app://`, never `file://`. A file:// page gets an opaque origin: localStorage (where the crash snapshot lives) is shared with every HTML file on the machine, and the File System Access API refuses. A scheme registered standard and secure gives the page a real origin (`app://emerald`) and a secure context without the app knowing it moved. 'No network calls' becomes enforced by the process: session.webRequest.onBeforeRequest cancels everything except the app serving itself, data:, blob: and DevTools, and logs it. localhost is blocked on purpose, so a later local runtime must open its own hole on the record (it did, in the main process: D-134). The dev server exception exists only when scripts/dev-desktop.mjs sets EMERALD_DEV_SERVER_URL. Two traps. The CSP needs 'wasm-unsafe-eval' or sql.js cannot compile and there is no database; it is the narrow directive, not eval. A sandboxed preload cannot require a sibling chunk, so electron/ is built in two passes into one directory. The preload exposes a typed, frozen contract, never raw ipcRenderer. No nodeIntegration, no webview, no external-link handler.
state: live

### D-127
date: 2026-08-25
milestone: M9
scope: engine
title: The document becomes a real file, the save becomes a rename, and the app stops waiting for focus to ask
question: How does the shell read, save and watch the document on the real filesystem?
answer: A third StorageAdapter (src/data/storage/desktop.ts over electron/documents.ts), picked by feature detection, not a build flag. A save is an atomic rename: bytes go to `<doc>.saving-<pid>` and are renamed over the document, so a crash leaves the old document whole and a reader never sees half a database. The temporary is unlinked if the write fails. Because writers rename, a per-file fs.watch goes deaf or follows the wrong inode, so the WATCHER WATCHES THE DIRECTORY, filtered to one basename, with fs.watchFile polling as the fallback. Our own writes are not reported back: the watcher holds the last mark (mtime+size, never a hash) and write sets it first, which also silences no-op touches and deduplicates events. The focus check stays as a second trigger. No ambient disk authority: a path works only after coming back from a dialog the user answered (a grant), so read/write/watch refuse anything else. There is no general readFile on the bridge. src/ imports electron/bridge.ts and nothing else. A cancelled native dialog returns undefined, and save() must not clear the dirty flag on it.
state: live

### D-128
date: 2026-08-25
milestone: M9
scope: engine
title: The app opens its own documents, the recents list is what grants them, and the window learns to close
question: How does a document handed over by the OS reach the app, and how are unsaved changes protected when one arrives or the window closes?
answer: The recents list is the access model: every path in it was chosen in a dialog once, so loading it grants exactly those paths across runs, and a recent that has vanished removes itself. It lives in userData/recents.json (paths only), not localStorage, because the grants and the menu are decided in main. A document arrives via argv, second-instance argv or macOS open-file, all through one function. The trap: a command sent before the page subscribes is lost, and at launch that is the normal case; did-finish-load is not enough. So the page calls documents.ready() right after subscribing, and main holds the pending document until then. An arrival over unsaved work becomes a PendingOpenBar (a bar, not a modal; the lossless choice leads), and 'Save, then open' opens only if the save landed. Closing: beforeunload silently cancels in Electron, so the shell asks Save / Don't Save / Cancel on a dirty flag the renderer PUSHES on every change, because close is synchronous. The File menu has no Reload (it discards unsaved work) and no Help (there is no website). One window, one document.
state: live

### D-129
date: 2026-08-25
milestone: M9
scope: engine
title: Two installers, two icons cut from the mark, and the one line that finally registers the extension
question: How is the shell packaged, where is the file association declared, and where do the icons come from?
answer: electron-builder.yml builds a Windows NSIS installer and a macOS dmg, both unsigned (D-124). Its `fileAssociations` is the ONE place an extension is registered with an OS (Info.plist on mac, registry on Windows), and that is the one-way door. The NSIS installer is per-user and not one-click: no admin prompt on top of the warning, and an unsigned installer that runs instantly looks untrustworthy. The README explains the SmartScreen and Gatekeeper warnings and the click path past each (right-click, Open on mac). Icons are generated from src/ui/emerald.svg by `npm run icons` into build/ (committed inputs, never hand-edited); the mark stays the one source. Electron itself rasterises SVG onto a canvas (not capturePage), and .ico/.icns are assembled by hand and read back before shipping. The script is .cjs because Electron's main hangs on whenReady() with an ESM entry. node_modules does not ship (app.asar about 2.4 MB). The installer was built but not run, because registering an extension on a development machine is the maintainer's call. appId com.emerald.desktop is permanent once anything ships. Version 0.1.0.
state: live

### D-130
date: 2026-08-25
milestone: M9.5
scope: engine
title: The sidebar comes back, because the premise it was rejected on was a browser tab
question: Should the window use a left sidebar and draw its own title bar, reversing D-054's top-nav decision?
answer: Yes. D-054 rejected a sidebar as a permanent width tax on a browser tab the app did not control; since the shell, the app owns the window and sets its minimum size. So: a left sidebar in a frameless window whose title bar the app draws, with the top header deleted rather than moved (two chromes is worse than either). Collapse-to-icons becomes a View preference, not a responsive breakpoint; there are no breakpoints below the window minimum. The document name is permanent context in the sidebar. It ran as M9.5, before M9's last unit (the empty/loading/error audit), because the shell's bars live in the header being deleted, and before M10/M11, because a review surface in a window is a pane. The same argument brought list/detail panes: a detail being a navigation away from its list was a browser habit. Hash routing stays by choice. electron/ gains a frameless BrowserWindow, a Windows overlay, a macOS traffic-light inset and renderer-declared drag regions. D-125's accent rule applies hardest in a sidebar full of active states. Nothing had ever decided what happens when several bars are true at once.
state: live

### D-131
date: 2026-08-25
milestone: M9
scope: engine
title: The closing pass, and the half of it a script can do
question: The author asked five times for docs cleanup. How does docs hygiene stop depending on being asked?
answer: A closing pass at every commit seam, scoped to what the session touched, with the mechanical half in `npm run docs:check` (scripts/docs-check.mjs). The script catches what is mechanical: a link to a missing note, a decision written in full with no index row, a finished unit still struck through in the roadmap, and size budgets. Errors fail; budgets only WARN, because a hard length limit makes people delete what belongs, and being over one means condense before adding. The fourth rot shape, a claim that quietly became false, no script catches, so the pass asks for exactly one grep for the claim you just made false. No pre-commit hook: it would run on commits that touch no docs, and a hook failing on warnings teaches --no-verify. No scheduled audit, which costs a whole session of reading docs/. Staleness is not regexed. Budgets are numbers at the top of the script, re-cut when a milestone closes and its entries move to the archive. Later additions: the review entry in the pass (D-198, then D-238's closing message), the closing-pass skill, and a decision with no memory.emd row failing (M22 U5).
state: amended

### D-132
date: 2026-08-26
milestone: M9
scope: engine
title: A model produces the mapping template and never the values, and two things stay out of its reach
question: What exactly may a model produce when the app ingests a document?
answer: A mapping template: which fields exist and where they live. Never the values and never what they mean. A model decides the schema; code moves the data. The user confirms the template, it is stored, and every later document of that format is read by EXECUTING it with no model. What matters is not determinism but who wrote the thing executed. Authored tables (headerAliases.ts, issuer lists, the Hebrew total vocabulary) are scaffolding that does not scale past one market. Generated-then-confirmed templates scale to any market. They are kept as seeds, expandable per market, and are the floor with no model; a seed is an accelerator, never the mechanism. A template is trusted while it verifies against the document's own proof (reconciliation delta, printed total) and invalidated when it does not. A format change is detected, not predicted: the signature misses or the delta is non-zero, and both route to re-derivation. Per-document model reads are not a steady state (38 minutes and no answer, once), and a stored template keeps the app whole with no model. Two things are out of a model's reach: deduction types (the template says where the line is, the user says what it is) and the paycheck splitter. Templates are versioned, and user corrections are never re-derived over.
state: live

### D-133
date: 2026-08-26
milestone: M9
scope: engine
title: The single file is retired, and three of the four rules it enforced go with it
question: The shell replaced the emailable single HTML file. What happens to the build and the rules it enforced?
answer: dist-single/, vite-plugin-singlefile and build:single are deleted; dist/ is the only browser build and the shell serves it. The four rules retired differently. Inlining is gone outright. The under-5MB budget is kept by hand, with chunkSizeWarningLimit at 3000 as a tripwire that can actually fire. No-CDN got STRONGER: it moved from 'the build fails' to electron/network.ts blocking everything not app://, blob: or data:, so a CDN link now fails to load in the shipped app; fonts, rulepacks and wasm stay bundled. Hash routing and document-based were never the single file's and stay as choices; do not re-derive dropping hash routing. Kept against tidiness: wasm-as-bytes, which lets Vitest run the database in Node with no setup and keeps dist-plugin/server.mjs self-contained. The crypto.randomUUID fallback stays for the Node plugin. A test grepping src/ for http(s):// is rejected; the real guard is tested in network.test.ts. A second output nobody tests is a rule already broken.
state: live

### D-134
date: 2026-08-26
milestone: M10
scope: engine
title: The page never reaches a runtime, the main process does, and loopback is a predicate
question: M10 needs a socket to a local model runtime, and D-124 says the page makes no request it did not serve itself. Where does the request come from, and what guards it?
answer: From the main process, never the page. The renderer asks over IPC (EmeraldRuntimes on the bridge, first capability detect()), and electron/runtimes.ts holds the connection. isAllowedRequest in electron/network.ts is not loosened, localhost included; its test naming Ollama's port still expects false. Loosening it would give every future screen and dependency permission to talk to anything listening. Node's fetch bypasses guardSession (net.fetch would need exactly that loosening), so the guard is our own: isLoopbackOrigin, pure string-in boolean-out. It allows exactly `localhost`, all of 127.0.0.0/8 and [::1], over http: only, with no credentials. Compare hosts whole, never by prefix (localhost.example.com is on the internet). Obfuscated spellings like 2130706433 are allowed, because URL normalises them. 0.0.0.0 and 169.254.169.254 are refused. Detection answers what is installed, not whether it fits, so ModelCard has no context field. `absent` (nothing listening) and `unusable` (something else answered) are different sentences. Nothing probes at launch. The browser build has no bridge and so no provider; the app is whole with the feature off.
state: live

### D-135
date: 2026-08-26
milestone: M10
scope: engine
title: The provider is a machine preference, and a chosen model is checked rather than assumed
question: Where is the chosen model runtime stored, and how does the app know it is still there?
answer: In localStorage, never in the document. A provider is a fact about this machine's installed software, and a document is a file you carry to machines without it. It sits beside the theme and sidebar as a preference no document sees (D-115: machine state belongs to the machine). It resolves to four states: unsupported, off, ready and missing. `missing` names why: the model was deleted, the runtime is stopped, the runtime moved ports, or what an unusable probe said. A choice nobody has checked yet reports `ready`, because nothing probes at launch (D-134) and blaming the user's setup for the app's own choice not to open a socket is wrong. The origin is part of the identity, so a runtime that moved is `missing`, not followed. Stored values are validated, and junk lands on `off`. /settings/model opens having looked at nothing, and a button says what it will probe before it does. The screen stays behind the document gate. Context is decided when a model is chosen (D-122); D-137 later found that context is per task.
state: live

### D-136
date: 2026-08-26
milestone: M10
scope: engine
title: The answer's shape is a grammar, the stream is what makes a ceiling enforceable, and every way of stopping is a result
question: How does the app ask a local model one question without hanging, running away or getting prose back?
answer: electron/generate.ts streams over node:http, constrains the answer with a JSON schema passed to the runtime as a grammar (Ollama `format`, OpenAI `response_format`), runs with reasoning off, and enforces three ceilings: generated tokens, wall clock and characters. It streams not to render tokens but because a non-streaming request waits for headers, and undici's 300-second header timeout is not per-request, while a cold model takes minutes. Streaming makes the cap enforceable as it happens and the cancel instant. The grammar is there because a model asked nicely for JSON sometimes answers in prose. Reasoning off matters: one slip with thinking produced 21,864 tokens over 38 minutes. stripReasoning runs as well. Every way of stopping is a result, never an exception: complete, capped, timedOut, cancelled, or failed with the runtime's own words. A capped answer is not parsed. One generation at a time, since one GPU does not do two requests faster. isLoopbackOrigin is applied again, because the provider comes from editable localStorage. Tokens are counted as characters/4, which overestimates Hebrew, so the ceiling trips early.
state: live

### D-137
date: 2026-08-26
milestone: M10
scope: engine
title: A task is what a host can do that a conversation cannot, and the model answers with a line number because a grammar truncated a Hebrew label
question: What may a local model be asked, and in what shape does it answer, when a JSON grammar cut a Hebrew label at its gershayim?
answer: Questions are declared as tasks in src/domain/modelTasks.ts: one job, its prompt, the vocabulary it needs (generated from the app's own lists, so a new role reaches the model in the same commit), its answer as a grammar, and a budget. A model answers with a POSITION, never text. A constrained decoder that emits the " inside סה"כ takes the cheap path and closes the string, so a label came back two characters long. Without the grammar the labels were perfect, but the answer carried every figure on the slip, which D-132 forbids. Asking for line numbers removes the possibility: an integer cannot be truncated, and code reads the text off the document (resolveLines). The invariant, held by a test: every leaf in every task schema is an integer, a boolean or a string from a fixed list, so there is nowhere to write a figure or mistype a label. One cheap verifier: a field's line must carry a digit, which caught a small model pointing at column headings. Context is per task, not per model. A small model plus a verifier beat a large one (5.8s vs 69s). Prompts stay out of electron/; generate.ts is a pipe.
state: live

### D-138
date: 2026-08-26
milestone: M10
scope: engine
title: The slip transcribes, and it proves itself twice — but only where an equation runs
question: How is a transcribed payslip checked, and what does a passing check actually prove?
answer: payslipTemplate.ts executes a template with no model: it reads the figures off the lines it names and checks the slip against itself twice. gross - Σ employee deductions - net must be zero, and Σ employee deductions must equal the printed total. Two checks, because they fail differently: two compensating errors can satisfy one, and a test builds that case. THE LAW: a verifier proves what it checks and nothing else. The first real run reconciled to ₪0.00 while the employer column was ₪1,470 short, because both equations run over the employee column. A figure with no arithmetic around it is a figure a person has to look at, which is why confirm surfaces separate proved from unchecked. A row reads right to left by print position: employer last, employee before it, basis left over. A blank amount prints a dash that is kept as a position; a blank basis prints nothing. No deduction gets a kind; the user says what it is (D-132). The executor lives in src/data/importer so it reuses parseAmountText. Runtimes are addressed by literal 127.x, never by name, so no resolver is ever asked.
state: live

### D-139
date: 2026-08-26
milestone: M11
scope: engine
title: The template is stored against the document's own words, and a version chain is what makes re-deriving safe
question: How is a label-value document (a payslip) read every month after the first, with no model on the path?
answer: A stored template (document_template, schema 21) holds a label signature, a field map anchored to labels, provenance, a confirmation stamp, the fields the user corrected, and a version chain. A label is what does not change. labelOf drops every token with a digit, because amounts, dates and IDs all carry one. The sorted distinct labels are the signature, stored rather than hashed, because the useful answer is 'how close' (intersection over union with a floor), not 'same or not'. Anchors, not line numbers: anchorFields converts the model's line number into the label on that line before anything is stored, so an inserted row shifts nothing. An anchor carries an occurrence ordinal, because a label printed twice (monthly and year-to-date) would otherwise read the wrong one while reconciling. A version chain, not an update: re-derivation stamps superseded_at and inserts, so a UNIQUE index keeps one current template per format and history says what read last year's slip. readableTemplates will not execute an unconfirmed template, including a re-derivation of a confirmed one. Corrected fields are pinned and carried across re-derivation (carryPinnedFields); user overrides win.
state: live

### D-140
date: 2026-08-26
milestone: M11
scope: engine
title: The confirm surface draws the difference between what the slip proved and what only a person can check
question: How does a reading reach the document, and how does the screen say what was proved and what was not?
answer: One review surface, one accept. The layout is the argument: the strip holds the figures the document's own arithmetic vouches for, beside its equations. The table below holds what it does not vouch for, and the doubts sit above the single accept (D-092: not forty confirmations, not a silent accept). A figure's region IS the claim about it. A failed check un-proves all the figures it runs over, never the one that looks wrong, since the arithmetic did not diagnose. What no equation runs through (the employer total) is always named in the doubts, even on a slip that reconciles. Types are proposed only by exact match on the user's own vocabulary; fuzzy matching reconciles while putting the wrong figure in the wrong place. An unmatched line blocks the save until a person names it. Accepting fills the ordinary form, and only the form's save writes, so no second write path exists. A template is stored (confirmed) only if the reading reconciled; a map that was never right is not remembered. derive.ts validates the model's answer on the way back, and a capped or truncated answer is never salvaged.
state: live

### D-141
date: 2026-08-27
milestone: M11
scope: engine
title: A payslip is a PDF, so the app reads one — pdf.js on the main thread, and the lines are rebuilt from glyph positions
question: How does the app get lines of text out of a PDF, when a PDF has only glyphs at coordinates?
answer: pdfjs-dist, loaded on demand by dynamic import (1.7 MB nobody who never opens a PDF fetches), on the main thread through globalThis.pdfjsWorker, because a Worker would need the shell's CSP widened. pdf.js does what must never be reimplemented: CID fonts, ToUnicode CMaps and the bidi pass. pdfText.ts rebuilds lines. Reading order is descending x on a right-to-left page; ascending x swaps the employer and employee columns and still reconciles. Direction is decided on runs carrying letters, because digits and punctuation are neutral. Gaps are kept proportional, since downstream reads whitespace (a label ends at two spaces, a missing figure is a gap). A gap under a fifth of the font size is kerning within one word. No CDN, no cMapUrl; everything is served from the app's origin. The fixture is generated (scripts/make-payslip-pdf.mjs), with embedded CID Hebrew written in visual order the way real producers emit it; real documents never enter the repo. A scan with no text layer is told it is a scan rather than sent to a model as an unknown format.
state: live

### D-142
date: 2026-08-27
milestone: M11
scope: engine
title: The first real payslip: a page is panels, not lines, and some fonts carry shapes with no record of what they say
question: The first real payslip went through the PDF reader. What did grouping runs by baseline get wrong, and what about a font whose text decodes to symbols?
answer: A page is panels, not lines. A real slip prints tables side by side at the same heights, so grouping purely by baseline joined a deduction row to an unrelated total. The reader took that total as an employer contribution, and both proofs still passed (D-138 again). linesFromItems splits at a panel boundary only when a gap is both wide (PANEL_GAP, eight font sizes) AND followed by a word. Width alone breaks every table with an optional empty column, where the next run is a figure. A page-wide gutter does not exist on a dense form. Splitting one table in two is the safe failure; merging two panels invents a figure. The synthetic fixture was too wide, in the direction that hid the bug, so it was narrowed; a fixture must not be kinder than reality. A font whose text decodes to symbols is detected per font and said out loud (unreadableLabels), because the figures are still right. Mangled labels still anchor a template. Discard keeps the text. scripts/inspect-pdf.mjs --numbers-only diagnoses layout without content. AMENDED by D-144: the 'unrecoverable' labels were recovered from the embedded font's own cmap, and D-143 made the detector judge run by run.
state: amended

### D-143
date: 2026-08-27
milestone: M11
scope: engine
title: A boxed total's name and figure are two lines, and a font is judged run by run
question: A boxed net printed its name and figure 4.3pt apart, so the named line had no figure; and the broken-font detector missed the broken font. What fixes each?
answer: No line-grouping tolerance fixes the first: widening it merges genuinely different rows whose pitch overlaps the box offset. So the repair is narrow and at the other end. When a named line has no figure at all, resolveFieldLines borrows from a neighbour only if that neighbour holds one figure and nothing else, and only if exactly one of the two neighbours does. A line with a label belongs to its own label. It is reported: PayslipReading.borrowed puts a sentence on the confirm surface naming both line numbers, because help nobody can see cannot be trusted. The arithmetic checks it either way. The prompt also asks for the line the FIGURE is on, but a prompt is a hope and the repair is the mechanism. Second, a font is judged run by run, not by counting characters across the font. The broken font also set the digits, so symbols never outnumbered them and it passed. A run with two or more symbol-block characters outnumbering everything legible in it is garbled, and three such runs condemn the font.
state: live

### D-144
date: 2026-08-27
milestone: M11
scope: engine
title: When a document lies about its own text, the font that drew the shapes is asked instead
question: A PDF's ToUnicode map says its Hebrew glyphs are Latin symbols. Is the text recoverable?
answer: Yes, from the embedded font program's own cmap. When the document contradicts itself, believe the font that drew the shapes. pdf.js sanitises fonts and rebuilds the cmap FROM the wrong ToUnicode, so pdfFontMap.ts opens the file a second time. It scans raw bytes between `stream` and `endstream`, inflates with DecompressionStream, and keeps sfnt programs. That is deliberately not a PDF parser: no xref, no object resolution. Traps: pdf.js detaches the buffer it is handed, so give it a copy; the EOL before endstream is not data. The chain: printed char -> code (invert ToUnicode) -> glyph id (identity for composite fonts) -> what the font's cmap says. It is self-checking, which makes it safe: a font's chart is accepted only if it turns that font's text into MORE letters than it had, and every chart is tried against every font with the best kept. It cannot make a readable document worse. The recovered Hebrew is reversed, because it was in visual order and pdf.js's bidi left it alone. A generated mislabelled fixture keeps it fixed without real documents. The unreadable notice remains for fonts broken both ways. The lesson: 'unrecoverable' was one experiment asking the wrong question.
supersedes: D-142
state: live

### D-145
date: 2026-08-27
milestone: M11
scope: engine
title: A deduction row's name is the user's answer, kept against the format rather than asked every month
question: A row whose label matches no type is named by the user. Why was the same question asked every month, and where should the answer live?
answer: On the format. DeductionAnchor gains an optional deductionTypeId, written only by nameDeductionRows from a human action on the confirm surface, and pinned so carryPinnedFields keeps it across re-derivation. A row's type resolves in three rungs: what the format was told, then an exact match on the document's own vocabulary, then nothing. There was no migration, because anchors is JSON decoded defensively. A wrong type reconciles perfectly, so it is the one field no arithmetic reaches, and a question only a person can answer is worth answering once. This narrows D-132 without reversing it: the model still never names a row, and the task schema has nowhere to write one (a fixed list stops inventing but not choosing wrongly). What changed is how long the user's answer survives. Fuzzy or alias matching stays forbidden, because it is right eleven times and silently wrong on the twelfth. A load-bearing fix rode along: rows are keyed by the anchor's own `field`, never by array position, since one unresolved anchor shifted every later key onto the wrong row.
state: live

### D-146
date: 2026-08-27
milestone: M11
scope: engine
title: A document that states no encoding at all is read from the order its glyphs are stored in
question: Some real slips have no ToUnicode, no cmap and no post table anywhere, and pdftotext extracts zero Hebrew too. Can they be read?
answer: Yes, by inference from glyph order, as the last rung. It runs only after repairFromFonts failed and only on a page already judged unreadable. glyphOrder.ts uses two bands whose alignment is PINNED, not guessed. First, glyph ids arriving as C0 controls are the Macintosh standard order and become ASCII (+0x1D): space, punctuation, digits. Second, a run of exactly 27 suspect codes can only be the Hebrew alphabet, which fixes its offset with no freedom left. An offset is taken only when it is the only one that fits; two candidates veto each other. A recovered run must score strictly more legible characters than it had, and the reconciliation proofs still apply on top. It is inference, not reading, and the screen says so (inferredFromGlyphOrder). Every export route (re-download, save-as, print-to-PDF) was tried on the real files and reproduced or destroyed the text. The letter band is Hebrew only. A Latin-script document cannot be helped: above the control line a real character and an unmapped id are the same byte (extending the band turned spaces into `=`). No fuzzy alignment: off by one gives confident wrong words.
state: live

### D-147
date: 2026-08-27
milestone: M11
scope: engine
title: The page says what the figures are and a picture of it says what the words are
question: Should OCR replace glyph-order inference for a document with no encoding?
answer: No: compose them, each used only for what it is exact at. Figures and positions come from the PDF through D-146 (45/45 figures), and words come from Tesseract reading a render of the page (8/9 labels vs 5/9). OCR alone read 41/45 figures, silently wrong in the employer and basis columns where no equation runs. More resolution made OCR worse. relabel.ts is the join and is pure geometry: a run keeps its own text unless OCR has words inside its box. It is Hebrew data only, since nothing asks OCR for a digit. Everything stays local and is enforced: bundled core and worker, heb.traineddata vendored in public/tesseract/, cacheMethod 'none' (D-115), and a missed override dies at the session guard (D-124). The CSP gains `worker-src 'self' blob:`, which grants nothing outward. Render runs under a Deadline<T> object, not T|undefined, because render resolves undefined on success. Traps: the hidden verification pane never runs rAF, so pdf.js never draws there; disableFontFace must be false for OCR; imageHeight does not exist, so take it off the canvas. Verification has to name the row, because a label printed twice can pass a page-wide check. A vision model is rejected: it hallucinates digits (D-132).
state: live

### D-148
date: 2026-08-27
milestone: M11
scope: engine
title: The formats are a screen, and a wrong match is a click rather than a dead end
question: A wrong stored format kept reading slips and there was nowhere to see it or change it. Fix the matcher, or make the match visible?
answer: Make it visible and correctable; leave the matcher unchanged. A better guess is still a guess, and the fault was that the guess was invisible and final. It came from three rules working as written: most-recently-used wins a tie, one employer yields two signatures (OCR vs native), and any reconciling template is stored. /settings/formats lists the stored templates (name, what each reads in the slip's own words, provenance, last use) with rename and delete. The confirm surface names the format it used and has a 'Read with' picker, so a wrong match is re-read under another format with no model, judged by the same reconciliation. Accepting a CHOSEN format stores a second row with the same anchors under this document's labels (derivedBy MANUAL, renamed to the chosen name), so the correction sticks. A close automatic match stores nothing, or a drifting signature breeds a row a month. Nothing is created or confirmed on the list: anchors mean nothing away from a page, and a checkbox is not the gate. Delete is the wide door, and the panel says what it costs. 'Reads like' flags pairs a slip could match either of, suppressed when they share a name.
state: live

### D-149
date: 2026-08-27
milestone: M11
scope: engine
title: A fund report is the same document as a payslip, and its proof is a third state
question: How is a second kind of document read without a second reader, and what is its proof when the page often prints nothing to check?
answer: The ladder is generic (documentLadder.ts); each document is twenty lines saying what it is (payslipLadder, fundLadder), and DocumentTextInput and openDocumentText are the one way in. Every PDF fix (D-141..D-147) arrives for free; a second copy is how one drifts two decisions behind. What is not shared is the proof, and it needs a THIRD STATE: proved, failed or unchecked. A document with no equation's terms printed is `unchecked`. Collapsing that into failed means the format re-derives forever; collapsing it into proved hides an unchecked misreading (D-138). The ladder and the store accept `unchecked` (`failed` never stores), and the human accept is what it rests on. The surface shows it in ordinary ink, not red: the absence of an opinion is not a problem. Proof is per figure when the equations cover different figures, and all-or-nothing when they share them. A `%` token is dropped before reading, because a rate is not an amount. The as-of date is the latest date on its line. A missing fee is a doubt, not a silence. Template `kind` and `anchors.shape` must agree, or the repository skips the row. No schema change.
state: live

### D-150
date: 2026-08-27
milestone: M11
scope: engine
title: A statement's columns are a proposal on the grid, asked for rather than offered
question: When the header alias table maps a statement's columns wrongly, how does a model help without becoming the default?
answer: A 'Work out the columns' button beside Save mapping, shown only when a model is chosen. It sends the header row plus four data rows (rows, because 4821 under a reference heading looks like an amount only to a string matcher). It gets back column indexes and an amount convention, and applies them to the draft on screen. columnProposal.ts validates and applies. Nothing new verifies it: the live parse preview and the parsed total against the printed total are the checks every hand mapping faces, and the user still presses Save. Asked for, never offered: the guesser is free and usually right, and a call on every import would make a screen that works without a model look like it is waiting. It replaces roles rather than merging, since a half-kept guess is two answers nobody gave. It leaves the header row, currency and date order alone. A whole map arriving at once needs two rules: no role on two columns and no column with two roles. The model does not name the header row. The renderer has no socket; the call runs in the shell.
state: live

### D-152
date: 2026-08-27
milestone: M11
scope: engine
title: The first real fund report: the movements block is exhaustive, and what nothing checks needs a person
question: A real pension report looked right and was ₪921 out. What did it teach?
answer: Six things. (1) A charge is a magnitude whatever sign the page printed; a fee is never a credit. (2) The movements block is exhaustive: every figure between opening and closing is one of five things, and the reading must account for all of them. Insurance costs and actuarial adjustments became repeating lists (chargeLines, adjustmentLines). Telling the model that sentence is what made it find them. (3) A report that rounds cannot add to zero, so the equation carries half a unit of tolerance per term, triggered by every term being whole rather than by the footnote. A report with agorot gets none. (4) Read everything the page states that matters (fee rates as basis points, track, employer, period) and put the rest in the snapshot's note, with no migration. (5) A quarterly is year-to-date, and the surface says so. (6) The outranking finding: the facts no equation runs through (fee rate, employer) are exactly what the model got wrong, twice, even with the trap named. So they are TYPED OVER on the surface, not ticked, and retargetToValue moves the template's anchor to the line carrying the typed value, so the next report reads right with no model. Never fit signs to zero a delta. A third prompt is hope.
state: live

### D-153
date: 2026-08-27
milestone: M11
scope: engine
title: A deposit table is anchored by its heading and read by shape, and the salary month is what it is for
question: How is a table with no labels and a different row count every quarter read by a stored template?
answer: Store what is stable: the heading line (words) and the order of its columns. Find the rows by shape on the document every time. Walk down from the heading; a line whose cell count matches the heading's and whose date and month cells parse is a row, and the first line that is not one ends the table (a total line, a second heading or prose all fail). Never anchor rows: that is wrong next quarter and passes every test until then. The heading is the only anchor allowed to skip the digit guard (resolveAnchorLine). DepositTableAnchor sits beside `fields`, so the exception is visible in the type. A third equation, rows against 'paid in', proves the amounts and nothing else. The salary month, which every deposit check keys on, has no arithmetic, so the surface flags it even when the sum passes. Overlapping year-to-date reports keep both rows; transcribe faithfully, reconcile afterwards (distinctDeposits dedupes by natural key where anything aggregates). No unique index, since it would reject every consecutive pair. One save writes the reading and its rows, and they are shown in the form, not merely carried. The model never produces the rows (D-132). Schema 22. It needed no change to the ladder: a new kind of thing on a page is a new template shape, not a second reader.
state: live

### D-154
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M13
scope: engine
title: A button may look pressable, and the only thing that can judge it is a person
question: docs/11 says no gradients and no shadows. May a button look raised, and who decides whether it is too loud?
answer: The `primary` weight only gains a 1px inset top highlight in a lightened accent, a hover that lifts by --shadow-pop rather than by moving, and the existing 120ms press settle. `secondary` and `ghost` are untouched. The fence: no gradient on any plane that is not a control, no shadow on anything but a button or a floating room, and no second accent hue. The rule that protects the app was never 'no shadow' but 'nothing competes with the figures'. If it ever needs a gradient or a second surface colour to look right, revert the entry rather than stretch it. The lasting finding is that the acceptance is a person's eye, and it is the maintainer's. A session can take the screenshot but cannot answer 'is the number still the loudest thing'. For a visual change the closing message names it as the maintainer's call rather than reporting a pass. This is D-138 where nothing checks: where nothing can check, a person does, and the surface says so.
state: live

### D-155
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M13
scope: engine
title: The app saves the document, and the dirty dot stops being a warning
question: Should the app auto-save, and what may auto-save not do to the document model?
answer: Auto-save is a property of an adapter that already has a path, never of the app. It never creates a file: a new document stays unsaved until Save as names it, and crashCache covers that window. It refuses rather than overwrites: wouldOverwrite (D-092, D-127) runs before every automatic write. It must not fire into its own directory watcher. It is debounced on idle with a ceiling, skipped while busy, and never on top of a save the user asked for. The dot becomes state (unsaved, saving, saved 14:32) instead of a warning. The download adapter cannot save silently and says so (canSaveSilently). There is no preference, and Ctrl/Cmd+S stays. The sentence behind every rule: auto-save makes the file more reliable and must not make the document optional. Saving to a default location is the D-115 mistake in one line. When it breaks it looks like the app arguing with itself about a file nobody touched, which is rule 3.
state: live

### D-156
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M13
scope: engine
title: The document is a `.emd`, and there is exactly one extension
question: What is the document's extension, and does the app keep opening the old `.emerald`?
answer: `.emd`, everywhere and alone: electron-builder.yml registers only it, and DOCUMENT_EXTENSION (electron/bridge.ts), suggestedFileName and the open dialog all use it. There is no compatibility path and no second association. The bytes are identical, so migrating an old `.emerald` is a rename by hand. The rule that got sharper: the app never renames a file the user named, not on save, not on open, not as a migration (D-021). `.gem` was rejected because RubyGems owns it, `.emr` reads as a medical record, and dual registration costs a branch everywhere forever for a population of one. The extension is registered with an OS only in electron-builder.yml, which is the one-way door (D-129). Verification is a real install and a double-click: an old `.emerald` should fail at the OS, not open half-read in the app.
supersedes: D-123
state: live

### D-157
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M13
scope: ledger
title: The nav follows the order a person gets ready in, and a view of a screen is not a place
question: What is the top-level navigation, and what is a place versus a view?
answer: Top level: Home · Transactions · Paychecks · Plan · Wealth · Categories · Settings — the order a person gets ready in (bring documents in, plan, look at the total). Insights is not a section: the month is Home, and year and trends are periods of it (#/, #/year, #/trends) behind a control; Home calls monthlyReport once and homeSummary becomes netWorthReport plus counts. Paychecks is top level (redirect from #/plan/paychecks). Wealth has three children — Savings & debts, Funds, Net worth — with money map, fee impact and deposit check as views. A third level exists but not in the sidebar: a child's tabs are views of one place, drawn by ViewTabs; the command palette lists all three levels, which is what made the demotion safe. The tab marker is an accent underline and colours no figure (D-125). Every old hash redirects (six in App.tsx); sectionFor and tabsFor match the root exactly. Known unfixed: in-screen toggles under a tab strip give two rows of switching.
supersedes: D-108
state: live

### D-158
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M13
scope: ledger
title: The picker that files a transaction is the picker that makes the category
question: How is a category created while filing a transaction?
answer: CategoryPicker offers to create the typed name as the last row of its list whenever that name is not already a category's own name or full path — not only when nothing matched. Taking it opens name, parent and kind in the same popover at a stated default (top level, expense); the kind control disappears once a parent is chosen, since createCategory takes the parent's kind (D-097). The new category is chosen for the row being filed. The picker itself writes, taking mutate from the store, rather than an onCreate prop — nine call sites would each be a chance to leave it off, and not one changed. ui/categories/inlineCreate.ts holds the testable rules: offersCreate, and twinOf, which chooses an existing same-named sibling instead of duplicating. Enter is handled explicitly; Escape returns to the list and refocuses search. Unchecked: whether the kind chosen is right (expense is silently wrong for a refund).
state: live

### D-159
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M13
scope: ledger
title: A price arrives the way every other number does — transcribed. D-124 stands
question: How does a security's price enter the document — a feed, or transcription?
answer: Transcribed: answered on 2026-08-28. A security's value comes off a statement the user already has, valued as of that statement's date, like pension funds (F13's rule; the deliberate exception to derived balances). A typed price is the escape hatch — entered by the user, dated, visibly hand-entered. The costly half of investments is composition (quantity, cost basis, account), which is the same transcription problem as a fund report: label-value header plus unlabelled rows found by shape (fund_deposit, D-153, F17's ladder). A price request leaks the portfolio, which is a privacy claim. F18 was specified as buildable with no network: security, investment_account, holding_reading, security_trade. Amended the next day by D-178, which allows an opt-in price fetch from the main process to one allowlisted host; transcribed and typed prices remain and are stored alike with a source and an as-of date.
state: amended

### D-160
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M13
scope: ledger
title: The settlement queue was complete and had never been asked
question: Why were unaccounted card settlements missed, and how is the user prompted?
answer: The queue (SettlementPrompt, D-064) stays on the accounts screen. What was wrong: onSetLast4 did not rescan settlements, nor did adding an account, so importing statements then typing the card's digits produced no link and no prompt ever; and the pending count appeared nowhere. Now rescanSettlements runs on account creation and on a last-4 change as well as a kind change — anything that changes which rows are settlements re-derives them (D-060, D-070). PENDING_SETTLEMENTS is an AttentionItem ranked first on Home, above unfiled rows, because an unanswered settlement doubles the month's total rather than skewing a breakdown. A Notices entry (fourth of five) carries the count and a link to the queue and implements nothing. 'Not now' lasts the session only. Rejected: moving the queue, a modal after import, an undismissable notice. Found only by typing digits into a real screen.
state: live

### D-161
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M13
scope: ledger
title: A category's colour is a fact about the level it is drawn in, not about the category
question: How are category colours kept from colliding on one chart while staying stable across months?
answer: ui/charts/categoryPalette.ts resolves the whole taxonomy at once and every multi-category screen uses it (MonthReport, Trends, YearOverview, the picker's mark, the categories row dot); categoryColor remains the single-category answer and the shared hash (slotOf exported). The peer set is a taxonomy level, not a month's segments, so a category keeps its colour between months. An explicit colour never moves; an automatic one takes the least-loaded slot at its level walking forward from its hash; members are walked in id order, so reordering does not repaint. Archived categories take no slot. Eight hues cannot cover forty top-level categories: resolution spreads them evenly (tested against a real migrated database) and the popover marks swatches a sibling already wears and says how many share. Rejected: warnings instead, more hues, a free colour picker, passing the palette as a prop. Rendering only; seeded automatic colours changed on this commit.
supersedes: D-105
state: live

### D-162
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M13
scope: ledger
title: A taxonomy screen has to say which categories are dead, or merge and archive are guesses
question: What does the categories screen show so merge and archive can be decided?
answer: Four things, no new arithmetic. categoryActivity: one query giving per category rows ever filed, last date, and outflow since a caller-computed cutoff; a row reads 'unused' or '128 rows · ₪2,340 in 90 days'; currencies kept apart; an excluded row still counts. A search that keeps a parent on screen, marked as context, to hold up a matching child, and never leaves a section folded. Groups that fold, with counts and how many are dead. Reparent via reparentCategory, same kind only (it sets parent_id alone). Reparent is a panel, not a per-row select: a select per row was 1,044 options and doubled the DOM. The chip is still a dot and a name; figures are ink, never accent (D-125). Rejected: sorting by usage (breaks position-based reorder), folding parents, a spend roll-up from children. taxonomyView.ts is new; daysAgo joins today in ui/today.ts. Unchecked: whether 90 days and 'unused' are right on real history.
state: live

### D-163
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M13
scope: engine
title: Transcribing a document takes the window, and a flow says so itself
question: How does a screen that transcribes a document get the whole window, and how do you leave it?
answer: FlowFrame is a component a screen renders while transcribing. It claims the window through useFullWindow: the sidebar is not navigable, ViewTabs draws nothing, and ListDetail hides the list so the detail fills the content area. There is one way out and it is the frame's, top-right and the same every time; the forms keep only their primary action. Escape leaves through a window listener, never from inside an input, textarea, select or contenteditable, and not if defaultPrevented. Two traps fail as flickers rather than errors. First, the claim is a counter whose release is deferred a microtask, because flow-to-flow handover runs cleanups before setups and useSyncExternalStore reads the snapshot on every render. Second, ListDetail keeps the <aside> in place and hides the list, because changing shape remounts the detail, drops the flow's state and loops. Confirm surfaces get a pinned footer in place of duplicate buttons at both ends. No route for a flow (D-050). A confirm on leaving a dirty form is still unanswered.
state: live

### D-164
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M13
scope: engine
title: Eight reports are a stack, not eight sittings — and dedupe is an ordering as well as a key
question: How are several documents of one kind read in one sitting, and does the order they arrive in matter?
answer: The reader takes several files as a queue owned by the screen rather than the reader, because accepting leaves the reader and comes back. Each document gets a fresh reader, with the step as its React key, so nothing crosses between documents. There are three verbs: Discard drops the reading and keeps the text, Skip drops the document, and leaving ends the sitting and says how many it drops. The batch gets cheaper as it goes: accepting the first stores its format, and the rest are read by that template with no model. The reading form's own save is still the only write, per document. Dedupe is an ordering as well as a key. A backfill arrives newest-first, so the survivor of a duplicate is chosen by the as-of date of the reading that stated it, not by read order. The figures never move but provenance would, and provenance that depends on the order of an afternoon is not provenance. A figure prints the span its readings cover, which is D-138 again. No cross-target batch that files by guessing.
state: live

### D-165
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M13
scope: engine
title: The movements block is exhaustive, and a line is in it once
question: A real five-line report balanced to the shekel but read ₪20 out, because the fee and yield lines were also counted as charges. How is a line kept from entering the equation twice?
answer: A charge or adjustment anchored to a line one of the equation's terms already reads (closing, opening, deposits, fees, yield) is dropped as a restatement before the equation runs. readResolvedReport builds the set of named lines and collect skips them. A line holds one figure, so a charge on an already-read line is that term restated, never a second charge. Exclude by line, never by value: two equal amounts can both be real. It is not surfaced as a doubt either, because a restatement is not a question for a person. This is D-138 from the other side: a verifier fed the same term twice fails a correct document, so what an equation runs over must be established as carefully as the equation. On the same document, fee rates came back swapped with a market average. No equation runs through a rate, so nothing could catch it, which is why rates are typed over rather than ticked (D-152).
state: live

### D-166
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M13
scope: ledger
title: Onboarding asks for the documents in the drawer, and stops setting the user homework
question: What does onboarding do after the first import, and what is on the checklist?
answer: A seventh step, documents, after the first import: a hub offering a payslip, each fund's stack of reports (D-164), and which payroll deduction feeds which fund (linkedFundAccountId — the one moment the link is obvious, since both sides were just created). The reading loops were extracted first into PayslipIntake and ReportIntake (plus savePaycheckDraft), used by the paychecks screen, fund detail and the step alike, so the accept rules (template stored only when a slip reconciles, D-145, D-148, D-152, the payroll deposit D-075) cannot diverge. The intake replaces the step rather than nesting a FlowFrame inside the wizard; FlowFrame's exit label is a prop (leaveLabel, 'Back to setup'). The checklist drops 'categorize what came in' — filing is the user's own process, not homework — and gains savings goals, the paycheck split and pension balances. The plugin's flow gains the step too; stored progress needs no migration. Unchecked: whether people do it rather than skip, and deduction links offered against seeded types before a slip is read.
state: live

### D-167
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M13
scope: engine
title: A figure animates when it changes in front of you, and never when it merely appears
question: When may a number count, and how is that enforced rather than remembered?
answer: A figure animates when it changes in front of you and never when it merely appears. useAnimatedNumber does not count its first value, so a route change cannot count a hero number up. useArrivals is the same rule for rows: a row settles only when it is the one you just made, with nothing on first render and nothing when the whole table is replaced. The money path is untouched. The interpolation is float, but every frame emits a rounded integer of minor units and the last frame is exactly the target (countAt). useAnimatedMoney snaps when the currency changes. A screen reader hears the settled value: the label is built from the target and aria-live sits on a hidden settled span. prefers-reduced-motion is read in JS, and a count started while the window is hidden snaps. Whether 200ms is right is the maintainer's eye (D-154). The browser pane never composites, so rAF never ticks there, and a hover measured in a hidden pane is not a measurement.
state: live

### D-168
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M14
scope: engine
title: The text is made out of the placed lines, so a highlight cannot point at the wrong row
question: How does a confirm surface show which line of the PDF a figure came off, without the highlight drifting from the text?
answer: The text is made out of the placed lines. placeLines(items, page) returns {text, box} per line, and linesFromItems is that with the boxes dropped, so there is no second array to keep in step. A blank page still yields one empty line. A box is PDF user space in points from the bottom-left, with the page size beside it; rotation and scale belong to the viewer (convertToViewportPoint), never stored in pixels. The source carried is the File, not its bytes, because pdf.js may detach a buffer and a detached array reads as an empty document with no error. A reading records which line each field came off. DocumentPane draws the pages and marks the used lines faintly and the pointed line strongly, in fixed amber on white paper, which is not `accent`. It is off until asked for. Never highlight by searching the page for text: labels repeat. Any edit to the textarea clears the source, because the boxes describe lines that moved.
state: live

### D-169
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M14
scope: ledger
title: The salary a deposit was computed on is a third figure, and neither document checks it
question: How is the salary a pension deposit was computed on checked against the payslip?
answer: The base the payslip prints on the line feeding a fund is stored and compared with the salary column of that fund's deposit table. The paycheck form gains a Base column writing PaycheckLine.basisAmount (the plugin always wrote it) — reversing draftFromSlip dropping it: the remedy for an unchecked figure is a visible, correctable column, not silence. ExpectedContribution.salaryBasis collects, never sums, the bases of the lines feeding one fund (pension and severance share one salary); lines that disagree name no base. depositsByMonth compares against FundDeposit.salary for the salary month and answers AGREES, DIFFERS or UNKNOWN; a sum forms only where every contributing row prints a figure. The counterpart is the pensionable base, never gross. Tolerance is one whole currency unit (report rounds to the shekel). Never derive the base from amount ÷ rate. UNKNOWN is never folded into AGREES. Shown as 'Computed on' in the fund screen and the plugin. Unchecked: no real report with a salary column has been read yet.
state: live

### D-170
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M14
scope: engine
title: A screen four other screens have to point at is not a setting
question: Deduction types lived in Settings and four screens had to send people there in a sentence. Where does such a screen belong?
answer: A screen that other screens keep pointing at is a place people need, not a setting. Deduction types became a tab of Paychecks (#/paychecks/deductions), and the old settings route redirects. It is a tab rather than a child or a top-level item because of D-157's rule: a section and its children are places, and a child's tabs are views of one place. The vocabulary is what the slips are made of. Every reference was repointed in the same commit, the plugin's hash included. The Paychecks screen also says when a saving deduction on a slip links to nothing (unlinkedSavingTypes). Otherwise every downstream surface says 'no payslips cover this', which reads as a missing document when it is a missing link. The notice is a finding, not furniture: only types actually on a slip, and nothing when there is nothing to say. Whether Paychecks is where a person will look is an argument, not an observation.
state: live

### D-171
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M14
scope: engine
title: A read belongs to the app, not to the screen that started it
question: A PDF read could take twenty seconds, said nothing, and died if you left the screen. How does a read survive navigation?
answer: A read belongs to the app. src/state/reading.ts holds exactly one read: what, whether working, the stage, tokens spent, whether its flow is open, and the value it got. The flow runs open, ladder and model as one entry, and DocumentTextInput only hands the file up. extractPdfText reports a ReadStage only for the expensive steps (loading pdf.js, font recovery, OCR), and readStageText is the sentence. ReadingBar sits at the window's foot whenever a read exists and its flow is not on screen. It is the way back, and the way back is what permits leaving. It is hidden while the flow has the window, and D-163 is untouched. One read at a time is deliberate, because concurrent main-thread PDF reads are slower and uncancellable. The open flag lives in the store, since a per-screen boolean broke 'check it over' from the same screen. A stale-read guard drops an answer from a superseded document. The predicted rAF trap does not apply: rAF follows document visibility, not component mount. Write down a trap that turns out not to exist.
state: live

### D-172
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M14
scope: engine
title: A month reads as a month, and the stored form does not move
question: The author wants 'July 2026' on screen; the same function wrote the stored month. How is a display change kept out of storage?
answer: Two kinds of function. toYearMonthString stays `2026-07`: the persisted form, the filter key, parseYearMonth's only input and the plugin's argument. Three display functions sit beside it in core/time.ts: formatYearMonth 'July 2026' for headings and rows, formatMonthShort 'Jul 2026' for axes and narrow columns (the year stays, because windows cross years), and formatMonthName 'July' where a table already names its year. Only display call sites moved; nineteen functional ones (keys, select values, monthRange, Map keys, router state, input type=month) did not. A display string that leaks into storage is a migration. A test asserts that nothing a display function returns parses as a stored month, so a wrong path fails loudly. The plugin keeps `2026-07` in both output and arguments, so a model cannot pass prose back as a key. No Intl locale: every string is English until a locale pack exists. Out of range falls back to the stored form.
state: live

### D-173
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M14
scope: engine
title: The app's first context menu, and it is on the row rather than on the value
question: How does a person make a rule from a ledger row, and what pattern does the first context menu set?
answer: Right-click anywhere on a row, or Shift+F10 or the Menu key, opens one menu. Every item pre-fills the rule editor and opens it; none writes a rule, because a broad rule can claim hundreds of unseen rows. The items are: rule from the highlighted words (CONTAINS, and absent unless it would match its own row), rule from the whole merchant, rule from the provider's category (last, because broadest), and open the transaction. The menu is on the row, not the value: one question, 'what do you want to do with this row?', rather than a clickable cell per rule. ContextMenu holds the behaviour. It focuses the first item, the arrows wrap, and Escape, an outside pointerdown or a scroll closes it. Capture the selection when the menu opens, because focusing the menu clears it. Store the selected words un-normalised; matching normalises (D-083). The drive also found a hook called after early returns ('Rendered more hooks'), a two-render fact no test saw. Really using the screen finds bugs too.
state: live

### D-174
date: 2026-08-28
milestone: M14
scope: ledger
title: The bar is the control, and the fixed expenses are a figure rather than a clause
question: How does the splitter's bar become a control, and where do fixed expenses sit in the split?
answer: The bar is draggable: each movable segment has a trailing-edge handle (role=slider, arrows 1%, Shift 10%); a drag resizes that bucket and the difference goes to the balance — the screen never normalizes. REMAINDER and the fixed share have no handle. The preview is local and the commit is one call; draggedTo and splitAllocation are pure domain functions shared by preview and commit, and a drag goes through redistribute so locked rows never move. Fixed expenses get their own line: total, confirmed vs worked out, already out of net (D-053), and where they go. SplitPlan.fixedInSplit (migration 023, schema 23): off the top, or as a share of the split — a separate question from fixed_expense_mode, stored with the plan because it changes what every percentage means. The share is SplitResult.fixedShare, not an allocations row. 'Split a slice in two' via the right-click menu (D-173); a split preserves the plan's total exactly. Rejected: neighbour-taking handles, writing per frame, a pie. Found: a stale closure that made each drag write the previous one's value.
state: live

### D-175
date: 2026-08-29
milestone: M15
scope: ledger
title: A place is where money sits, a holding is what it sits *in*, and only the second has a return
question: Where does an expected return live, given a bank has none but the fixed deposit held there does?
answer: HoldingPlace becomes a location only (name, institution, kind, note, archived). A new `holding` belongs to a place and says what form the money takes — cash, a fixed deposit, an index fund, a pension track, and from M16 a position in one security. The rate and its CONTRACTUAL/ASSUMED basis, isLiquid, term end and fee move to the holding. A SavingDeposit lands in a holding, never a place, and every place has at least one: the migration gives each place one holding carrying its old rate and repoints holding_place_id to holding_id. Cash is an ordinary holding, so there is one rule — money always lands in a holding — and M16's reconciliation is cash-in minus purchases against the cash holding. Rejected: an instrument layer beside the rate on the place (two homes for one fact); a transcribed balance per holding (a stored balance in savings, never allowed); renaming to Account (collides with import's Account). The migration's one acceptance test: no figure in the document moves. Projection compounds per holding for free.
supersedes: D-074
state: live

### D-176
date: 2026-08-29
milestone: M15
scope: ledger
title: A balance says how it is known, and that is what the money map's third column is for
question: What replaces the rate as the money map's third column, and how are 'different kinds of savings' told apart?
answer: A holding declares its provenance — RECORDED (the sum of movements the user entered, nothing will confirm it), TRANSCRIBED (a statement says what it is), or VALUED (positions times a price, M16) — with a lastConfirmedAt beside it, and the money map's third column shows this. It is declared, not inferred: inferring from whether snapshots exist would make a holding change kind by being neglected. Rejected: a kind on the goal (a goal spreads across holdings); a boolean isTracked (cannot separate transcribed from valued, which M16 needs to know whether a missing price matters); showing the rate. The map becomes where the money is, how much, and how much to believe each row; Home's attention list gains an item for a RECORDED holding nobody has counted in months.
state: live

### D-177
date: 2026-08-29
milestone: M15
scope: ledger
title: A fund is a row on the money map and a record of its own, and those do not conflict
question: Should long-term funds stay separate from savings places, or be folded in?
answer: Both halves: every FundAccount appears as an ordinary money-map row (balance, institution, TRANSCRIBED provenance with the snapshot's as-of date), and fund_account, fund_balance_snapshot and fund_deposit keep their own tables and screens. The map is a query over both, not a table either writes into. The record stays separate for D-015's reason — employee/employer/severance components, two fee bases, and the payslip-vs-fund reconciliation per salary month — and folding it in would put five nullable fields on every row. The complaint was presentation: funds were folded into the locked subtotal and never listed. Rejected: one table with nullable fund fields; leaving it; a HoldingPlace row per fund kept in step (two records, drifts on rename). One domain function assembles the rows and holds liquid + locked = total. A fund row leads to F13's screen.
state: live

### D-178
date: 2026-08-29
milestone: M16
scope: engine
title: A price may be fetched, on purpose and never by default — the page's rule does not move
question: May the app fetch a security price over the network, given D-124 says nothing inside it reaches the network?
answer: Yes, from the main process only and never from the page. electron/prices.ts reaches one allowlisted host. It is off by default and enabled by an explicit prompt, never runs at launch or on a schedule, and only runs on a refresh the user presses. D-124 is amended in the shell and untouched in the page: electron/network.ts still cancels every renderer request that is not app://, blob: or data:. This is the third socket and it takes D-134's shape, which moves the request rather than loosening the guard. It is a single-host allowlist, not a loopback predicate. Say plainly what leaves the machine: the list of securities asked about, plus an IP. The README and the enabling prompt carry that sentence; never round it to 'nothing'. A price is never load-bearing. Every figure must be right from the last price the document holds, whether it was transcribed, typed or fetched, and all three are stored alike with a source and an as-of date. 'Works with the network off' stays literally true. The static dist/ build shows the feature as unavailable. A security with no quote says so rather than showing a stale number as fresh. The provider is behind an interface because free providers cover TASE thinly.
supersedes: D-124
state: live

### D-179
date: 2026-08-29
milestone: M17
scope: ledger
title: A milestone that says "seamless" never closes, so M17 is five edges and one view
question: How is 'connect every data point seamlessly' turned into a milestone that can close?
answer: By naming the members up front and admitting nothing after it starts: five connections and one trace view. The edges: a paycheck line to a debt payment; a split plan writing a debt payment on apply (D-106's migration); the labeling queue's promotion to a fixed expense and to a debt payment; and the import review screen's rule offer. The view traces one salary month from the slip, through the split, to the holding it sits in. Anything else goes to the suggestions file. It absorbs four tail items carried since M2/M5 so the tail stops being one. The trace view is a read over existing rows; if it needs a column, that means an edge is missing. AMENDED by D-189: the paycheck-to-debt edge already existed, so M17 is four edges and a view, and does not grow to refill the slot.
state: amended

### D-180
date: 2026-08-29
milestone: M15
scope: ledger
title: The four things building M15 settled that its three decisions did not
question: What did building M15 have to decide that D-175 to D-177 left unnamed?
answer: M15 shipped at schema 24. (1) A goal's default moves to a holding: saving_goal.default_holding_id, because a default naming a place would make applyPlanDeposit and syncPayrollDeposits silently choose which holding money lands in; GoalHasNoHoldingError. (2) The goal × place matrix stays at the place grain, summing a goal's holdings at one place into one cell; liquid/locked lives on the holding rows beneath. (3) The migration rewrites foreign keys by string, each place getting one holding at 'h-' || place.id — a join would drop deposits naming a missing place, and a derived id keeps the migration reproducible across app, plugin and tests. (4) UNCONFIRMED_HOLDING ranks last on Home's attention list, after a fund with no balance, at five months (F13's stale threshold minus one), because nothing is missing, only possibly drifted. Plugin's record_deposits and create_saving_goals take a holding with a place inside; onboarding's brokerage tick makes a place and a cash holding.
state: live

### D-181
date: 2026-08-29
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: A position is a holding, so every figure M15 built already knows about it
question: Is a security position its own record, or a kind of holding?
answer: A holding with kind = 'SECURITY'. security_position is a 1:1 extension keyed on the holding carrying only which security and how many units; cash at the same broker is an ordinary CASH holding beside it, not nested. So is_liquid, expected_return_bp (ASSUMED), fee_bp (expense ratio) and provenance VALUED with last_confirmed_at already mean the right thing, and projection, money map, liquidity split and net worth gain investments untouched. Rejected: a separate position table, which duplicates four columns and needs a which-copy-wins rule. Cost: thirty positions are thirty map rows; a fold is presentation. createPosition writes both records and deletePosition removes both. Trades name their security directly so a realised gain survives the position. A VALUED holding's balance is units × price, not Σ deposits; documentPortfolio joins savings.ts cash with investments.ts positions, neither computing the other's number.
state: live

### D-182
date: 2026-08-29
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: A quantity is an integer of millionths, for the reason money is an integer of agorot
question: How is a share count represented, given fractional shares and D-003's no-float rule?
answer: As Quantity — an integer of millionths of a unit in @/core/quantity.ts, never a float. fromUnits/toUnits are the only float boundary, and valueOf(quantity, unitPrice) is the only multiplication that turns one into money, done in BigInt via divideRounded, which throws rather than silently rounding out of the safe range. It is not Money: no currency, no sign convention, and adding counts across securities is meaningless. Six digits is generous (brokers quote four or five). Rounding is half away from zero, so negating an amount negates its rounding. Rejected: decimal strings, a Money with a fake currency, whole units only. @/core/rate.ts shares divideRounded. (D-204 later moved valueOf to @/core/price.ts when a price stopped being Money.)
state: live

### D-183
date: 2026-08-29
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: Cost basis is a moving average, and there is one method rather than a setting
question: Which cost-basis method does the app use, and can the user choose?
answer: A moving average, with no FIFO, no lots and no setting. costBasis keeps a running pool of units and of cost; a buy adds to both, a sell removes units at the carried average and books the difference against actual proceeds. Fees sit inside the cost. The methods differ only in realised gain, which is a tax question this app has no opinion on, and two methods would be two answers to one question. Two states are reported, not smoothed: oversold (more sold than ever bought — owner started recording midway; unbacked proceeds must not show as a return) and quantityDisagreement (position units vs trade units — the statement is the authority, neither is corrected). Cost basis never crosses a currency: foreign-currency trades are skipped and counted. A position with no trades has no cost, not zero cost, so unrealisedGain is undefined.
state: live

### D-184
date: 2026-08-29
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: A rate is a stored record with a date, because a position is the case D-003 did not cover
question: How is a position in a foreign currency valued in the document's currency?
answer: fx_rate stores base, quote, date, millionths and source, and @/core/rate.ts's convert is the only function allowed to combine two currencies. A position no stored rate reaches is reported in its own currency and left out of the document-currency total by name — never converted at an invented rate, never dropped. D-003 (no rate in the document) was about card charges that already arrived converted; a USD position has not been converted by anyone. convert handles the minor-unit scale between currencies once. The TASE trap: a Tel Aviv price is printed in agorot while the security is in shekels; security.currency is the price's currency, stored in whole units, and the ÷100 belongs to the edge that read the quote, never a total. Source precedence is transcribed, then typed, then fetched — preferRate and latestPrice sort alike so a refresh never overrules the statement in hand.
state: live

### D-185
date: 2026-08-29
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: Investments is a place in Wealth, because a portfolio asks four questions the map has no column for
question: Where does the investments screen live in the navigation?
answer: /wealth/investments, a child of Wealth between Funds and Net worth, with What to buy (purchase plans) as a tab of it — not a tab of the money map nor of Savings & debts. Positions already appear on the map (D-181), which tempted building nothing, but a portfolio asks four questions the map has no column for: what is it made of, what did it cost, what has it made, does it agree with the statement. Order follows D-107: savings, funds, investments, and net worth last as the answer. The purchase plan is a tab by D-055's rule (a view of investments, not a destination). The palette reaches both by name (D-130). Nothing else in the rail moved.
state: live

### D-186
date: 2026-08-29
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: A valued holding's balance is its valuation, and the goal matrix totals goals rather than places
question: How does a position's market value reach the savings figures, and does it reach goals?
answer: holdingBalances takes an optional valuation per holding that replaces the deposit sum (not added to it — the deposits bought the units, so both would double-count); positionValuations produces them, and they reach the money map, liquidity split, net worth, Home's attention list and the projection, but deliberately not a goal's balance. A gain belongs to where money is held and reaches a goal only through deposits, so inGoals stays Σ deposits while liquidity.total carries valuation — F13's asymmetry again. So the money map matrix's bottom row totals the goals in each place, not the place. An unvaluable position is absent (deposit sum stands), not zero. Net worth gets the rows, not today's valuation, since a price belongs to a date; months before the first price are unknown (D-089). Found by driving the screen: Investments said ₪12,345 while the map said ₪0.00, with 2,339 tests green. savingsRepository imports investmentRepository only; createPosition and documentPortfolio live in portfolioRepository above both.
state: live

### D-187
date: 2026-08-29
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: The price provider is one host, symbols rather than ISINs, and unmapped is the default
question: Which provider does the price fetch D-178 allowed use, and how are securities mapped to it?
answer: Stooq: one allowlisted host, https, CSV, no key or account — the only free source a free unsigned app can use without making a person register. CSV because its worst case is one bad row. electron/prices.ts holds it and isAllowedPriceUrl guards it; the request carries only normalized symbols. No free source takes an ISIN, so securities map to symbols conservatively: a ticker with a suffix is taken as given, a ticker on a named US exchange in USD becomes <ticker>.us, and everything else is unmapped and not asked about — a symbol guessed onto the wrong exchange returns a real price for a different instrument. Consequently no Tel Aviv security is mapped, and the surface says how many were left out. The permission is localStorage, off by default, never in the document. A fetched price is stored FETCHED at the provider's date so a statement outranks it; every failure returns a reason and writes nothing. AMENDED by D-202: providerSymbol became providerMapping, which returns why a security is refused.
state: amended

### D-188
date: 2026-08-29
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: A holdings table is a deposit table, and the rung that always terminates is pointing at it
question: How is a broker statement read, and what is the manual fallback when there is no model?
answer: A broker statement is F17's third document kind, INVESTMENT, on the same ladder. Its repeating part is a HoldingsTableAnchor — one heading anchor and a column order, DepositTableAnchor's shape (D-153): rows are found by walking down from the heading each time. holdingsTable.ts differs from depositTable.ts in one test: a row must carry a name, ticker or ISIN rather than a date. The third rung is TableMapper — a thirty-row table cannot be retyped, so the user clicks the heading and says what each column holds, with roles guessed from the heading words and a live row count. Identification of each security is a confirm-surface control defaulted from ISIN then exact ticker, never a name. The statement date is required (a price with no date is not a price). Also fixed: documentTemplateRepository never decoded anchors, so a fund report's deposit table silently vanished on read since D-153; decodeTable is now the one reader. Migration 026 rebuilds document_template for the kind CHECK; an investment template may have no fields.
state: live

### D-189
date: 2026-09-02
milestone: M17
scope: ledger
title: M17's first edge was built by M6, so the milestone is four edges and a view
question: What happens to M17's scope when one of its five edges turns out to already exist?
answer: The milestone shrinks to four connections and one trace view and does not grow to refill the slot — D-179 settled that the scope is named and fixed. A paycheck line paying a debt had existed since migration 020: deduction_type.linked_debt_id, payrollPaymentRef keyed paycheckId:debtId, payrollPayments, syncPayrollPayments, run on every savePaycheckDraft; the deduction-types screen offers a debt as the third destination for LOAN_REPAYMENT types. The lesson: a tail item carried a long time reads as unbuilt, and nothing distinguished 'carried' from 'carried and since done' — a claim never checked against the code. D-179 is superseded rather than edited. Migration 027 remains the plan's debt payment.
supersedes: D-179
state: live

### D-190
date: 2026-09-02
milestone: M17
scope: ledger
title: A plan writes a debt payment, and the row it writes says what the app would have found anyway
question: How does a split plan's DEBT bucket write a payment on apply?
answer: At schema 27, debt_payment.source_kind gains FROM_PLAN with ref planId:period:bucketId, and applyPlanPayment upserts on it — applyPlanDeposit's shape. The bucket, not the debt, is in the key, so two buckets may pay one debt. It is written confirmed (a person pressed the button), all principal (interest_minor 0, editable), and dated the first of the plan's month. notYetSupported is removed rather than left empty. A debt that already discovers its own payments (merchant pattern, category, installment group, or a payroll link on a deduction type) is marked alsoDiscovered by selfDiscoveringDebts and the panel warns of a double count — a warning, not a refusal, because an extra payment is a real thing indistinguishable from the mistake. Accepted trade: a plan payment counts as actual in How it went, as FROM_PLAN deposits already do. Bug found: orphanedPayments tested sourceKind !== 'MANUAL', which would have flagged every plan payment orphaned; now DISCOVERED_SOURCE_KINDS lists what is checked, so a new kind is excluded by default.
state: live

### D-191
date: 2026-09-03
milestone: M17
scope: ledger
title: The queue's two promotions are one question, and the debt half writes no payment
question: How does the labeling queue promote a name to a bill or a debt payment?
answer: One 'Bill or debt?' panel with two answers, both existing writes reached from where the user knows the answer. A bill writes a FixedExpense matched on the merchant, seeded by proposeFixedExpense — arithmetic over that name's whole-ledger charges, not a detector: cadence may be undefined (never a default MONTHLY), the source is USER_DEFINED, not DETECTED_CONFIRMED. A debt payment writes no payment: it points the debt's MERCHANT_PATTERN tracking at the name and runs syncDiscoveredPayments, so charges arrive unconfirmed in the debts review queue, covering past and future. trackingOffer, in the domain, says what agreeing costs per debt: FREE, ALREADY (disabled), REPLACES (names what goes), or REFUSED (closed debt, or split purchase). A debt is chosen, never created. Category stays optional (D-009), and the panel says what skipping it costs. A name that is both a bill and a debt double-counts; debtFixedExpenseOverlaps warns on both sides, neither refuses. No schema change.
state: live

### D-192
date: 2026-09-03
milestone: M17
scope: ledger
title: The import review's rule offer is a scoped re-run, and it fills blanks rather than arguing
question: How does the import review screen offer a rule, given its suggestions were computed at stage time?
answer: Picking a category there offers the rule through offerForCorrection and the LearningOffer bar; accepting writes a LEARNED_FROM_CORRECTION rule and re-runs it over this import's rows (listUncategorizedStagedRows + planReapply), because an offer with no visible effect teaches that rules do not work (D-047's real blocker). It fills blanks only: NEW rows with no chosen category and no suggestion, so it never overrules the screen or a hand pick (D-009). That also makes the preview count exact — the count beside the buttons is what accepting produces, and moves live as the pattern is edited. It writes suggestions (suggested_category_id, suggested_rule_id), never chosen_category_id. Offered only when every row just categorized shares one merchantNormalized. The 'N times' count adds staged hand-picks and committed corrections, which are disjoint. Only the ledger half has undo; the import half needs none. ReapplyChange.transactionId became rowId. No schema change.
state: live

### D-193
date: 2026-09-03
milestone: M17
scope: ledger
title: The trace view is a list of arrows whose useful half is the ones that stop
question: What does the trace view show, and how?
answer: #/paychecks/trace, 'Where it went', a tab of Paychecks: one salary month from the slip, through the split, to its holding, as three sections of one-line arrows plus a destination list — not a Sankey. traceMonth is a pure function over existing rows; it writes nothing and needed no column, which was D-179's test that the edges were right. TraceEnd has four ways to arrive — holding, fund, debt paid down, spent — and BROKEN with one of six named reasons, rendered as a sentence about the link, never the money. Spent is not stopped; a fund end is legitimate (its balance is a snapshot). A plan bucket is followed only where a FROM_PLAN deposit or payment was actually written. generatedDepositHoldings asks deposits where they landed rather than trusting a goal's default. Driving it fixed 'followed' to include debt repayments (₪8,400 vs ₪9,900), and a #/paychecks/deductions self-redirect since D-170. M17 built: four edges and a view.
state: live

### D-194
date: 2026-09-03
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: A real broker statement read nothing, and the four assumptions that made it so
question: Why did a real broker statement read zero rows, and what does the reader rely on instead?
answer: Four fixture assumptions were false of the document; each is now settled by something the document states. (1) Heading ≠ column count (wrapped and split headings): tableShapeUnder takes the count from the first data line under the heading; LEADING_SKIPS is four. (2) A non-row ends the table (the cash line one cell short cut 31.9% of the portfolio): one column may be blank, chosen as the alignment leaving a name in the name column; two consecutive misses end the table and skips are counted. (3) TASE prices are agorot: priceScaleOf votes on quantity × price = value, multiplying value up rather than price down; atPriceScale applies it at the edge and the surface says so. (4) A summary's last number is a percentage: valueColumnOn reads it through the table's columns. Also מספר נייר is not an ISIN (seven digits): parked in ticker, needing its own column. Both statements then proved to the agora. AMENDED: D-195 gave the security number its own column, and D-204 made prices carry sub-agora digits.
state: amended

### D-195
date: 2026-09-03
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: The security number is a key and gets a column, because a ticker was never one
question: Where does a TASE security number live, given it is neither an ISIN nor a ticker?
answer: security.security_number at migration 028 with its own partial unique index. HOLDING_COLUMN_ROLES gains securityNumber; guessRole tests for it before the name (מספר נייר contains נייר). Matching is ISIN, then security number, then exact ticker, never a name, and matchedBy says which. upsertSecurityByIsin becomes upsertSecurityByKey trying both keys, and accepted NEW rows go through it (a colliding key would otherwise throw inside the accept). The migration copies (not moves) all-digit tickers into the column, and backfills neither when a number names two securities, since the unique index would fail and the document stop opening. Rejected: putting it in isin; making ticker unique; matching on name; migrating stored templates. Schema 28. A second statement now moves figures rather than opening twins. A row with no key (the cash line) still defaults to NEW. PositionEditor has no number field yet (U4).
supersedes: D-194
state: live

### D-196
date: 2026-09-04
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: A trades table is a sequence of dated events, which is what rejoins its split rows
question: How is a statement's trades table read, given its rows split across lines?
answer: As a sequence of dated events, not a holdings table with other roles: a line whose first cell is a date starts a row, and a following non-date line is the rest of it (MAX_ROW_LINES = 2); pdfText.ts is unchanged. tradeShapeUnder takes the widest row in a window, not the first. The blank column can move, so every position is tried and the alignment kept is the one where every named column gets a cell of the right kind. The trades table votes its own price scale on quantity × price = amount ∓ fee. upsertTrade's natural key is security, day, side, units, price — not the amount — since monthly and quarterly reports repeat trades. A broker's internal bookkeeping (tax paid printed as a purchase) reconciles perfectly, so a movement is written only against a security held at that place, and the confirm surface names the reason. Side comes from the kind word, never the quantity's sign; sideOf's vocabulary is closed, erring toward 'not a trade'. No schema change. Sales untried on a real document.
state: live

### D-197
date: 2026-09-04
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: A cost basis is shown as the walk that produced it, on a screen rather than in a pane
question: How does a position show where its cost came from, and where do per-position actions go?
answer: A position gets a screen at #/wealth/investments/:holdingId, linked from the row's name. costBasisSteps is the walk and costBasis is a fold over it, so the summary is literally the last step — one implementation (D-071). Price it, Record a trade, Edit and Close move off the list row onto the detail; Add position and Read a statement stay on the list. It is a screen, not a ListDetail pane (the walk is eight columns, and the list's flows claiming the window caused a flicker loop — tried and reverted). Deleting a trade or price needs a confirm. positionDetail takes its PositionReport out of documentPortfolio so figures are the same objects. PositionEditor and TradeEditor are FlowFrames. Trades in another currency are counted and named on the detail. No schema change. A real broker's trades through this screen untried.
state: live

### D-198
date: 2026-09-04
milestone: M17
scope: engine
title: The review is a phase of its own, per milestone, and what a unit leaves it is written down
question: Where does a unit's 'what the maintainer should try' go, when the session's answer dies with it and what gets accepted is a milestone, not a unit?
answer: Into docs/review.md as a queue, and the trying becomes a phase: the maintainer's own session, run when every unit of a milestone is built. A unit session appends one entry: route, file, what should happen, where it will likely break, and what could not be tried. Nothing to try means no entry. A milestone is closed by the review phase, never by the session that built its last unit. A fault found there becomes a unit on a tail; the review criticises and does not build. The review is also where the next milestone is chosen. A blocker still goes in the message. The review-phase skill is the procedure, and docs-check budgets review.md. AMENDED by D-238: the queue went unread (thirty entries), so review.md is frozen and the 'try this' is back in the closing message while it is warm. What survives is that only the maintainer closes a milestone, and review entries now live as review_entry rows.
state: amended

### D-199
date: 2026-09-04
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: The add-position form asks for the key its statement prints, and editing is not adding
question: What should the add-position form ask for, and what can editing a position change?
answer: The form asks what a Tel Aviv statement answers: Name, Security number, Kind, Priced in; Ticker, ISIN and Exchange sit behind a disclosure (closed for a new security, open when any is set). Without the number a hand-added position could only create, duplicating a security a statement already made. Validation is validateSecurity, not a copy in the form; normalizeSecurityNumber moves to the domain so '1,159,169' and '1159169' match the unique index. isValidSecurityNumber is only a typo check (digits; catches a ticker in the field). Units are offered from the trades with one click, never derived, since quantityDisagreement is information. Editing is not adding: the security picker and identity block disappear once the position exists, and the six changeable facts are now actually saved — HoldingDetail had only called setPositionQuantity, so four fields silently reverted (D-138). No schema change.
state: live

### D-200
date: 2026-09-04
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: A trade names the money that paid for it, and the difference is the answer rather than an error
question: How is a broker purchase linked to the bank transfer that funded it?
answer: security_trade.funding_transaction_id, nullable, at schema 29 — on the trade, not a link table, since a trade has at most one funder and only a person writes it (no guess, no confidence). tradeFunding reports EXACT, LEFT_AS_CASH or FROM_CASH as a sentence, never flagged or tinted, because a round transfer against exact purchases is normal. Partial funding is not modelled. fundingCandidates offers outflows within a fortnight either side, ranked by exact amount, then could-cover-the-whole, then closeness in days — never chosen automatically. It is a tab at #/wealth/investments/funding, since one transfer buys several securities. Rejected: auto-linking on exact amount, matching by the broker's holding_place, modelling broker cash as an intermediary. upsertTrade's key excludes the link so a re-import cannot clear it. A trade whose transaction was deleted is asked about again. daysBetween/addDays use civil day numbers (D-023); asFundingTransaction flips the ledger's negative outflow.
state: live

### D-201
date: 2026-09-04
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: Investments get a first-run step, and the app records an intention rather than offering one
question: How does a new document learn about investments, and can a plan name something not yet held?
answer: An eighth onboarding step, investments, between documents and checklist, as a member of ONBOARDING_STEPS so the wizard, the plugin's stepDetail and skipAll move together; skippable. It is a hub: Read a statement (StatementIntake, moved verbatim out of screens/Investments.tsx — untested accept path), Add a position by hand (PositionEditor), and a plan (PlanForm, shared with the What to buy tab). A plan may name a security nobody holds: resolvePlanSecurity creates one from a typed name, kind OTHER, no key, matched case-insensitively by name — the only place a name is a key. The checklist item is the one conditional item, shown only where a brokerage or position exists. The app records an intention and never offers one: no watchlist, no advice surface, and the plugin's guidance says so in the imperative. No schema change; the rail says Step N of 8.
state: live

### D-202
date: 2026-09-04
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: A refusal to price a security is a sentence, and the mapping that refuses is the one that says why
question: How does the price refresh explain the securities it cannot price?
answer: providerMapping replaces providerSymbol outright and returns either a symbol or a refusal — NO_SYMBOL, NOT_A_US_LISTING, CURRENCY_DISAGREES — so the sentence shown and the request made cannot disagree (D-036). securitiesToQuote stops filtering on ticker (held is the whole filter), so TASE holdings are named as uncovered; still only mapped symbols leave the machine. Three surfaces: the refresh panel names each unpriced security and whether it was not asked about or asked and unanswered; the portfolio's gap list gains a clause per unpriced position; the holding detail says what a refresh would ask it as, or that it never will. All end at REFUSAL_REMEDY: a price off the statement or typed by hand is the floor. Shown only where fetch exists. PriceRefresh.unmapped is UnmappedSecurity[]. Labels carry no Hebrew; מספר נייר goes through Bidi. No schema change. M16's first tail complete.
supersedes: D-187
state: live

### D-203
date: 2026-09-04
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: The statement's own arithmetic checks the reading, and a check that could not run says so
question: What catches a reader that mapped the wrong column, when the price-scale vote and the total proof both go silent?
answer: The statement's own arithmetic, per row. A local model mapped cost as value, so priceScaleOf returned unknown and prices stayed 100× too big, while the missing printed total let the proof decline — the screen looked green. A reader is a proposer; checks must survive its errors. holdingReconciles: units × price = value per row, after atPriceScale, with an agora-per-unit slack. pricePossible: no holding exceeds the portfolio (printed total, else Σ rows + cash) — needs no value column. A vacuous equation (the cash line, 0 × 0) is unchecked, not failed. unknown scale is now said out loud on the confirm surface, for holdings and movements. readHoldingRows finds a bare סה"כ summary line under the table through valueColumnOn (not a sub-total like סה"כ ניירות ערך); a pointed-at line wins. The mapper reports whether rows multiply out. Rejected: a better reader instead, recomputing value, magnitude thresholds, storing verdicts. No schema change; some readings now fail, which is the point.
state: live

### D-204
date: 2026-09-05
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: A price is not an amount: it carries six digits past the minor unit, and the value is where the rounding happens
question: How is a price finer than an agora stored, now that it is multiplied out and shown as an editable field?
answer: A price stops being Money. UnitPrice in @/core/price.ts holds micro — millionths of one minor unit — plus a currency; it does not add and has no sign, it meets a quantity and yields an amount. valueOf moves there, divisor 10¹², and the value's rounding happens in that one function only. Millionths of a minor (not major) unit because migration 030 is immutable and a major-unit backfill would need the currency table frozen into it; ×1,000,000 is right for every currency. formatPrice prints the digits the price has. Migration 030 rebuilds security_price (price_micro) and security_trade (unit_price_micro) by × 1,000,000 — no figure moves; fee and consideration stay Money. upsertTrade matches price within half an agora so old rounded rows do not duplicate. CostBasis.unitCost is a UnitPrice. Fetched quotes keep their decimals. Schema 30. The real money-market row now multiplies out exactly.
state: live

### D-205
date: 2026-09-05
milestone: M16
scope: engine
title: Every figure a reader produces is an editable field on the confirm surface, and what is edited is what is written
question: A reader mis-assigned a column (cost read as value) and every check downstream followed a wrong premise. Checks say a reading is wrong but not what is right; where does the last word sit?
answer: On the confirm surface. Every reader is a proposer: a person pointing, a model, or a stored template. None can be made right by being made better, so every figure a reader produces is a field, and the accept writes what is in the field. That covers per-row keys, units, prices and values, the as-of date, the cash and the printed total. An enum-like column such as trade side is a control, since an unrecognised word otherwise leaves the cost basis silently. The mapping is reachable from the surface over the text already read, so a column read as the wrong thing is one re-mapping, not twenty edits. The sum is derived, not read. restateX recomputes the proof from the figures the reading now holds, on the way out and on every keystroke; a proof line describing the model's figures would break D-138 one layer up. The edit goes into the reading (D-171's read entry), not an overlay beside it. A format still stores where the figures were, never the figures: an edit corrects one document and teaches nothing. ReadField.tsx holds the field kinds. Blank means the document printed nothing, and a keystroke that would make the text unreadable is refused, so there is no error state.
state: live

### D-206
date: 2026-09-05
milestone: M16
scope: engine
title: The same shape on the other three readers, and a reading's arithmetic is a function rather than a fact it stated once
question: How does D-205's editable confirm surface apply to the payslip, the fund report and the bank statement?
answer: The same way. The rule it produced: a reading's verdict is a function, never a fact computed once on the way out. There is one exported restate per document (restateSlip, restateReport, restateStatement), which the reader and the surface both call, so they agree by construction. A restate deletes a delta whose term is gone rather than carrying it. exactOptionalPropertyTypes keeps 'printed nothing' apart from 'is zero'. A figure the reader did not find is a field, not a missing row: every slot is listed whether or not it was printed, so typing a fee off the page turns an absent equation into one that runs. Rows (charges, adjustments) stay conditional. The import review is the fourth surface: its fields sit in the foot panel beside the file's own cells, and editStagedRow writes them. An edit never touches natural_key or status. It corrects what a row says, never which row it is, which keeps imports idempotent. A derived figure (a total, the direction) is text rather than a field, because the input is the place to correct it; the sign in the amount box is the gesture. MonthField exists because nothing on a slip checks its month.
state: live

### D-207
date: 2026-09-05
milestone: M16
scope: ledger
title: The plugin gets investments, and the conversation that transcribes a statement is checked by the statement's own arithmetic
question: How does a conversation help with a broker statement, and what checks its transcription?
answer: Five plugin tools: investments (read), record_positions, record_trades, record_exchange_rates, create_purchase_plans — four writes because a portfolio is four facts. The write is a confirm surface: record_positions takes the statement's value column and statement_total beside units and price and puts holdingReconciles and pricePossible verdicts in the dry run; record_trades runs tradeReconciles. The model is a proposer like any reader. The agorot trap is stated in the description (price per unit in whole currency units; TASE ÷100) and checked by printing units × price beside the printed value. Checks inform, never refuse. record_positions is idempotent (upsertSecurityByKey; existing position's units updated; recordPrice per day per source); record_trades leans on upsertTrade and reports already recorded. create_purchase_plans records an intention, never offers one (D-201). record_exchange_rates makes the plugin the only fx_rate writer — the app has no screen for it, so USD positions were UNCONVERTED. Rejected: a tool that opens the file, converting agorot in the tool, one combined tool.
state: live

### D-208
date: 2026-09-07
milestone: M16
scope: engine
title: The reading vocabulary is the fault rather than the reader, and the investments structure is the maintainer's to redraw
question: The second M16 review found the broker reader still failing. Is that the reader's fault, and should the vocabulary be widened?
answer: It is the vocabulary's fault. HOLDING_COLUMN_ROLES has no `cost`, so a money column printed beside the value had only `ignore` or `value` to go to, and a model chose `value`. TRADE_COLUMN_ROLES could not name half the movements page either. When the vocabulary does not contain what the page prints, that is not a proposer getting it wrong. Widening it was declined: the investments structure is to be redrawn by hand. M16 is HELD, not closed and not tailed. No session builds inside it until the structure is drawn, and a fault list against a design being replaced is not written. The hold is on building further, not a verdict on the price fetch, the funding link or the plugin's tools. record_positions and record_trades already carry the confirm checks (D-207), so a statement transcribed in a conversation reaches the document without the in-app reader. That is the road that survives removing it. This finding is what moved the reading vocabularies into the document (D-212).
state: live

### D-209
date: 2026-09-07
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: Emerald is an engine with four layers, and the line is that a format declares what a document says while code computes what it means
question: D-208 held M16 because a compiled-in vocabulary could not name a column a real statement printed — and payslips, fund reports and trades had hit the same wall. Is this a finance app, or a domain-independent mechanism with finance as content? And if the latter, what may a user declare and what must stay code?
answer: A domain-independent engine in four layers. The core (money, quantities, dates, the .emd, the ingestion ladder, mapping, the confirm surface, the proved/failed/unchecked triad, the model contract) knows nothing of money's purpose. An extension is a domain package and is code. A format is a document type and is data, living in the document: typed columns, identities, links. A template is how one issuer's file lands on a format, and it is unchanged. The line: a format declares what a document says, and code computes what it means. Identities, links and simple aggregates are declarable. Loops, traversals and decision trees (the splitter, cost basis, the trace view) are code. Correctness comes from declarations compiling to real DDL. Untyped storage (EAV, a value TEXT column, JSON blobs) is rejected outright as the version that becomes Notion. Judge every generalisation by one test: does it make the next extension cheaper? That rules out a declarative UI layer, a formula DSL and a general query tool. The work is sequenced so stopping after any step is allowed: vocabularies, then identities, then declared tables.
state: amended

### D-210
date: 2026-09-15
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: A vault of recipes is the engine built by hand, and it moves the line
question: Can a real domain that is not money be expressed as formats alone? The test case is an Obsidian recipe vault — twelve ingredient notes, two recipes and a targets note, built with Claude over three days.
answer: Yes, and the vault moves the line. Its frontmatter is already a format declaration, and it drifted within a week: two shapes for one price fact. Its `micros_basis: analogue` is the third state reinvented, and its prose runs Atwater identities by hand. But recipe nutrition needs aggregates across a join with unit conversion, which D-209 undercosted. The line that survives: stateless and relational is declarable; temporal, iterative or semantic is code. The forbidden set is unchanged, and the permitted set grows by a unit system (H7). Provenance and freshness is a new layer the engine owes: a source and a verified date per figure, downstream flagging, and notes that go stale visibly (H8). The reason is Daily Targets, figures a model computed once and froze into prose. A pack is mostly data — a seed, not a dependency — and generated tools make a format usable with no screen. The first unit, restated: make `Ingredients/Pasta.md` expressible.
supersedes: D-209
state: live

### D-211
date: 2026-09-15
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: The engine gets its thesis: it is where what a person and a model worked out goes to stay true, reading is one adapter, and the ledger is a pack enabled by default
question: Sessions could build against engine.md's mechanism and still not say what the product is for, because it had no thesis. What is it for, where does the ledger sit, and is this a new project?
answer: Thesis: Emerald is where what you and a model worked out becomes structure and stays true. Reading a document is one intake adapter, not the identity, so no screen may assume a document behind a format. Provenance and staleness (H8) is the reason the product exists. The privacy cut is a principle: designing a format is public-safe by construction and filling one is private by construction. A frontier model designs; a local model or a pair of hands fills. The ledger is a pack, enabled at first run unless declined, because its link into the card statement is the differentiator. D-227 later staged it behind a flag. The tie-breaker: the app never does the thinking the user came to do; it does the bookkeeping that stops the thinking from rotting. That rule is why H3 prompts and H8 flags rather than regenerates. The audience is not widened: it is the person who already keeps a vault by hand and is tired of it. Not a new project: the reading half was earned by four failures. The honest nearest neighbours are Grist and Airtable, and the delta is ingestion with proof, provenance and staleness. Opens H10 (cross-domain matching) and H11 (the authoring session).
state: amended

### D-212
date: 2026-09-15
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: The reading vocabularies move into the document, and the list stays closed at the moment the model is asked
question: A broker statement's cost column had no role in the closed HOLDING list, so a model put it in `value` and the portfolio read ₪72,000.00 against a printed ₪76,000.00 (D-208). How do the four compiled-in vocabularies stop being the fault without opening the lists a model answers from?
answer: The list stays closed at the moment the model is asked. It is read from the document instead of from a const. Migration 031 (schema 31) creates `column_role`, seeded with all 46 members of the four vocabularies in order. ModelTask.system and .schema become functions of the context. No document means no vocabulary, and then the model is not asked. The role union types survive: a stored mapping holds a string ColumnRoleId, and each reader narrows with an isXRole guard first, so an added role cannot reach a reader in the type system. A user-added role is named, not stored: it gives a model a right answer other than `value` until a format declares a column for it. `cost` is deliberately not seeded — it is to be added by hand. There is no remove (H9 at a small scale). This unit changed no behaviour, because the validators and pickers still read the const; D-213 is the other half.
state: live

### D-213
date: 2026-09-15
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: A role can be added where the gap is discovered, and every check moves from what the build shipped to what the document declares
question: D-212 moved the vocabularies into the document, but four validators and three pickers still filtered against the const, so an added role would be offered to a model and then thrown away. How is the other half done, and where does a person add a role?
answer: Every check that asked "is this a role this build shipped?" now asks "is this a role this document declares?" It is still a closed set, so D-114 is untouched. Model answers are checked against the same array their grammar's enum was built from; stored mappings and templates are checked against the document's vocabulary. A role is added in the select where the gap is found ("— add a role —" in SheetGrid and TableMapper), never on a settings screen. Validation runs before `mutate`, so a typo never marks the document dirty. A role must be a lowerCamelCase identifier, because it goes into a JSON Schema enum and through a constrained decoder (D-137). Readers that switch on a role gained an explicit guard. That guard is load-bearing in depositTable.ts, whose default arm would otherwise store an added role. `applyMapping` is pull-based and needed none. The plugin reads the same list. `cost` is still not seeded. Left open: toFundReportLines never returns depositColumns.
state: live

### D-214
date: 2026-09-15
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: Three arrows are the spec, the spine is scratched rather than the repo, and a declared table needs a reconciler rather than a migration chain
question: After U1a and U1b the question was whether to start from scratch, and the product was stated: read a document / talk with a model → format (which is the data) → make connections to other formats. New repository or not — and how does a document carry tables the build never shipped?
answer: The three arrows — intake, format, links — are the spec. Not a new repository: intake is scar tissue from real documents and cannot be re-derived, so it is imported rather than rebuilt. The format model is built clean in this repo, beside the finance app and touching none of it, in the same document with new tables. The first format is not money: it is Ingredients/Pasta.md. Per-document migrations were the wrong shape. Declarations are the source of truth and live in the document, and on open the app makes the tables match them: an idempotent reconciler, not a migration chain. schema_version governs only the built-in tables, and only the declaration language needs a version. The reconciler refuses rather than guesses on two changes: dropping a column that holds data, and changing a column's type or unit. M19 is re-scoped. U1a and U1b are the U0 prelude (D-208's fix, not the engine). Then U1 compiles a format to a table, U2 generates tools, U3 adds identities, U4 adds links and units, and U5 points intake at a format. U1 and U2 together are the minimum viable engine, and stopping after any unit is allowed.
state: live

### D-215
date: 2026-09-15
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: Emerald is the domain layer between a person and a model, prior structure is a dial rather than two adapters, and a format has to be able to come into existence
question: The author called it "the domain layer between AI and humans… whether they had original structure (card statements) or not". Is that the positioning? What does it change about intake, and what was M19 missing?
answer: The positioning is domain layer: the model is the interface, the person is the intent, and Emerald is the typed middle. The test it gives: if it is about presentation or storage, it is not the domain layer. That one test rules out a declarative UI layer and a formula DSL. Prior structure is a dial, not two adapters. A card statement, a payslip, a photograph and a bare intent are the same job; what changes is how much of intake runs, and the output is always a format — which is why U5 is a retarget. The scope is abstract data: enumerable, typed, comparable, linkable things, never abstract reasoning. Typed and proved, or it is a folder. U1 gains a clause: the declaration must be an artefact a model can emit and a person can read and diff. A format with no way to come into existence is a table nobody can have (H11 is the first arrow at the zero-structure end). The name stays Emerald, and "engine" survives only as the informal word.
supersedes: D-211
state: live

### D-216
date: 2026-09-15
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: A format is declared in markdown and compiled to a real table, and the reconciler refuses four things rather than guessing
question: Building U1: what is the declaration artefact, which types exist, and exactly what does the reconciler refuse?
answer: Seven types — text, integer, real, date, boolean, enum, money — and every property compiles to SQL: storage class, NOT NULL, IN, comparisons, GLOB. There is never a free-text CHECK, because a declaration carrying SQL is a program and an injection. `money` is two columns, minor units and currency, and may not carry a unit. The type list is closed (D-114). The reconciler refuses four changes to a column that holds data: dropping it; changing its type or unit (a unit change is caught declaration-to-declaration, so planReconcile takes three inputs); re-constraining it; and adding a required column to a table that has rows. Each is an ordinary step when the column is empty. A plan applies whole or not at all, and a refusal never stops a document opening. Every declared table is prefixed `fmt_`, so "touches only what it has a declaration for" is checkable. Reconciliation runs from the document store and never in the plugin (D-094). The authoring half is reversed by D-219: markdown as the primary artefact, `#/formats` as a review step only, and the skill as the authoring home. `#/settings/formats` is relabelled Reading formats.
state: amended

### D-217
date: 2026-09-15
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: A declaration generates a tool's vocabulary rather than the tool list, and the surface stops only adding where nothing else can edit
question: U2 was "the plugin's tools are generated from the declaration" — but tools/list carries no document. What does a declaration generate, and may the plugin correct and remove rows?
answer: A declaration generates a tool's vocabulary, schema and refusals, resolved against the document named in each call. There are never per-format tools: the tool list belongs to the server, and a format belongs to one file. Five tools, and `formats` says what this document can hold. Both rules are kept: no general query, and every tool takes one path. The tools reach only `fmt_` tables and take no SQL. The filters are match, search, order and limit — a floor, not a first instalment; a question none of them can ask is a new shaped filter, decided on its merits (D-095). The surface stops only adding: update_format_rows and delete_format_rows exist because a declared format had nowhere else its rows could be corrected. The no-edit assertion was narrowed to name its exceptions. An unknown key refuses the whole batch and names the real columns, because a caller that sent `protein` for `protein_g` believes it wrote the figure. Only unambiguous slips are forgiven: a number sent as a string, and an enum member in the wrong case. The checks live in row.ts so a refusal names its column. A table whose declaration is ahead of it is refused, not reconciled. A mutation that can refuse in advance takes no .bak.
state: live

### D-218
date: 2026-09-16
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: The ledger becomes a pack and the orientation re-centres on the engine; the docs move and the code does not
question: After D-216's authoring half was rejected, the engine carried too much finance-oriented code and knowledge to emerge, and starting from scratch looked easier. Rewrite, or what?
answer: No rewrite. About 30k of ~112k lines is domain-independent and was found by failing: the importer, the model contract, the shell, the document machinery, the plugin harness. The format model was 1,569 lines and two days old. The real fault was orientation: CLAUDE.md, conventions.md and status.md all opened as a finance app. So the ledger becomes a pack in the docs now — docs/packs/ledger/, with its own M0–M18 roadmap and its own half of the decision index — and in code only once a pack mechanism exists. The decision log splits into two indexes over one untouched archive. Numbering continues, so the engine's first milestone is M19. A decision is indexed by what it governs, not by the milestone that produced it. The history is told once, in archive/origins.md. The rule this buys: if a question can be answered by building the smallest version of the thing, it is not answered by another decision entry. Everything M19 answered by building held, and everything it answered by reasoning was rejected on sight.
state: live

### D-219
date: 2026-09-16
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: A format is edited in a form, the markdown becomes an import and an export, and a problem is drawn beside the field that caused it
question: The author rejected `#/formats` in ten seconds: it was a box you paste markdown into, and no user does that. He asked for a real UI editor. What does it edit, and where do problems appear?
answer: `#/formats` edits a declaration as fields: name, type, unit, required, members or bounds, and a sentence. The markdown becomes Copy the declaration and Import a declaration, and an import opens the editor rather than touching the document. The draft (domain/format/draft.ts) holds every field as the text typed, because a half-typed `-` is neither a number nor nothing. `declarationOf` is total, and legality stays `declarationProblems`' question. `declarationRejection` becomes the first line of `declarationProblems`, and each problem carries a column index and is drawn beside its control. Controls a type cannot use are not drawn, and a retype clears them (no unit on money). A blank field is unfinished rather than red. An existing format's name is not editable. The rest is unchanged: the plan before the button, refusals in `negative`, Save disabled while anything is refused, whole or not at all. This reverses D-216's last two sections. D-237 later replaced the single form with a four-step walk.
supersedes: D-216
state: amended

### D-220
date: 2026-09-17
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: A format is declared from the conversation, and the tool creates but never alters
question: The second ask: the write becomes an MCP tool rather than a repo-only skill. What does declare_format take, and may it change a format that already exists?
answer: declare_format, the sixth format tool, takes the declaration as structure — name, title, prose, columns — not as markdown. It is the third thing a model may produce, a declaration (D-137, D-132), and nothing in it can carry SQL. It creates and never alters. A format the document already declares is refused and sent to `#/formats` → Edit, because a change is drawn against the rows the table holds and a conversation is the wrong place to read that plan. It checks that the plan is exactly one CREATE_TABLE, which refuses an orphan `fmt_` table and an unreadable declaration language. An unknown key refuses the declaration; only a number sent as a string and a type in the wrong case are forgiven. Every problem comes back at once. A dry run that will refuse no longer invites a commit — one fix to five tools. The disease it names: a shared harness appending a sentence that is true only of the usual path.
state: live

### D-221
date: 2026-09-17
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: A format is worked out from an example, and the grammar it comes back in has no numeric leaf
question: The third ask: a local model infers a format's columns from an example — a label, a receipt, a note, a spreadsheet. Where does its answer land, and what may the grammar ask for?
answer: The answer is a draft that lands in D-219's editor as fields, with the plan above Save, and nothing is written until someone presses it. The grammar keeps D-132's mirror rule: every leaf is a string, a boolean or a member of the closed type list, and no leaf is a number. So min and max are not asked for — a bound read off one example becomes a CHECK on every future row. Notes are not asked for either: they are the person's argument, and a model's version would be filler. The seven types are handed over (D-114). ModelTask moved to src/domain/modelTask.ts so the engine does not import the pack, and the boundary became a test (format/boundary.test.ts). A spreadsheet is the same question, not a second adapter: its first rows go in as pipe lines, capped at 12 rows and 60 lines. A proposal is always a new format, never a revision. It is not a plugin tool, because the plugin has no socket; this is for the model on the machine. The model's real answers are unproven, because the socket is the shell's.
state: live

### D-222
date: 2026-09-17
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: Identities: a `CHECK` says what cannot be stored, an identity says what should add up
question: U3: how does a format say what has to add up — 4P + 4C + 9F + 2Fib ≈ kcal, units × price = value — without a free-text CHECK? And what happens to a row that does not add up?
answer: A CHECK refuses the row; an identity reports on it. No identity becomes SQL, so changing one alters no storage, and a verdict is computed on read and never stored. The syntax is a text expression: + - * /, brackets, numbers and this format's own numeric columns. × · ÷ are accepted, and implicit multiplication is a parse error. There are no functions, conditionals, loops, aggregates or other formats. The engine parses it and evaluates it in TypeScript, so it never reaches db.run. A declaration that does not parse is refused when it is declared. Verdicts are proved, failed or unchecked. Unchecked — an empty column, a zero divisor, or two currencies with no rate — is the common case, not a failure. Money enters as minor units, so a tolerance on money is in minor units, and a zero tolerance means exact. H3 is built as prompted: a format with nothing that must hold is allowed only if it says so (`nothing_must_hold`), and that is enforced in declarationProblems. A model is not asked to propose identities. Schema 33. Σ rows = printed total waited on U5, and the cooked-to-dry check on U4's link.
state: amended

### D-223
date: 2026-09-17
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: The engine owns dimension and conversion; a factor that crosses a dimension is a column
question: H7: should the engine know about units? And when a figure is in ml and an identity wants grams, who supplies the density?
answer: The engine owns dimension and conversion, and a factor that crosses a dimension is a column somebody declared. kg to g is arithmetic and the engine does it. ml to g needs a density, which is a fact about pasta, so unit.ts stops and names the missing fact. There are six dimensions, taken from physics rather than food: MASS, VOLUME, LENGTH, TIME, ENERGY and COUNT, with COUNT seeded with only `each`. Units are rows in the document, not a const, because a tablespoon is 15 ml here and 20 ml in Australia. A convention says so in its note. record_units adds a unit or corrects a factor, and refuses to change what a unit measures. A conversion is written, never inferred: `in` is a postfix operator that binds tighter than `*`, and nothing else in an expression consults a unit. Full dimensional analysis would reject Atwater, whose 4 is kcal/g. A column may not be called `in`. A declaration is refused for an unknown unit, a figure of unknown unit, or a cross-dimension `in`. Schema 34. Never put a density in unit.ts.
state: live

### D-224
date: 2026-09-17
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: Links, and the aggregate across one — one hop out, one hop back, and no traversal
question: U4b: a recipe's kcal is the sum of its lines, and each line's is an amount times an ingredient's figure. How does one format reach another format's rows without the language becoming a query language?
answer: A `link` column, the eighth type, holds the id of a row of a declared format and may point at its own format. Its REFERENCES clause enforces nothing, because foreign keys are off (D-067); the refusal that holds is missingTargets, at write time. An aggregate has a name, one of five folds (sum, count, minimum, maximum, average), the rows of another format whose link names this row, and an expression evaluated on each. One hop only: `ingredient.kcal` is a name and `ingredient.supplier.kcal` is a parse error, so a traversal has no syntax. A fold is unchecked in whole if any row it covers cannot answer, and it names that row: a sum that skipped a row is not a smaller sum. A sum over zero rows is 0, and a minimum, maximum or average over zero rows is unchecked. declarationProblems takes the neighbouring declarations, so it can refuse an aggregate gathering by a link that points somewhere else. An aggregate is folded on read, never stored, and compiles to no SQL. An identity may name one. Nothing cascades: deleting a referenced format is refused, naming its referrers. A model is not offered `link`. declare_format cannot set up the recipe case alone. Schema 35, declaration language 3.
state: live

### D-225
date: 2026-09-18
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: A quantity carries its own unit per row, and a conversion crosses a dimension through a declared ratio
question: A recipe line is 150 g on one row and 200 ml on the next, and converting ml to grams needs a density that sits on the ingredient, across the link. How does a unit vary per row, and how does a conversion cross a dimension without the engine knowing any density?
answer: `quantity`, the ninth type, compiles to two columns — a REAL figure and a TEXT unit — as money does. It is the right type only when the unit genuinely varies between rows; otherwise use a real with a declared unit. A quantity takes no bound, and a model is not offered the type. The bridge is written `amount in g via ingredient.density`. The ratio is a column declared as one unit `per` another (density: g per ml), and the reconciler refuses a change to `per` just as it refuses a unit change. The bridge is read only when it is needed: a line already in grams converts without it, so one identity covers grams, millilitres and eggs. It converts in either direction. A `via` that could never fire is refused when it is declared. A column may be called neither `in` nor `via`. convertAcross only does arithmetic on a fact it was handed; putting a density in unit.ts is the mistake H7 was opened to prevent. Schema 36, declaration language 4.
state: live

### D-226
date: 2026-09-18
milestone: M19
scope: engine
title: Intake targets a declared format: a reading is a mapping onto its keys, and the proof is the document's own
question: U5: every row reached a declared format one at a time. How does a spreadsheet of four hundred lines get read onto a format, and who proves the reading?
answer: A reading maps cells onto the format's keys (offeredKeysOf — the same keys record_format_rows takes), so a sheet and a tool call meet in one coerceRow. Money and quantity need no special case. Intake proves nothing itself. row.ts says whether a value types, and the format's own identities, tallied over the staged rows before any exists, say whether they add up. Those are two counts and stay two. A file column may never fill a link, because H10 is open. A link can instead be fixed for the whole file, and so can a currency or a unit — each is a fact about the file, not about a row. Idempotency is by file hash only. Re-reading a file offers to remove what it wrote, and a person says yes. The engine will not invent what makes two rows the same row, so there is no declared key. Provenance is three tables, never a hidden column on a declared table. Superseding removes the rows and keeps the reading. The engine has its own parseFigure rather than reaching into the pack. A label-value PDF onto a format is not built. Schema 37.
state: live

### D-227
date: 2026-09-18
milestone: M20
scope: engine
title: The engine gets its own surface, and the pack is staged behind it
question: M19's review did not run, and the surface was judged not intuitive, not fitting of new structure, confusing, and making things untestable. Is that a fault in a unit, or a verdict on the milestone — and what does the app do about an engine that can declare a kind of thing and never show you one?
answer: M20 builds the engine's own surface and the ledger is staged behind it — hidden, not deleted, because if the engine's surface turns out wrong the cost was a flag. The line the milestone is drawn on: a format is the user's surface, not a declaration the person made once, so it is a place with a name and its rows are on it. And from here on, anything a person can do on a screen a conversation can do too — the audit that proves it is a unit, and what the rule then requires is M21.
state: live

### D-228
date: 2026-09-18
milestone: M20
scope: engine
title: The staging flag is per machine, filters only what is offered, and moves the root
question: D-227 fixed that the ledger is staged. Where does the switch live, what exactly does it hide, and where does a document land with the pack off?
answer: One boolean in localStorage, off by default, switched at `#/settings/packs` — per machine, because a rail is not a fact about a file. The filter is on what you are offered and nowhere else: `railSections` thins the rail and the palette while every question about a path reads the whole list, so a bookmarked `#/wealth/funds/3` still lands on a working screen. The root moves to `#/formats`, and the wizard about banks goes with its pack.
state: live

### D-229
date: 2026-09-18
milestone: M20
scope: engine
title: A format's rows are a screen, the form checks nothing of its own, and a verdict is drawn before the row exists
question: Building the hole D-227 named — does the row form validate what a person types, and when can a verdict be shown?
answer: The form checks nothing of its own. Every field on `#/formats/:name` collects a string, the whole draft goes to `coerceRow` — the same function `record_format_rows` calls — then `danglingLinks`, then the write, and whatever sentence comes back is printed verbatim. A screen that knew on its own what a date looks like would be a second answer to a question the document already answers, and two answers drift. The verdict is folded on the draft before the row exists.
state: live

### D-230
date: 2026-09-18
milestone: M20
scope: engine
title: A format begins at three doors, and the app's worked example stops being one domain
question: The three ways into a format all worked and none of them read as a choice. And what should the worked example be, now that pasta is too specific to read as anything but a recipe app?
answer: The three ways are a chooser in the middle of the screen, one card each, ordered by D-215's dial of how much prior structure you hold, and each card says what the way costs you: a document you already have, a file somebody wrote, or an empty form. The worked example stops being one domain — and the one that mattered was in the domain layer, where the no-identity refusal quoted Atwater at somebody declaring a book.
state: live

### D-231
date: 2026-09-18
milestone: M20
scope: engine
title: The nine types get a glyph and a sentence, written once and read by both surfaces
question: Three of the nine type names are jargon, one is a relation rather than a value, and two compile to two columns without saying so. Rename them on the screen?
answer: No. Renaming gives the language two vocabularies, and the half a person typed into a screen would stop matching the half a conversation wrote — exactly the drift the parity rule exists to stop. Every tile is headed by the declaration's own word, with a glyph and a sentence added beside it. `TYPE_WORDS` in `src/domain/format/vocabulary.ts` is the one place either is written, read by the editor's tiles and by `declare_format` alike; the hand-written list it replaced named seven of nine.
state: live

### D-232
date: 2026-09-18
milestone: M20
scope: engine
title: An identity is drawn as a balance, and the same balance draws the verdict
question: The identity text box is the least intuitive control in the app and it guards the one thing the engine has that a spreadsheet, Notion and Grist do not. What replaces it — and is it a node graph?
answer: A balance, not a graph: an identity is not a network, it is an equation with two sides, and every real one has the shape these things, weighted, equal that thing. Two pans of weighed columns with the tolerance under the fulcrum, and the same picture draws a row's verdict — each chip its contribution, a chip that cannot answer greying the whole thing. A view over the expression text, never a second storage, with the text box one press away for the algebra two pans cannot say.
state: amended

### D-233
date: 2026-09-18
milestone: M20
scope: engine
title: A connection is a drag between two endpoints, and the ports carry the direction
question: A `link` and an aggregate are facts about two formats, and the one-format form had to ask a person to remember the other one — it printed its own ordering problem as an apology. Where do connections live?
answer: Connections is a place: `#/connections`, a rail item of the engine's, which is why staging the pack does not touch it. Each format is a bubble, its columns are endpoints around it, and a connection is a drag from one endpoint to another into a dialog that defines it. The ports carry the direction, so the drag is symmetric, and a figure dropped on a format that points nowhere here writes the link and the fold together — U4b's ordering problem answered from the side where both formats are on the screen. A view over the declarations, storing nothing new.
state: live

### D-234
date: 2026-09-18
milestone: M20
scope: engine
title: Parity is derived from the writes both halves share, and a gap carries the proof it is still open
question: The parity rule has been prose since D-227, and prose is how the drift it was written against happened in the first place. How is it counted rather than promised?
answer: `src/parity.test.ts` derives the capabilities rather than listing them. Both halves write through the same functions — a screen inside `mutate(...)`, a tool inside `mutatingCall(...)`, neither holding a line of SQL — so every exported function in the format repositories that runs a statement is a way this engine changes a document, and every one must be named by an entry saying which screen and which tool reach it. A half that is deliberately missing is a `gap` carrying the assertion that keeps it honest. Six are one-sided; one thing was fixed rather than filed, because the From a declaration door refused every `link`.
state: live

### D-235
date: 2026-09-19
milestone: M20
scope: engine
title: Intake gets its tool half — the dry run is the preview, and the link a caller names is the one thing the screen never had to check
question: D-226 retargeted intake at a declared format and U6 counted what that left: a screen and no tool. A person with four hundred lines in a file could be told to go and click, and nothing else. What is the tool half, and does absorbing the pack's readers mean rewriting the pack's screens?
answer: `read_onto_format` and `supersede_reading`, and they are the general form of `statement_preview` and `import_statement` — the reader itself generalized, not the pack's three screens rewritten, because the pack is parked and its tables are not `fmt_` tables. Nothing in them parses anything, and the dry run is the preview. Two things a grid gave a person for free had to be built: the mapping printed with the file's own header and first value beside every key, and a link a caller names checked, which the screen got from its select.
state: live

### D-236
date: 2026-09-19
milestone: M20
scope: engine
title: An identity is not an equation — it is one column, and whether the others work it out
question: *What has to add up* has been rebuilt twice — a text box in M19, a balance in M20 — and read as obvious neither time. Does it need a third control, or is the question wrong?
answer: The question was wrong. The review drove the balance twice in one session, once as a way to read a verdict and once as a way to write one, and the two halves came back with opposite verdicts: flow 3 was okay against counterintuitive. So the balance is a good way to read a verdict and a bad way to write one — reading, you are handed a picture and asked what it says; writing, you are handed two empty pans and asked to assemble algebra, which is a harder question than the one a person actually has. An identity is asked as one column and whether the others work it out.
supersedes: D-232
state: live

### D-237
date: 2026-09-19
milestone: M20
scope: engine
title: Declaring a format is a walk, and a format that exists is added to or removed
question: The by-hand door was one screen holding every question about a format at once, and it was judged very heavy, full of text, and not in the chronological order of the actions. Fewer fields, or a different shape?
answer: A different shape — the fields were not wrong, the shape was, because a form asks everything at once and a person decides one thing at a time. Four steps in the order the decisions are made in: what kind of thing this is, what you know about one of them, what has to add up, anything worth writing down. Step 2 is a sub-walk per column, type first, so only that type's questions exist. All three doors land in it, opening on the first step the draft has not answered. A refusal now says which part of the declaration it is about (`FormatAspect`), because routing it by what is missing lands three sentences on one step. The only edits left are add a column or remove the format.
state: amended

### D-238
date: 2026-09-20
milestone: M22
scope: engine
title: Emerald is Excel that will not let you be wrong quietly — the ledger is the proof, and the engine's finish line is D-214
question: What is Emerald for, who is it for, and why does the build feel heavy and unfocused when the commit rate says it is fast?
answer: Pace was never the problem — 267 commits in eight weeks. The cause is that M20 U0 staged the pack and so removed the only real documents from the default path, leaving the engine with no user, the maintainer included. The pitch becomes 'Excel that reads your documents and won't let you be wrong quietly'; the ledger is the front door and the proof rather than a rival; declaring is the escape hatch. The engine gets the finish line it never had, which is D-214: done when the ledger is nothing but declared formats, identities and links and no non-fmt_ table is left. M20's tail stops, T9 survives as a standing bar, and the order becomes M22 dogfood, M23 two doors, M24 the engine eats the pack.
supersedes: D-198
state: amended

### D-239
date: 2026-09-20
milestone: M22
scope: engine
title: An engine-only server — the definitions are the bill, the filter is on what is offered, and it is an argument rather than document state
question: What does using the MCP actually cost, and is it the calls or something else?
answer: The definitions, not the calls. tools/list was 46 tools and ~23,890 tokens, ~19,250 of it the ledger's 38, and it sits in context on every turn of every session the server is loaded in — a 300-turn session spends ~7M tokens on schemas before making one call. --engine-only cuts it to 9 tools and ~4,970. It must be a server argument rather than a document's pack flag because tools/list carries no document (D-217). The filter is on what is offered and nowhere else, which is D-228's own rule: a caller naming a ledger tool still gets an answer rather than 'unknown tool'.
state: live

### D-240
date: 2026-09-20
milestone: M22
scope: engine
title: Four formats hold this project's memory: a link resolves by id and not by name, and the proof machinery is idle exactly as predicted
question: Can the engine hold this project's own notes, and what does it cost to find out?
answer: Yes, and nothing needed building first. docs/memory.emd holds M20 entire — 11 decisions, 11 units, 3 review entries, 4 open questions — declared through the MCP alone. The ruler held: M20's ten built units come back in one call of a few hundred tokens against a 95-line roadmap section read end to end 28 times. Two findings the ingredient fixture could not have produced: a link resolves by generated id while the name a person uses is a different column, so a self-referential batch is two passes; and a link prints as a uuid in both views. All four formats carry nothing_must_hold, which is gap #1 on schedule.
state: live

### D-241
date: 2026-09-20
milestone: M22
scope: engine
title: A link is read back as a name — and the rule for it already had four copies
question: A link prints as a uuid where a person expects a name. Should the engine print the target's first text column, or should a declaration name its own display column?
answer: Neither — it was never an open question. IntakePanel's comment had already decided it (the first text column, and a declaration has no display column) and the rule had been copied four times and applied only where a person chooses a link, never where one is read. defaultOrder's comment said it outright: 'without the language having a word for it'. So labelColumn in declaration.ts, one copy, four call sites, resolved by the ids a batch references rather than the target's first page. The same rule fixed the default order, which was the generated id in the tool and the naming column on the screen — the same rows, read two ways, in two orders.
state: live

### D-242
date: 2026-09-20
milestone: M22
scope: engine
title: Won't let you be wrong quietly is two mechanisms, and most formats only get the first
question: All four memory formats declared nothing_must_hold. Does that mean the engine's headline does nothing for list-shaped data, and is the notes crowd therefore a distraction?
answer: No. The claim is two mechanisms that were being treated as one: it refuses what contradicts what you declared — types, required, enum members, a link that must resolve, checked at write on every format — and it proves what has to add up, which is identities and only where figures relate. A list-shaped format only ever gets the first, and that is still everything a folder of markdown gives you none of. Saying the sentence and meaning identities writes off everyone whose data is a list. What the notes crowd is actually missing is H8, provenance and staleness, which D-211 called the reason the product exists and which is unbuilt.
supersedes: D-238
state: live

### D-243
date: 2026-09-20
milestone: M22
scope: engine
title: A repo's memory is the second crowd, and the markdown is rendered from the document rather than replaced by it
question: How does a session know to use the document instead of grepping the docs, and what does the repo-memory idea open?
answer: It did not know — status.md said the file existed and nothing told a session to ask it, and CLAUDE.md never named it. CLAUDE.md now carries four questions and the call that answers each. Checking that section caught a second problem: the document holds M20 and every decision since went to markdown alone, so M22 U5 is keeping it current. Git is what decides whether this leaves the repo — a .emd is 784KB of binary a commit, undiffable and unmergeable — so the markdown is rendered from the document rather than replaced by it. Grep keeps working and keeps being right, and if the idea is wrong what is left behind is the markdown.
state: amended

### D-244
date: 2026-09-20
milestone: M22
scope: engine
title: The unit is the serializer, not the file — a git filter and a generated markdown are the same work plumbed two ways
question: If the markdown still exists, is the efficiency not lost and the data not duplicated — and why not make the .emd itself diffable instead of adding a file?
answer: The efficiency never came from the markdown being absent; it came from asking a filtered question instead of reading a file, and a rendered file costs nothing until something reads it. It is duplicate, and that is only safe because it is generated and a check enforces it. The 40x was measured against this project's prose-over-six-files and leading with it would test the wrong claim — what survives is a write that is refused and a shape that cannot drift. Making the .emd diffable is git clean/smudge filters, which wins on nearly everything and loses on a fresh clone being broken until configured. It does not replace U4, it renames it: both shapes and the reverse import all need one deterministic, complete, round-trippable text serialization, so the unit is the serializer and the plumbing is a later cheap choice.
supersedes: D-243
state: live

### D-245
date: 2026-09-20
milestone: M22
scope: engine
title: A serialized row is a block named by what it is called, the id is not written, and the empty string is the one value a tool and a table disagree about
question: D-244 said the serializer had to be complete, deterministic and logical rather than byte-level, and left the shape open. What does a row look like, where does its name go, and what happens to the id?
answer: A row is a ### heading and a key: value line per column, never a pipe table row — a pipe cell cannot hold a newline, so a text column holding a paragraph would lose it silently. The heading is the row's naming cell itself rather than a copy of it, so the two cannot drift. The id is not written at all: every link is by the target's name (D-241), reading back mints fresh ids and rewires the links, and the property that matters is that text to document to text is byte-identical. Rows order by name then by rendered body, both of which the text carries, because an order depending on something the text lacks would not survive its own round trip. The price is three refusals — a linked-to format must have a text column, and its names must be filled in and unique — plus a fourth for a link whose row is gone. Text is the one type that skips coerceValue: a tool reads an empty string as nothing given, and a NOT NULL column holding one is a row SQLite permits. Output is a list of named parts, so a file, a git filter or both stay a later choice.
state: live

### D-246
date: 2026-09-20
milestone: M22
scope: engine
title: A document may be text, and the choice is per file — which retires the rendered markdown, the git filter, and the argument between them
question: A rendered markdown file and a git clean/smudge filter both solve 'the document is a binary git cannot review' by adding something beside it. Is either sufficient, or can a .emd simply be text?
answer: It can. The premise that a .emd is SQLite bytes on disk is an implementation choice, not a property of the product, and had never been written down as a decision. Measured: opening the binary is 1.1ms and rebuilding the same document from text is 9.5ms; as text it is 149KB against 792KB of SQLite, because the binary is mostly empty pages for 52 tables holding 285 rows; no column anywhere is a BLOB, so nothing needs base64; ~29 microseconds a row extrapolates to ~1.8s for D-118's 60k-row ledger, and that figure is optimistic because it is node's native SQLite rather than sql.js in WASM. The seam is two functions wide — openDatabase and exportDatabase — because everything else talks to an in-memory Database and sql.js has no other mode. U4's writeDocument/readDocument is the other half, already built. So: text or bytes, chosen per file and detected on open. A repo's memory is hundreds of rows and text wins on every axis; a decade of transactions is 60k rows and binary wins. It retires the rendered file, its staleness check, the git filter and the broken fresh clone. It costs two formats supported for good and a serializer that today covers fmt_ tables and not the ledger's thirty.
state: live

### D-247
date: 2026-09-20
milestone: M22
scope: engine
title: A text document's row ids are derived from its rows, because a random one is stale before the caller can use it
question: D-246 said a .emd may be text, but the text carries no row ids (D-245) — so a document read from text mints them on open. Does that matter, and where does the schema version live in a text file?
answer: It matters and it was a bug: random ids meant the same file had a different identity on every open, so writing a link over an id a previous plugin call handed back refused every time with 'no row with that id'. Ids are now derived from the row's own content plus its occurrence — a uuid with version nibble 8, which RFC 9562 reserves for derived bits. Editing a row changes its id; adding one leaves the others alone. The seam is one module, data/storage/encoding.ts: two openers, one writer, a sniff. The schema version is the first line of the text, above the parts, so splitParts needed no knowledge of it. A text save is verified against a freshly created document at the same schema rather than against a list of the pack's tables, and refuses a ledger, an edited seed row, or a document that declares nothing. Re-measured in WASM: 38.5 KB against 796 KB, but 400us a row, which is tens of seconds at 60k — the argument for per-file rather than against it. Still open: an id printed by a write to a text document is stale, because the row is written back with no id in it.
state: live

### D-248
date: 2026-09-23
milestone: M23
scope: engine
title: A blank sheet is a door with no steps: types are guessed from the cells, the press says nothing has to add up, and it adds no write
question: What is M23's grid door, how does it decide types and H3, and is adding a column to an existing format a new write?
answer: The third door is a blank sheet (src/domain/format/sheet.ts, ui/formats/Sheet.tsx): title, headers, typed cells; Create adopts the format and inserts the rows in one mutation. A type is guessed only when every cell agrees (integer, real, date, boolean, else text), never enum or money; a pick wins; enum values are the distinct cells. Seven types, no link or quantity. It declares nothingMustHold: true, with the sentence beside Create as the deliberate answer. It adds no write: adoptFormat already creates and alters, and add-a-column stays parity's existing gap. Cost: edit only adds a column, so a sheet-born format cannot gain an identity on screen.
state: amended

### D-249
date: 2026-09-23
milestone: M23
scope: engine
title: Tools first, the ledger second, the development pack third, and every screen waits for one UI milestone — parity keeps the list of what it owes
question: After M23, what order does the work take, and what happens to engine screens and the parity rule while the ledger moves onto the engine?
answer: M23 closes short (U2 built; U1 and U3 move to M27). Order: M24 — what the ledger needs from the engine, as tools, opened by U0 (parity becomes lopsided) and U1 (a survey of every ledger table for what the engine cannot hold; that list is the milestone). M25 — the ledger becomes a pack (D-214): screens unchanged, backend onto fmt_ tables, old documents convert on open with a backup proven on a copy first; first question is whether a pack may carry code. M26 — the development pack from memory.emd's four formats. M27 — UI/UX for engine and packs, done when parity's derived 'screen trails' list is empty. Parity: the tool half stays mandatory; a missing screen half is a gap the test derives itself with the proof no ui/ file reaches it; the ledger is not exempt. M21 dissolves: its tools land in M24 behind the dry-run commit gate, its permission screen is M27's.
state: amended

### D-250
date: 2026-09-23
milestone: M23
scope: engine
title: UI before the development pack: M26 is the base UI, M27 is the first pack after the ledger, and its views are what it is for
question: Where does the development pack go, and what does 'with UI' mean for it?
answer: Amends D-249's order: M24 tools the ledger needs -> M25 ledger becomes a pack -> M26 UI/UX for engine and packs (no engine screen before it; done when parity's screen-trails list is empty) -> M27 the development pack, the first pack after the ledger. The pack is views as well as formats: a roadmap (milestones in order with units and decisions), a board by state, a decision page, and what's open. It needs a milestone format (ref, title, goal, state, position). A view is declared by the pack as data and drawn by generic engine UI, so no pack gets hand-written screens.
supersedes: D-249
state: live

### D-251
date: 2026-09-23
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: A missing tool is owed or refused, a missing screen is derived, the ledger is a ratchet, and a proof follows imports
question: How does src/parity.test.ts make D-249's lopsided parity mechanical — what may a one-sided capability say, how is the ledger counted, and does a proof follow imports?
answer: A capability with a screen and no tool needs a gap whose answer is OWED by M24 or REFUSED by a decision found in the log (all three engine gaps are owed by M24). A capability with a tool and no screen writes no gap: it is a derived screen trail, proven unreached from ui/, and exported with everything else each half owes to docs/parity.md via toMatchFileSnapshot. The ledger is derived, not listed: 174 writes, 118 with no tool and 4 with no screen at M24 U0 — two ceilings in the test that may only fall (amends D-249's claim that the ledger has both halves). Proofs follow imports through a name-level call graph over every non-surface file, chunked by indentation; reach stops at the first write it meets (the open path adds units through applyDocument); by name and by mention, so its error is a false reach that turns a proof red, never a missed one. Settles M22 U6 #2.
state: live

### D-252
date: 2026-09-23
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: The ledger needs eleven things from the engine and none is a new shape: polymorphic references are several links with at most one set, reconciliation is declared, and the rest is code
question: What must the engine be able to express before the ledger's 37 tables can become declared formats, and what of the ledger cannot become a declaration at all?
answer: Eleven gaps, M24 U2-U12: connect tool (gap #11); add-a-column and take-a-format-away tools; unique column (M22 U3 #7); link written by name (M22 U3 #6); fixed-point, month and instant types plus a ratio dimension; row constraints compiled from structure (required-when, at-most-one-of, sign-when) - never free-text CHECK; filtered aggregate; file column fills a link (H10); a reading writes its own header row so reconciliation against a printed total is an aggregate plus identity; removing a linked row names what dangles; a pack's seeded rows. Polymorphic references become several links with at most one set - no link that names any of N formats. Tables: 20 easy after U6-U8, 9 hard (type/constraint/seeds), 8 are intake machinery the engine replaces. Categorisation, statistics, fixed-expense detection, installments, fees, FX, splitting, projections are temporal/iterative/semantic - code; M25 cannot finish unless a pack may carry code or the line moves. Map: docs/packs/ledger/onto-formats.md. Rejected: polymorphic link type, PDF intake in M24, many-valued link, argmax fold.
state: live

### D-253
date: 2026-09-23
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: A conversation connects two formats that exist, and it only ever adds
question: What is the conversation's half of #/connections (parity gap #11), given declare_format creates and never alters?
answer: connect_formats (src/plugin/tools/formats.ts): format, target, optional column (defaults to target name), required, note, optional aggregate {name, of, works_out, unit, note} with no over_* (the link is the over). Pure shape in connect() in domain/format/connections.ts; judged by declarationProblems on the merged set then planFor; adopted by adoptFormat, link first, in one transaction. Only adds: any plan step other than ADD_COLUMN is refused; required on a table with rows is the reconciler's REQUIRED_ON_ROWS. Naming an existing link to the target folds a new aggregate over it; a non-link column or a link elsewhere is refused (retype/re-point stay at #/connections). declare_format is unchanged. The panel still builds its own drafts (buildDrafts) because it also edits and removes.
state: live

### D-254
date: 2026-09-23
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: A conversation adds columns and identities, and removes a whole format no other refers to — a tool, not a refusal
question: Do 'add a column' and 'take a format away' get tools (D-251's last two owed engine gaps), and on what terms?
answer: Yes, both. extend_format (formats.ts) takes columns and identities in declare_format's shapes and only adds: every plan step ADD_COLUMN, REQUIRED_ON_ROWS reported not defaulted; adding an identity withdraws nothing_must_hold. delete_format removes declaration, table, rows and intake records via deleteFormat; refused while formatsPointingAt names any format; dry run states row count; destructiveHint true. Rename/retype/drop column, unit change, re-point/remove connection stay app-side. The screen still cannot add an identity (M23 U2 #1, M26's). Engine parity now owes no tool.
state: live

### D-255
date: 2026-09-23
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: A column may be unique: an index the reconciler owns, refused over repeats, and a write that would repeat names the row that holds the value
question: How does a declared column say no two rows share its value (M22 U3 #7), and where is a repeat refused?
answer: ColumnDeclaration.unique; schema 38 format_column.is_unique; declaration language 5 (UNIQUE_LANGUAGE) required to say it. Compiled to CREATE UNIQUE INDEX <table>__<column>__unique; observe() finds it by name and counts COUNT(DISTINCT); reconcile steps ADD_INDEX/DROP_INDEX; making a column with repeats unique is refusal NOT_UNIQUE (extend_format lists the repeated values via repeatsIn). Types: text, integer, date, link only. Empty cells never collide. repeatedValues() (formatRowRepository) refuses a repeat against table and batch, naming the holding row; called by record/update_format_rows, read_onto_format, FormatRows screen, IntakePanel. extend_format takes unique:[existing columns]. Artefact writes a 'unique' cell only when a format has a unique column (no diff churn in text documents). Draft carries unique; the form has no control (M26). No composite uniqueness.
state: live

### D-256
date: 2026-09-23
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: A link may be written as its target's name when that name is unique, and a batch may name its own rows
question: May a row write give a link the name a person uses (e.g. ref 'D-240') instead of the generated id ('M22 U3 #6'), and how does a batch whose rows name each other resolve?
answer: linksByName (formatJoinRepository) runs after coerce and before danglingLinks in record_format_rows and update_format_rows: a value that is a row's id is kept; else it is looked up in the target's labelColumn (first text column) only if that column is unique (D-255); else left for danglingLinks, whose message now says 'no row with that id or that <col>' or 'only when <col> is unique'. record_format_rows draws ids in the plan (insertRows ids param) so a self-link may name a row of the same batch, any order; danglingLinks takes the batch ids as pending. Update resolves against the document as it stands. Screen picks links from a list, so no screen half; read_onto_format filling a link is U9 (H10). No schema or language change. Still worth it if 'Stable ids' lands: people and models know rows by name. Rejected: a {column: value} input shape, first match under a non-unique column, name winning over id.
state: live

### D-257
date: 2026-09-23
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: A figure to fixed places is its own type, stored as a whole number and written as a string; a month and a moment are types; a ratio is a dimension
question: How does the engine hold the ledger's scaled integers (quantity_micro, rate_micro, basis points), its YYYY-MM periods and its moments, when its only fraction is REAL?
answer: Declaration language 6, schema 39. `decimal` is a new type (not integer with a scale): it declares a scale 1-9, stores the figure times 10^scale as INTEGER, is written and read as a decimal string, and refuses more places than its scale rather than rounding. Bounds are written as the figure and compile onto the integer; it may be unique. In an identity it enters as its figure (a double), so an identity over decimals needs a tolerance. A scale change is TYPE_CHANGED. `month` is YYYY-MM (a date is refused; intake turns a date cell on the 1st into a month). `instant` is UTC to the millisecond, written with its offset or refused. RATIO is a seventh dimension, base %, seeded with % and bp; nothing converts a ratio into money. A text document rebuilt at an older schema leaves seeded units to the migration that knows them. The editor gives a decimal scale 2 with no control until M26; a model proposing a format is not offered decimal.
state: live

### D-258
date: 2026-09-24
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: A closing message ends with what's next, and asks the maintainer to try something only when it really should be tried
question: Should every unit end by telling the maintainer what to try?
answer: No. The closing message ends with what's next: the next unit and why, anything that is the maintainer's to do or decide first, and whether the next session can simply start. A request to try something survives only when the unit's rightness rests on something only the maintainer can judge or only real material shows (a real document, a real .emd, a change the maintainer will see and might not want), in a line or two. review.md stays frozen; a milestone is still closed by the maintainer.
supersedes: D-238
state: live

### D-259
date: 2026-09-24
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: A row constraint is one of three sentences, stored as the sentence and compiled to a pair of triggers the reconciler owns
question: How does a format declare what must be true across the columns of one row — the ledger's cross-column CHECKs — without a free-text CHECK?
answer: Three shapes and no more (language 7, schema 40): `x is required [only] when kind is A, B` (only = and empty otherwise), `at most one of a, b, c` (polymorphic references as several links, D-252), `x is at most|at least 0 when kind is A` (reads money by its minor units). The deciding column is an enum of the same format. Stored as the sentence in format_constraint.rule and parsed on read; the artefact grows a `constraint | note` table only when a format has one; tools take `constraints: [{rule, note}]`. Compiled to BEFORE INSERT/UPDATE triggers named <table>__check_<key>__insert/__update raising the sentence — a trigger because SQLite cannot add a CHECK to an existing table. row.ts brokenConstraints refuses first on every write surface, naming the rule; an edit is judged on the row as it would stand. Adding one that rows break is the sixth refusal NOT_HELD, and extend_format lists the rows by id. A plan that drops a column takes all triggers down first and back last. No OR, no column-vs-column comparison, no arithmetic; the category two-level cap stays code. The screen carries constraints and does not show them (M26).
state: live

### D-260
date: 2026-09-24
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: An aggregate may fold only the rows that match: one enum or boolean, one hop at most, one member
question: The ledger needs a filtered fold (D-252): net pay is gross less the lines whose deduction type reduces net, not every line. How does an aggregate say which rows, without becoming a query language?
answer: An aggregate takes an optional `where <column> is <member>`: one enum or boolean of the source row, or one hop through its link (deduction_type.reduces_net), equal to one member (true/false for a boolean). No or, no list, no range; two hops refused. Checked when declared (a member the column lacks would sum to zero). A row with nothing in the filter column makes the whole fold unchecked, naming the row (D-138) - unlike U7, where empty is not a member. Stored as format_aggregate.where_column / where_member (schema 41), not a sentence: two parts, nothing to parse. Artefact spelling in the over cell: `paycheck_line.paycheck where deduction_type.reduces_net is true` (still six cells); declare_format, extend_format and connect_formats take the same sentence as the aggregate's `where`. Language 8, since an older build would fold every row; connect_formats raises the owner to 8 only for a filtered figure. IdentityInputs gains `members` (enum/boolean values, own and one hop). Proved on a synthetic payslip: net = gross - deducted proves at 15,400; unfiltered it fails. Draft carries the filter unshown (screen trails to M26). Rejected: a member list, number/range filters, empty-as-non-member, a seventh artefact cell.
state: live

### D-261
date: 2026-09-24
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: A column of a file fills a link by the target's unique name, exactly, or the line is refused
question: May a column of a file fill a link (H10)? A reading could hold a link only fixed for the whole file; a statement with several cards and a category column need one per line.
answer: Yes, only when the target's naming column (first text column) is declared unique; otherwise readingProblems refuses the binding, naming what would make it a name. Each cell resolves through linksByName (an id, then the unique name, matched exactly) in resolveReadLinks (formatJoinRepository, read-only), called by read_onto_format. A cell naming nothing refuses that line by line with the name, like a line that did not type - never an empty link, and the rest of the file is read. A link cell is read as its text (keeps 0417's zero). A fixed link may be a name too; a dangling fixed one is still a whole-file refusal. Links are never guessed by the mapper. The dry run prints the file's name, not the resolved id. Proved on a real Max statement: 91 lines linked to card and category by name, 3 lines naming a missing category refused by line - the first real document through intake. Screen trails (select still fixed-only; no parity capability since resolution only reads). Not answered: resemblance matching (receipt line -> ingredient). Rejected: fuzzy/case-insensitive matching, creating the missing target row, refusing the whole file, guessing a link column.
state: live

### D-262
date: 2026-09-24
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: A reading writes one header row of another format, read from positions, and every line links to it
question: How does a statement's printed total get checked as a declaration rather than pack code?
answer: read_onto_format takes header {format, link, cells, fixed}: one row of another format, its keys read from cell positions (never values, D-137; a negative row counts from the last non-empty row, so a total under the lines is [-1, col]) or fixed for the file. The header is written first in the reading's transaction and every line's link set to its id; a header that does not read refuses the whole file; the link is the reading's to fill, never also a column or fixed value. The check is the header format's aggregate over the lines plus an identity (lines_total = printed_total, exact on money), folded over staged lines so the dry run prints the verdict before anything exists. format_intake_row records the header with its format_name so supersede takes it back with the lines, refusing rather than taking only the lines. Schema 42. Proved on a real Max statement: 94 lines, printed 19,000.00 proved exactly. Screen trails (M26).
state: live

### D-263
date: 2026-09-24
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: A row other rows link to is not removed: the delete is refused, naming what links to it
question: What happens when a row is removed while rows of some format still link to it (foreign keys are never on, D-067)?
answer: Refused, never cascaded. deleteRows(db, declaration, ids, formats) throws LinkedRowsError while any row of the given formats links to a row it would remove; rowsLinkingTo counts, per linking column, how many rows name each removed id; linkingSentences writes them ('2 rows of Recipe line name “Pasta” through ingredient.'). A row removed in the same batch does not count, so a self-linking chain goes in one call. The rule lives in deleteRows, so the screen (FormatRows delete panel lists the sentences, no Remove), delete_format_rows (dry run and commit refuse, point at update_format_rows) and supersedeIntake all inherit it. Supersede's dry run does not yet say a user row links to a line - the refusal comes at commit, nothing written. No schema or language change. Rejected: a then: cascade/clear/refuse argument, turning foreign keys on, refusing only in the tool.
state: live

### D-264
date: 2026-09-24
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: A pack seeds rows by name, and a row is the pack's only while its record says so
question: How does a pack ship rows (a category tree, deduction types, institutions, built-in rules) so a later build can correct its own rows without overwriting a person's?
answer: A seed is a row of the pack's .format.md under ## rows (the text-storage file, read by readFormat; links by name). Origin lives beside the table in format_seed (format_name, seed_key, state SEEDED|REMOVED), keyed by the row's name under a unique naming column (required; ids re-mint in text, D-247). seedRows: no record + no row -> add; SEEDED -> correct to this build's values in the seed's cells only; no record + row of that name -> the person's, untouched; REMOVED -> stays removed; a SEEDED seed a build stops shipping is released (record goes, row stays). updateRow deletes the record on an edit that changes a cell; deleteRows sets REMOVED. Text documents carry records in a seeds.md part only when there are any. seedRows is the open path's (M25 calls it), not a screen or a tool.
state: live

### D-265
date: 2026-09-24
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: A pack is declarations, seeded rows and code, and its code writes SQL only against the tables it declares
question: M25's first question (D-252): categorisation, statistics, fixed-expense detection, installments, fund fees, FX and splitting are temporal, iterative or semantic. May a pack carry code, and how does that code reach its tables?
answer: The author: yes. A pack is its declarations (.format.md), its seeded rows (D-264) and TypeScript shipped in the build; D-210's line does not move, and D-214's finish line is about tables, not logic. Pack code reaches its tables with SQL against fmt_ tables, fenced by a boundary test that fails when pack SQL names a table the pack does not declare or any non-fmt_ table. The database still refuses bad writes (CHECKs, unique indexes, constraint triggers). Ids become text: a converted row's id is derivedId(table + old integer id), so conversion is repeatable and old FKs map without a lookup table. Conversion is root-first, one group per unit, on open with a backup, proven on a copy of a real document; each unit rewrites the old tables still pointing at its group and its repositories, and leaves a working app. Order: reference, transactions, holdings and investments, savings and debts, funds, paychecks and splitting, the rest and the front door. The ledger is un-parked for M25's units only. Rejected: moving the line; writes only through engine row functions; one big-bang conversion at the end.
state: live

### D-266
date: 2026-09-24
milestone: M25
scope: ledger
title: The ledger is on per document, and its seeds wait for the tables they replace
question: How does the ledger become a pack in a document: which documents get its declarations, when are its seeds planted, and what do the old tables' shapes become?
answer: 31 .format.md files in src/data/packs/ledger/formats/ (every ledger table except import_mapping, import_session, staged_row, document_template, reapply_*, app_setting, column_role); pack.ts adopts them in link order. Per document: a document has the ledger when it declares a ledger format, has an onboarding setting, or holds an account; on open it gets every declaration it lacks, and an engine-only document gets nothing. An adopted declaration is the document's; a later build does not re-adopt over it. A format is seeded only when its old table is gone or empty and the formats its seeds link to are seeded; U2's conversion must record rows copied from migration seeds (cat:/grp:/inst:/ded: ids) as SEEDED. Built-in rules are seeded under their rule-pack id (il.fuel.delek) since names repeat. created_at/updated_at/deleted_at are not declared; soft-deleted rows are not converted. bp -> decimal scale 2 in %, _micro -> decimal scale 6; polymorphic refs -> several links plus a row constraint, or source_key text where code derives it. Nine old uniques span several columns, which D-255 cannot say; owed before U3.
state: live

### D-267
date: 2026-09-24
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: Three kinds of logic become declarations — a rollup by period, the latest by an order, and a matching rule — and the rest stays pack code
question: Should the ledger's logic become declarations too, rather than pack code (D-265)?
answer: In part (amends D-265; the maintainer's call). Declarable, each as a closed vocabulary with no expression language: (1) a rollup by a period — sum/count/etc of a column of a format, grouped by a month column and optionally a link, compiled to SQL GROUP BY; (2) the latest by an order — a new aggregate kind that picks the row with the greatest/least ordering column and reads one of its columns; (3) a matching rule — rows of one format set a link on rows of another when a text column matches (closed list of match kinds; a pattern is a value), and a rule never overwrites a value a person set, so the written format declares its value's source. Stays pack code: tree rollups beyond one hop, detection of recurrence, bank-to-card matching, and iterative work (splitter, amortisation, projections). Engine units E1 composite uniques, E2 rollups, E3 latest-by-order, E4 matching rules go after U2 and before U3.
state: live

### D-268
date: 2026-09-24
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: The conversion keeps a row's id, renames a person's clashing name, and the reference tables move first
question: How does a document's ledger move from the old tables onto the pack's fmt_ tables, and what happens to ids, clashing names, seeds, deleted rows and the old tables that point at them?
answer: src/data/packs/ledger/convert.ts, run by installLedgerPack on open, one group per transaction (U2: institution with aliases as institution_alias rows, category_group, account, category, category_rule). Rows keep their old ids (not derived). A name two rows share: the pack's seed and then the older keep it, the person's other row is qualified (kind, parent, number) and the conversion says so — a real ledger's Everyday Bank became 'Everyday Bank (bank)'. Copied migration seeds are claimed SEEDED only while unchanged, REMOVED when renamed or deleted, the person's otherwise (claimSeeds). Soft-deleted rows are not copied; links to them are cleared, rules that could never fire are left out, all said. A value that won't fit refuses the whole group naming the row. Old tables pointing at the group are rebuilt with REFERENCES onto fmt_, then dropped. Built-in rules are rows under their builtin: ids; rules.builtIn folds into is_enabled and goes; hit_count optional and hits are plain SQL. A rename through the engine records the seed REMOVED. Declared rows are removed, not soft-deleted: deleting a category removes its built-ins and is refused while a person's rule assigns it; disableOrphanedRules is gone; the two-level cap is pack code. The shell writes <name>.pre-M25.emd beside the file before autosave; the plugin refuses an unconverted ledger (NOT_CONVERTED); a ledger screen in a document without one offers to add it.
state: live

### D-269
date: 2026-09-24
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: A pack's formats are read anywhere and written only through the pack
question: The engine's generic surfaces (Formats screen, record/update/delete_format_rows, extend_format) can write a pack's tables past the pack's code — a third category level now, a transaction that skips dedupe after U3. How is a pack's format protected?
answer: A format a pack declares is owned by it. Rows: read anywhere; written only through the pack's own screens and tools — a generic write refuses in one sentence naming the pack's screen or tool (D-227 still holds via the pack's tools). Declaration: the pack's columns are the pack's; a person may add a column of their own but not change or drop the pack's. Built as engine unit M25 E5, before U3. Folding a pack's formats into one group in the rail and on the graph is M26's (UI changes go to the UI milestone). Rejected: hiding pack formats (reading them is the engine's claim) and registering pack checks with the engine (a plugin system).
state: live

### D-270
date: 2026-09-24
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: Unique across several columns is a fourth row constraint, `one row per`, compiled to a unique index
question: How does a declaration say that two or more columns are unique together — and one column under a condition, like one remainder per plan?
answer: A fourth row-constraint sentence in D-259's constraints table: `one row per a, b`, optionally `when kind is A, B` (an enum of the format; may be one of the columns). Language 9. Compiled to CREATE UNIQUE INDEX <table>__check_<key>__index, partial under a when; observe finds it under the trigger prefix, drop-by-key drops both shapes. Types: UNIQUE_TYPES plus enum, boolean, instant. repeatedValues refuses a repeat against table and batch on every write surface, naming the holder; update_format_rows passes the row as it would stand. Adding one over colliding rows is NOT_HELD, listed by id. One column with no when is refused (that is `unique`). The ledger declares eight. installLedgerPack adds a pack constraint missing from a held declaration and nothing else. Screen half is the refusal; authoring on screen is M26's.
state: live

### D-271
date: 2026-09-24
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: A rollup by a month is a fifth table of the declaration, one sentence compiled to one `GROUP BY`
question: How does a figure per month (and per link) become a declaration rather than pack code, without becoming a query language?
answer: A rollup is one sentence on the format whose rows it folds: `<kind> of <column> by [month of] <period> [or <fallback>] [and <link>] [where <filter> and ...]`. Kinds are the aggregate's five; the column is one integer/real/decimal/money column (no expression; no quantity; no average of money); the period is a month column or `month of` a date with one fallback (D-006's charge-else-transaction date); at most one link; D-260 filters joined by `and`, never `or`. Stored as its sentence in format_rollup (schema 44), a fifth .format.md table (rollup | works out | note), language 10. Compiled by rollupQuerySql to one GROUP BY over month, link and currency; money summed per currency, never across two. A row with nothing in the folded column or a filter column makes only its own group unchecked, naming it. Tool: format_rollup; screen: a view switch on #/formats/<name>. The ledger declares `spending` on transaction and reproduces categoryObservations on a fixture. ~110 ms over 60k rows. Open for U3: ledger counts amount < 0, the declaration direction is OUTFLOW — they differ only on a non-excluded transfer out.
state: live

### D-272
date: 2026-09-25
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: `latest` and `earliest` are aggregate kinds that pick one row by an order column, and a tie is unchecked
question: How does a format say 'the latest price' — a pick of one row by an order — without a traversal or a query language?
answer: Two more aggregate kinds, LATEST and EARLIEST, with `by`: a date, month, instant or integer column of the source's own row (never a hop; refused on a fold). In the artefact it follows the source in the over cell before any where: `security_price.security by as_of`. The expression is read on the picked row only; a tie at the top or a row with no order is unchecked and names the rows. The value carries `at`, the order value it came from. Picked in SQL when there is no filter (rows at the extreme order plus rows with none, and a count); with a filter every row is read. Rollups keep the five folds. Schema 45 rebuilds format_aggregate with by_column; language 11. The ledger declares security.price and fund_account.balance; the pack reads formats without aggregates first and adds aggregates in a second install pass, since an aggregate can point against link order. A pick of a non-figure (a task's status) is not built.
state: live

### D-273
date: 2026-09-25
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: A matching rule is a rule set declared on the rule format, applied on a write of the target and on demand, and never over a person's value
question: How does a format's rows become rules that set a link of another format — which kinds, what order, and how is a person's value kept safe?
answer: A sixth .format.md table on the rule format, `| rule set | sets | note |`, one sentence: `<target>.<link> from <own link> when <text>[ or <text>] matches <pattern> (as <kind> | by <enum> (<MEMBER> <kind>, …)) first by <required col> [then …] [where <own-row filter>, or `is empty`], recorded in <enum> as <rule member>, never over <person member>[, noting <link to rules>]`. Kinds equals, contains, starts with, regex (regex belongs: a value, bounded 200 chars, case-insensitive; a bad or empty pattern refuses its rule's write); all fold case and spaces on both sides. Order: required integer/date/month/instant/enum (enum by member order); a top tie naming different rows is left and named. A row is the rules' while its source is empty or the rule member, or its link is empty and not a person's; a writer changing the link is recorded as the person member. Applied by the generic writes before judging (page, record/update_format_rows, read_onto_format) — never in insertRows, the open path — and on demand by apply_rules / the rule format's Apply. Schema 46, language 12. The ledger declares `categories` on category_rule; its matcher calls patternMatches for its three text kinds and agrees with firstMatch on all 162 built-in text rules. Account/amount conditions, the other kinds, kind specificity and learning stay pack code; U3 decides specificity and normalized patterns.
state: live

### D-274
date: 2026-09-25
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: A pack's ownership is kept in the document, and the lock sits in the generic callers
question: D-269 said a pack's formats are read anywhere and written only through the pack. Where is ownership kept, and where does the lock sit?
answer: In the document: format.pack, pack_written, pack_columns (schema 47) and front matter pack:/written:/pack columns: (language 13), because the engine-only server never loads the pack's code. The pack supplies the sentence per format; owner.ts wraps it (rowWriteRefusal, declarationChangeRefusal, deleteFormatRefusal). The lock is in the generic callers, never in insertRows/updateRow/deleteRows. Refused: record/update/delete_format_rows, read_onto_format (and its header format), supersede_reading, apply_rules onto a pack's target, delete_format; the format's page offers no add/edit/remove/Apply, the Formats list no Read a document/Delete. extend_format, connect_formats, the editor and Connections take a person's own column and refuse changing or dropping a pack column or removing anything the format held. installLedgerPack stamps older documents on open. The editor's draft now carries rollups and rule sets, which it used to drop.
state: live

### D-275
date: 2026-09-26
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: Transactions and statements move, an import is a `statement` every line links to, and intake is a unit of its own
question: How do transactions and what names them move onto the pack's tables, where does a transaction's provenance go when the old import_session does, and is intake part of the same unit?
answer: U3 moved import_session, transaction, card_settlement_link, category_override, reapply_run and reapply_change through U2's machinery as a second group (convert.ts); the pack declares 34 formats (statement, reapply_run, reapply_change new; transaction links statement). The brief was split: an import is a row of `statement` (D-262's header shape: file, sheet, counts, printed total, balance check; `mapping` is the importer's mapping by id, text until U3b), and moving the importer onto format_intake with import_mapping and staged_row is M25 U3b. Removed, not soft-deleted: a merge removes the source's duplicates and repoints corrections, settlements, re-apply changes, trade funding and debt payments to the twin. A link names a row that is there: a transaction a deleted rule categorised keeps its category as RULE with no rule; deleting a rule clears it; deleting a category drops corrections into it and forgets it as a previous one; merging carries both. The conversion tallies its sentences, turns the rate float into six places, gives the original total the transaction's currency, and leaves a missing moment empty. The backup is per unit: .pre-M25-U3.emd when U2 already ran. readFormat reads one format (6 -> 1.5 ms), insertRows prepares once per call, and a statement commits in one write: 60k rows import in 2.2 s (3.2 s at D-118) and convert in 3.5 s; four copies of real documents convert in ~0.6 s with every count and sum the same. M25 E2 #1: no real document holds a counting transfer out, so amount < 0 and direction OUTFLOW agree; both stay. D-273's specificity and normalized patterns stay pack code (the importer keeps its matcher). documentCurrency answers ILS where there is no fmt_transaction until U4-U6. Rejected: keeping a deleted rule's id as text; intake in U3; a per-document declaration cache.
state: live

### D-276
date: 2026-09-26
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: The ledger moves; old documents' data does not come with it
question: Should M25's remaining units keep converting an existing document's rows onto the pack's tables, with a backup and a proof on a copy of a real document?
answer: No (there is a sole user, and migrations and old files do not matter). M25 still moves the ledger's tables, code and screens onto declared formats; what stops is carrying rows across. From U3b on a unit moves its group's code and drops the group's old tables on open, rows and all, no copy, no backup; old tables pointing at the group are still repointed onto fmt_. U2's and U3's conversion stays until U8, which deletes convert.ts, drops any remaining old tables in a migration and stops new documents getting them. Proof is reading a real source document (broker statement, pension report, payslip) into a fresh file, not converting a copy of an old one. The one real file gives up what it holds in unmoved tables (holdings, a trade, a savings goal, a fund reading, a payslip, a split plan, PDF templates). Amends D-265 and D-268. Rejected: keeping conversion for the remaining groups.
state: live

### D-277
date: 2026-09-26
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: Intake moves: the importer's mappings and staged rows are pack formats
question: Where do the importer's mappings and staged rows go when the ledger moves onto declared formats — the engine's format_intake, or the pack — and does a review still survive closing the app mid-import?
answer: Both are ledger pack formats (import_mapping, staged_row); old tables dropped on open with their rows (D-276). format_intake cannot hold a mapping: it lacks an amount convention, a date order per column, an account binding, a name and usage counts. The author kept the resume: staged rows persist in the document, and staged_row declares the sign rule. statement.mapping is a link now (cleared on the open that drops the old tables). Deleting a mapping clears statements' mapping; deleting an account unbinds its mappings; deleting/merging a category or rule clears or repoints staged rows. Proved on a real Max statement (94 rows, printed total ₪19,000.00 agrees); a mapping's use was counted twice per plugin import and now only the commit counts it.
state: live

### D-278
date: 2026-09-26
milestone: M25
scope: ledger
title: Holdings, investments and FX move; a trade outlives its position
question: How do holding_place, holding, security, security_price, security_trade, security_position and fx_rate move onto the pack, what happens to a trade when its position closes, and does the code read the declared security.price?
answer: All seven are the pack's fmt_ tables; on open the old tables are dropped with what they held (D-276), the old tables pointing at them repointed first, and the formats taking over are brought up to this build's declaration on that same open. security_trade.holding is optional: closing a position removes the position and its holding and clears the holding on its trades, which keep the security, so a realised gain survives. Deleting a security deletes its prices; one with a position or trade is refused. A replaced price or rate is deleted and reinserted (one row per security/pair, as_of, source). The code keeps latestPrice (bounded by a date, a person's figure over a fetched one); security.price stays the declared figure, unchecked on a tie. Proved on a copy of a real .emd and six real broker statements; the stored template reads no date and no trades and is unchecked — M16's broker fix, still owed.
state: live

### D-279
date: 2026-09-26
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: Savings, debts and purchase plans move; a payment found in a transaction links it
question: How do saving_goal, saving_deposit, debt, debt_payment and purchase_plan move onto the pack's formats, and which source references become links?
answer: The five tables are the pack's fmt_ tables; on open the old ones are dropped with what they held (D-276). A debt payment found in a transaction (TRANSACTION, INSTALLMENT_SLICE) links it with `transaction`; one from a payslip or a split plan keeps the code-derived `source_key`, since neither names one row; required-only-when constraints and two filtered one-row-per say which is set. A saving deposit keeps source_key only. A debt's tracking is auto_track_pattern and auto_track_category, the kind deciding. emergency_months is months to a hundredth. Moved rows are removed, not soft-deleted. Engine fix: installLedgerPack no longer adds a grown constraint to a held declaration while a column it reads is missing — it waits for the step that brings the whole declaration up (the drop); without that, the triggers read a missing column and the next DROP COLUMN failed the open of a real document. Still loose: adoptFormat does not refuse a constraint naming a column the declaration lacks.
state: live

### D-280
date: 2026-09-26
milestone: M25
scope: ledger
title: Funds move, and the ledger declares its first identity
question: How do the fund tables move onto the pack, and where does the funds screen's parts-against-total check live?
answer: fund_account, fund_balance_snapshot and fund_deposit are fmt_ tables; old tables dropped on open with their rows (D-276). The snapshot declares identity `parts`: employee + employer + severance = total_balance, ± 100 minor; fund_deposit declares the same over total. validateSnapshot no longer sums parts; fundRepository.snapshotProblems adds COMPONENTS_DISAGREE when the document's `parts` verdict is FAILED, used by the SnapshotEditor and record_fund_readings, so form, tool and format_rows agree. A missing part is unchecked and refuses nothing. Fees stay basis points (% scale 2). Deleting a fund removes readings and deposits, children first. Proved against a real June 2026 pension report: 8/8 deposit rows proved, balance unchecked (report gives no split).
state: live

### D-281
date: 2026-09-26
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: Paychecks, splitting and fixed expenses move; net against gross is the paycheck's identity
question: How do deduction types, paychecks, split plans and fixed expenses move onto the pack's formats, and does the paycheck's net check become a declaration?
answer: The seven old tables drop on open with what they held (D-276). The paycheck declares an aggregate deducted (sum of employee over paycheck_line.paycheck where deduction_type.reduces_net is true) and an identity net = gross - deducted, exact; it agrees with reconciliationDelta, which stays pack code because it is signed and shown on unsaved drafts. The pack holds a format's identities back with its aggregates on the first pass. Built-in deduction types are seeded fresh under their ded: ids; names are unique and a duplicate is refused by name. A bucket's target and a fixed expense's match are three links each; fixed_expense gains match_category. Removing a goal, debt or category frees buckets aimed at it. split_plan drops U1's two required-when constraints: a basis may be chosen before its paycheck or amount, and deleting a paycheck clears plans based on it. Proved on a real July 2026 payslip: net proved at 14,000.
state: live

### D-282
date: 2026-09-28
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: The ledger keeps no table of its own, and is at the front door again
question: M25 U8: where do document_template, column_role and app_setting go, how do the old ledger tables stop being made, and how is M20 U0's staging undone?
answer: All three are ledger pack formats: `setting` (unique key, JSON value — a pack's settings are a format it declares, not an engine table), `document_template` (its version chain a state CURRENT/SUPERSEDED/REMOVED, one current per kind and signature as a filtered `one row per`), and `column_role` holding only roles a person added — the shipped ones are SEEDED_ROLES in code. Migration 048 (schema 48) drops every table migrations 001-031 made for the ledger, with what it holds (D-276); a new document never keeps one. convert.ts, NOT_CONVERTED and the conversion notice are deleted; seeds keep their cat:/grp:/inst:/ded: ids from seededIds.json. Finish-line test: a document holds no table that is neither fmt_ nor the engine's; fence's old-table list is empty. The staging switch is gone: rail, palette, root and drop follow whether the open document keeps a ledger; #/settings/packs adds one; the empty window offers New ledger. M20 U0 #2: the rule stays — onboarding is reached only by choosing a ledger, and every step skips.
state: live

### D-283
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M25
scope: ledger
title: M25 closes, and investments leave the ledger whole
question: The M25 trial: can M25 close, and what does the ledger keep of investments?
answer: M25 closed 2026-10-01. Investments leave the ledger pack whole: securities, holdings, positions, trades, the broker statement reader, the investments screen and tools (investments, record_positions, record_trades). A person declares their own format and links it; a session helping a person do that is the proof. FX goes only as far as investments are what reads it; the unit settles whether foreign-currency transactions still need exchange_rate. M16 ends, not re-driven. Two trial faults go to M28: money moved to something you own counts as spent (a category is a label; fix is D-279's pattern — a transaction a declared row links is moved, not spent), and a second payslip layout gets a dialog instead of 'doesn't fit — make a new reader?' (engine half is M26 U5). A reading template stores labels and positions, never a figure.
state: live

### D-284
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M26
scope: engine
title: M26's shape: short verbs, a format's page is a sheet, an entry is a view the format has, and the graph keeps its positions
question: What does the M25 trial ask of the engine's screens, and in what order?
answer: M26 U1-U7. U1 design language — short verbs/icons not sentences, square corners, hover — on the formats list only, judged before it spreads. U2 a format's page is a sheet: Add, Search across all columns, Ctrl+F, resizable columns, empty rows to type into, paste a block. U3 Edit replaces the declaration panel; units explained beside a quantity column. U4 the graph is a canvas — pan, zoom, drag — positions saved in the document. U5 reading mappings are 'readers', shown on the format they feed; bulk reading; a reader picked by fit. U6 an entry is a view the format has (not its declaration): an input per column, long text as markdown, no block that is not a column. U7 an MCP screen with the connect snippet. Rejected: input kinds on the column (presentation in the domain layer), free blocks, positions in localStorage.
state: live

### D-285
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M26
scope: engine
title: One document is one file; a way out is an export, and a way back in is an import that checks
question: Should a document be many files, like a folder of markdown notes?
answer: No. A folder would buy outside editing, per-file diffs and sharing one format, and would cost the guarantee (checks hold only while Emerald is the only writer), the atomic rename save (D-127), stable links, and open time on thousands of files. Text storage (D-246) already diffs per row; the entry view reads like a note; export a format as .format.md or markdown per entry is the way out, and edits come back through a checking import, never as live storage. Reopens only if editing in other tools becomes the maintainer's normal way of working.
state: live

### D-286
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M26
scope: engine
title: A fact is written once: the unit row is the record, a brief names its files, and the maintainer sees a unit before it is built
question: What should change in how sessions work, after measuring two weeks of them?
answer: A finished unit is recorded only in its unit row (built, landed, decision); the roadmap and status change only when a milestone does; the log keeps the argument; the commit says what changed. A planned unit's brief ends with 'Files:' (what to open first), corrected by the building session to what it touched. docs:check errors on a built unit with no landed and warns on a planned brief with no Files:. A session that writes units lists them in its closing message, one line each with the question it is least sure of: the maintainer sees a unit before it is built. M27 gets unit.files and unit.undone_by -> decision. Measured: unit sessions ~110 turns and 10-40 min (half of M19-M20); reading code is ~37% of the bill; the expensive part is reversing built work.
state: amended

### D-287
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M26
scope: engine
title: A milestone is driven: one session relays, a fresh subagent builds each unit, and it stops for the maintainer where a unit needs judging
question: Why not drive M26's units instead of starting each session by hand, and how should the maintainer work?
answer: Amends D-286. /run-milestone <M> (.claude/skills/run-milestone): the driver reads planned units once and hands each to a fresh subagent, sequentially, in the same tree. The subagent builds, verifies, runs the closing pass and commits, and reports in under 15 lines; the bill matches separate sessions. The driver stops after every unit that is the maintainer's to judge (all of M26) and writes the verdict into the rows before the next unit starts: a change for a later unit is appended to its brief, and a fault is a new unit planned next. Engine-only units run straight on. The driver never builds, repairs or closes a milestone; a failed or red unit stops it; past ~150k context, start a fresh driver. The maintainer: plan by bringing notes to a session; build with /run-milestone; close by trying it on real documents.
state: live

### D-288
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M26
scope: engine
title: The formats list is cards: the card opens, its other actions are icons, and the corners are square on trial
question: How does D-284's 'the words are verbs' look on the formats list, and how does it stay on one screen until the maintainer judges it?
answer: Each format is a .fc-card (tokens.css): the whole card is a stretched link to its table; title in ink, not accent. Other actions are icons (IconAction in DeclaredFormats.tsx) with aria-label and title: Edit (today's add-a-column until U3 moves editing onto the sheet), Read a document (kept against the brief: the only screen mounting IntakePanel, and parity.test.ts would not notice its loss; remove in U5 or on the maintainer's word), Delete (negative on hover only). Show the declaration and the units panel leave the list; ?show= from the rows screen still opens the declaration in its card until U3. Square corners are --radius-card and --radius-control (0px); spread by spending them, never by redefining --radius-sm/md/lg. Hover moves nothing: 2px accent rule grows down the leading edge, plane tints to surface, border firms; no lift, no shadow (D-154). docs/11 'Actions: a verb or an icon, never a sentence' has the tokens and the verb list.
state: live

### D-289
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M26
scope: engine
title: A format's page is a sheet: column widths are kept in the document beside the declaration, and a paste is the tool's batch
question: Where do a sheet's column widths live, and how is a paste or a typed foot row checked?
answer: Widths are presentation: table format_column_view (migration 049), per format and column, never in the declaration; text .emd carries them as views.md, only for columns that exist; no tool writes them (parity gap refused by D-289). A paste and a foot row go through stageRows (formatBatchRepository.ts), the same plan record_format_rows uses: whole or nothing, refusal names row and cell. Search reads every column as shown (money in major units with code, decimal, quantity with unit, link by its row's name); a yes/no column is not searched. 'Rows' are entries on the screen; Add, Delete, Cancel.
state: live

### D-290
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M26
scope: engine
title: A format is changed on its sheet: a rename is asked for and keeps its values, and a cell's choices are the app's own list
question: Where is an existing format changed, how does a rename keep its data, and what replaces the native menus in a sheet's cells?
answer: On the format's sheet (FormatRows + ShapePanel): a header Edit icon opens name/type/checks (required, unique, decimal places), a '+' header adds a column, an '=' icon opens identities (closes M23 U2 #1). No declaration panel, no Edit screen; the formats list's Edit opens the sheet; ?show= and AddColumn are gone. Saving is adoptFormat; the plan is said in entries. Renames are passed explicitly (new->old, from draft keys via renamesOf) to planFor/adoptFormat; the reconciler emits RENAME_COLUMN first (ALTER TABLE RENAME COLUMN, unique index remade), then judges the rest as if renamed; NAME_TAKEN refuses a taken name; widths and intake keys follow; this format's identities follow (renameInExpression); another format that reads the column is reported as breaking and the save refused. The conversation still only adds (D-254): parity gap refused by D-290. Cells use ChoiceField: a link is an autocomplete keeping the picked row's id; enum and yes/no a styled menu; both stay text fields for paste.
state: amended

### D-291
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M26
scope: engine
title: The connections graph is a canvas whose bubbles stay where they are put, and a pack is one bubble until opened
question: How does the connections graph pan, zoom and move bubbles, where are positions kept, and how are a pack's formats shown?
answer: Pan by dragging the background; zoom by wheel, Ctrl+wheel (trackpad pinch) or a two-finger pinch, about the pointer; corner control zooms and fits. The view is per visit. A bubble moves by its grip (six dots left of the title), never its head (the head is the row endpoint); the grip takes arrow keys (16px). One write on letting go. Positions live in format_graph_place (migration 050: kind FORMAT|PACK, name, integer x/y within +-100000) and a graph.md part in a text .emd, dropping places nothing answers to; never in the declaration, never localStorage (D-115). No place = today's rank layout; 'Arrange' clears every place. A pack's formats fold into one bubble with a place of its own: press opens, drag moves it and shifts its placed members by the same delta; opened, members lay out by rank from where the group stood; 'Fold <pack>' closes; open/closed is per visit. Edges into a folded format run to the group's edge; edges inside it are not drawn; folded formats are not drop targets. No tool moves a bubble: parity gap refused by D-291.
state: live

### D-292
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M26
scope: engine
title: A reader is a format's accepted readings grouped by header; files are read in bulk, each by the reader that fits, and a reconciled file is ticked but never written past review
question: What is a reader, how is one picked for a file, and when may a reading be written without a person ticking it?
answer: A reader is not a table: it is the format's accepted readings (format_intake) grouped by header signature, newest standing for the group (readersOf/listReaders), named by the file it last read; no migration. Readers show on the format's sheet ('Read from ...') with a Read icon; the card icon opens the same screen, /formats/:name/read, which reads any number of files. fitReader picks: header equal cell for cell (at the reader's header row or the guessed one) and the reading still valid for the format; a close reader is named ('doesn't fit - make a new one?') and never used. The plugin's remembered rung uses fitReader too. read_onto_format's plan moved to data/intake/readingPlan.ts (planReading) and the screen uses it per file. Reconciled = every line read, nothing refused, every declared check proved on lines and header row, at least one check ran; a reconciled file is ticked, nothing is written until Write (one transaction). The ledger's readers stay in Settings, renamed Statement readers / Payslip readers, until M28.
state: amended

### D-293
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M26
scope: engine
title: A reader keeps its file's column order and follows a moved column by name; it can be renamed and forgotten; a failed check names its lines
question: How does a reader handle a file whose columns were reordered, how is a reader renamed or forgotten, and how does a failed check say which lines failed?
answer: Migration 051 stores format_intake.header_cells (header in file order). fitReader follows each bound column (and header cell) to where its name is in the new file; an empty or duplicated name cannot be followed and the reader is CLOSE (MOVED), never applied. A reader with no stored order (made before F1) is CLOSE (UNORDERED) and must be made again. Rename = a format_reader row keyed by (format, signature); forget = format_intake.forgotten = 1, which hides the readings but keeps rows and supersede. Screen: the read screen's reader list with rename/forget icons. Tool: readers (list with readerId, rename/forget lists, dry-run until commit). planReading returns failing lines with verdict sentences; screen and read_onto_format print them. Amends D-292.
supersedes: D-292
state: live

### D-294
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M26
scope: engine
title: An entry is a page: a view the format has, its columns in order, each drawn by an input chosen per format and kept beside the declaration
question: How does a format get a readable, one-entry-at-a-time view without turning into a free-form workspace, and where does that view's configuration live?
answer: #/formats/:name/entry/:id (and entry/new) renders EntryView inside FormatRows; the sheet stays the default, every row has an 'Open as a page' button, Add opens entry/new. Columns run in declaration order with no free blocks; the first text line column is the title. Each column's input is chosen per format from inputsFor(type), default first: text line|markdown, bounded number field|slider, enum/boolean menu|buttons, others one each. Markdown is parsed by our own ui/formats/markdown.ts into blocks drawn as elements (no HTML, links not followed). Inputs live in format_view_column (migration 052: format, view, column, setting) and in '### <format> entry' blocks of views.md; one table for every view kind so M27's board/roadmap are more kinds. A pack's entry is read-only. No tool chooses an input (parity gap refused by D-294).
state: live

### D-295
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M26
scope: engine
title: The MCP screen reads a plain-words catalog the renderer can import, pinned name for name to the server's own definitions by a test
question: How does the MCP screen list the server's tools without importing the node-only server, and without drifting from it?
answer: src/plugin/catalog.ts is pure data (no imports): groups, one plain sentence per tool, and reads/writes/removes. catalog.test.ts asserts its names per half equal ENGINE_TOOLS and LEDGER_TOOLS and each effect equals the definition's readOnlyHint/destructiveHint — a new tool needs a catalog line or the suite is red. #/mcp is in the rail; ledger groups show and --engine-only is dropped when the document keeps the ledger. The document path goes in a sentence for the conversation (StorageAdapter.location(), shell only), not in the configuration; the server's path is a placeholder until the server ships.
state: live

### D-296
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M26
scope: engine
title: An entry page is headings over inputs, and its order is the view's — dragged, kept beside each column's input, never the declaration's
question: How should an entry page lay its columns out, and may a person reorder them?
answer: Each column is its name as a heading with its input under it, stacked, for every input. While arranging, a column is dragged by its grip (or moved a step with arrows) to another place; the place is kept in format_view_column beside the input in the same setting string — 'markdown', '#2' or 'markdown #2' — and views.md carries it as written. No migration, so the drag commit reverts alone. No stored order = declaration order; unplaced columns follow placed ones in declaration order; a page put back into declaration order stores nothing. The sheet keeps declaration order. Title = first text-line column in the view's order. No tool reorders (D-294's parity gap extended to setEntryOrder).
state: live

### D-297
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M26
scope: engine
title: M26 closes on the maintainer's word, with five screen trails carried rather than built
question: M26 was reviewed and judged to work now, to be closed. Can it close with parity's screen trails not empty, and what is next?
answer: M26 closed 2026-10-01. U1-U5 and U7 accepted; U6 a fault fixed by F2 (D-296) and F3 (type picker no longer snaps back to text), both accepted; F1 finished U5. Its bar (screen trails empty) was not met: the engine's 'add a unit, or correct one' (record_units) is carried to whichever milestone next works on units; the ledger's four (setCategoryFromAssistant, recordRate, the two merchant-suggestion writes) are M28's. Next is M27, the development pack; it has no unit rows yet, so planning them with the maintainer comes first (D-286). M26's decisions D-283-D-296 moved to docs/archive/decisions/d283-d296-m26-ui-and-ux.md.
state: live

### D-298
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M27
scope: engine
title: M27 is six units, the engine's link risk before its views, and the live roadmap reads while a session writes
question: What are M27's units, in what order, and what does the live-roadmap idea include?
answer: Six units: U1 the development pack and a milestone format (unit.milestone becomes a link; unit gains files and undone_by), U2 many-valued link (before any view: blocks and ready need it, and it is the riskiest), U3 declared views and a board, U4 roadmap and decision page, U5 ready view and start-here call, U6 live tracking — unit-level in_progress/built shown as a session writes, the view read-only while tracking so the untested second writer is not opened, and a conditional write for claiming. Left out: note format, found-while-working-on link, Code facts. Rejected: step-level live progress.
state: live

### D-299
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M27
scope: development
title: The development pack is the memory's five declarations, with no seeds and no owner, and `unit.milestone` stays text until `match` reads a link by name
question: How is docs/memory.emd's set of formats made a pack, what does a milestone look like, and can unit.milestone become a link without engine code?
answer: src/data/packs/development/: five .format.md files (decision, unit, milestone, open_question, review_entry) identical to memory.emd's declarations, pack.ts reads them in link order and can install them; pack.test.ts fails on drift. No seeds, no owner: rows are written by the engine's tools and the sheet. milestone = ref (unique), title, goal, state planned|open|closed, position (unique integer, gapped by 10); rows M19-M28. unit gains files (the briefs' Files: lines moved there; docs:check, conventions and closing pass read it) and undone_by -> decision (M25 U4 -> D-283). decision.ref and unit.ref declared unique (nothing repeated; a link by name needs the declaration). unit.milestone stays text: tools and screens refuse a retype with data, a hand edit of the text works, but format_rows match compares a link with its stored id, so match:{milestone:M20} found nothing; resolving match values by name is engine code. Rejected: search instead of match; leaving the query broken.
state: live

### D-300
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M27
scope: engine
title: A link can name several rows: `links` is a thirteenth type kept in a member table, and `match` takes a link by its target's name
question: How does a link name several rows (a unit blocked by three others) without a delimited cell or a query language, and how does format_rows match a link column in a text document whose ids change on every write?
answer: Column type `links` (language 14, schema 53 widens format_column's CHECK): the ids of several rows of `target`, in order, stored as rows of fmt_<format>__<column> (row_id, member_id, position; PK row_id+member_id), never a cell. Not required, not unique. Reconciler makes it with the format, renames it with the column, drops it only empty (DROP_WITH_DATA otherwise). Writes take a list of ids or unique names (a lone string is one member), a repeated member is refused; an edit replaces the whole list. Reads put the list in cells[column]. match on it is membership (null = empty list); search reads members' names. Deleting a row a list names is refused like a link (LinkedRowsError); a deleted row's own lists go with it. Text writes it as a JSON list of names, blocks: ["M27 U1", "a, b"], so a separator in a name round-trips. An expression may not read through it (parse refusal); an aggregate may fold over it (rows whose list holds this one). Screens: chips (MembersField) on the entry page (input `chips`) and in the sheet's foot cell. Separately, format_rows `match` on any link column resolves the value through linksByName (id, else unique name), so unit.milestone is now a link to milestone and match:{milestone:M20} works.
state: live

### D-301
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M27
scope: engine
title: A view is a line the pack declares beside its formats, and the first kind is a board: lanes by an enum, a card by its name and the columns that say `card`
question: How does a pack declare a view the engine draws, and what is the first one?
answer: A view is a kind plus a setting per column in format_view_column (D-294's table, no migration); `board` joins `entry` in VIEW_KINDS. A board keeps `lanes` (the enum whose members are lanes) and `card` (columns shown under a card's name); a card shows the name column and those, nothing else. Any format with an enum has a board, laid by its first enum when none is named; every member gets a lane, empty or not. A pack declares views in views.md (the views part of a text document), so memory.emd holds the same text and pack.test.ts holds them together. Moving a card is update_format_rows's checks and updateRow; choosing the lanes column is screen-only, refused a tool. Route #/formats/<name>/board.
state: live

### D-302
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M27
scope: engine
title: A roadmap is rows in the order of a number with the rows that name each folded beneath, and a page is one row with its links read one hop each way
question: How are the roadmap and the decision page declared and drawn on D-301's view mechanism, and how far do they read?
answer: `roadmap` and `page` join VIEW_KINDS in format_view_column (no migration). A roadmap keeps `order` (the number column; first number by default), `under` (on another format's link: fold its rows under the named row; with none said, every format linking in folds) and `line` (columns after a row's name); any format with a number that another format links to has one at #/formats/<name>/roadmap. A self-link never folds; rows naming no root are drawn under 'Under none of these'; a row whose state is its enum's last member starts folded shut. Every row has a page at #/formats/<name>/page/<id>: `body` columns in full (every text column but the name by default), `line` columns beside the name, what it names and what names it, one hop each way (D-224) - a self-link (supersedes) shows its next step and the page says so. Nothing is kept: both re-read the document every revision (formatLinkViewRepository.ts). Reads only, so no parity entry. decision.milestone is text, so decisions reach the roadmap through unit.decision, not as a fold.
state: live

### D-303
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M27
scope: engine
title: A ready view is rows in a ready member whose own links name nothing still open, and start_here answers every question a document's views declare in one call
question: How does a pack say what can be started now, and how does a session get the start-here answers without a hand-written table of format_rows calls?
answer: View kind `ready`: `waits` on a link into the format's own rows, `ready <members> done <members>` on an enum, `line`; a row is ready when it is in a ready member and every row it waits on is in a done member (done is declared, so dropped counts and a new member like in_progress holds). Held rows are shown with what holds them. View kind `start`: `is <member>` on an enum, `latest <n>` (1-50) on a date/month/number, `line`. Tool `start_here(path)` answers every ready view then every start view, a line per row, each with the format_rows call that lists it whole; CLAUDE.md's table is generated from the same startQuestions and pack.test.ts guards it. A row may not name itself through a link into its own format (selfLinks), refused by stageRows, update_format_rows and the entry page. Route #/formats/<name>/ready. Engine tool-list budget 30k -> 31k chars.
state: live

### D-304
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M27
scope: engine
title: A unit is claimed by ref on condition, plugin writes take a lock, and a clean window follows the disk
question: How does the roadmap open in the app track a session live, and how do two sessions claim one unit without both getting it?
answer: unit.state gains in_progress (between planned and built), plus claimed_by (text) and started (date); the unit roadmap line shows both, since a dead session leaves a unit in_progress for ever. update_format_rows edits may name the row by `where` (values matching exactly one row, e.g. {ref:'M27 U6'}) instead of id, and carry `expect` (values the row must still hold, compared as match compares); an unmet expectation refuses the whole batch. Address by ref, never by an id from an earlier call: a text document renews ids on every write (D-247). Every plugin write holds <document>.lock from open to rename (wx create, 10s wait, a lock older than 60s is taken over), so of two concurrent claims exactly one wins. The app's followDisk re-reads a changed file without asking when it holds no unsaved change; with unsaved work the conflict bar is unchanged. A visible browser tab polls the mark every 2s; the shell keeps its directory watch. /run-milestone claims each unit (expect planned) before handing it out; the closing pass moves it to built by ref with expect in_progress. The app is not a second writer here: M22 U3 #8 (the two windows in documentStore.save) stays open, and the lock is the plugin's alone. A page route by id in a text document loses its row on reload.
state: live

### D-305
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M27
scope: engine
title: A document keeps a pack by its own test, the development pack only whole, and a kept pack with views is drawn in the rail from them
question: How does the app know which packs a document keeps, what does a partly declared pack show, and how is a pack's rail section drawn without a branch per pack?
answer: src/data/packs/packs.ts lists PACKS; each has keptBy(db): the ledger by a seed record, the development pack by declaring all of its format names. A document declaring only some keeps nothing of it (the Packs screen says which formats it holds). useKeptPacks() is the hook; useKeepsLedger() is that set asked for 'ledger'. A kept pack whose views include places (roadmap with order, board with lanes, ready with waits) gets a rail section before Formats: its views, then its formats, which leave Formats. A kind drawn once is labelled by kind (Roadmap, Ready), several by format title too (Unit board). The MCP screen shows a tool group whose half is a kept pack. A third pack costs a pack file and a PACKS entry. Installing the development pack from the Packs screen is not built (needs a tool half, D-234).
state: live

### D-306
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M27
scope: engine
title: The views are drawn from the approved mockup: a timeline roadmap, motion only for a change, one theme, and accent on live work
question: How do the declared views (roadmap, board, page, ready) look and move, per the approved mockup in docs/mockups/m27-u8/?
answer: Roadmap is a timeline: a dot per row on one line, solid to the live row and dashed after, a segment bar per row coloured by tone with counts; folds on grid-template-rows, starting as D-302 said; milestone roadmap gains goal: line. A state's tone (src/ui/formats/tone.ts) is presentation only, a UI word list: live (in_progress, open) wears accent, done (built, closed, live) filled ink, struck (dropped, superseded) dashed, anything else waiting/hollow. Motion explains a change only: useChanges (keyed by row name, never id, since text documents renew ids) makes a moved card slide from the side it came from and a changed row pulse its dot and flash accent-soft; nothing on first paint; reduced motion turns it off. One theme: square cards, uppercase label chips, lanes on surface with dashed 'Nothing <member>', page body left and every link column one hop each way on the right with 'Nothing'. Amends D-125: accent marks live work.
state: live

### D-307
date: 2026-10-01
milestone: M27
scope: engine
title: M27 closes on the maintainer's word, and M28 gets the new design on the ledger's screens, mockup first
question: U7 and U8 were reviewed, judged to look nice, and M27 was to close. What does M28 take from it?
answer: M27 closed 2026-10-01: U1-U6 as planned (D-298-D-304), and the review's two faults built as U7 (the development pack shown as a pack in the rail, D-305) and U8 (views built from the approved mockup, D-306). The note for M28: follow the new design and layout and improve the UI in general, perhaps with an HTML preview like the one just made. The ledger's M26 screen pass is now M28 U4. It aims at D-306's look and layout, and it starts with an HTML mockup in docs/mockups/m28-u4/, published to be judged live and approved before any screen is built. Carried to U4: tone.ts's undeclared live/done/dropped word list, and light mode not visually checked. M27's decisions D-297-D-306 moved to docs/archive/decisions/d297-d306-m27-development-pack.md.
