### D-308
date: 2026-10-02
milestone: M28
scope: ledger
title: Investments leave the ledger: the pack retires what it no longer declares, `exchange_rate` stays, and the price socket goes
question: What happens to the investment half of the ledger, to the formats old documents already hold, to exchange_rate, and to the price fetch?
answer: Removed whole: security, security_position, security_price, security_trade, purchase_plan, fx_rate, the broker statement reader (template shape, HOLDING/TRADE vocabularies, model task), their screens, the onboarding step and the five tools. The pack lists them in RETIRED_FORMATS: on install a retired format it still owns is deleted when empty, released (lock off, the person's own) when it holds rows; a person's own format of that name is untouched. exchange_rate stays on transaction and staged_row: the statement prints it and nothing converts with it; fx_rate goes. electron/prices.ts goes: the app makes no network call, only the two loopback sockets to a model runtime. Enum members (holding SECURITY, holding_place INVESTMENT, document_template INVESTMENT, column_role HOLDING/TRADE) stay because held declarations are not re-adopted. The proof (a person's own investments format on a real .emd) waits for the maintainer's yes.
state: live

### D-309
date: 2026-10-02
milestone: M28
scope: ledger
title: Money moved to something you own is not spent: a transaction a person's declared link names is saved, whatever its category
question: Transfers to a broker, categorised under the savings group, counted as 'went out' because 'saved' counted only goal and fund deposits. How does the ledger know money was moved rather than spent, without making the savings group a rule?
answer: A transaction that a row of a declared format links (a link or links column pointing at transaction that is not one of a pack's own columns) is moved, not spent, whatever its category. movedRepository.ts says which rows; statistics leave them out of outflow and inflow and add their net (out minus back) to saved as MonthObservation.moved; monthlyTotals and the ledger filter total leave them out too, the filter total counting them unsummed and saying how many. The ledger's own links (overrides, reapply, card settlement, debt payment) do not count. Proved with synthetic formats only.
state: live

### D-310
date: 2026-10-02
milestone: M28
scope: ledger
title: Payslips are read in bulk on Paychecks: a slip's labels choose its reader and its own arithmetic is the fit; the ledger's readers leave Settings for the screens they feed
question: How are payslips read in bulk, and how does a PDF slip with no header row fit a reader?
answer: #/paychecks/read takes any number of slips through F17's ladder, ticks each that reconciles, writes the ticked ones as paychecks in one transaction on Write (slipBatch.ts, writeSlipBatch). The label signature chooses the reader; the reader fits only when the slip's own arithmetic reconciles under it, otherwise it is close (named, never trusted). A reader the model works out in a batch is tried first for the rest of it and stored once on Write, only off a reconciled slip. Unnamed rows are named on the batch screen once per reader and kept against it. A month already held is not pre-ticked; the same bytes twice are never writable. Open alone hands a slip to the single confirm surface. Payslip readers are the Readers tab of Paychecks, statement readers the Readers tab of Transactions; #/settings/mappings redirects; fund report readers stay in Settings.
state: live

### D-311
date: 2026-10-02
milestone: M28
scope: ledger
title: A stored reader whose labels drifted still reads a slip its figures close on; the batch is the store's, not the screen's
question: Why did a reader worked out on one OCR'd payslip not carry to the next month of the same layout, why did naming a row on a non-reconciling slip do nothing, and where does a payslip batch live?
answer: OCR garbles the small print differently each month, so two months of one layout fell under CLOSE_ENOUGH (0.75 < 0.8) and rung 1 never tried the reader. Rung 1b (documentLadder.ts): when no reader matches by labels or the matched one does not add up, each stored reader is executed as-is and the first whose reading positively closes (shape.proves; payslips only, not fund reports whose verifies passes unchecked) reads the document, with an attempt sentence. A model-read slip that does not reconcile has no reader, and the batch named rows only against a reader; every slip now has namingKey (reader or slip). The batch lives in src/state/slipBatch.ts per document and SlipBatchBar names it, goes back and stops it. Fund report readers moved to #/wealth/readers; #/settings/formats redirects.
state: live

### D-312
date: 2026-10-02
milestone: M28
scope: ledger
title: The frame every screen shares is a tree rail, a split-tile button and the emerald indicator, and Paychecks is one month with three ways between months
question: What do navigation, buttons and loading states look like app-wide, and how is Paychecks laid out, after the M28 U4 mockup was approved (nav A, buttons B, all paging, cycling indicator)?
answer: Sidebar is a square tree: a section's pages (children, or its own tab views) beneath it when open; ViewTabs draws only a child's views (or a section's while the rail is collapsed); a section with running work shows the indicator. Button.tsx is a split tile (.fc-btn in tokens.css): icon in a leading tile (--color-action-deep on primary, hairline on secondary) that turns emerald on hover, count inside, busy swaps icon for the indicator; no icon means no tile; ghost is accent text with a drawing underline; D-154's highlight/shadow retired. Working.tsx (facets in gem.ts, tested against emerald.svg) is the one progress indicator, cycling for as long as work runs (docs/11's one-cycle rule amended for it alone), never counting. Paychecks: header with month + pager (arrows, arrow keys), clickable net-line points, the slip beside Where it went (WhereItWent.tsx; /paychecks/trace redirects), then All paychecks with Add by hand / Read payslips, missing-month rows, a drop zone; months.ts answers next; a written batch lands on its newest slip.
state: live

### D-313
date: 2026-10-02
milestone: M28
scope: ledger
title: Home is Month and Year, statements are read in a batch with no review screen, and the four flows carry their own colours on Home's figures
question: How does Home follow the approved M28 U5 mockup — names, paging, intake, the import review screen, and the flow colours D-125 forbade?
answer: Home is Month (#/) and Year (#/year); #/trends lands on Year, which carries Trends' category lines, merchants and year-apart table. Month opens on the newest month with data, pages by arrows/←→/chart column/All months row, has the drop band (Read files, Add by hand → Paychecks' form), five figures with 3-month averages, one-line Getting started, and missing statements as All months rows (domain/statementGaps.ts). Statements are read on #/read (statementBatch.ts): exact saved mapping → judged in memory, ticked when printed total and running balance agree; a disagreeing file waits and #/read/<id> shows the gap and failed rows, corrected by typing the cell; new layouts go to the mapper, which hands the sheet back. Write = stage + acceptAllNew + commit, as import_statement does. Review screen and 'N imported' notice removed; CSV export in Settings › Export. Flow colours flow-in/out/saved/tax on Home's headline figures, charts and totals only, amending docs/11's 'outflows are plain ink'; accent never colours a figure. Ctrl+Shift+O reads files (Ctrl+O is File › Open). Left over = balance (came in less went out).
state: live

