title: One editor makes a format and changes it: a new format is the editor on an empty one, the walk and the blank sheet go, and what has to add up is a named band
question: How is a format created once the walk read as 'the old view' and the edit sheet as better, and how does 'what has to add up' stop being a small, unintuitive button?
answer: Creating is NewFormat (src/ui/formats/NewFormat.tsx): title in the header (name derived; asked only when it cannot be, e.g. Hebrew), the sheet's headers with +, the band, and ShapePanel always open with Save = 'Create the format' via adoptFormat, landing on #/formats/<name>. FormatWalk, walk.ts, Sheet.tsx and sheet.ts are deleted. All three doors open it (Start empty replaces A blank sheet); an imported declaration naming an existing format opens that format's editor with the change in the panel (location state ImportedShape). creationOf withholds the not-yet-caused refusals (no name, no column, nothing about adding up) and names them as 'Still to say'; pruned (draft.ts) drops blank columns before judging/declaring; Escape does not cancel a new format. AddsUpPlace is a band under the toolbar on new and existing formats: identities as written / Nothing, on purpose / Not answered yet, the verdict tally, and a split-tile Say/Change what adds up button (equals glyph); the = icon is gone. Dropped on screen: typing rows before the format exists, and the notes field (still kept from imports and tools). Copy the declaration moved to ShapePanel's footer in both modes. A derived column (U3b) will be declared from its header in the same panel.
