### D-333
date: 2026-10-03
milestone: M29
scope: engine
title: A percent column: millionths of the whole as an integer, entered and shown as 12.5%, read by an identity as its exact fraction, and a money side rounded to the agora, halves away from zero
question: How is a percent stored, how precise is it, how does it multiply money and how is that rounded, and what does an identity's tolerance mean for it?
answer: percent is the 14th type (language 15, schema 54). Stored as INTEGER millionths of the whole (percent x 10^4: 12.5% = 125000), four places of a percent fixed, a fifth refused never rounded; no scale, no unit, not unique, not a rollup figure; bounds are percents compiled to millionths. Input is always the percent figure ('12.5', '12.5%', 12.5; 0.125 means 0.125%); shown 12.5% everywhere (sheet, page, format_rows, search, text .emd). An identity reads it as its fraction, exactly: the evaluator carries a BigInt n/d beside the double whenever all inputs are exact (integer, money minor units, decimal, percent, literals); real/quantity/unit conversion fall back to the double. A side carrying a currency is rounded to whole minor units, halves away from zero (roundHalfAway in percent.ts; SQL: (x + sign(x)*d/2)/d with truncating division) before comparing, for every money identity; so 'exactly' means to the agora and an absolute tolerance is agorot from the rounded figure. Without money, an absolute tolerance is in the fraction (0.0001 = a hundredth of a point). For U3b: price_minor * (1000000 + vat) / 1000000 in int64. Rejected: fraction at a declared scale, basis points, a % unit on real/decimal, per-product rounding, banker's rounding. Engine-only tool budget 31k -> 31.5k.
state: live

### D-334
date: 2026-10-03
milestone: M29
scope: engine
title: A derived column: an identity's expression producing a value, compiled by the engine into one SQL view per format from the parsed tree, never stored and never editable
question: How does a column get worked out from other columns, Excel-style, and how is it queryable from SQL without letting typed text reach db.run?
answer: A derived column is { name, expression, note } on a declaration (language 16, schema 55, table format_derived; a .format.md table `derived | works out | note`). The expression is an identity side with no `=` (parseExpression): own numbers, one hop link.column, an aggregate's name, another derived column, `x in g`. derived.ts types it at declare (money / number in a unit / number; refuses money*money, number+-money, number/money, unit on money, an unconverted quantity, cross-dimension, non-numbers, naming nothing, cycles; not yet: via, latest/earliest, identities or other formats naming a derived) and compiles it from the parsed tree into one view per format, fmt_<name>__derived (t.* plus derived; money as <name>_minor and <name>_currency). Hops and aggregates are correlated subqueries, units a subquery on measurement_unit. Figures carried as n/d and divided once; money rounded half away from zero in integer SQL (D-333). NULL for an empty input, x/0, two currencies, or an aggregate over a row that cannot answer (sum of none is 0). Views dropped before any declared-table change or migration and rebuilt after (adoptFormat, reconcileFormats, deleteFormat, migrate). App and format_rows run the same compiled SELECT; an empty cell's reason comes from the TS evaluator naming the input. Amends D-222: no typed text becomes SQL; identities unchanged. Screens: read-only sheet column, `= expression` on hover, edited in the shape panel (DerivedQuestions), declared via a `worked out` tile. Tools: declare/extend take derived; formats and format_rows show it. Budget 31.5k -> 32k.
state: live

### D-335
date: 2026-10-03
milestone: M29
scope: engine
title: A time-of-day column, and `end - start` of two times, dates or instants as a span in hours or days, two times wrapping at midnight
question: How does a shift's start and end hour become hours that add up, and how do dates and times take part in arithmetic without a conditional in the grammar?
answer: `time` is the fifteenth column type (language 17, schema 56, 056_time.sql widens format_column's CHECK): HH:MM text, 24-hour, CHECKed by shape and <= '23:59'; typed plainly (9, 9:30, 0930, 9.30, 9pm, 17:30:00) and kept as 09:30; a number is refused; a spreadsheet time cell (fraction of a day) is converted on reading; 24:00 refused; no unit, no bounds. A date, a time and an instant are points: the only arithmetic is `end - start` of two columns of one point type, a span in h (times, instants) or day (dates), in the document's time units, so `in min` converts it and aggregates and rollups sum it; anything else is refused at declare (span.ts pointProblem) and UNKNOWN on a row. Overnight: two times wrap modulo a day, ((e - s) % 1440 + 1440) % 1440 minutes, so 22:00 -> 06:00 is 8 h and 09:00 -> 09:00 is 0 h; dates and instants do not wrap (negative span). Shown as a figure in its unit, six significant digits (8.5 h, 8.33333 h), not 8:20. Exact in both evaluators: n/d with d = 60, 1 or 3,600,000; SQL via substr and julianday, TS via civil-date arithmetic; points enter IdentityInputs as `points`, never `numbers`. A rollup may fold a derived column that is a number, read off the view's SELECT; derived money is refused there. Screens: type picker and glyph, plain field with 09:00 placeholder, derived hint; balance picture draws no chips over points. Tools: declare/record/update take "09:30", derived words name spans; budget 32k -> 32.5k. Rejected: a conditional, a typed duration type, integer-minute storage, seconds, 24:00, date-minus-time, month spans. Found, not fixed: a text .emd with an aggregate over a format that links back to it does not reopen (readDocument fixpoint).
state: live

### D-336
date: 2026-10-03
milestone: M29
scope: engine
title: A text document checks every declaration against all the others, so formats that point at each other reopen
question: Why did a text .emd with an aggregate over a format that links back to it (a person summing shifts, a recipe summing lines) save but not reopen, and what is the rule now?
answer: readDocument read the parts to a fixpoint, checking each declaration only against those already read; a link and an aggregate point opposite ways, so each waited on the other (two mutually linked formats failed the same way; SQLite bytes never did). Now every declaration is parsed first (artefact.ts parse) and each is checked against all the others (declarationRejection); a cycle is just a document, a missing target is still refused. Formats come back in link order where there is one. applyDocument needs no second pass. The ledger and development pack loaders still carry their own two-pass/link-order workaround for the same reader.
state: live

### D-337
date: 2026-10-03
milestone: M28
scope: ledger
title: Two exports of one bank are one account: a file whose rows another account already holds stops and says so, the mapper offers the existing accounts first, and a reader learns its account on first use
question: Two exports of one bank in two layouts (an app CSV and a website report) became two bank accounts with an overlapping week written twice. Why, and what keeps one bank one account?
answer: The layouts genuinely differ (a second reader is right) and natural keys match across readers, but the mapper offered only a new account named after the file, and the unique index is per account. Now: judgeSheet flags rows already held by another account (same key and direction, at least two rows or every row) as state 'elsewhere' with Into <account> / Keep apart, never ticked; the mapper's StageBar offers existing accounts first, defaulting to the overlap holder; the read screen judges by peekAccountForImport (bound, last-4, then name) and shows which account each sheet goes to; recordMappingUse binds an unbound reader to a numberless account on first commit, and mergeAccounts binds a reader that only read into the source to the survivor; default reader names use a provider in the footer and drop dates from the file stem. import_statement's plan names the overlapping account_id.
state: live

### D-338
date: 2026-10-03
milestone: M28
scope: ledger
title: A queued file comes out before Write, by its × or Delete, and the window's drop overlay lets go in the capture phase
question: How does a person take a file out of a batch read before Write, and why did the window's drag overlay stay up after a drop?
answer: Every queued file on the three batch reads (Home's Read files, Paychecks' read, a format's read) has a × and, focused, Delete/Backspace removes it (removableRow in IconAction.tsx); withoutFiles takes a statement's tick, Write anyway and mapper trip with it. The overlay stuck because each screen's drop zone stops its drop from reaching the window (so the ledger does not read it twice), which also hid the drop from the overlay's depth count. watchFileDrag clears the overlay on a capture-phase drop, on a dragleave with no relatedTarget (out of the window) and on Escape; the reading drop stays in the bubble phase so a zone's stop still wins.
state: live

### D-339
date: 2026-10-04
milestone: M28
scope: ledger
title: One batch makes its accounts once: every sheet is judged against the sheets before it, the mapper offers the accounts the batch is about to make, and a reader drawn for one file reads the files waiting behind it
question: How does a file later in a statement batch land on an account an earlier file of the same batch will only make on Write, without writing the first files first?
answer: judgeBatch judges the batch's sheets in order; every sheet Write will write (writesSheet: ticked or forced) leaves its new rows under its destination key (account id, or new:#last4 / new:@name as findOrCreateAccount matches). placeRows counts a later sheet's rows on their way to the same destination as duplicates and rows on their way elsewhere as D-337's overlap ('N of M rows are also in <file>', Into / Keep apart). Into a pending account copies the request that makes it (pointAt), so both resolve to one account whichever is written first; no sheet refers to another. StageBar offers the batch's pending accounts ('Everyday Bank, new with everyday.csv') beside the document's, defaulting to the overlap or the same-name account. After a mapping, readWaitingLayouts re-reads every waiting new-layout file with the new reader, and a sheet read by a reader another batch sheet used (same last-4 or none) takes that sheet's account request. Write and the overlap removal are unchanged. import_statement stays one file per call (already sequential); its commit names the account_id written into and, for a new account, tells the next file to pass it.
state: live

