### D-389
date: 2026-10-05
milestone: M36
scope: ledger
title: A fund is a holding fed by rows: a report is a reading that may carry its parts, its deposit table is reading_deposit rows, payslip lines are its deposits worked out on read, a month whose employer part never arrived fails by name, and the fund formats, inFunds and the Provident and Study fund place kinds go
question: Should a pension, study or provident fund stay a second model (fund_account, fund_balance_snapshot, fund_deposit, totals via inFunds) beside holdings, where one pension entered both ways counted twice?
answer: A fund is a holding with fund_type set (no place, kind FUND, TRANSCRIBED, not liquid; optional fund columns on holding; fee = fee on accumulation; agreed return = CONTRACTUAL expected_return). A report is a holding_reading with optional parts (employee, employer, severance, deposits, fees_charged, return_amount, deposit_gap_acknowledged) under the parts identity; the per-deposit table is reading_deposit rows linked to the reading. deduction_type.linked_holding and split_bucket.target_holding replace linked_fund_account and target_fund. fundRepository keeps its API over the holding tables; the reading source picker goes. depositsByMonth names missingParts and a month with one is MISSING; fundMissingMonths drives the Funds chip, Needs a call and the funds tool on one report. listHoldings leaves funds out unless includeFunds; netWorth.funds replaces inFunds; liquiditySplit has no side parameter; FUND_WITHOUT_SNAPSHOT goes (Home still names a fund with no reading). The pack retires the three formats after rebuilding its own tables (an empty one goes, one with rows becomes the person's own), retires the two links, relists holding_place.kind without PROVIDENT and STUDY_FUND. INVESTMENT stays, labelled Investments. No old document converted.
state: live

### D-390
date: 2026-10-05
milestone: M36
scope: engine
title: Nothing personal in the repository or its history: synthetic stand-ins in the tree, a denylist kept outside the repo that a test reads, and the history rewritten once with filter-repo plus a callback for the document stored as SQLite bytes
question: How is the repository kept free of the maintainer's personal names, numbers, file names and figures, in the tree and in git history?
answer: Tests and docs use synthetic stand-ins (Everyday Bank, round figures). The real terms live in a denylist outside the repo at ~/.emerald/denylist.txt (or $EMERALD_DENYLIST), in git filter-repo --replace-text form, never committed in any form. src/privacy.test.ts checks every tracked text file against it, reports file and denylist line only, and skips where the file is absent. History was rewritten once (filter-repo --replace-text/--replace-message, main force-pushed 2026-10-05). --replace-text skips binary blobs, so the SQLite-bytes versions of docs/memory.emd were cleaned by opening each as a database, replacing in cells, VACUUM and integrity check; a document stored as bytes needs a blob callback, never a byte-level replace. Any other clone must be deleted and re-cloned.
state: live

### D-391
date: 2026-10-05
milestone: M36
scope: ledger
title: A conversation says what a payslip line pays into: set_deduction_types sets a list of deduction types' Counts as and Pays into through the screen's own write, whole batch or nothing, refusing a target of the wrong kind by name
question: How does a conversation set what a deduction type counts as and pays into — the link a fund finds its payslip lines through — when only the Deduction types screen could?
answer: set_deduction_types takes a list of {type (id, name or slip name), counts_as (out_of_net_saving|out_of_net|saving|not_cash|guess), pays_into (fund, goal or debt by id or name; null unlinks; one destination clears the others), pays_into_kind}. It writes through updateDeductionType and runs syncPayroll after a link, as the screen does. A fund or goal needs a line that counts as saving (after the entry's counts_as), a debt a LOAN_REPAYMENT; wrong kind, no match, ambiguous name or a type named twice refuse the whole batch by name. Dry run until commit:true. Flags off the kind's guess read 'set by hand' (countsAsWords now in domain/paycheckEntry.ts). Ledger parity ratchet 78 -> 77; adding, renaming, reordering and deleting a type stay screen-only.
state: live

### D-392
date: 2026-10-05
milestone: M36
scope: ledger
title: set_deduction_types is the whole Deduction types screen: one list makes, renames, re-kinds and removes types and an order puts them first, through the screen's own writes, with removal refused by name where the screen refuses it
question: How does a conversation make, rename, re-kind, order and remove a deduction type — e.g. the loan-repayment type a payroll loan needs — when only the Deduction types screen could?
answer: set_deduction_types (extended, not a second tool) takes types:[{type?, name?, on_the_slip? (null clears), kind?, counts_as?, pays_into?, pays_into_kind?, remove?}] and order:[type by id or post-call name]. No `type` = new (needs name + kind; flags start as the kind's guess). `name` renames; `kind` changes kind via kindChange (flags follow while guessed); remove:true takes nothing else; order puts the named types first, the rest keep their order. Writes: deleteDeductionType first, then updateDeductionType, createDeductionType, reorderDeductionTypes, syncPayroll after a link. Refused by name, whole batch: removing a built-in, a type a stored payslip line names, or one another row links to (rowsLinkingTo); a name another type has or will have (case-insensitive, so a swap takes two calls); order naming nothing, a removed type or one twice. Marked destructive (catalog removes, annotation). Ledger parity ratchet 77 -> 74.
