### D-397
date: 2026-10-06
milestone: M34
scope: engine
title: A total may fold every row of a format, written `every course` in place of a link — one row states a figure (a degree's average) and an identity proves it against all of them; the total lives on a format and is read by its name, so one hop stays a parse rule
question: M34 U4: a degree's average typed by hand as 85 while its courses give 84.52 could not be checked, because an aggregate folds only the rows whose link names this row (D-224) and no course names a degree. Where does a total over every row live, and how does it read in an expression without breaking one hop?
answer: On a format (in practice one with one row, e.g. degree with stated_average): an aggregate whose source has no column folds every row of that format, and every host row shows the same figure. Spelled as a word, never a missing column: `every course` in the artefact's over cell, `every: true` in place of over_column on declare_format and extend_format (which now takes `aggregates`), and 'Which courses: Every course' in the connection window when has many… is dropped on a format that names nothing here. Stored as over_column = '' (no migration); format language 21 (EVERY_ROW_LANGUAGE). Read in an expression by its name like any total — stated_average = weighted / credit — so no new syntax; the per-row expression still reaches one hop and a second hop is a parse error. On the graph the curve ends on the whole bubble. A failed identity names both figures in format_problems, after a read and in the What looks wrong band ('Bachelor of Science (85 against 84.52)'); the band's and the format list's tally are judged through the join (joinedTally). Rejected: a document-level figure (a new scope and a new spelling in every expression), a source with no column meaning every row (D-224's silent fold-everything turned into a typo's default), a general query (D-095). Folded per host row, so a total over a format's own rows costs rows x rows, uncut until measured. Engine-only tool budget 35.5k.
