title: A copy from before — a read or batch of 100 rows or more, and anything somebody asks for, first keeps `<name>.before-<when>-<what>.emd` beside the document, the last three apart from the rolling five, and the answer names it; a browser holds it in the page until it is downloaded
question: The rolling five .bak copies lost the document from before an import within six writes. What keeps it, when, what removes it, and what does a named copy mean in the shell and in a browser?
answer: Before any write of 100 rows or more (NAMED_COPY_ROWS: a read, one file or many, on read_onto_format or the screen's Write on #/formats/<name>/read; record/update/delete_format_rows; delete_format; supersede_reading) and whenever asked (keep_copy tool; 'Keep a copy as it is now' in the palette), keep <stem>.before-<YYYYMMDDTHHMMSS>-<slug>.emd beside the document, in its own encoding. A mutation opts in through Mutation.copyBefore. The last three named copies per document are kept, pruned by the stamp in the name (copyFile keeps the mtime on Windows), and only names isNamedCopyOf recognises; the rolling .bak prune never sees them. The answer names the path first; a copy that fails refuses the write. Shell: written by the main process through the backup channel. Browser (and an unsaved shell document): held in the page, a notice offers Download it; nothing downloads unasked. Naming lives in electron/bridge.ts. Ledger tools take none (parked).
