title: The Markdown reader's small faults: a note is called by its frontmatter `title` or its file name and its body is kept whole, a hidden file is never a row, the body's `#tags` are an `inline tags` column, `columns` maps a column to `null` and applies over the mapping shown, a fixed currency or unit goes only to rows with an amount, a dry run cuts long text, and an emoji written as a character reference survives a spreadsheet
question: What did M34 T1's trial find wrong in reading a folder of notes and a spreadsheet, and how is each fixed?
answer: A note's title is frontmatter title else the file name, never its first heading, and the body is kept whole (amends D-400). A name starting with '.' is no note: skipped and named as not read, in the plugin and on the Read screen. The body's #tags (not headings, numbers, addresses or code) are an 'inline tags' list column, present only when a note has one; the guess maps it to a free 'tags' column. read_onto_format's columns accepts null for a column (reads nothing) and applies over the mapping the dry run showed (remembered reading, else guess), so send only what changes. A fixed currency/unit is dropped on rows with no amount. Dry runs cut text at 200 chars (mapping samples at 60) with its length; format_rows prints whole. sheetTable.ts spells out astral character references (&#x1F375;) before SheetJS 0.18.5, which truncated them to private-use characters. Verified: a quantity in 'ug' is refused naming known units; a real column declared unit 'ug' is still accepted (checked only where an identity converts), left as is.
