scope: engine
title: A source read again updates what it wrote, by a key: a note's file name, or the value of the format's unique column the reading fills; a changed line is written over its row, a new one added, and a row whose line is gone named and removed only on request — a row a person edited since is left and named; the row remembers its line beside it (`format_intake_row.source_key`, `written`), and a text document carries what was read in (`readings.md`)
question: What does reading the same file or folder a second time do, what keys a line, where does a row remember its source, and what happens to a row whose line is gone?
answer: A source is its file name (not hash). Key: a note's file name; for a spreadsheet the format's unique column the reading fills (name column first; text/integer/date/decimal/month). No key, or a reading with a header row: the read adds as before. Matched line: same (no write), changed (written over, empty keys cleared), or edited-here (row no longer matches its `written` mark: left and named). New key: added. Missing key: gone, named and kept; `remove_gone: true` / the screen's Remove it takes it away, except rows something links to. Records live in format_intake_row.source_key/written (migration 060) and move to the newest reading. Text documents carry all intake tables in a readings.md part, rows by RowRef (name + occurrence); reading ids are derived from format, file, hash, moment. No Undo on the screen after a read again.
