title: The tool surface a stranger tripped on: `format_rows` takes an `offset` and hands back the next one, and a total or derived column by name in `columns`; a link names its row by id, by a name exactly one row holds (unique or not), or by columns of that row — `{name, brand}` — exactly one or refused; the full plugin introduces formats first and `document_info` leads with them; a format with no identity needs no flag — `nothing_must_hold` left `declare_format` and no surface refuses its absence; and the first rows written into a document that holds none take a named copy first, whatever their number
question: Where did a stranger driving the plugin (M34 T1) trip, and what does the tool surface do instead?
answer: format_rows takes offset and a cut-short answer names the next one (250 rows = two calls at limit 200); columns takes a total (aggregate) or derived column by name. A link value may be an object of the target's columns ({name, brand}) resolved to exactly one row or refused (linksByColumns, in stageRows and update_format_rows); a plain name resolves under a non-unique naming column when exactly one row holds it (amends D-256). SERVER_INSTRUCTIONS opens formats first, the ledger is its own later section; document_info leads with declared formats and row counts, ledger counts only when kept. The MCP key financial-companion lives only in Claude Desktop config and was not renamed (the maintainer's call). No identity is the default (amends D-222/D-345): declarationProblems no longer refuses it, nothing_must_hold left declare_format's schema, both-at-once still refused. The first rows written into a document holding none take a named copy (mutatingCall, holdsNoRows), like a 100-row batch (D-399).
state: live

### D-412
date: 2026-10-06
milestone: M38
scope: engine
title: A conversation changes a format as its sheet does: `extend_format` takes `rename`, `change` and `remove` through the same `adoptFormat` and reconciler, dry-run until `commit`, refused naming the rows where a value would be lost or re-meant; and a list every row fits — a text made an enum, an enum re-listed or made a text — is rebuilt over its rows on any format
question: Can a conversation rename, retype or remove a column, or remove an identity, of a format that exists — and can a text column become an enum over rows that already hold values?
answer: Yes. `extend_format` takes `rename` ({old: new}), `change` (columns named with only what changes; a new type keeps only required, unique, note) and `remove` (columns, identities, totals, derived, rollups, rule sets by name), applied before additions, through `adoptFormat` with the renames — the sheet's path. Identities and derived columns follow a rename; another format it breaks refuses by name. Refusals name the rows (`rowsHolding`): a column dropped with values, a list a row does not fit. A type, unit or target change over values stays refused on both halves, and the answer says: add a column in the new shape, copy with update_format_rows, remove the old. The reconciler rebuilds a list every row fits (text<->enum, enum re-listed) on any format, not only a pack's. Supersedes D-290's refusal; amends D-386.
supersedes: D-290
state: live

### D-413
date: 2026-10-07
milestone: M33
scope: engine
title: The showcase is tours over the real app: a drawn pointer walks a workflow a visitor can take over, a tour's steps are rows the document holds, the document is built at build time by the engine's own tools, and one deployed build serves Emerald's page and the maintainer's site through an iframe with parameters
question: A tour demo was chosen for the maintainer's personal site and for Emerald's page (2026-10-07): the real app, played like a video, with a toggle to take over. How is it built so nothing is duplicated, nothing is staged, and nothing can drift from the product?
answer: One demo entry (demo.html, src/demo/) loads a bundled document through documentStore.openBytes; Open, Save, recents and their shortcuts are absent and every browser-storage read is guarded, because the frame lives in a cross-origin iframe. The document is not committed: scripts/make-demo-document.mjs drives the MCP server over stdio at build time, as local-host-seed.mjs does, from committed synthetic sources, so the demo is rebuilt against the current schema on every build and cannot show what the engine cannot do. A tour is rows of a declared tour_step format in that document (tour, position, anchor, action click|type|press|wait|say, value, caption), read by a runner that moves a drawn ring, not an arrow, to a data-tour anchor, dispatches the real event, waits for the next anchor to exist and never for a fixed delay, and loops by reopening the bytes; a visitor's click or the toggle stops it, Reset replays, a dot jumps by fast-forwarding, reduced motion drops the travel. Tours as rows keep the runner generic, so a pack can one day carry its own tour (M32). Every tour has a check that replays it at fast-forward in a real browser, so a screen change breaks a tour loudly; the Electron binary already present is preferred over a new dependency. One build, deployed to Pages from this repo, is embedded by both sites as an iframe with ?tour= and autoplay; below a width the frame is scaled and plays as a movie with take-over off. Not a submodule. M33 is re-planned around it, U1-U9: tours replace the islands, since an island's 'try to make it wrong' is a step a visitor takes over; the custom-format tour goes first because it is the one on the maintainer's site; ordered before M32 on 2026-10-07, superseding the 2026-10-05 order. The AI half stays a recording.
state: live

