### D-430
date: 2026-10-08
milestone: M33
scope: engine
title: Emerald is AGPL-3.0: the code AGPL-3.0-only, the notes CC BY 4.0, every format declaration, pack and synthetic fixture CC0, the name and mark excluded, a commercial licence on request — published under the maintainer's own name from a fresh repository, after one history rewrite that is the maintainer's act
question: Under which licence and which identity does the repo open to strangers, so that nothing has to be taken back later?
answer: AGPL-3.0-only for everything that runs (app, shell, MCP server, scripts, tests), chosen 2026-10-07: a sole author can always relax to a looser licence later and can never withdraw a permissive one, so the strictest acceptable one goes first; it is open source by the definition the local-first crowd checks, stops a proprietary fork, and is the one choice compatible with both D-124 and the Monetization question's first answer, since a commercial licence is a document between two people and never a key, tier or locked feature. Every runtime dependency is MIT, ISC, Apache-2.0 or OFL. LICENSING.md adds: notes and essays CC BY 4.0; every .format.md, pack declaration and synthetic fixture CC0; the name and the mark excluded; a person's .emd is theirs; until a contributor agreement exists the project takes issues and discussion, not code, so dual licensing stays possible. package.json says AGPL-3.0-only and names the maintainer (it had said MIT; nothing was ever released). The identity, set 2026-10-08: Kfir Raphael Eliyahu; kfireliyahu on GitHub; commits and npm by me@kfireliyahu.com; no Emerald domain yet, the repo's Pages site is the demo's home; the repo's local git identity is set to it from this commit on. The slug, set the same day: emerald-emd — github.com/kfireliyahu/emerald-emd, Pages at kfireliyahu.github.io/emerald-emd, npm package emerald-emd; package.json's repository and homepage say so. The gate before anything is public, the maintainer's act and never a session's: a FRESH repository under kfireliyahu rather than a transfer of the old one (force-pushed after holding personal data; unreachable commits stay fetchable by hash); one history rewrite first, re-authoring every commit to the new identity because every commit so far carries a private address that must never be public, done once at a seam with every session stopped, right before the first push; the pre-rewrite git deleted or moved offline; the server renamed; the privacy test run with the denylist present — whose line holding the maintainer's surname goes, since the name is public by design, and the old address goes in as a scrub line.
