title: The project's rules are rows of its own `rule` format in `memory.emd`, not lists in conventions.md and CLAUDE.md; CLAUDE.md carries the few marked `always`, rendered and checked by docs:check, and points at the query for the rest
question: Where do the project's working rules live — the non-negotiables, the traps, the laws, the style — now that its notes are read in Emerald?
answer: As rows of a `rule` format declared in docs/memory.emd, the project's own beside the development pack's (the pack is untouched): name, statement, why, kind (law|non_negotiable|trap|style), scope (engine|app|shell|plugin|practice|product), decided_by -> decision as a list, enforced_by (repo paths), always, state (live|retired). 52 rows from conventions.md's Non-negotiable rules, Style, Things that will bite you (with The shell's) and the findings, and CLAUDE.md's Rules that are easy to break by accident, merged where they said the same thing. Reverses CLAUDE.md's 'the prose notes never move': conventions.md keeps only argument and procedure (175 lines from 325). CLAUDE.md carries nine rows marked always (money, src/domain imports nothing, the engine/pack boundary, parity, offline, a model's output, real documents out of the repo, a dirty memory.emd, bidi), rendered into a marked block by `npm run docs:check -- --write`; docs:check fails when the block differs from the rows, fails when a live rule's enforced_by names a missing file, and warns when a live rule rests on a superseded decision. The rest: `format_rows rule match:{scope:...}` or `search:`. A new rule is a row, never a bullet. Answers the open question Code facts; opens Rules on a superseded decision (the engine cannot fold forward over a row's own links).
