### D-439
date: 2026-10-08
milestone: M44
scope: development
title: The development pack gains an `inbox` another project's session writes to: `from` is the sending document's path, `start_here` lists what is `new`, and who writes what is a convention its notes state, not a guarantee the engine gives
question: How does a session in another project hand this project facts and findings so they arrive and last, when the clipboard lost three of six and a message to a session lasts only as long as the session?
answer: A sixth development-pack format, `inbox`: subject (first text column, heads the row), from (the sending document's absolute path, never a session title), sent_on, body (markdown), refs (the sender's own refs), state new|triaged|applied|dropped, and what it became: answered_by -> decision, became_unit -> unit, became (text, for an open question or review entry by ref, a rule or term by name, or why it was dropped; open_question.ref is not unique and review_entry has no naming column, so neither can be a link target in a text document). A sender writes a row once with state new and never edits it; the owner moves state and fills what it became; a foreign session writes no other format. That is a convention in the notes, not enforced (D-138). Writes go through record_format_rows (no new engine write, D-234 untouched); a write from another project is the concurrent-writers case the lock, whole-batch apply and copy-first already handle. start_here lists state new from the pack's `inbox start` view. A document keeping the pack gains it through keep_packs (D-405), which is how docs/memory.emd got it. Waking a session on a new row is M44 U3's.
state: live

### D-440
date: 2026-10-08
milestone: M44
scope: engine
title: The server holds one kind of state: which documents a connection subscribed to. A document is a resource addressed by its `file://` URI, watched through its directory, and every change is sent as `notifications/resources/updated`; tools stay stateless
question: How does a client learn that a document changed, and what may the MCP server now remember between calls (amending D-092's 'nothing is remembered between calls')?
answer: The server declares resources.subscribe. resources/subscribe takes the document's file:// URI or its absolute path; the server watches the document's directory (D-127, src/plugin/watch.ts watchDocument: one fs.watch per document, refcounted, 150 ms settle, reported when size/mtime/file id moved, fs.watchFile fallback) and sends notifications/resources/updated with the exact string the client sent, for every change including this server's own writes. resources/unsubscribe or the client closing stops it. Documents are never listed or read as resources (read with the tools). The only state held is which documents a connection subscribed to; tools stay stateless and nothing of the document is held. Measured ~200 ms save-to-notification over stdio. Claude Code 2.1.177 never sends resources/subscribe and registers no handler for resources/updated. M44 U3 and U4 listen through watch.ts.
state: live

### D-441
date: 2026-10-08
milestone: M44
scope: engine
title: A subscription can name one format — the document's URI with the format as its fragment — and is told only when that format's rows, compared as printed, differ; `server.mjs watch` prints one line per such change so a session's Monitor wakes on it
question: How does a subscriber hear only one format's changes, and how is a Claude session (which ignores MCP notifications) woken when its inbox gets a row?
answer: A format is a resource: the document's file:// URI (or absolute path) plus '#<format>' (file:///F:/notes/memory.emd#inbox); everything before '#' is still D-440's document URI, and a bare path splits at its last '#' only when a format name follows. On each change the server reads that format's rows read-only as format_rows prints them (links by target name, never ids, since a text document renews ids) and compares the readings as multisets; it notifies only when they differ, refuses at subscribe a format the document cannot read, and holds that last reading (amends D-440's 'only which documents'). src/plugin/formatWatch.ts (readFormat, compareRows, matching, watchFormat). The wake path is a subcommand of the one server file: `node dist-plugin/server.mjs watch <doc> [--format <name>] [--match col=value]...` (src/plugin/watchCommand.ts, dispatched in main.ts) run under the Monitor tool: one stdout line per change, e.g. 'inbox (state=new): 1 new row in F:/.../memory.emd — "<subject>"'; with --match a row leaving the match is silent (own triage); reads only, no network, no polling; refusals said once while it keeps watching; document gone for 1 s → a line and exit 0; bad args/missing doc or format → stderr, exit 2. Measured ~380 ms save-to-line under Monitor; other-format writes and the row's deletion woke nothing.
state: live

### D-442
date: 2026-10-08
milestone: M44
scope: engine
title: A drawn view is drawn again when its document changes: the frame subscribes through the host and asks again only when told, and where the host refuses, checks the file's stamp with `frame_copy` — an app-only tool, listed only to a client that draws — on coming into view and every 15 s while in view; it says which in the host's log
question: How does a view drawn in the conversation (M41's frame) stay current when the document changes, given nobody knows whether a real MCP Apps host forwards a frame's resources/subscribe or the server's resources/updated?
answer: The copy carries path, uri and stamp (watch.ts's size:time:id mark). The frame sends resources/subscribe through the host: taken → push (refetch only on notifications/resources/updated); refused or 5 s silent → check: frame_copy {path, since} returns the stamp alone (~150 B) when unchanged, the copy when moved, on coming into view/focus (spaced 2 s) and every 15 s while in view, never while hidden; neither forwarded → off (copy as shown). A push host caught passing no word on (a check on coming into view finds an unannounced change after a 3 s grace) moves to check. The choice is said via notifications/message. frame_copy has _meta.ui.visibility ['app'] and is listed only to clients advertising MCP Apps. Redraw is openBytes again, so route, open entry and scroll regions stay mounted. The stand-in `npm run frame:try -- <doc> <format> --live [--no-push]` plays both hosts; what real hosts do is unmeasured.
state: amended

### D-443
date: 2026-10-08
milestone: M44
scope: development
title: A pack lists the columns it renamed, and keeping it again renames them in place with their values; a document that keeps a pack already is brought up to date by keeping it again, when this build's pack has grown or renamed a column since
question: Why did a document that kept the development pack before D-420 still have unit.claimed_by, and how does a pack's change reach a document that keeps it already?
answer: Two causes: bringUpToDate (D-378) brings only the ledger up to date on open, and keepPack left a kept pack alone; and even when kept again, fit.ts compares by name, so taken_by would have been added empty beside claimed_by. Now the pack lists its renames (DEVELOPMENT_RENAMES in src/data/packs/development/pack.ts: unit.claimed_by -> taken_by; PackRename and followingRenames in fit.ts); planKeep reports them (KeepPlan.renames) and the install follows one only where the document holds the old name and not the new, through adoptFormat's renames (values, widths, entry inputs follow). keepPack runs on a kept pack unless upToDate(plan) (kept, nothing to declare, complete or rename); keep_packs says 'bringing it up to date' and the Packs screen offers 'Bring it up to date' on a kept pack's card, both through keepPack, no new write. Not on open: no development-pack code assumes its columns, and an open should not dirty a document. Proved on a copy of a second project's document: one keep_packs declared inbox and renamed the column with its 25 values. Amends D-420's last bullet. A document kept before D-439 lacks inbox and is not kept until kept again, by the same call.
state: live

### D-444
date: 2026-10-08
milestone: M44
scope: development
title: The development pack's `open_question` gains `answer`, text: a question answered by a fact says the fact there, and a decision row is written only for a decision
question: Where does the answer go when an open question is answered by a fact rather than a decision?
answer: open_question gains answer (text, optional, last column) in src/data/packs/development/formats/open_question.format.md and docs/memory.emd (which took it by keep_packs, D-443). A question a decision answered names it in answered_by; one a fact answered says it in answer with state answered, and no decision row is invented. Same name and type as the identity plan's own column, so its document's answer becomes the pack's on its next keep, no clash. blocks is not taken: unit.blocks says what holds a unit up; the identity plan's own blocks stays its own column (D-265). Rejected: a decision row per fact.
