### D-445
date: 2026-10-08
milestone: M41
scope: engine
title: The frame wears the host's surfaces, ink, hairlines, small radii and type from `hostContext.styles`, re-applied on `host-context-changed`; the accent, the verdict and figure colours and the square shapes stay Emerald's and must read at 4.5:1 on the host's surface, or the frame keeps Emerald's colours whole; no font origin is allowed in the CSP
question: Can the frame drawn in a host's conversation sit on the host's surface and look native, and what of the host's look may it take?
answer: src/frame/hostStyle.ts maps MCP Apps' standard variables onto Emerald's tokens, inline on :root, only those given: background-primary -> background and elevated, background-secondary -> surface, text-primary/secondary/tertiary -> ink/ink-secondary/ink-tertiary, borders faintest first -> hairline and hairline-strong, --border-radius-sm/md/lg -> --radius-sm/md/lg, --font-sans/--font-mono -> themselves. Never taken: accent, action, positive, negative, flows, categories, --radius-card/--radius-control; the host's ring, accent and status colours are not read. After the theme and variables are applied, accent, positive, negative, ink and ink-secondary must each read 4.5:1 on the resolved background, else the host's colours come off whole (type and radii stay); the frame logs which, with ratios, in the host's log. A host surface sets data-host-surface (page background transparent); the page declares color-scheme light dark; the resource is prefersBorder:false. css.fonts is injected but no resourceDomains is declared (it would open script-src too; D-433), so a host font file is refused and the family falls back. Merged and re-applied on ui/notifications/host-context-changed. The stand-in plays a themed host with --host-theme=vscode[-light|-dark] (src/frame/hostThemes.json).
state: amended

### D-446
date: 2026-10-08
milestone: M41
scope: engine
title: No tool result carries a copy of the document: the drawn view fetches it with `frame_copy`, now listed to every client and refused without the ticket the view's result carried; the server logs what each client advertised; and the text beside a drawn view names every row it shows and nothing longer
question: VS Code's Copilot Chat drew format_sheet's frame with nothing in it (the copy went only to clients advertising io.modelcontextprotocol/ui in the form the server read), and the 15 KB text beside it was format_rows' whole answer cut at 25 rows. How does a drawn view get its document in every host without a model being handed it, and what text goes beside it?
answer: format_sheet and start_here put {name, format, view, path, uri, ticket} (~250 B) under _meta['emerald/sheet'] for every client and never the bytes. The frame, once drawn, calls tools/call frame_copy {path, ticket} through the host (src/frame/live.ts firstCopy) and passes the ticket on every later check. frame_copy is listed to every client (a host refuses a page's call to an unlisted tool), keeps _meta.ui.visibility ['app'], and refuses without the ticket: an HMAC-SHA256 of the path under a secret made at server start and written nowhere (src/plugin/tools/sheet.ts ticketFor, NO_TICKET); a view drawn before a restart is refused and says so. Claude Code 2.1.177 shows its model no _meta (measured via claude -p: text alone; frame_copy without the ticket got the refusal). The frame names the failed hop (host refused the call, server refused, _meta lost). createServer's log option (main.ts: stderr; VS Code Output > MCP: <server>) prints on initialized the client's name, version, its io.modelcontextprotocol/ui entry and whole capabilities (clientLine); nothing else turns on that entry. format_sheet's text (viewText) names every row the view shows: sheet rows with their first enum, board lanes with their rows, roadmap rows in order with state and folded rows counted by state, ready and held rows; then the format_rows call; cap 500 names. memory.emd's milestone roadmap: 15,292 B cut at 25 -> ~1.2 KB naming all 27. start_here's text unchanged. Stand-in: frame:try --no-ui; replay records the first frame_copy; launch.json frame-live-noui on 4188. Amends D-432 and D-442.
supersedes: D-432
state: amended

### D-447
date: 2026-10-08
milestone: M41
scope: engine
title: The frame takes what to draw from the call's input and proves itself with a key the server writes into the page it serves; no result carries the proof, and `frame_copy` answers in `structuredContent`
question: VS Code's Copilot Chat hands a drawn view the tool result without its _meta, where D-446 put the path, view and ticket, so the frame said it did not know which document to draw. How does the frame know what to draw, and prove to frame_copy that it is the drawn view, without the result's _meta?
answer: What to draw is the call's input (ui/notifications/tool-input): format_sheet's path, format, view; start_here's path alone -> view 'start'. The result's _meta['emerald/sheet'] {name, format, view, path, uri} then its structuredContent answer only where no input came (host.ts sheetOf(input, result)); the result still reports isError; a result arriving before the input is waited on 1 s; a format the document lacks is found once the copy is open and said. The proof is a key made at server start (sheet.ts KEY, randomBytes(32) base64url), written nowhere but into the served page as <meta name="emerald-frame-key"> (servedPage, server.ts resources/read); frame_copy {path, key} refuses without it (NO_KEY, starting NOT_THIS_VIEW in contract.ts). The key itself, not an HMAC of the path: the threat is a model pulling a megabyte into its context, and an HMAC needs crypto.subtle, which a sandboxed srcdoc frame need not have. frame_copy answers in structuredContent {stamp, name, uri, bytes?}; the frame reads _meta['emerald/copy'] where only that came. A stale key (page from before a restart) shows STALE_KEY: ask to be shown again, else reload the host's window; whether VS Code caches the page across a restart is unmeasured (the spec allows caching). The frame logs once what it was handed (handedOver: tool-input, content, structuredContent, _meta). Stand-in: frame:try --strip-meta strips _meta from every tools/call result the frame gets; launch.json frame-live-noui adds it. Amends D-446.
supersedes: D-446
state: live

### D-448
date: 2026-10-08
milestone: M41
scope: engine
title: The frame paints no page of its own in any host, and inside VS Code's webview its root declares no colour scheme, so the browser lays no backdrop under it; it says in the host's log and its own console what the host handed
question: Why did the roadmap draw on a darker panel of its own in VS Code's dark Copilot Chat, and what makes the frame sit on the conversation in every host?
answer: src/frame/frame.css: html and body paint transparent in every host (a named --color-background-primary need not be the chat's: VS Code sends var(--vscode-editor-background)); Emerald's surfaces stay where the app paints them (cards, popovers, footer rows, chips, a selected row, a sticky header). The panel was the browser's opaque Canvas backdrop: a frame whose root color-scheme differs from its iframe element's gets one, and VS Code's webview iframe declares none while Emerald's root declared dark. The root keeps the host's theme scheme (as the MCP Apps SDK's applyDocumentTheme does), except inside VS Code's webview (html:root:has(> body[data-vscode-theme-kind]), marked before scripts run): there color-scheme normal, with the theme on body. Root 'light dark' rejected: Chromium's in-frame prefers-color-scheme follows the system, not the element (measured). Guard (D-445) kept where measurable: a host surface failing 4.5:1 sets data-own-surface and Emerald paints its own. With no host surface the frame assumes the named theme's usual surface (Emerald's own background); hostStyle.test.ts proves text and verdicts clear 4.5:1 there and on the stand-in's VS Code surfaces. The frame logs what the host handed (handedLine: theme, every style variable name, resolved colours of the ones it reads, fonts, count of --vscode-* on the page), to notifications/message (VS Code: Output > MCP: emerald, [App UI]) and to console.info [emerald-frame] (Developer: Open Webview Developer Tools). Stand-in: a vscode host leaves its iframe unschemed and marks the page as VS Code does; --no-styles / the 'theme only' menu entry sends the theme alone. Amends D-445.
supersedes: D-445
state: live

### D-449
date: 2026-10-09
milestone: M43
scope: engine
title: The installers say AGPL and carry every bundled package's licence: a build writes THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt beside what it built, the app's id is `com.kfireliyahu.emerald`, and SheetJS comes vendored from its own CDN at 0.20.3, because npm's 0.18.5 is unpatched
question: Before the repository is public, what would a stranger's download get wrong that no test sees: the licence it states, the notices its bundled code requires, the app id it is known by, and a known-vulnerable parser of untrusted files?
answer: electron-builder.yml's copyright names Kfir Raphael Eliyahu and AGPL-3.0-only (it said MIT). scripts/third-party-notices.mjs writes THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt from `npm ls --omit=dev` plus vendored files (heb.traineddata, Apache-2.0) into dist/, dist-plugin/ and dist-demo/ at the end of build, build:plugin and build:demo, and fails the build when a package declares no licence; it lists the whole production tree, erring towards too many. appId is com.kfireliyahu.emerald, with app.setAppUserModelId in electron/main.ts matching; nothing had shipped, and an install under the old id is a separate app to Windows (uninstall it first). xlsx is 0.20.3, vendored at vendor/xlsx-0.20.3.tgz and named by path, because npm stopped at 0.18.5 (CVE-2023-30533, CVE-2024-22363), the fixes are only on SheetJS's CDN, and npm 12 refuses remote tarball dependencies by default (EALLOWREMOTE); upgrading is replacing the file. Left for M43 U7: the About with licence and source, the not-advice line, CI-built checksummed releases, PRIVACY.md.
state: amended

### D-450
date: 2026-10-09
milestone: M43
scope: engine
title: Before the repository is public: another product is described by its category, never named, except a host Emerald runs inside, a runtime it speaks to and the maintainer's own story; outside code is not taken for now — issues and discussion are welcome, and a contributor agreement comes first if code ever is; there is no code of conduct; and security reports go to the address LICENSING.md gives
question: Before the repository is public, how are other products named in what a stranger reads, does the project take outside code, does it need a code of conduct, and where do security reports go?
answer: Ruled 2026-10-08 (all as recommended), carried out in M43 U4. Names: a competitor is described by its category (a spreadsheet; an all-in-one notes-and-database workspace; a notes app over plain Markdown files; typed, relational spreadsheet-databases; a bank-connected budgeting subscription; a licensed account aggregator; a design tool; a sync folder; another app or service that shares the name) — a rule row. Named still: hosts Emerald runs inside (Claude, Claude Code, ChatGPT, VS Code, Goose), runtimes and protocols it speaks to (Ollama, LM Studio, llama.cpp, OpenAI-compatible) and a tool whose behaviour the code works around (a spreadsheet program's CSV encoding); and the maintainer's own story in the maintainer's voice (the notes-app prototype, the Notion trial, the pitch's Excel). docs/archive/ and the decision rows are dated record and keep their names. Mockups draw made-up institutions (Everyday Bank, Second Bank); the ledger pack's seeded institutions stay because the readers match them. Contributions: no outside code for now, issues and discussion welcome, a CLA before code is ever taken — the commercial licence (D-430) needs the maintainer to hold every line's copyright, and a DCO would foreclose it. No code of conduct while there are no outside contributions. SECURITY.md sends reports to me@kfireliyahu.com, the address LICENSING.md gives. The history rewrite stays the maintainer's act (D-430's gate on M33 U4).
state: live

### D-451
date: 2026-10-09
milestone: M43
scope: engine
title: The chosen names land everywhere at once: a paycheck is never a payslip or a slip on a screen or in a tool, to categorize is never to file, a bill's form says bill, a link is made under *Links to*, a computed column under *Computed*, the table view is *Table*, a pack's list is *Ready to start*, the rail says *Assistants*, a backup is *Back up now*, a goal is a goal, a reader is never a mapping, and a source read is a file — a document is only the .emd
question: M43 U2 gathered every term a person or a model meets and the maintainer marked the 31 flagged ones (rename_to or keep). How do the renames land so the app, the tools, the tours and the notes never disagree, and which names of tools change?
answer: 17 renamed, 14 kept, in one unit (M43 U3). Renamed on every screen, in tool text, the tours and the notes describing screens: payslip/slip → paycheck; file (to categorize) → categorize ('Categorized this sitting', the tour's caption); the bill form says New/Edit/Add bill; aria 'Card bills' → 'Card payments'; ledger text where a debt 'claims' charges says 'covers' (claim is the engine's alone); the column form's 'Works out' → 'Computed'; a link's 'Points at' → 'Links to' (the far entry keeps 'Links here'); the view picker's 'Entries' and arrange_views' 'sheet' → 'Table' (a spreadsheet's tab stays Sheet; `view: sheet` is still accepted by arrange_views and format_sheet, both list `table`); 'Ready' → 'Ready to start'; the rail's 'MCP' → 'Assistants'; 'Keep a copy as it is now' → 'Back up now'; saving/savings goal → goal; tool text's 'mapping' → 'reader'; a source read onto a format is a file ('Read a file'), a document is only the .emd; the import role label 'balance' → 'running balance'. Monthly debt obligation → 'Paid each month' and Snapshot → 'Reading' (what the screens already said). Kept: Balance, Decision number, Declaration, Declared table prefix, Entry, Row, Format, Reading, Rollup, Rule, Rule set, Unchecked, Unit, What looks wrong. One tool renamed: create_saving_goals → create_goals, no alias (only the demo script called it). savings, keep_copy, format_sheet, the fixed_expense family and import_statement's save_mapping_as keep their names. The ledger pack's format titles follow (Goal, Bill, Bill in a plan, Card payment, Reader (spreadsheet), Reader (PDF or text)) for documents that keep it from now on; a document that already keeps it holds its own titles. Rejected: renaming every tool carrying an old word; rewriting 'payslip' in the parked ledger notes, where it names the printed page.
state: live

### D-452
date: 2026-10-09
milestone: M43
scope: engine
title: What a downloader is owed: Help opens the license and the third-party notices from the app's own files and an About that names the version, AGPL-3.0-only with no warranty, the source and the not-advice line; Source Code hands the repository — one address, read from package.json — to the system browser on a click; a tag builds the unsigned installers in CI with SHA-256 checksums and attestations; the privacy policy is PRIVACY.md
question: What does a person who downloads an unsigned installer get from the app and the release: the licence, the source of exactly that build, a way to check where the file came from, the not-advice line and a privacy policy at a stable address — without the page reaching the network?
answer: electron/about.ts fills app.setAboutPanelOptions (macOS app menu About; Help → About Emerald on Windows/Linux): version, 'Free software under the GNU AGPL-3.0-only, with no warranty', the not-advice line, the repository and /tree/v<version>. Help adds License and Third-Party Notices, plain windows over dist/LICENSE.txt and dist/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt served by app:// (.txt served as text; scripts/third-party-notices.mjs copies LICENSE in). Text windows get no commands, no unsaved-work question, do not reset the page's listening flag, and close with the document's window. Help → Source Code calls shell.openExternal(SOURCE_URL) — the one outward address, a constant from package.json's repository, https only; network.test.ts fails on any other openExternal. D-124 unchanged: the page still reaches nothing. .github/workflows/release.yml: a v* tag equal to package.json's version builds on windows-latest and macos-latest (--publish never, bash shell), attests each installer, then a Linux job writes SHA256SUMS, attests it and publishes the release with notes linking the tagged source; workflow_dispatch is a rehearsal with no release. The Mac build makes arm64 and x64 dmgs (Emerald-<version>-<arch>.dmg). README drafts (for M43 U1): not-advice line, macOS Privacy & Security → Open Anyway, how to check a download, source-and-licence line; the privacy policy moved unreworded to PRIVACY.md. SECURITY.md: model-read document text is untrusted input (prompt injection), in scope where a tool does more than it says; GitHub private reporting named behind a comment until the maintainer enables it. The repository URL is the planned github.com/kfireliyahu/emerald-emd, not live yet.
state: amended

### D-453
date: 2026-10-09
milestone: M43
scope: engine
title: A fresh clone builds the same on every machine: every text file checks out with LF whatever core.autocrlf says, the notices are read from package-lock.json rather than from what npm ls prints, and the three dependencies with install scripts are declined in package.json, because nothing Emerald needs runs at install
question: What does a stranger's fresh clone need so that npm ci, the suite, every build and the tours pass with no step of theirs, on Windows as anywhere?
answer: (1) .gitattributes has `* text=auto eol=lf` (with `*.emd -text` after it): every text file checks out LF whatever core.autocrlf says, because tests, migrations and pack notes are read as bytes; no narrower list, since the next ?raw import would be the same trap. The rewrite-history runbook and preflight no longer ask for core.autocrlf=false. (2) scripts/third-party-notices.mjs reads the production tree from package-lock.json (entries not marked dev, an uninstalled optional package skipped), never from `npm ls` output, which redacts UUID-shaped path segments. (3) package.json `allowScripts` declines electron-winstaller, esbuild and tesseract.js with `false`: nothing Emerald needs runs at install (Electron 44 fetches its binary on first run). A new dependency with an install script gets an entry, true only if a build needs it.
supersedes: D-449
state: live

### D-454
date: 2026-10-09
milestone: M43
scope: engine
title: No known advisory ships: the MCP SDK goes to 1.32 and React Router to 7, because no 6.x release carries the fix; a navigation is drawn as a transition, so the tour runner waits for the routed view to say it drew the address; the dev-only advisories left are major upgrades of build tools that never ship
question: npm audit printed 8 production advisories on a fresh clone (the MCP SDK and its HTTP-transport chain, React Router). Upgrade, or record them as unreachable — and within React Router 6 or across to 7?
answer: Upgrade regardless of reachability (none was reached: stdio server, never an OAuth client, no HTTP transport imported; HashRouter with literal paths, no SSR). @modelcontextprotocol/sdk ^1.32.1; the transitive ones by npm audit fix within ranges; react-router-dom ^7.18.4 because no 6.x release is fixed. v7 types navigate() as void | Promise<void> (handlers say `void navigate(...)`) and renders a navigation as a transition, so the routed view in App.tsx carries data-route and the tour runner's routeDrawn waits until it names the address before looking up anchors or outlines. npm audit --omit=dev reports 0. Dev-only advisories left need majors (Vite 8, Vitest 5) or have no fixed release (electron-builder → @electron/get → global-agent → roarr → sprintf-js) and never ship; never `npm audit fix --force`.
state: live

### D-455
date: 2026-10-09
milestone: M45
scope: engine
title: The copy taken before a write goes to Emerald's own per-user folder, one folder per document, and the app lists them to restore; restoring is a person's, on the screen; a named copy stays beside the document
question: Where does the plugin's automatic copy before a mutating batch go, now that .bak files beside a document spam the person's folder, and how does a person find and restore one?
answer: Emerald's own local per-user folder, computed in plain Node by backupsRoot in electron/bridge.ts (both halves use it): Windows %LOCALAPPDATA%\Emerald\Backups, macOS ~/Library/Application Support/Emerald/Backups, Linux $XDG_STATE_HOME/emerald/backups else ~/.local/state/emerald/backups; never Electron's roaming userData. EMERALD_BACKUPS_DIR overrides it (tests set it per file in src/testing/setup.ts; make-demo-document passes a temp dir). One subfolder per document: <stem>-<FNV-1a of the resolved path, 8 hex>, the path compared case- and separator-insensitively on Windows. Copies are still <name>.emd.<UTC stamp>.bak, the last five (BACKUPS_KEPT) per document, now pruned by the stamp in the name (rollingCopiesPast); the tool's answer names the full path. In the desktop shell File > 'Earlier copies of this document…' (and the palette, shell only) lists them newest first with time and size and Restore, through the bridge (earlierCopies, restoreEarlierCopy): restore reads the chosen copy, copies the current file into the same folder, writes the copy over the document atomically, notes it as the shell's own write, and the page reloads; refused while the window has unsaved work. The page names a copy by its file name, never a path, and main refuses any other. Restore is screen-only: no tool overwrites a document from a copy unasked; the exemption is FILE_ACTIONS in src/parity.test.ts, and the server's instructions tell the model to name the copy and send the user to the app. Named copies (D-399) stay beside the document. No migration of .bak files already beside documents; .gitignore's *.emd.*.bak line stays.
supersedes: D-098
state: live

### D-456
date: 2026-10-09
milestone: M45
scope: engine
title: The rulings on the survey: the old-document leftovers go, the twins that read differently become one reader each in @/core/ or the engine, a popover is dismissed one way, and a basis point prints one way
question: What becomes of the twelve survey findings M45 U1 left to the maintainer: old-document handling, duplicated parsers that read the same input differently, six popover dismissals, two percent formatters and the fixed-expense editor's labels?
answer: Kept: retire() (S-027) and both today() (S-064). Gone: column_role's HOLDING and TRADE, the legacy goal and fund links, the pre-024 transfer note, scripts/local-host*.mjs. One reader each: stringListOf (src/domain/format/stringList.ts, strict, damaged input refused); parseNumericDate and inferNumericDateOrder (src/core/time.ts: space separator, 26 is 2026; four-digit first is ymd, a date counts only when it reads one way, ties are dmy — the engine's old weighting gave the same answers); parseFigureText(text, strip) (src/core/text.ts; importer strips CURRENCY_NOISE, engine [₪$€£%]). useDismiss (src/ui/components/useDismiss.ts): pointerdown outside by composedPath; Escape from outside caught in capture on document and stopped, from inside the popover's own handlers first, then the returned onKeyDown closes it and stops it. basisPointsText (src/core/percent.ts): up to 2 decimals, trailing zeros trimmed, under formatPercent (digits is now a maximum), the CSV and the inputs; the engine's is formatStoredPercent. FixedExpenseEditor uses the shared Field.
state: live

### D-457
date: 2026-10-09
milestone: M45
scope: engine
title: Earlier copies clean up after themselves: a gone document's folder goes 30 days after its last copy, the Windows uninstaller takes the folder, and Help › About shows it
question: D-455 capped each document's folder of earlier copies at five but nothing removed a folder: how do the copies of renamed, moved or deleted documents, and the folder itself on uninstall, stop piling up?
answer: Each per-document folder records its document's absolute path in document-path.txt (written when made, and on the next copy into one without). Every copy either half takes (plugin before a batch, shell before a restore) runs sweepBackupFolders in electron/bridge.ts: a sibling folder goes only when its record hashes back to the folder's name, nothing is at that path while its drive answers, and its newest copy (by the moment in its name) is older than ORPHANED_COPIES_DAYS = 30. A folder with no record goes only when a known path (the shell's recents) names it and is gone the same way. Real directories directly in the root only, never a link; anything unreadable is left; the sweep never throws or fails the write. The Windows uninstaller (build/installer.nsh, customUnInstall) removes %LOCALAPPDATA%\Emerald\Backups and then Emerald if empty, never on an update; macOS has no uninstaller, the sweep bounds it there. Help › About is now the page's dialog (APP_CHANNELS, EmeraldApp on the bridge): what the native panel said plus the folder's path, size, document count and Open folder; shell only, and in the palette as About Emerald (macOS's app menu keeps the native panel).
state: live

### D-458
date: 2026-10-10
milestone: M46
scope: engine
title: The command palette has a button beside Back and Forward on every screen, its tooltip teaching Ctrl+K (⌘K on a Mac); every item in the shell's menu is a palette command, Help its own group, and a test fails when one is not; the menu bar stays hidden behind Alt
question: How does a person find the command palette, and the shell menu's items hidden behind Alt (About, Earlier copies), without knowing a key?
answer: CommandPaletteButton in the title bar right after Back/Forward (search glyph, aria-label Commands, title 'Commands (Ctrl+K)' or '(⌘K)' on a Mac via commandPaletteKey), on every route in shell, web and demo. Every leaf of buildMenuTemplate maps to a palette command id or a written reason in src/ui/menuParity.test.ts (Edit roles, zoom/fullscreen/devtools keys, quit/close, macOS system menus are the reasons); stale names fail too. New palette rows: License, Third-party notices, Source code (EmeraldApp.openText/openSource, optional, IPC; page never names an address) and Clear recent documents (EmeraldDocuments.clearRecents, optional). Help is its own palette group (help:guide, help:about, help:license, help:notices, help:source). autoHideMenuBar stays: native fallback, Alt still shows it, nothing is behind Alt only. Clicks to About/Earlier copies: Alt+menu+item (3, only if you know Alt) became button, two letters, Enter.
state: live

### D-459
date: 2026-10-12
milestone: M33
scope: engine
title: Emerald's page is the demo folder's root and the tour frame is `tour.html` beside it; the page is laid out as the app's roadmap and its words are the approved mockup's, the maintainer's to change before rollout; it may make the two requests the app never makes — a form post and a visit count — each only once PRIVACY.md says who processes it and the maintainer has picked the service, so today it offers the maintainer's address and counts nothing; D-124 is unchanged
question: Where does Emerald's page live in the build, what may it send that the app never may, and whose are its words?
answer: The page (demo-page.html, src/demo/page/) is a third Vite entry in demo mode, written as dist-demo/index.html — the root a domain points at — and the frame moves beside it as tour.html, so the embed contract of D-418 becomes <demo URL>/tour.html?tour=…&autoplay=1 (the maintainer's site told by an inbox row); the frame reaches its app and documents by relative path, so nothing in it changes but its name. The page may post a form and load a counter — D-124 is the app's rule and unchanged — but each only once PRIVACY.md says who processes it, how long it is kept and how to have it deleted (D-452), and once the maintainer has picked the service: until then FORM_ACTION and COUNT_SCRIPT in page.ts are empty, no form is drawn (a form that posts nowhere is a lie), the foot offers the address LICENSING.md gives, nothing is counted, and PRIVACY.md says so. check:tours opens the page, waits for each frame near the view to post a step, and refuses any request outside the folder. A frame is loaded when within half a window of the view and let go when it leaves, since four apps share one process. The look is the app's roadmap — the timeline down the left in the surface plane, solid to the row under the eye and dashed after, that row's dot live and pulsing once, the dots the app's own .fc-tl-dot and .fc-dot — and the words are the approved mockup's (M33 U8a), the maintainer's to change; M33 U9 carries the reminder to re-read them before rollout.
state: live

### D-460
date: 2026-10-13
milestone: M47
scope: engine
title: A week of real use on three documents is measured, not remembered, and what it found is M47: the inbox reaches a session by a hook rather than a poll, the first call carries the columns, a unit lands in one call, a link may cross documents, a format says when it last changed, the document is searched whole, and three small faults
question: notes.emd, planner.emd and site.emd were used for a week (2026-10-06 to 10-13), and the question was what Emerald actually did for the work, what it cost, and what was missing. Is the answer a feeling or a measurement, and what does it change?
answer: Measured from the 17 session transcripts, not from memory: 2,564 tool calls, 465 of them Emerald's, 349 rows kept, 42 inbox rows crossing four documents with 39 applied. The value was real and specific: the inbox replaced the clipboard (one of its rows says three of six clipboard hand-offs were lost in the first week), start_here answered 'what's next' cold six times, driver sessions claimed units by ref, review entries drove the maintainer's test passes. The cost was specific too, about 15% of those sessions in avoidable turns: 302 of 346 writes carried one row, the first call was never enough (formats followed start_here 48 times, once per format about to be written), one session spent 27 calls polling an inbox that a watcher nobody had started would have pushed, 32 dry-run duplicates, 28 re-reads for renewed ids. So M47 is planned from the evidence, one unit per measured gap, ordered after M33 and before M42 because M42's plugin ships U1's hooks: U1 hooks (SessionStart prints start_here, UserPromptSubmit prints new inbox rows, through server subcommands), U2 start_here carries every format's compact declaration so the first call says what a row looks like (2026-10-13: the solution lives in the document, not in a file beside it; a generated CLAUDE.md section was rejected), U3 land_units and claim_units over the development pack, U4 a link to a row in another document by path and ref, U5 a last-changed mark per format, U6 search_document and a denylist scan, U7 a units roadmap, store_document_as, and a retried rename while the app holds the file. Not planned: an identity pack (asset, login, dns_record recur across the three documents; a candidate for the pack catalogue question), and the auto-mode classifier blocking a row write to a scratchpad copy, which is Claude Code's, not ours. Rejected: a before/after token number, because the pre-Emerald sessions on the same project were different work and the comparison would be invented.
state: live

### D-461
date: 2026-10-13
milestone: M47
scope: engine
title: A column may name a row in another document — `elsewhere`, the seventeenth type: the document's path and the row's name, read there when the file can be read and unresolved, never refused, when it cannot
question: D-241 says a text document writes a link as the name of the row it points at. Every reference between the three documents of D-460's week was prose, and a hand-off had to say 'M1 U10 can be marked built'. What does a link across documents write, and what happens when the other file cannot be read?
answer: A new column type, elsewhere (language 23, schema 61 — migration 061 only widens format_column's type CHECK), stored as TEXT: the other document's path (absolute, or relative to the holding document's folder) and the row's name, 'D:/projects/other/other.emd S1 U5', CHECKed by shape only (a path to the first .emd, then a name; backslashes become slashes). By name, never by id: a text document renews ids (D-245); 'Stable ids' stays open. No target and no unit: the row is found by name across every format of the other document (labelColumn), and two rows sharing the name is unresolved, naming both formats. A write never refuses a value for being out of reach; the plugin reads it (src/plugin/elsewhere.ts, each document once per call, read-only, one hop) and prints the row's format, name and first enum column — format_rows '… → unit S1 U5 · built', start_here 'other.emd: unit S1 U5 · built' — or 'unresolved — no file at …', '… has no row called …', '… could not be read: <first sentence>'. The page cannot read a file a person did not pick (D-128), so the sheet draws the cell as the row's name and its document's file name: a link that opens that document in the shell, plain text in a browser. The development pack's inbox gains about, such a column, listed by its start view; keep_packs now raises a held format to the pack's language when a grown column needs it, and carries the pack's view settings for grown columns only. Rejected: a link whose value may be a path (every id check, join and fold would need a branch); the format in the value; resolving at write time; an id beside the name. Cost: a document at schema 60 is refused by the rebuilt plugin until the app opens it once.
state: live

### D-462
date: 2026-10-13
milestone: M47
scope: engine
title: A format says when it last changed: one mark per format, `format.updated_at`, moved by triggers on every write to its rows and kept by a text document as the day in `changed.md` — no migration, no per-row timestamp
question: site.emd's copy_slot and asset went stale unread while dns_record was used, and nothing said which was which: a document knew a format's row count, never when anybody last touched it. Where does that mark live so text and sqlite documents both carry it?
answer: format.updated_at (there since schema 32, read by nothing) is now when the format last changed: a row of it added, changed or removed (its table or a member table), or its declaration saved. Three AFTER triggers per table, <table>__changed__insert|update|delete, set it to SQLite's clock, so every write moves it including the ledger's raw SQL; keepChangeMarks makes missing ones after every adopt and reconcile and on every plugin open. A text document keeps the day in a changed.md part (format: YYYY-MM-DD, name order), restored last on read; a format with no line is unmarked ('not marked yet'), never dated by the read. formats prints it beside the row count, start_here names the 3 formats beyond the packs' touched longest ago, the Formats screen shows it per card. No migration. Rejected: a per-row timestamp, session transcripts, a new table (migration), caller-set marks, TEMP triggers (sql.js export reopens the database).
state: live

### D-463
date: 2026-10-13
milestone: M47
scope: engine
title: The whole document is searched in one call, and a scan before it is shared is the same call with a denylist: every row and column by format, the rest of the file counted, the file's bytes read for a deleted value
question: Committing planner.emd took thirteen shell commands dumping the SQLite file to find an old address, a private note and an old account name, because format_rows' search is one format a call; and the dump missed the deleted text still in the file's free pages. What is the one call, and what is the moment before a document leaves the machine?
answer: search_document (src/plugin/tools/searchDocument.ts) over searchDocument (src/data/repositories/documentSearchRepository.ts), the repository M37's palette search is to share (D-227). With terms: every row of every declared format, each column searched as format_rows searches it (as shown, folded), a hit named by format, row id and name, and column, with the text around it; a link is not a hit (the row it names is). Every other table in the file (format notes, column choices, file names read, settings, orphan tables) is counted by table and column, never shown. With denylist (a git filter-repo --replace-text file, the one src/privacy.test.ts reads; one parser, denylistTerms in src/domain/format/searchTerms.ts): case kept, regex as regex, hits only, a term named by its line, no value shown, a row whose name is a hit not named, and the file's own bytes read so a term in the bytes and in no live value is reported as a deleted value still in a SQLite-bytes file. src/privacy.test.ts scans every tracked .emd through it. Rejected: format_rows without a format (a general query, D-095); showing a scan's values; folding case in a scan; a separate scan tool; vacuuming on save.
state: live

### D-464
date: 2026-10-13
milestone: M47
scope: engine
title: A roadmap asked of rows that fold under one is drawn as that one, the storage choice is a tool, and a rename a held file refuses is tried again for a second
question: Three faults from the week: format_sheet unit view:roadmap refused when asked for M9's units on a roadmap; a conversation could say a document was SQLite but not make it text (a screen with no tool, D-227); and two writes to notes.emd failed with EPERM renaming the scratch file over it while the app had it open.
answer: Three faults, one unit. (1) A format with no roadmap of its own is drawn in the one its rows fold under (roadmapAbove in formatLinkViewRepository.ts): format_sheet unit view:roadmap match:{milestone:M9} draws the milestone roadmap at M9, the text naming each unit under it; only the folding link carries over (matched as the milestone's naming column), any other filter is refused with the call that draws one lane; no new drawing. (2) store_document_as (tools/storeAs.ts, storeDocumentAs in write.ts): as text|sqlite, dry run until commit, backed up first, under the writers' lock, written through exportEncoded so it refuses exactly where the app's Save as text does (a ledger; M25 U1 #2 stays open); parity.test.ts holds it to convertTo as a file action. (3) writeAtomically (electron/bridge.ts) tries a rename refused with EPERM/EACCES/EBUSY six times 200 ms apart, then refuses saying the app has the file and the document is as it was; the shell's save and every plugin write share it. Rejected: a units roadmap of its own, a refusal only, a storage argument on every write, retrying in the plugin alone, waiting longer.
state: live

