title: A folder of notes is read with its subfolders on `recursive: true`, each note carrying its `folder`; a body link to a file that is not a note is no link, counted and never named; and a .json file of rows is a kind of file, read as a .csv is
question: How does read_onto_format take notes in subfolders, embedded images, and rows a model already holds as JSON?
answer: `recursive: true` walks every non-hidden folder under the one read; `file` is then the note's path under it and a `folder` column (only when a note is nested) holds its folder path, mapped like any column. A body link whose target has a non-.md extension (with a letter, up to 8 chars) is left out of `body links` unless a note read is called that, and counted once in the head line. A .json file — an array of objects, or an object whose keys hold arrays of objects (each a sheet) — becomes the ReadTable a .csv does (jsonRows.ts), in the plugin and on the Read screen. declare/extend/connect_formats all say 'A `count` needs no `works_out`'; record/update_format_rows show a link found by one unique column.
