title: A drawing tool's answer opens with whether anything was drawn, from what the client declared at initialize and never its name; a host may draw only the servers it runs itself, as the Claude desktop app's Code tab does
question: Why did the Code tab draw nothing for 'show me what's ready', and how does a session know whether a view was drawn?
answer: The Code tab draws MCP Apps only from a server the desktop app runs itself (claude_desktop_config.json: emerald-ledger), never from one a project's .mcp.json starts (emerald): 0 of 28 emerald calls mounted a frame 2026-10-08..18, 21 of 24 emerald-ledger calls did (claude.ai-web.log '[MCP Apps] oncalltool handler replaced'). Not U7, not the rename. show_format and start_here now open with drawnLine: a client advertising io.modelcontextprotocol/ui should show it but the server cannot see whether it did; one that did not -> 'Nothing was drawn, as far as this server can tell', and call the same tool on a server the host runs itself if the session has one. The instructions say never to call a view drawn or 'above' unless the line says so and the person sees it. The probe of a drawn frame is the app's claude.ai-web.log, never the CLI's MCP log.
state: live

### D-490
date: 2026-10-18
milestone: M51
scope: development
title: A closing message ends with the host's next-step box when it offers one, and a driver hands units to the host's sessions when it offers them
question: How does a session end, and how does run-milestone hand a unit over, when the host draws a next-step box or starts sessions of its own?
answer: If the host offers a next-step box (desktop app: mcp__ccd_turn__wrap_up), the closing pass ends with it: recap = what landed and what is not verified; first button 'Start <next ready unit>' from start_here, sending its ref and 'claim it first'; ask only for what is the maintainer's. A child run by a driver reports instead. No box: prose as before. If the host offers sessions (desktop app: start_session), run-milestone gives each unit a fresh session: sequential ones share the checkout (use_worktree false) and land themselves; units dispatch_plan puts under At once may run in their own worktrees (own npm install, never a node_modules junction), with driver-assigned decision numbers, merged with git merge and landed by one land_units inbox. The driver verifies git log, status, docs:check and the row, never the 400-character note. No sessions: the Agent tool as before. Supersedes only D-287's subagent-only, sequential-only hand-over; its stops for the maintainer stand.
supersedes: D-287
state: live

### D-491
date: 2026-10-18
milestone: M51
scope: engine
title: Keeping a pack again brings its formats' notes and its views' settings to this build's wherever the document still holds a value an earlier build of the pack declared there; any other value is the person's, kept and named in the dry run; the pack lists what every build declared (past.ts)
question: When a pack the document already keeps changes a format's notes or a view's settings, how does keeping it again bring them along without overwriting what a person changed in their own document?
answer: planKeep compares each held pack format's notes (by fingerprint) and each view setting (by `format view column`) with the pack's PackDescription.past, which lists every value every build declared, the current build's included. If the document still holds a past value, keepPack writes this build's (notes through adoptFormat, settings through writeViewSettings, a dropped line taken off). Any other value is the person's: kept, and both halves name it (keptParts). A place the pack never had a line for counts absence as the pack's, so new lines arrive; a place the pack always had a line for, now empty, was cleared by a person and stays clear. keep_packs and the Packs screen say the same phrases (planParts). When views.md or a format's notes change, pack.test.ts fails and prints the line to add to src/data/packs/development/past.ts — the one edit left, in code, never in a document. No migration, and nothing new recorded in the document.
state: live

