### D-495
date: 2026-10-18
milestone: M42
scope: product
title: The repository goes public before the showcase, as one fresh commit with its history kept privately; what a visitor or a new session reads first says what holds, not who decided it; `docs/archive/` leaves the repository; and M33 U4 then deploys with GitHub Pages and a getemerald.dev record at Cloudflare
question: The release sequence of 2026-10-15 put the showcase first, deployed and answering at getemerald.dev while the repository was still private, and only then the repository, npm, the registry and the plugin on one day. GitHub Pages serves a private repository only on a paid plan, so M33 U4 was to move to Cloudflare Pages or a direct upload. Once going public was approved (2026-10-16), the order was decided again, and an audit before publishing (2026-10-18) found that the repository read as one person's notebook: real document names and paths of other projects, the publisher's employment, personal finances, and "the author" narrating 307 of 1,335 rows and about 1,500 lines.
answer: "- **The repository goes public first, and the showcase deploys after it.** M33 U4 deploys with the GitHub Pages workflow the repository already holds, free once it is public, and getemerald.dev answers through one DNS record at Cloudflare, where the domain lives. No Cloudflare Pages, no direct upload. The frame's contract (D-418), the no-request rule and the poster hand-off are unchanged. Nothing in the project competes with the publisher's employer or relies on its code or material, and nothing new may.\n- **Published as one commit.** A single orphan commit of the cleaned tree goes to a fresh, empty repository, with a message saying it is the first public release and that the history is kept privately. The full history stays in a private bare mirror. A unit's `commit` names a commit of that private history, and the format's notes say so.\n- **Personal content goes everywhere.** No employer, no real document or project names of the publisher's own (the stand-ins of M47: notes.emd, planner.emd, homepage/site.emd), no personal finances or figures, no machine paths but this repository's own.\n- **What a visitor or a new session reads first says what holds.** CLAUDE.md, the skills, README, PRIVACY and SECURITY, `docs/*.md`, code comments, and the rows still acted on (planned units, open milestones, live rules and terms, open questions and review entries): a statement with its D-ref or date, \"the maintainer\" where a role is needed, a quote turned into the decision it carried.\n- **`docs/archive/` leaves the repository.** It is ignored and kept in the maintainer's checkout; `scripts/archive-decisions.mjs` still moves a closed milestone's entries there, and the index and every link name the plain D-ref. `npm run docs:check` fails on a link into it.\n- **The inbox is emptied.** Its rows were hand-offs from the publisher's own projects; what each carried already landed as a unit or a decision. The two of 2026-10-15 and 2026-10-18 became this.\n- **Past rows and entries were reworded for voice only, on 2026-10-18.** Decision rows, built units, closed milestones, findings and the log's open entries say who said, felt or approved something differently, and nothing about what was decided, built, rejected or measured; refs, dates and figures are as they were. This is the one exception to \"a past entry is never edited\", and the wording before it is in the private history.\n- **Amends D-450**, whose consequence kept `docs/archive/` and the rows \"dated record, never edited\", and **supersedes the order** of the 2026-10-15 release sequence. A private copy of the document before this was made outside the repository.\n\n- Rejected: leaving the history. Scrubbing the current files leaves every earlier version readable in history, including the commits that carried the employment rows.\n- Rejected: a second `filter-repo` rewrite. It swaps tokens, so the names would go and the voice in 745 commits would stay, and every hash would change again anyway.\n- Rejected: flipping the existing private repository public. A known hash can still reach a commit that a force-push left behind."
state: live

### D-496
date: 2026-10-18
milestone: M52
scope: engine
title: A tour plays only while its frame is seen, and the frame decides it: the player holds the first step and pauses every hold while an `IntersectionObserver` on the frame's own document says less than a third of it is in view or the tab is hidden, so `?autoplay=1` means play when seen on any page that embeds it
question: Emerald's page loaded each tour within half a window of the view with `?autoplay=1`, and a loaded frame played at once, so a visitor scrolling down found a tour at a late step; a site embedding a frame below its fold had the same problem. Should the page hold `autoplay` back or post the frame a start, or should the frame itself know whether it is seen?
answer: The frame decides. `src/demo/tour/seen.ts` watches the frame's root with a rootless `IntersectionObserver` (it measures against the top page's viewport even across origins) and counts it seen at 35% in view with its tab showing. `TourPlayer.setSeen` gates every row before it moves and counts every hold only while seen, keeping what was left, and restarts the caption's reading from when it was seen: a seen frame starts at step one, a frame that leaves stops where it is, one that comes back goes on. It starts nothing — a frame waiting for Watch or taken over stays as left — and `?check` plays unwatched. D-418's contract keeps its shape: `?autoplay=1` now means play when seen, and no `autoplay` still holds the poster. The page still loads and lets go of frames by distance, so a frame scrolled more than half a window away starts again at step one. No `IntersectionObserver`: the frame counts as seen.
state: live

### D-497
date: 2026-10-18
milestone: M42
scope: engine
title: The MCP server is published as one npm package, `emerald-mcp`, that is `dist-plugin/` as built: every package bundled and no dependencies, a manifest generated from the root one, and a bin each for the server (full or `--engine-only`), the hooks (`emerald-hooks`) and the status line (`emerald-status-line`)
question: How is the MCP server shipped on npm, and how does the Claude plugin reach its hooks and status line?
answer: One package, emerald-mcp, which is dist-plugin/ as `npm run build:plugin` leaves it: scripts/npm-package.mjs writes its package.json (version, licence, author, repository from the root manifest; engines node >=24; no dependencies, since every package is bundled, sql.js as its browser build) and README. Bins: emerald-mcp (server.mjs; --engine-only by flag, watch as a subcommand), emerald-hooks (hooks.mjs: start-here, rows, status), emerald-status-line (status-line.mjs). A plugin calls `npx -y --prefer-offline emerald-mcp@<v> --engine-only` and `npx -y --prefer-offline -p emerald-mcp@<v> emerald-hooks …`. An npx call costs ~2–2.7 s of npm start-up on Windows against ~0.35 s for node, on every prompt for the inbox hook — M42 U4 decides how its hooks pay it. Publishing is the maintainer's: `npm publish --access public` in dist-plugin/.
state: live

### D-498
date: 2026-10-18
milestone: M42
scope: plugin
title: The Claude plugin is `hosts/claude/`, listed by a marketplace at the public repository's root and built by nothing: it pins the published `emerald-mcp` at the manifest's `version`, runs the server through `npx --prefer-offline`, and runs the hooks through `emerald.mjs`, a hand-written launcher that keeps a copy of the package in `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA}` and starts it with plain `node`; no `.mcpb`, because the directory no longer takes one
question: How does Emerald install in Claude in one step, how do its hooks avoid npx's two seconds before every prompt, and is a desktop extension built too?
answer: A plugin at hosts/claude/, listed by .claude-plugin/marketplace.json at the repo root: `/plugin marketplace add kfireliyahu/emerald-emd`, `/plugin install emerald@emerald-emd`. Server: `npx -y --prefer-offline emerald-mcp@<version> --engine-only` (once a session; connects on Windows as written). Hooks: `node "${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/emerald.mjs" emerald-hooks …`; the launcher reads the version from plugin.json, keeps emerald-mcp at it in ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_DATA}/node_modules (installed by npm --prefer-offline at the first start-here after install/update, ~10 s once), and imports the bin in-process: ~0.35 s per prompt. The per-prompt rows never installs; it is silent until the copy exists. Version written once: plugin.json version = the server pin = root package.json version (hosts/claude/plugin.test.ts); release is bump, build, publish, then push, since the marketplace serves main. Status line stays the person's setting, at ~/.claude/plugins/data/emerald-emerald-emd/node_modules/emerald-mcp/status-line.mjs. No .mcpb: Anthropic's publish page (2026-10-18) says the directory no longer accepts desktop extensions.
