### D-501
date: 2026-10-18
milestone: M51
scope: engine
title: A unit left `in_progress` by a session that stopped is taken over with `claim_units take_over:<its taker>`, refused unless that taker holds it, with `started` reset and the taker replaced kept on `taken_over_from`; `start_here` lists a unit `in_progress` for more than a day as stalled; `land_units` refuses a landing with no `commit` unless it says `same_commit: true`, and keeps the builder's `report` on the unit
question: How does a driver take over a unit a stopped session left in_progress, see such units at all, make every landing name its commit, and read a child's whole report?
answer: claim_units take_over:<taker> (refused unless that taker holds each unit; started reset; the replaced taker appended to taken_over_from). A ready view's `stalls <days>` on a date lists rows being worked and taken longer ago, or undated, under Stalled in start_here; the unit pack says `started: stalls 1`. land_units refuses a landing without `commit` unless `same_commit: true`. land_units takes `report` (≤ 4,000 chars) onto the unit's `report` column; read it with format_rows unit match:{ref} columns:[report]. start_here's column list ends with a write's shape read off the tools' schemas.
state: live

### D-502
date: 2026-10-18
milestone: M42
scope: engine
title: The desktop app makes no network call unless the person turned updates on, or pressed Check for Updates Now — then one request, to `api.github.com/repos/kfireliyahu/emerald-emd/releases/latest`, from a session of its own that the page never runs in, open only while that check asks; the setting is off by default and the app's, a newer version is a quiet notice in the window whose Download opens the release page, and M42 U9's download will reach `github.com/kfireliyahu/emerald-emd/releases/download/` and the asset hosts it redirects to (`release-assets.githubusercontent.com`, `objects.githubusercontent.com`) only on the same terms; amends D-124 and D-452
question: D-124 said nothing inside the app reaches the network, and the shell blocked every request; once the app is public, how does a person who installed one version learn of the next — without the app phoning home unasked or interrupting anyone?
answer: Opt-in, off by default. The setting `automaticUpdates` lives in `settings.json` in the app's own folder (anything but true is off) and is switched in About. While on, the main process checks at start and then on an hourly tick once a day has passed; Help > Check for Updates Now (and its palette row) checks once with the setting off — consent for that one check. The check asks exactly `https://api.github.com/repos/kfireliyahu/emerald-emd/releases/latest` (one request for both platforms, no redirect host, exists today, skips drafts and pre-releases; `latest.yml` rejected until U9) from `session.fromPartition('emerald-updates')`, in memory, guarded by `isAllowedUpdateRequest(url, open)`: that URL only, only while a check is in flight; redirects are errors; User-Agent `Emerald/<version>`, Accept-Language `en`. The page's session (`isAllowedRequest`) is unchanged and refuses the address on or off. Only `tag_name` is read. A newer version is the lowest notice row — `Emerald <v> is out. You have <v>.` with Download (`shell.openExternal` of `<repository>/releases/latest`, a constant) and Later (hidden until the next start or the next day's check); Check now answers in the same row (Checking…, You have the latest, Could not check). No dialog, nothing takes focus, nothing downloaded or installed. U9 widens the predicate to the download hosts on the same terms.
supersedes: D-124
state: live

### D-503
date: 2026-10-18
milestone: M42
scope: engine
title: On Windows, with updates on, the app downloads the release its check found — `releases/download/v<version>/latest.yml` and `Emerald-Setup.exe` through electron-updater, in its own session, gated to that release while it downloads — checks the installer against the release's sha512, and installs only on Install and restart, after the window closes the ordinary way, reopening the document; macOS stays a notice; the installers lose the version from their names and the page links `releases/latest/download/`
question: With updates on, how does Windows get a newer version without anything intrusive or unasked, without a second check or a wider network rule than D-502 named, and how does the page always link the newest installers?
answer: M42 U8's check decides; electron-updater (generic feed at releases/download/v<version>/, autoDownload and autoInstallOnAppQuit off, differential off, no staging id) downloads that release's installer in the 'electron-updater' session, which isAllowedDownloadRequest holds to that release's latest.yml and Emerald-Setup.exe plus release-assets/objects.githubusercontent.com, only while it downloads, only with the setting on. The sha512 in latest.yml is the guarantee (the unsigned installer has no publisher to verify). Install and restart closes the window through the unsaved-work question (Cancel cancels), runs the installer /S --force-run on quit, and reopens the document from reopen.json. Installer names are version-free; the page links releases/latest/download/<name> once a release carries them (NAMED_INSTALLERS_RELEASED), the release page until then.
state: live

### D-504
date: 2026-10-18
milestone: M42
scope: plugin
title: The Claude plugin ships the development pack's run-milestone, closing-pass and review-phase as generic skills; its version is how an installed copy learns of an update, so a change to what it ships cuts <pin>+<n> (pin-plugin.mjs --plugin), the bare package version when the pins move, and plugin.test.ts fails when the shipped files' digest moves under an unchanged version; amends D-500
question: How do people who already installed the Claude plugin get a change to its skills or hooks when no npm package is published, and where do the development pack's workflows live for a project other than this one?
answer: Claude Code replaces an installed plugin's cached copy only when the version it computes (plugin.json's version first) differs from the one installed, compared as a string; auto-update is off by default for third-party marketplaces. So plugin.json's version is the plugin's release: the bare package version when pin-plugin.mjs moves the pins after a publish, and <pin>+<n> for a plugin-only release by `node scripts/pin-plugin.mjs --plugin`, which asks npm nothing. hosts/claude/release.ts digests the shipped files (manifest without version, hooks.json, skills, README; LF), hosts/claude-plugin-release.json records it with the version, and plugin.test.ts fails when they differ. The pins stay D-500's. Generic run-milestone, closing-pass and review-phase ship in hosts/claude/skills, finding the document as the hooks do and naming the project's own checks; this repo's copies stay in .claude/skills, and skills.test.ts fails when a generic one names this repo's decisions, units, docs, scripts or npm scripts. A driver lands worktree proposals with inbox:[{id, other_branch:true}]. The README says how to update: /plugin → Marketplaces → Enable auto-update, or `claude plugin marketplace update emerald-emd` then `claude plugin update emerald@emerald-emd`.
state: live

### D-505
date: 2026-10-19
milestone: M52
scope: engine
title: The Mac app is signed ad hoc after packing (codesign --force --deep --sign - from an afterPack hook, mac.identity: null kept), so a download is an app Apple could not verify, which Open Anyway opens, rather than a damaged one, which nothing does; the release workflow fails a Mac build whose signature does not verify; no certificate, account or notarization, so D-124's unsigned stands; amends D-124
question: Why does the Mac download say Emerald is damaged with no way past it, and how is it fixed without an Apple Developer account?
answer: mac.identity: null makes electron-builder skip signing entirely, not even ad hoc, and packing renames Electron's app and rewrites Info.plist, breaking the signature Electron shipped with; a quarantined bundle whose signature does not match is 'damaged', which has no Open Anyway, so the README's path never worked. electron-builder 26.17 has no ad-hoc setting (identity '-' is looked up in the keychain). scripts/sign-mac.mjs, the afterPack hook, runs codesign --force --deep --sign - on the packed .app on a macOS build (both arches, before the dmg), failing the build if codesign fails, nothing on Windows; no hardened runtime or entitlements. release.yml's Mac job runs codesign --verify --deep --strict on both apps and logs Signature=adhoc. The README says what a Mac shows (could not verify; Done, then Privacy & Security, Open Anyway) and, for 0.1.2 or earlier, xattr -cr /Applications/Emerald.app. D-124's unsigned (no certificate, account, notarization) holds; the Mac updater still needs a real identity, so a Mac stays a notice (D-502).
state: live

### D-506
date: 2026-10-19
milestone: M52
scope: plugin
title: The MCP server says what Emerald is before it says what a pack is: both instruction blocks open on a format for any kind of thing and on what it reads (spreadsheet, .json, a folder of Markdown notes read in place), development and ledger as optional packs; no model-facing text measures work against money; engine tools listed first in both modes; create_document keeps no pack unless asked, in both modes; reverses D-405's default
question: Why did a model given this server call Emerald 'built for money first' and argue a person out of reading their notes into it, and what does the server say instead?
answer: Our text taught it: formats said 'besides its finances', the full server opened on the ledger as a personal-finance application a new document keeps unless told otherwise, 'Not everything here is money' / 'know nothing about money' / 'where work that is not money begins' measured the engine against the ledger, 57 finance tools were listed first with document_info saying 'Call this first', create_document defaulted to the ledger on the full server (D-405), and the Markdown-folder reader sat past the 2,000 characters a host may keep. Now both heads open on any kind of thing, what it reads (csv, xlsx, json, a folder of Markdown notes such as an Obsidian vault, read where it is and never changed) and packs as optional, inside 2,000 chars with how-to-show intact; a paragraph tells the model to answer whether something belongs here about the thing; server.test.ts fails on each money-first phrase in both modes; engine tools listed first; create_document keeps no pack unless named; an audit's fixes (neutral examples, packs enum with purposes, record_units 'unit of measure', development tools say they need the pack, connections removed with extend_format, a what-else paragraph); READMEs lead with --engine-only; plugin 0.1.2+2. Reaches models only once emerald-mcp is published to npm (D-497). Rejected: flipping the binary's default (installed ledger configs would lose 57 tools).
state: live

### D-507
date: 2026-10-19
milestone: M52
scope: plugin
title: The server says first what a model gains from a document — it asks for the matching rows instead of re-reading files, a typed field filters exactly, a link is followed, every session sees the same rows — and checks are one benefit among them, never the reason; whether something belongs is answered by its shape, never by whether it has numbers; amends D-506
question: Why did a model given the D-506 server still call checking numbers Emerald's main strength and advise against moving notes without figures, and what does the server say first now?
answer: The formats section called a claim 'the reason to keep this here rather than in a spreadsheet', server.json said 'a model reads and checks', the plugin and npm descriptions said 'real types and real checks', and nothing said what a model gains. Both heads now open on it: a person's things are rows of formats, so a session asks instead of reading (matching rows in one call, not forty files re-read; exact field filters; links followed; a write checked; every session sees the same rows), inside the 2,000-char budget before how-to-show. A claim is one more thing a document does. Belonging is answered by shape (tasks, meetings, people, projects, decisions, bugs); free prose gains least and stays files, a body column or a link. Listings lead with asking. server.test.ts fails on the old phrases and if the head mentions checking before asking. Ships as emerald-mcp 0.1.5. Rejected: keeping checks first because the pitch says 'won't let you be wrong quietly' — the pitch is the maintainer's to change.
state: live

### D-508
date: 2026-10-20
milestone: M52
scope: plugin
title: Where the host starts a plugin's skills and hooks but not its MCP server (a cloud Cowork task, CLAUDE_CODE_SKIP_PLUGIN_MCP_SERVERS), the plugin tells the session how the person adds emerald-mcp to the Claude desktop app's own configuration: an emerald-setup skill holds the steps once, and a third SessionStart hook, plain Node run from the plugin folder, prints it only there; no new package
question: Why did a Cowork task with the emerald plugin have no Emerald tools, and how does the next one know what to tell the person?
answer: A cloud Cowork task loads a plugin's skills and hooks but skips its MCP server on purpose (its log: CLAUDE_CODE_SKIP_PLUGIN_MCP_SERVERS is set), and a server there couldn't open a .emd on the person's computer anyway. A server in the Claude desktop app's own claude_desktop_config.json reaches Cowork on that computer; the maintainer's Windows PC has run it that way all along. So the plugin carries an emerald-setup skill (find npx, the file on macOS and Windows, the entry, cmd /c npx on Windows, quit and reopen, where the log is), and hooks.json runs node "${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/no-server.mjs", which prints the skill only when that variable is set. Both live in the plugin folder, so they ship as plugin 0.1.5+2 with no npm publish; pin-plugin moves the skill's pin. Untested: whether the hook sees the variable on a real cloud host (the skill covers it if not). Rejected: the hook in the package, a remote server (D-124), printing the steps everywhere.
state: live

### D-509
date: 2026-10-20
milestone: M52
scope: plugin
title: The server also ships as a Claude desktop extension: emerald.mcpb on every tagged release, run --engine-only on the Node the Claude desktop app ships, installed by double-click into the app itself, where chat and Cowork on that computer reach it; the plugin keeps npx, because Claude Code runs a plugin's .mcpb on the system node; reverses D-498's 'no .mcpb' for direct install only
question: Can a person add Emerald to the Claude desktop app, and to Cowork on their computer, without installing Node or editing a configuration file, and should the Claude plugin run that bundle instead of npx?
answer: Yes, for the desktop app: npm run build:mcpb packs dist-plugin/server.mjs, its licences, a 512 icon and a 0.3 manifest (server.type node, node ${__dirname}/server.mjs --engine-only, darwin and win32, runtimes.node >=20.0.0, the bare author name the app makes the id from) into release/emerald.mcpb, which the release workflow builds, attests and attaches on every tag. Measured on Windows: installed by opening the file and pressing Install, the desktop app 2.42304 (Electron 44.7.2, Node 24.21.0) ran it in its own utility process ('built-in node is compatible'), no node.exe, serving chat (claude-ai) and Cowork (local-agent-mode) from that one process; isUsingBuiltInNodeForMcp is on unless set false and was set nowhere, and with no system Node the app falls back to its own anyway. Unpacked, the bundle's five files answer create, declare, write, read, start_here and formats on Node 20, 22 and 24. No for the plugin: Claude Code 2.1.299, the Code tab's binary, unpacks a plugin's .mcpb and starts it with the system node, and fails to connect with Node off PATH, so mcpServers stays npx. The emerald-setup skill and the plugin README offer the extension first once a release carries it.
state: live

### D-510
date: 2026-10-20
milestone: M52
scope: plugin
title: The npm package's engines.node is >=20.19.0, the oldest Node a CI job runs it on: .github/workflows/package.yml builds it on Node 24 and runs scripts/check-package.mjs on 20.19.0, 22 and 24 — the handshake, a document written and read back, both hooks, and an npx install that fails on EBADENGINE; the desktop extension's runtimes.node follows; amends D-497's >=24
question: What Node range should emerald-mcp claim, now that npm prints `npm warn EBADENGINE` before every plugin hook on a cloud host's Node 22 while the range is >=24?
answer: The oldest Node a CI job runs the built package on, and nothing lower. .github/workflows/package.yml builds dist-plugin/ on Node 24, switches to the matrix's Node (20.19.0, 22, 24) and runs npm run check:package: server.mjs --engine-only answers initialize and tools/list, creates a document with the development pack, commits an inbox row and reads it back with start_here; the full server lists more tools; hooks.mjs start-here and rows print the row; status-line.mjs exits cleanly; npm pack, then npx -y -p <tarball> emerald-hooks rows from an empty cache with the npm that ships with that Node, must print the row and no EBADENGINE. engines.node is >=20.19.0 from 0.1.6, and scripts/mcpb.mjs's runtimes.node follows. Measured locally: the EBADENGINE step failed on Node 22.20 against >=24 and passed on 20.19.5, 22.20.0 and 24.15.0 against >=20.19.0. Node 20 is past end of life; when it stops passing, the floor and the matrix move to 22 together. Rejected: no engines at all, --loglevel=error on the plugin's npx, building on each matrix Node.
state: live

### D-513
date: 2026-10-20
milestone: M52
scope: plugin
title: A team weighing Emerald for its development planning is answered before its model objects: both instruction blocks say, right after the 2,000-character head, that plans nobody owns are what it is for, that one document holds work across repositories, stored as text and committed or kept as one file outside every repository, that documents talk through an inbox and an `elsewhere` column, and that a worktree session's landing goes through the main document's inbox; the head says SQLite as bytes or text
question: Why did a model asked to keep a development team's planning in Emerald refuse until argued out of it, and what does the server say before it can object?
answer: Each objection had an answer the server never gave: git and a tracker cover planning (the plans between them, a PLAN.md or notes, drift because nobody owns them); the pack means per-repository documents (one document holds work across repositories); a .emd is binary SQLite (store_document_as text diffs and merges in git, and the head called it 'a real SQLite database'); a document outside git is in no worktree or cloud session (a committed text file is in every checkout and reads as Markdown); two documents cannot talk (an inbox, and an elsewhere column reading the other row's state live, not enforced); parallel worktrees' claims collide (a worktree session proposes its landing to the main document's inbox and one driver lands it; one document outside every repository is one copy, written under a lock). A section in both blocks says so straight after 'When they ask to see', since the head is full (1,996 of 2,000); the head says 'SQLite, as bytes or text'. server.test.ts pins the section's place and each claim beside store_document_as, land_units, claim_units and keep_packs, and fails on 'a real SQLite database', 'binary file', 'cannot be committed', 'not meant for development', 'gitignore' and 'one document per repository'. ask-cold.mjs asks the planning question with its own flags; --question and --server added. What a model still holds fairly is not argued: CLAUDE.md stays Markdown, a link does not cross documents, a write needs the server, a one-file fix costs more here. Ships in emerald-mcp 0.1.6. Rejected: squeezing it into the head (D-482's budget); naming the products the model named (D-450).
state: live

### D-514
date: 2026-10-22
milestone: M53
scope: app
title: A view can be saved, and a dashboard holds only views: a saved view is a format's filter, order, columns and kind under a name, kept beside its other views; a dashboard, one per format and one for the document, is an ordered set of saved views, charts of rollups and headline totals, never a free block; neither is a format; recent changes wait and D-462 stands; amends D-402
question: Does a customizable dashboard, per format and for the whole document, fit the premise, and what is it made of? The maintainer's task format had no view that suits tasks, the entry page felt cluttered and adding by hand was heavy; D-402 said an entry has no free blocks, the line between a view and a workspace.
answer: A saved view is a format's match, search, order, columns and view kind under a name: what the address already holds and what format_rows and show_format already take, kept beside the format's other views (format_view_column, views.md), saved by arrange_views, drawn by show_format, listed under its format in the rail. A dashboard, one per format and one for the document, holds saved views, charts of a rollup and headline totals (a count or sum under a filter), arranged in place, and nothing else: no free text, no embeds; D-402's line moves from the entry to the dashboard. Neither is a format: rows are the person's data, a view is how to look at it. The entry page and adding by hand get lighter in the same milestone (mockup first), and tasks get a view kind of their own, list. Every write has both halves (D-227). Rejected: a dashboard as rows of a format; free blocks; recent changes for now, since rows carry no time of change (D-462) and that decision would have to be reopened. Answers the open question 'entry overview'.
state: live

### D-515
date: 2026-10-22
milestone: M52
scope: engine
title: Tests stop costing more than the work: CI runs typecheck and the suite on every push to main, the demo deploys only after it, and a session runs the tests related to its change
question: Who runs the whole suite, and what does a session run before a commit?
answer: .github/workflows/test.yml runs typecheck and the full suite on every push to main; its demo build needs the tests and its deploy needs the build (demo.yml is gone), so a red push publishes nothing. A session runs `npx vitest related <changed files> --run`, `src/privacy.test.ts` and typecheck while building and before the commit, names by path any test that reads a changed file rather than importing it (a skill: hosts/claude), and runs the full suite locally only for a broad change (src/domain/, a dependency, vite.config.ts, src/testing/), once. A red CI run on main is the next session's first task, and a stop for a driver. The privacy check runs in CI only with an EMERALD_DENYLIST secret. No slow project: no few files dominate.
state: live

### D-516
date: 2026-10-22
milestone: M53
scope: app
title: One general chart draws plain series as bars or a line, only what its axis can hold, read by a screen reader as a table; a rollup's chart is bars by month, one series per linked row, the eight largest drawn and the rest said, never summed
question: How does Emerald draw a chart that is not the ledger's, and what does a rollup's chart show?
answer: src/ui/charts/Chart.tsx takes plain series (chart.ts): labels, points that are Money, a plain figure, a word (unchecked) or nothing, and an axis {currency} or {unit}. Money becomes a number only in drawnValue; money in another currency or a plain figure on a money axis is not drawn. One series is ink, several take the category palette by key; the SVG is aria-hidden and an sr-only table reads every point. rollupChart.ts turns a rollup into bars by month (a line on a toggle), every month first to last, one series per linked row plus none, the axis the most common currency, at most 8 series; rows with no month, other currencies and series past the eighth are said under the chart, never folded into an other. Nothing in src/ui/charts/ imports the ledger (chart.test.ts).
state: live

### D-517
date: 2026-10-22
milestone: M53
scope: ui
title: Hiding a field is the entry view's, beside its order; one Fields list replaces the Arrange mode; a field moves only within its part
question: How does a person hide a field on an entry and reorder the page without the Arrange mode, and how does a conversation do the same?
answer: `hidden` and `new` are words of a column's entry setting in format_view_column (and views.md), beside the way, place and header: hidden keeps it off this entry view even with a value (never the title or a required column, drawn whatever it says); new marks it for a new entry to ask (M53 U3 reads it). One Fields list behind the entry's icon ('Fields: order, shown, new entry') lists every column but the title by part, with grip, eye and New entry tick; a field moves only within its part (movedWithin keeps parts in their slots), by its label, its row, its ⋯ menu (Move up/down, Hide) or Alt+Up/Down on a row. 'N hidden' at the foot shows hidden fields dimmed until the entry is left. arrange_views takes hidden and new_entry and refuses hiding the title, a required or a computed column. Amends D-296.
state: live

### D-518
date: 2026-10-22
milestone: M53
scope: ui
title: An entry draws what it holds; empty columns are one Empty line of names; a new entry asks for the title, the required columns and the ones ticked New entry
question: What does an entry draw of a format with many columns, and what does a new entry ask for?
answer: On a row that exists a column is drawn when it holds a value, is required (saying needed while empty), or was opened from the Empty line / is being typed in; every other non-hidden column is '+ name' on the Empty line at the foot of the left column (after a hairline, in the view's order, U2's 'N hidden' at its end); a name opens its field in place, focused (a menu opens), and left empty it folds back. A worked-out figure or total is drawn when it has a value; a check is always drawn (unchecked is its verdict). Labels read as words, dropping a suffix naming the column's unit (entryLabel). Two figures or fewer sit in the line under the title unless a check, a worked-out figure or a roomy way (large, bar, ring, slider) claims the grid (entryShown). A new entry asks for the title (focused), every required column and the columns ticked New entry; a hidden one stays off; no Links here; Add is never disabled and says the first missing required column in one line ('A task needs a title.', entryNeeds) only once pressed; Enter in the title adds. A required enum nothing implies starts at its first member (entryDefaults, implied for U4's quick add) — a view default, never a model's value, and not counted as touched. EntryList shows the draft as its first marked row ('A new task' until titled) and gains a '+' ('Add a task') opening entry/new.
state: live

### D-519
date: 2026-10-22
milestone: M53
scope: engine
title: Every format has a list: one line per row, a tick, grouped by an enum, a link or a date's buckets, and quick add; the list beside an entry is the same list, narrow; the View menu became the format's one tab strip
question: How does the list view keep its settings, tick a row, and add one in place?
answer: View kind 'list'. A column's format_view_column setting holds the words together: group, line, done <members> (done last); group and done are one column's each (setListSetting takes them off the other column, keeping its other words). Defaults: no group; done = first enum's last member, else first boolean; line = first enums, first link, first date (date last, relative: today, tomorrow, 'Sat, in 2 days', '5 Nov', late open rows 'N days late' in negative). Tick = updateRow through move's checks; untick restores the member held before (session memory by line name), else the first non-done member. Quick add = title + group's value + the address filter's exact values + entryDefaults, staged and written as record_format_rows' batch; a required column still empty opens entry/new with the draft. EntryList renders the same groups narrow (flat past 1000 rows). Parity: arrange_views view:list, show_format view:list, formats' **List:** line.
state: live

### D-520
date: 2026-10-22
milestone: M53
scope: engine
title: A saved view is its own table, format_saved_view (063): a format, a name, a kind and the address as the address spells it; Save this view on the FilterBand, listed and renamed/deleted in the rail; arrange_views view:saved; show_format and format_rows take view:"<name>"; an entry's back link names the view it was opened from
question: Where is a saved view kept, what does it hold, and how do the screen and a conversation reach it?
answer: Table format_saved_view (063): format_name, name (1-60, one line, not '?…', not a view's own word), kind (table|board|list|roadmap|ready), address (<=2000, canonicalAddress: parts in name order). Not format_view_column: its setting is one 32-char word per column. The address keeps filter, folds, columns and sort as addressFilter spells them, so 'today'/'within 7 days' stay phrases. Screen: FilterBand 'Save this view' while the address holds anything (sort alone draws 'Every entry'), name asked in place, a taken name = Replace, 'Saved as X' when the address is one (same kind + same canonical address); the rail lists them under the format, opens savedViewPath, lights the current one, renames/deletes in place. Agenda saves none. Tools: arrange_views {view:saved, name, kind, match, search, order, descending, columns} saves (checked as format_rows checks a filter), {name, rename} renames, {name, delete:true} deletes; show_format view:'<name>' draws its kind with its filter; format_rows view:'<name>' reads it (a given match adds, anything else given replaces); formats lists them. Frame reads the name from the fetched document. Text: '### <format> saved' blocks with name/kind/address. Entry back link: route state from:list|board, '← List'/'← Board', else the format's title.
state: live

### D-521
date: 2026-10-22
milestone: M53
scope: engine
title: A dashboard is an ordered set of blocks in format_dashboard_block (064): a saved view drawn compact, a rollup's chart, or a headline total (count or sum under a filter), nothing else; one per format (last tab) and one for the document (rail's Dashboard: first and #/ without a ledger, under Home with one); arranged in place; arrange_views view:dashboard and show_format view:dashboard
question: What can a dashboard hold, where does it sit, how is it arranged and kept, and how does a conversation reach it?
answer: Blocks: saved view (compact: list 5 open lines + 'N more'/'N done', grouped 2 a group; table 4 rows; board lanes with count + 2 cards; roadmap/ready 5 names), rollup chart (bars, 150px), headline total (count, or sum of a money/integer/real/decimal column, under a filter kept as an address so 'today' stays a phrase; money summed per currency). Grid of 4 columns: total 1, others 2; order is the only arrangement. In place: 'Add a block' (saved views + rollups, grouped by format on the document's; 'Headline total' made in place with conditions from the saved views, the list's done turned round and date windows), drag by grip, menu: Open in full / Move earlier / Move later / Remove from this dashboard (a total goes with its block; a view or rollup stays). Title opens in full with '← Dashboard' back (route state from:dashboard). Placement: #/formats/<name>/dashboard, last tab after a rule, format still opens on Table; #/dashboard, the rail's Dashboard: first and #/ without a ledger, right under Home with it. Storage: format_dashboard_block (dashboard NULL = document's, position, format_name, kind saved|rollup|total, name 1-60, sum, address); saved view rename/delete cascades; dropped rollup drawn as gone; deleteFormat clears. Text: '### <format> dashboard' / '### Document dashboard' blocks (kind, name, format, sum, address). Tools: arrange_views {view:dashboard, format?, add:[{saved}|{rollup}|{total, count|sum, match, search}], order:[names], remove:[names]} ('format/name' when ambiguous); show_format view:dashboard (no format = document's) names every block with what it holds; formats lists a format's blocks. start_here unchanged. 'Dashboard' is refused as a saved view's name.
state: live

### D-522
date: 2026-10-22
milestone: M52
scope: engine
title: A spreadsheet keyed by a unique column is matched against every row of its format, whichever file or person wrote it; gone stays per file, a row moves to the reading that last wrote it, a read fills only the columns it maps, and a read with no unique column says so and matches on nothing else; amends D-408
question: Does a second export of the same thing under another name (next month's file) duplicate, refuse, or update the rows the first wrote?
answer: When the format has a unique column the reading fills, it updates them: each line whose value a row holds is that row, written over cell by cell where the file differs; the rest are added. Only rows this file wrote can be gone. Without a unique column the read says so in one sentence and adds every line, with no matching on other columns (D-224).
state: live

### D-523
date: 2026-10-22
milestone: M52
scope: engine
title: A repository is set up by `npx emerald-mcp init`: the document, a marked CLAUDE.md section from it, and the hooks only where no Emerald plugin is enabled
question: How does a repository adopting the development pack get the document, the CLAUDE.md start-here and column table, and the session hooks?
answer: `npx emerald-mcp init [document.emd]`, a subcommand of the server's bin (not a fourth bin). Document: named, else .claude/emerald.json's, else the one .emd in the folder, else docs/memory.emd; a new one keeps the development pack, text inside the repo and SQLite bytes outside; an existing one is only read; .claude/emerald.json names it. CLAUDE.md gets one section between <!-- emerald-init: ... --> and <!-- /emerald-init --> written from the document (its views' start-here table via startTableOf, and compactDeclarations), replaced in place. The plugin's two hooks go into .claude/settings.json only where no emerald@ plugin is enabled, never over a hook it did not write. Every file is compared before writing, so a second run changes nothing.
state: live

### D-524
date: 2026-10-22
milestone: M53
scope: app
title: A list ticks only where someone said what done is, and the tick says what it sets; amends D-519
question: Which rows of a list get a tick, who decides what a tick writes, and how does a person see and undo it?
answer: No default: listOf gives a done only where a column's list setting says `done <members>`; otherwise the list is plain lines with no tick column (List tab, narrow list beside an entry, dashboard list block, show_format, formats' List: line). Done is set in place from the list header (No ticks / Ticking marks <column> <member> opens 'Tick to mark…' with each enum member and yes/no, and 'No ticks'), by arrange_views view:list, or by a pack's views.md. The header says 'Ticking marks state done'; each circle's hover and accessible name says 'Mark done' / 'Undo: back to <member>' (tickWords). A ticked row stays in place 4 s with an Undo that writes back the member it held. The development pack declares unit built, milestone closed, open_question answered, review_entry accepted, inbox applied; decision, rule and term none; the ledger none. finding (this repo's own format) is set to state done in memory.emd.
state: live

### D-525
date: 2026-10-22
milestone: M53
scope: app
title: A dashboard makes the view or chart it needs in place: Add a block offers New view (a list, table or board of one format, its filter, order and columns, a name) saved and placed in one step, and New chart (a count or sum by month of a date, per a link or not, under a filter) declared as a rollup of its format and placed in one step; picking existing views and rollups stays; no new write; amends D-521
question: Can a person make the saved view or chart a dashboard needs without leaving it, and how?
answer: Add a block lists the existing saved views and rollups as before, then New view, New chart, Headline total (the 'saved from its own tab' note is gone). New view: format (document's dashboard only), List/Table/Board (board disabled without lanes), rows via ColumnFilter whose ColumnMenu now also sorts (ColumnFilter takes offered, order, onSort), columns via ColumnsChoice for table (beside its name) and board (cards) only, a name; Save and add = saveView + setDashboardBlocks in one transaction; a name already a saved view's or a block's is refused, never overwritten; writes no list done (D-524). New chart: Count of rows or Sum of a number/money column, By month of a date or month column (preselected only when there is one), And per a link or nothing, Only rows where via ColumnFilter over enum/boolean/date columns translated to a rollup where (members, empty, clock windows; search and ranges refused), name folded to snake_case (Done by month -> done_by_month) and shown with the sentence it declares; Declare and add = declarationProblems + declarationChangeRefusal, then adoptFormat + setDashboardBlocks in one transaction. Parity: no new write; a conversation does the same with arrange_views view:saved, extend_format rollups and arrange_views view:dashboard. Code: src/ui/formats/madeInPlace.ts (judge and write), MakeBlock.tsx (the two forms).
state: live

### D-526
date: 2026-10-22
milestone: M53
scope: app
title: A chart is listed in the rail under its format beside the saved views, renamed and deleted there or on its tab; a rollup's dashboard blocks follow its rename and go with its delete, in adoptFormat; amends D-521
question: Where is a chart (a rollup) made on screen found, renamed and deleted, and what happens to its dashboard blocks?
answer: The rail lists each rollup a format declares under it, after its saved views, with a chart glyph; its name opens its tab, the pencil renames it (typed words folded as New chart folds them) and the bin deletes it, Delete asking once; a refusal (a column's name, a pack's rollup) is said under the row. Its tab has Delete chart. adoptFormat follows a dashboard's chart blocks: a rollup the declaration drops deletes them, one renamed (renames carries it new-to-old, as a column) renames them; so extend_format remove and rename (which now takes a rollup's name) do the same as the screen. A block's Remove still leaves the chart, and its note says to delete it under the format in the rail or on its tab. No new write: the screen adopts the declaration with the rollup renamed or gone.
state: live

### D-527
date: 2026-10-23
milestone: M53
scope: app
title: A dashboard block is resized by dragging its edge — right for width, bottom for height, corner for both — snapped to the grid's four columns and rows of 40 px (16 between); kept as width and height on format_dashboard_block (065) and in a text document's dashboard lines; a block with none keeps its old size; arrange_views view:dashboard size sets it; amends D-521
question: How is a dashboard block resized, how big can it be, and where is its size kept?
answer: Drag its right edge (width), bottom edge (height) or corner (both); it snaps to the grid's four columns and to rows of 40 px with 16 between, so N rows is 40N + 16(N-1) px; each edge is also a focusable handle moved by the arrow keys. Width 1-4 columns, height 2-20 rows; either may be absent, and absent is the old default (headline total one column, other blocks two, height by content). Given a height, a saved view draws all its rows (up to 200) and scrolls inside; a wider table shows more columns beside its name (1, 2, 4, 6 at widths 1-4), a board of width 3-4 its lanes side by side, and a chart is drawn to the block's height. Kept as width and height on format_dashboard_block (migration 065) and as width:/height: lines under a block in a text document's views part, only when set. Parity: arrange_views view:dashboard size:[{name, width, height}], judged after add and before remove and order; show_format view:dashboard draws it at its size and its text names a sized block's size. No reset-to-default on screen: the maintainer's if wanted.
state: live

<!-- emerald: finding.format.md -->
---
format: finding
title: Finding
language: 24
claims: none
---

One finding of M45's code clean-up pass (2026-10-09: a code clean-up pass, removing duplicate, old or bad code). U1 surveyed and wrote these rows and changed no code; U2 takes the delete rows, U3 the merge and fix rows, and the author rules on the author rows first.

How U1 found them: a script over the TypeScript AST built the import graph of src/, electron/ and scripts/ from the entry points (src/main.tsx, src/frame/main.tsx, src/demo/main.tsx, src/demo/tour/main.tsx, src/plugin/main.ts, electron/main.ts and preload.ts, every script, every *.test.ts, import.meta.glob, new URL(..., import.meta.url)), and marked each export used by production code, by tests only, or by nothing. Dependencies were checked by import specifier across src/, electron/, scripts/, vite.config.ts and the CSS; tokens and classes by a scan of every .ts/.tsx/.html for the name (Tailwind's utility form included, templated class names read by hand). Catch blocks by the AST. Duplicates and retired code by reading.

Migrations are never findings: a document's schema history runs through every one. A dependency used only by package.json scripts or electron-builder is not dead.

The queries: U2 `format_rows finding match:{action:delete, state:open}`; U3 `format_rows finding match:{action:merge, state:open}` and `match:{action:fix, state:open}`; the maintainer `match:{action:maintainer, state:open}`.

| column | type | points at | unit | per | required | unique | values | min | max | note |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| ref | text |  |  |  | yes | yes |  |  |  | S-001 onward; a later session names the row by it, since a text document renews ids on every write. |
| found_by | link | unit |  |  | yes | no |  |  |  | The unit whose survey found it (M45 U1). |
| kind | enum |  |  |  | yes | no | dead, duplicated, retired, bad |  |  | dead: nothing imports or reads it; duplicated: two implementations of one job; retired: it serves a decision that no longer holds; bad: a type hole, a swallowed error, a stale comment. |
| area | enum |  |  |  | yes | no | core, domain, engine, data, importer, storage, ui, plugin, electron, scripts, demo, frame, deps, css, fixtures |  |  | Where it lives, so a session takes a batch of one area at a time. |
| title | text |  |  |  | yes | no |  |  |  | What it is, in a line. |
| location | text |  |  |  | yes | no |  |  |  | Exact paths, with line numbers and symbol names — every twin of a duplicate. |
| evidence | text |  |  |  | yes | no |  |  |  | Why it is what kind says: no importer (and how that was checked), the decision that retired it, the twin's path and how the bodies differ. |
| action | enum |  |  |  | yes | no | delete, merge, fix, maintainer |  |  | delete and the retired code go to M45 U2; merge and fix go to M45 U3; maintainer is the maintainer's to rule on first — removing it would change what a person or a tool can do. |
| into | text |  |  |  | no | no |  |  |  | For a merge, the path and symbol that should own the job; for a fix, what the fix is. |
| risk | text |  |  |  | yes | no |  |  |  | What reaches it: a screen, a tool, a test, a person's document — and what to run after the change. |
| state | enum |  |  |  | yes | no | open, done, kept |  |  | open until the unit that takes it lands; done when it did; kept when it should not be done, with outcome saying why. |
| outcome | text |  |  |  | no | no |  |  |  | What the session that took it did, or why it was kept. |

| constraint | note |
| --- | --- |
| into is required when action is merge | A merge names what owns the job afterwards. |
| outcome is required when state is kept | A finding left alone says why. |

