<!-- emerald: inbox.format.md -->
---
format: inbox
title: Inbox
language: 23
claims: none
---

What another project's session hands this document: a fact it found, a finding about this project, a gate it knows of. A message to another session lasts only as long as that session; a row lasts as long as the document.

`from` is the sending document's absolute path — `/work/shop/plan.emd` — and never a session's title, because a title means nothing once the session ends and a path is where the sender's own record can be read. `refs` is the sender's names for the same things in its own document, so the owner can ask that document rather than the session that wrote it.

The rules are a convention, not a guarantee: nothing knows which session writes a row, so nothing here can enforce them. A sender writes a row once, with `state: new`, and never edits it. The owner moves `state` and fills what it became. A session from another project writes to no other format of this document.

`about` is the row the hand-off concerns, in whichever document holds it — the sender's own unit, a decision of a third project — written as that document's path and the row's name: `/work/site/plan.emd M2 U3`. It is an `elsewhere` column: `format_rows` and `start_here` read the other document and print what the row holds now beside it — `unit M2 U3 · built` — so the owner sees that the thing a row asks about is already done without asking the sender. A document that is not there, or a name it has no row for, reads *unresolved* and is never refused, because the other file may be closed, moved or on another machine. By name and never by id: a text document renews its ids on every write.

What it became is three columns, because a link names its target by that format's first text column and only some formats have a unique one: `answered_by` and `became_unit` are links, to a decision and a unit; `became` is text for everything else — an open question or a review entry by its ref, or why it was dropped.

A row written from another project while a session here is writing is the ordinary case of two writers: writers take the document's lock in turn, a batch applies whole or not at all, and a copy is taken before it. A session is told a row arrived without asking: the plugin's prompt hook prints the rows in state `new` before every prompt, and nothing when there are none, so a session never polls; the row is printed again until the owner moves its `state`. A session that must wake between prompts can watch the document for `state=new` instead.

**A row is for what the receiver lacks.** Before writing, the sender reads the receiver's document — `start_here`, its latest decisions — and writes only what it does not already hold. A document made before the pack changed is brought up to date by `keep_packs`, which declares what the pack added since and renames what it renamed, values kept; a kept pack does not update itself.

**A session run by a driver proposes its landing here.** It lands nothing itself: it writes one row, `state: new`, whose `refs` is its unit's ref and whose `body` is a line or two for a person and then one fenced `json` block in the shape of one of `land_units`' `units` — `unit`, `files`, `commit`, `branch`, `decisions`, `review_entries` — and never `close_milestone`, which is the project owner's. It writes to the document path its hooks printed, which in a worktree is the main checkout's copy, never one under the worktree's own folder. `land_units inbox:[ids]` lands every proposal named as one dry run and one commit, refuses the batch by name for any that is not a proposal or not `new`, and moves each to `applied` with `became_unit` and `answered_by` filled. The proposing session writes no other format of this document.

An inbox row makes no claim.

| column | type | points at | unit | per | required | values | min | max | note |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| subject | text |  |  |  | yes |  |  |  | The hand-off in a line: what `start_here` lists and what a text document heads the row with. |
| from | text |  |  |  | yes |  |  |  | The sending document's absolute path — `/work/shop/plan.emd`. Not a session's title: a title means nothing once the session ends. |
| sent_on | date |  |  |  | yes |  |  |  | The day it was sent. |
| body | text |  |  |  | no |  |  |  | The fact or finding in full, in markdown, written for a session that has none of the sender's context. |
| refs | text |  |  |  | no |  |  |  | The sender's own refs for it, in its own document — `plan.emd D-10, D-18`. |
| about | elsewhere |  |  |  | no |  |  |  | The row it concerns, in the document that holds it — `/work/site/plan.emd M2 U3`. Read back with what that row holds now, or unresolved. |
| state | enum |  |  |  | yes | new, triaged, applied, dropped |  |  | A sender writes `new`, and `start_here` lists those. `triaged`: the owner has read it and knows what it will become. `applied`: it became that. `dropped`: read and not acted on, and `became` says why. |
| answered_by | link | decision |  |  | no |  |  |  | The decision it became or answered. |
| became_unit | link | unit |  |  | no |  |  |  | The unit it became. |
| became | text |  |  |  | no |  |  |  | Anything else it became, by ref or name — an open question, a review entry — or why it was dropped. |

## rows

### A running plugin server writes schema 65 and reads only 64 after a rebuild under it
from: another document of the maintainer's
sent_on: 2026-10-23
body: "Seen 2026-10-23 while working in `another project's document`. The desktop extension's server had been started on a schema-64 build, and `dist-plugin` was rebuilt to 65 at 09:46 while it ran. After that:\n\n- a write at 09:50 left `another project's document` at schema 65 (its `schema_version` table holds 62, 63, 64, 65), and every later call refused it with `SCHEMA_AHEAD` (plugin understands 64);\n- `create_document` on a new path replied \"Created ... at schema version 64\", but the file's `schema_version` already held 65, and the next call (`declare_format`) refused it with `SCHEMA_AHEAD`.\n\nSo one server process wrote with the new build's code and read with the old build's. That looks like part of the code being loaded from disk lazily. Restarting the extension fixed it. The user can't recover without a restart, and `create_document` reports a version it didn't write."
state: new

### Development pack format notes describe Emerald's own repo, not the adopting project
from: another document of the maintainer's
sent_on: 2026-10-23
body: A new document created with `packs: ["development"]` (`another project's document`, a game design project) gets format notes written about Emerald itself. `decision` talks about docs/09-decision-log.md, D-227 and M22 U1; `open_question` about docs/engine.md's H-numbers, src/parity.test.ts and D-226; `milestone` about docs/07-roadmap.md and M27 U1. Column notes do the same (`D-224's one-hop rule`, `M44 U5, D-444`). In another project these read as noise or as instructions that don't apply. The pack's notes should describe the format in general terms, with Emerald's own history kept in Emerald's document.
state: new

### M51 F6 ready to land: test.yml on every push, demo after it, related tests in sessions
from: F:/projects/emerald/docs/memory.emd
sent_on: 2026-10-22
body: "M51 F6 is built at 0f5508f on worktree-agent-a382626d80a322b2d: merge the branch, then land this.\n\n```json\n{\"unit\":\"M51 F6\",\"files\":\".github/workflows/test.yml (new), .github/workflows/demo.yml (removed), docs/conventions.md, docs/09-decision-log.md, .claude/skills/closing-pass/SKILL.md, .claude/skills/run-milestone/SKILL.md, hosts/claude/skills/closing-pass/SKILL.md, hosts/claude/skills/run-milestone/SKILL.md, hosts/claude/.claude-plugin/plugin.json, hosts/claude-plugin-release.json, electron/windowsUpdater.test.ts, src/testing/setup.ts, src/testing/tempDir.ts\",\"commit\":\"0f5508f\",\"branch\":\"worktree-agent-a382626d80a322b2d\",\"report\":\"M51 F6 built at 0f5508f on worktree-agent-a382626d80a322b2d (three commits: ee9d513 work, 7cb6a89 Linux fixes, 0f5508f log). D-515.\\n\\nMEASURED (2026-10-22, maintainer's machine, 8 cores, Windows, Vitest 4.1.12, one full run): 340 files, 4,786 tests, 115 s (transform 22 s, import 113 s, tests 594 s summed over workers; 587 s of summed file time). Fixed startup of a one-file run: ~2.3 s wall (Vitest's own 0.3 s). Typecheck: 24 s. Slowest twenty files (s): plugin/tools/formats 28.2, tools/landing 25.6, tools/categorize 20.7, data/packs/ledger/pack 19.0, plugin/formatWatch 16.3, hosts/claude/hooks/commands 14.1, tools/ingest 14.1, plugin/server 13.3, tools/taxonomy 11.9, data/storage/encoding 11.4, data/scale 11.0, plugin/subscriptions 10.9, tools/searchDocument 10.2, tools/funds 10.1, tools/setup 9.2, tools/goalDeposits 9.1, tools/claim 9.0, tools/plan 8.5, tools/rules 8.4, tools/goalRhythm 8.3 = 338 s, 57% of the summed time; src/plugin/ is 333 s of 587. No few files dominate (top five = 19%, the slowest is 28 s of a 115 s wall), so no slow project / test:slow: it would hide the plugin's tests from the sessions that change the plugin, and related already runs a slow file only when touched.\\n\\nBEFORE / AFTER, a typical unit's local testing: before, the suite plus typecheck, ~140 s a run (140-165 s under the load of parallel worktrees), and several runs a unit (M42 F3: five). After, related + privacy + typecheck: a plugin change (M42 F16's files) related = 9 files, 22 s; a UI change (M42 F14's) 27 files, 26 s; plus typecheck 24 s and privacy ~5 s = ~50-55 s, once. A src/domain/format/ change (M42 F11's view.ts) is broad: related reaches 178 files, ~117 s, as the rule says.\\n\\nCI: .github/workflows/test.yml, on push to main and workflow_dispatch: npm ci (vendored SheetJS, no Electron binary), typecheck, npx vitest run; demo build needs test, deploy needs demo and runs only on main. demo.yml removed (one workflow so the chain is seen in one run and could be tried on a branch). Done-test on a throwaway branch (a temporary trigger line, deleted with the branch): run 39736428289 green (tests 5m36s, of which the suite 5m; demo build 2m26s; deploy skipped off main); run 39737370962 with a deliberately failing test: red, demo build and deploy skipped. Remote branch deleted. The first two runs were red for real, Linux-only: electron/windowsUpdater.test.ts asked for latest-linux.yml (now runs as Windows), and src/testing/setup.ts made the backups folder up front, which a fully skipped file (privacy, without its denylist) never removed (now tempPathSync, made by the first copy).\\n\\nPRIVACY in CI: runs when the repo has an EMERALD_DENYLIST secret (written to RUNNER_TEMP), skips otherwise; failures name file and denylist line, never a term. No secret is set: adding one is the maintainer's choice. Sessions now run src/privacy.test.ts before every commit, since related never reaches it.\\n\\nSESSION RULE: docs/conventions.md (testing bullet; Before you start item 2, a red CI run first), .claude/skills/closing-pass (step 3), .claude/skills/run-milestone (prompt; the driver reads gh run list after each unit, red is a stop), and the generic copies in hosts/claude/skills. A test that reads a changed file (a skill, a script, views.md) is named by path. The plugin is 0.1.6+1 (pin-plugin --plugin), because its skills changed.\\n\\nFOR THE DRIVER after merge+push: test.yml green on main and Demo build + Demo deploy following it; docs:check is green once this lands D-515's row (in the worktree its only error is that missing row). If M53 U2/U6 also cut the plugin, plugin.json/claude-plugin-release.json conflict: rerun node scripts/pin-plugin.mjs --plugin after merging.\",\"decisions\":[{\"ref\":\"D-515\",\"title\":\"Tests stop costing more than the work: CI runs typecheck and the suite on every push to main, the demo deploys only after it, and a session runs the tests related to its change\",\"question\":\"Who runs the whole suite, and what does a session run before a commit?\",\"answer\":\".github/workflows/test.yml runs typecheck and the full suite on every push to main; its demo build needs the tests and its deploy needs the build (demo.yml is gone), so a red push publishes nothing. A session runs `npx vitest related <changed files> --run`, `src/privacy.test.ts` and typecheck while building and before the commit, names by path any test that reads a changed file rather than importing it (a skill: hosts/claude), and runs the full suite locally only for a broad change (src/domain/, a dependency, vite.config.ts, src/testing/), once. A red CI run on main is the next session's first task, and a stop for a driver. The privacy check runs in CI only with an EMERALD_DENYLIST secret. No slow project: no few files dominate.\"}],\"review_entries\":[]}\n```"
