### M22 U6 #4
question: Where do the 400 microseconds a row actually go, and is 'text cannot hold a ledger' true or just unprofiled? U6 measured 400 us/row in WASM and the roadmap turned that into the argument for per-file. Re-measured in the same sql.js WASM: a raw insert is 3 us prepared and 10 us via db.run, and the shape applyDocument actually uses — derive an id, insert with the link null, then UPDATE the link by primary key — is ~32 us a row, which is ~2 s at 60k and not tens of seconds. So roughly 12x of what was measured is somewhere the write path does not explain, most likely the parser in readDocument. There is also a concrete win available: D-247 derives ids from row content, so every id is knowable before any database work — build all the name-to-id indexes up front and the two-pass write becomes one pass with links already filled, which measured ~8 us a row, a 4x. Not yet done honestly: nobody has profiled applyParts on a large real document, and these figures are synthetic — eight columns, one format, no coercion, no parsing. Per-file is right regardless, on size, readability and git. What must not calcify is the sentence 'binary wins for the ledger' resting on a number whose attribution is unknown.
opened_by: D-246
state: open

### M22 lost write #1
question: Why was a row written through the plugin silently lost? 'M22 U1 #1' (an open_question) was written with record_format_rows at 10:29:54Z on 2026-09-20 and was gone by the next write. ANSWERED 2026-09-23 from the transcripts, the .bak files and git: not the app. At 10:49:16Z a second session, building U6 at the same time, saw docs/memory.emd modified in git status, took it for its own test's leftover, and ran `git checkout -- docs/memory.emd`, restoring c82cfd5, which predated the write. The app's 11:13Z save (adding `brief`) lost nothing: the 110851 and 111440 backups hold the same 13 units and every open question. Reproduced in a scratch repo with the real plugin. The .bak cannot recover such a row, because it is taken before a write. Fixed by a rule (conventions.md: a diff on memory.emd is somebody's write) and a PreToolUse hook (scripts/guard-memory-emd.mjs) that refuses checkout/restore/stash/reset --hard while the file is dirty. Still open elsewhere: nothing stops two rows sharing a ref (two 'M22 U6 #1'), and documentStore.save() has two millisecond-wide windows (check-then-write, mark-after-rename).
state: answered

### M22 market #1
question: Is the development pack a product or a proof? Researched 2026-09-23: every slice of it has a serious occupant — issue trackers built for coding agents (MCP, dependency graphs, multi-agent claiming), decision records validated in CI, local markdown-plus-SQLite memory over MCP, and version-controlled SQL databases. What none of them does is let the user declare the record types — each ships a fixed schema — and refuse a bad write at the moment it is made. So pitched as 'typed tasks and decisions for agents' it is head-to-head with mature trackers and far behind; pitched as 'declare what you need and it gets types, refusals, links and an MCP surface', with a repo's memory as the demo, it is the engine. Also open inside this: at least one tracker began with records as text in git and SQLite as a cache — D-246's shape — and left it in early 2026 because the text file conflicted on rebase and merge under several branches or agents. A text .emd is untested with more than one writer, and the day-job proof of concept is a team. Products are deliberately not named in this project.
