### On any format, press Read a document and give it a real spreadsheet of yours (a pantry list, a supermarket export, an old payroll sheet). Declare the format first via From an example if needed. Check the guessed mapping, correct it, and read the two counts and the proof under the grid before pressing the button. Then build the case on purpose: a parent format with an aggregate and an identity, and a child with a link to it. Read the child's lines, picking the parent once in Every row belongs to which…
unit: M19 U5
should: The header is found under a couple of lines of preamble. `paid on` maps to `paid_on`. `12,000.00` and `13/04/2026` read as a figure and a date. Blank trailing rows are skipped and said to be. A row that will not type is listed with its column named. If the format declares an identity, the verdict under the grid is the document's own, computed on rows that do not exist yet. In the parent/child case, the parent's identity proves or fails the whole file: Σ rows = printed total, runnable for the first time.
breaks: The guesser, which D-114 says costs most: it matches a header only by name, words and unit, so a Hebrew export will probably map nothing and leave every select on Ignore — silent rather than wrong. parseFigure is the engine's own twenty lines, not the pack's, so an issuer's format may parse in the ledger and not here. Worth arguing with: a file column may never fill a link (H10) — is that right for a receipt whose lines each want an ingredient? And idempotency is by file hash only: an overlapping longer export silently duplicates the overlap, because there is no declared key.
not_tried: A real document — the only kind that has ever found anything here. The screen's own file input has seen only a .csv (M20 U7 put an .xlsx through the tool), multi-sheet workbooks are unproven, nothing has run at scale, and a label-value PDF onto a format does not exist yet.
