### M23 U1
milestone: M26
scope: engine
title: describe it in text, and a local model infers the columns
decision: D-249
state: dropped
brief: DROPPED 2026-10-01: a local model probably will not be good at inferring columns from a description. D-221's example door widened from 'show me one' to 'tell me what it is'. On T1's walk: the windows for declaring a format by hand were poor and unfitting, except the MCP. And Excel means availability for general usage and the user's brainstorming, not the interface — so the types stay and the GATE goes. Carries T6 from M20's stopped tail: a long model read must be leaveable, with an indicator at the bottom that is clickable to return, because every model task in the app currently holds a screen hostage. Where it breaks: the model half is only provable in the shell against a real runtime, which is the only place the socket exists (D-124, D-134); U2c was shell-proven against a real qwen3:30b-a3b in M20's review and the fault it produced was the hostage screen, not a wrong answer.
files: src/ui/formats/FormatDoors.tsx, src/ui/formats/ExamplePanel.tsx, src/domain/modelTasks.ts, electron/generate.ts

### M23 U2
milestone: M23
scope: engine
title: a blank sheet: name the columns, type the rows, the types are guessed
landed: 2026-09-23
decision: D-248
state: built
brief: No steps, no order, nothing answered before a row can exist. This is the second of the two doors specified and it is the anti-walk: T1 asked four questions before a format existed and its own review rejected that shape. Excel's real property is that there is no declaration step — the grid exists before you have a thought — so structure is discovered by typing rather than declared up front. Keep T1's walk as 'edit this format', which is what it was always good at. Where it breaks: declare_format creates and never alters (D-219, D-220), so a grid that lets someone add a column to a format that already exists is either a different code path or the parity gap M21 is for — decide which before building, not during.

### M23 U3
milestone: M26
scope: engine
title: the row view is a spreadsheet, for familiarity
decision: D-249
state: dropped
brief: The verdict still draws (D-232); what changes is that the surface is one a person already knows. FormatRows is already a table with the first column pinned and horizontal scroll for wide formats — this is about it reading as a spreadsheet rather than as a report. Where it breaks: the thirty-one-column ingredient format drove the current design and is no longer the ruler (M20's review), so do not design for it; and D-125 is not negotiable here — accent marks what you can act on, positive reports what the app found, and accent never colours a figure, which is exactly what a spreadsheet's conventions will tempt you to break.

### M24 U0
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: parity becomes lopsided: the tool half is mandatory, a missing screen is a derived gap
landed: 2026-09-23
decision: D-249
state: built
brief: D-249 (order amended by D-250). Before any M24 tool lands. In src/parity.test.ts: a capability with no tool still fails the build, as today. A capability with a tool and no screen becomes a 'screen trails' gap the test DERIVES rather than an entry someone writes — its proof is the existing 'nobody on that side calls it' shape against ui/, so it goes red the day a screen lands. Keep the hand-written gaps for the no-tool side (they must now all be closed or justified). Print or export the screen-trails list so M26 (the UI milestone) can read its scope from it. The ledger is NOT exempt: its capabilities have both halves and must keep them. Where it breaks: 'M22 U6 #2' — the proof greps two folders, so a call reached one file further out (data/storage/encoding.ts) stayed green while untrue; decide here whether the proof follows imports. D-234 is the mechanism being amended; read it, not the whole test.

### M24 U1
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: the survey: every ledger table, and what the engine cannot yet hold
landed: 2026-09-23
decision: D-252
state: built
brief: D-249. Produces M24's remaining units and nothing else — no code. For each non-fmt_ table in the schema (docs/04-data-model.md, schema 37): what declared format would hold it, and which engine capability is missing (a type, a constraint, a link shape, a tool, intake binding a link). Separately list the ledger LOGIC that is not a table — categorisation rules, the category tree, fixed-expense detection, statement reconciliation against a printed total, fund fees, FX — because M25's first question is whether a pack may carry code. Expected on the list: parity gap #11 (a tool makes a connection), H10 (a file column fills a link), 'M22 U3 #6' (name a link target by name), 'M22 U3 #7' (unique column). Write each gap as a planned unit row with a brief, scope engine, milestone M24. Where it breaks: sizing M25 honestly — say which tables are easy and which (money, holdings, the self-linked category tree against D-224's one hop) are not.

### M24 U10
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: a reading writes its own header row
landed: 2026-09-24
decision: D-262
state: built
brief: D-252: reconciliation against a printed total becomes a declaration. A statement file is one header (printed total, billing period, card) and many lines. Intake reads lines onto one format; this lets a reading also write ONE row of a second format from fixed values and cells outside the table (the printed total's position - a model answers with a position, never a value, D-137), and link every line to it. Then the statement format carries an aggregate (sum of its lines' amount) and an identity (sum = printed_total, exact on money) and the ledger's printed-total check (import_session.printed_total_*) is declared. format_intake_row should record the header row too, so supersede removes it. The bank running-balance check stays code.

### M24 U11
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: removing a linked row names what dangles
landed: 2026-09-24
decision: D-263
state: built
brief: delete_format_rows (formatRowRepository.deleteRows) removes a row other rows link to and says nothing: the links dangle (foreign_keys is never ON, D-067). A dangling link is refused on write but created silently on delete. Ledger need: deleting a category or an account with transactions. The dry run must list, per linking format, how many rows point at each deleted row; the commit refuses unless the caller says what happens to them - recommended: refuse, and let the caller re-link or clear first (no cascade, which is the same silent loss one step removed). Same rule for both halves (parity).

### M24 U12
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: a pack's seeded rows
landed: 2026-09-24
decision: D-264
state: built
brief: A pack is more than declarations: the ledger ships a category tree, built-in deduction types, institutions and BUILT_IN category rules. Measurement units already do this (origin SEEDED vs USER, D-212/D-223) so a later build can correct its own rows without overwriting a person's edit. Generalise: a declaration may carry seed rows, each tagged as the pack's, reseeded idempotently on open, and a row a person edited becomes theirs. Decide where origin lives without adding an undeclared column to a declared table (D-209, FormatIntakeRow's argument) - likely a side table like format_intake_row. The .format.md artefact needs a rows section.

### M24 U2
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: a tool makes a connection
landed: 2026-09-23
decision: D-253
state: built
brief: Parity gap #11 (open_question 'parity gap #11'), owed by M24 per D-251. #/connections writes a link column on one format and, optionally, the aggregate folded back across it, in one drag, through adoptFormat. The tool half: one shaped tool (e.g. connect_formats) that takes from-format, link column name, target format, and optionally the aggregate (name, kind, expression, unit) on the target; dry-runs to the reconciler plan and writes nothing until commit:true. It must reuse the repository function the screen calls, so parity.test.ts derives the capability as having both halves and the OWED gap disappears. Where it breaks: a link added to a format that already holds rows is an ordinary add (links are nullable); an aggregate whose over_column does not point back must be refused as declare_format refuses it. Answer the open_question row when landed.

### M24 U3
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: add a column and take a format away, as tools
landed: 2026-09-23
decision: D-254
state: built
brief: The other two OWED engine gaps in docs/parity.md (D-251): 'Add a column to a format that already exists' and 'Take a format away, with its table and every row in it' - both adoptFormat/deleteFormat, both with a screen and no tool. Add-a-column: a tool that takes one or more column declarations and applies only the additive step (the reconciler already refuses a required column on a table with rows - report that refusal, do not work around it). Take-a-format-away: D-251 says it may become a REFUSAL instead; decide here. Recommended: a tool, dry-run showing the row count and every other format whose link targets it, commit:true required, and refuse while another format links to it. Also add an identity (open_question 'M23 U2 #1') if it is the same path - it is the one alteration that loses nothing.

### M24 U4
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: a unique column
landed: 2026-09-23
decision: D-255
state: built
brief: Answers open_question 'M22 U3 #7'. A declared column may say unique; ddl.ts compiles it to a UNIQUE index (ddl.ts says today it has none). Ledger need: transaction.natural_key refuses a re-imported line (the duplicate check today is code); security_position is 1:1 with holding, so unique must work on a link column too; security.isin. Refuse the write at the moment it is made, naming the column and the existing row (D-242's first half). Reconciler: adding unique to a column that already holds duplicates is a fifth thing it will not decide by itself - list the duplicates. Text storage: D-245 already refuses duplicate names for link targets at serialization; this makes that a declared fact rather than a special case. Composite uniqueness is NOT needed by the ledger (staged_row's pair goes away) - do not build it.

### M24 U5
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: a link written by its target's name
landed: 2026-09-23
decision: D-256
state: built
brief: Answers open_question 'M22 U3 #6', and part of 'M22 U6 #1'. Needs U4: record_format_rows / update_format_rows may give a link the value a person uses (the target's naming column, e.g. ref 'D-252') instead of the generated id, provided that column is declared unique. Resolve names to ids before the write; a name that matches nothing is refused as a dangling id is today; a self-referential batch (rows naming each other) resolves in one pass. D-241 already reads links back as names and D-245 writes them to text as names, so this closes the write path. Weigh against open_question 'Stable ids' - if ids become written and stable, this is still worth it for people and models, but say so. Ledger need: category_rule.target -> category by name, paycheck_line -> deduction_type by name.

### M24 U6
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: fixed-point, month and instant types, and a ratio dimension
landed: 2026-09-23
decision: D-257
state: built
brief: The ledger stores quantity_micro, price_micro, rate_micro and basis points as scaled integers; the engine's only non-integer is REAL (and quantity is REAL, D-225), which is the float the money rule forbids in anything money-adjacent. Add a fixed-point number: an integer column with a declared scale (or a decimal type) that stores an integer and reads and writes a decimal string - decide which in a decision. Also a month type (YYYY-MM: billing_period, salary_month, paycheck.period, applied_to_period) and an instant type (committed_at, last_used_at) - the type enum is closed (D-114), so each is a declaration-language change and the language integer may need to move. And a RATIO dimension in measurement_unit (%, bp) so fee_bp is a unit and not a convention. Keep D-223: no conversion from a ratio into money is added.

### M24 U7
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: row constraints: required-when, at-most-one-of, sign-when
landed: 2026-09-24
decision: D-259
state: built
brief: D-252: the ledger's cross-column CHECKs are three shapes, and each is declared as structure that the engine compiles to a CHECK - never free-text SQL (D-216). Required-when: debt.auto_track_ref is required iff auto_track_kind is not NONE; allocation.basis_points vs amount_minor by mode. At-most-one-of: the ledger's polymorphic references become several ordinary links with at most one set (deduction_type.linked_*, split_bucket target, debt_payment/saving_deposit source) - D-252 rejected a link that names any of N formats. Sign-when: transaction amount <= 0 when direction is OUTFLOW, >= 0 when INFLOW - a money column takes no bounds today. The reconciler treats adding one to a table with rows as re-constraining (refused, lists the violators). The .format.md artefact needs a syntax for them. The category two-level cap (migration 005's trigger) is NOT one of these - leave it to code.

### M24 U8
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: an aggregate over only the rows that match
landed: 2026-09-24
decision: D-260
state: built
brief: An aggregate folds every row whose link names this row (D-224). The ledger needs a filter: net = gross minus the paycheck lines whose deduction_type reduces net; an account's outflows; the lines of a statement that are not transfers. Add an optional 'where' to an aggregate: an enum or boolean column of the source row, or one hop through its link (deduction_type.reduces_net), equal to a member. Stateless and relational, so it is inside the engine's line. No OR, no ranges beyond what an enum member says - keep it the smallest thing that answers the paycheck identity, and prove it on a synthetic payslip.

### M24 U9
milestone: M24
scope: engine
title: a file column fills a link (H10)
landed: 2026-09-24
decision: D-261
state: built
brief: Answers open_question H10. Intake may bind a link key only to a fixed value today (format_intake_column). Needs U5 (and so U4): a file column may fill a link by the target's unique name column - a card statement holding several cards fills account from card_last4, a category column fills category by name. A cell that names nothing is a row the reading refuses, reported by row, never a silently empty link. Prove it on a real document through read_onto_format - no real document has been through intake at all (status.md), and real statements are in the real-documents folder (never write to a real .emd).

### M25 E1
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: unique across several columns
landed: 2026-09-24
decision: D-270
state: built
brief: D-267, answers open question M25 U1 #1. A declaration says that two or more columns are unique together — a transaction's natural_key per account, a fund snapshot per fund and day, an allocation per plan and bucket, a price per security, day and source. A fourth table in the .format.md (one row per unique set), a language bump, a UNIQUE index named like D-255's, refusals by the reconciler when existing rows already collide, and row.ts refusing the write with the columns named before SQLite does. Also: a filtered unique ('one remainder per plan': unique on plan where mode is REMAINDER) — decide whether it is the same unit or refused. Screen half and tool half both (D-234). Then declare the ledger's nine in src/data/packs/ledger/formats — their tables are still empty, so the change is free.

### M25 E2
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: a rollup by period
landed: 2026-09-24
decision: D-271
state: built
brief: D-267. A declared figure keyed by a period and optionally a link: 'spending: sum of amount of transaction by billing_period and category, where direction is OUTFLOW'. Closed vocabulary: the existing aggregate kinds, a month (or date truncated to a month) column to group by, at most one link column to group by, the existing D-260 filter. Compiled to one SQL GROUP BY — never folded in TypeScript; time it against the 60k-row copy. Money sums group by currency too, and a rollup with a row that cannot answer is unchecked for that group only. Where it is shown: a declared view on the format's screen and a tool (format_rollup or a mode of format_rows) — both halves (D-234). Update the line stated at the head of src/domain/format/aggregate.ts ('temporal is code'), which this makes false. Proof: the ledger's month-by-category statistics reproduced by a declaration on a fixture, figure for figure.

### M25 E3
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: the latest by an order
landed: 2026-09-25
decision: D-272
state: built
brief: D-267. A new aggregate kind, LATEST (and EARLIEST): over the rows that point here, pick the one with the greatest (least) ordering column — a date, month, instant or integer — and read one of its columns: 'price: latest price of security_price.security by as_of'. Ties are a refusal to guess: the verdict is unchecked and says which rows tie. Still one hop (D-224). Compiled to SQL where it can be. Use cases: the latest price, a fund's current balance, a task's current status. Declare the ledger's in its formats (security, fund_account) once built.

### M25 E4
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: a matching rule
landed: 2026-09-25
decision: D-273
state: built
brief: D-267. A format may be declared a rule set over another: its rows set a link column of the target format when a text column matches. The declaration names the target column, the text column(s) matched, and which of its own columns carry the match kind (closed list: equals, contains, starts with, regex — decide whether regex belongs), the pattern, the link to set and the priority. Applying is the engine's: on a write of the target, and on demand with a dry run. User overrides win: the target declares a source column the engine writes (rule/person), and a rule never overwrites a person's value. The ledger's category_rule becomes the first rule set, replacing its matcher for the kinds the list covers; learning from corrections, provider-category mapping and model suggestions stay pack code. Screen and tool both (D-234).

### M25 E5
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: a pack owns its formats: read anywhere, written only through the pack
landed: 2026-09-25
decision: D-274
state: built
brief: D-269, answers open question M25 U2 #1. Before U3. The engine learns which formats a pack owns (the ledger's 31 — decide where it is stored: a column on `format`, or derived from the pack's declarations the way format_seed already half-knows). Every generic row write refuses on an owned format — record_format_rows, update_format_rows, delete_format_rows, supersede_reading/read_onto_format, and the #/formats/<name> screen's add/edit/delete — in one sentence naming the pack's own screen and tool for that format (the pack supplies the sentence). Reads stay open everywhere. Declarations: extend_format and the Formats screen may add a person's own column to an owned format but refuse changing or dropping a pack column; delete_format refuses an owned format. The pack's code keeps writing through insertRows/updateRow/deleteRows, so the lock sits in the generic callers, not the row functions — check parity.test.ts still derives every write. Proof: the Formats screen and update_format_rows both refuse a third-level category, and the Categories screen still makes a second-level one. The rail/graph grouping is M26's, not this unit's.

### M25 U0
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: the plan: a pack may carry code, and the order the ledger moves in
landed: 2026-09-24
decision: D-265
state: built
brief: The author answered M25's first question: a pack is declarations, seeds and code; code writes SQL only against its own fmt_ tables, fenced by a test. Produced M25 U1-U8 and nothing else.

### M25 U1
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: the ledger is a pack: its declarations, its seeds, and the fence on its SQL
landed: 2026-09-24
decision: D-266
state: built
brief: D-265. Write the ledger's .format.md: one declared format for every ledger table in docs/packs/ledger/onto-formats.md except the intake machinery (import_mapping, import_session, staged_row, document_template, reapply_*, app_setting), using U6-U12's types, constraints, uniques and filtered aggregates. Seeds under ## rows: the category tree and groups, deduction types, built-in category rules. On open, a document with the pack on gets reconcileFormats plus seedRows for the pack (D-264 said M25 calls it). Nothing reads the fmt_ tables yet, and no old table is touched. The fence: a boundary test that the ledger's repositories name only tables the pack declares, the engine's own, or the old tables still on a list that shrinks each unit — that list is M25's progress bar, and it must be empty at U8. Proof: a fresh document gets every table and seed, and each constraint refuses a bad row. Where it breaks: a table the survey called easy that the language still cannot say — write it down rather than bend it; and whether the pack being on is per document now (open question 'M20 U0 #1') — decide it here, since the tables live in the file.

### M25 U2
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: the conversion, and the reference tables move first
landed: 2026-09-24
decision: D-268
state: built
brief: D-265, D-266. Build the conversion machinery once: on open, a document holding rows in an old table gets a backup, its rows copied into the fmt_ table with id = derivedId(table + old id), the old tables that still point at the group rebuilt with TEXT foreign keys onto the fmt_ table, and the old table dropped — in one transaction, idempotent, refusing rather than guessing (a value that does not type is a refusal naming the row). Group: institution (aliases become institution_alias rows), account, category_group, category, category_rule, plus the merchant groups and settlement aliases if they are tables. Rows copied from migration seeds (cat:, grp:, inst:, ded: ids) are recorded SEEDED in format_seed so they stay the pack's (D-266); soft-deleted rows are not copied. Once the old table is gone, installLedgerPack seeds what is missing — and the built-in rules, which were code, become rows: the rule engine reads them from fmt_category_rule and the rules.builtIn setting goes. The two-level category cap moves from the old triggers into pack code. Take the tables off STILL_OLD in fence.test.ts. Rewrite their repositories; numeric ids in their screens and routes become text. Every ledger screen behaves as today; parity holds. Proven on a copy of a real .emd (the real-documents folder — never the file itself): row counts match before and after, and the category screen reads the same. Where it breaks: a real document with two categories or two rules of the same name, since both names are unique now.

### M25 U3
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: transactions and statements move
landed: 2026-09-26
decision: D-275
state: built
brief: D-265, U2's machinery. Group: transaction, card_settlement_link, category_override, reapply_run and reapply_change. natural_key becomes a unique column (D-255); the sign rule a row constraint (D-259); billing_period a month and exchange_rate fixed-point (D-257). import_session and import_mapping become format_intake and its reading (D-262's header row carries the printed total, so reconciliation is declared); answer the survey's staged_row question — the ledger's review persists staged rows across a save and a dry run does not. The 60k-row document is the proof: time the conversion and the transactions screen on a copy of the real one, and write the figures down (open question 'M22 U6 #4').

### M25 U3b
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: intake moves: the importer onto format_intake
landed: 2026-09-26
decision: D-277
state: built
brief: D-275 split it from U3; D-276: no data is carried. Group: import_mapping and staged_row, the two tables still on STILL_OLD from U3's group. Their repositories and the importer move onto the pack's or the engine's tables; on open the old tables are dropped with whatever they hold (convert.ts's drop, no rows copied). import_mapping becomes a reading the engine keeps (format_intake / format_intake_column, D-226) or a pack format if the ledger's column roles, amount conventions and date orders will not fit one — decide which, and write down what the engine's reading lacks. Then statement.mapping becomes a link (it is text since U3). Answer the staged_row question: the ledger's review survives closing the app mid-import (F01) and an engine dry run does not — either staged rows become a pack format, or a review is a dry run held in memory and the resume goes; put that choice to the maintainer. Every ledger screen behaves as today; parity holds. Proof: import a real statement from the real-documents folder into a fresh document, the printed-total check agreeing.

### M25 U4
milestone: M25
scope: engine
title: holdings, investments and FX move
landed: 2026-09-26
decision: D-278
state: built
brief: D-265, D-276: no data is carried. Group: holding_place, holding, security, security_price, security_trade (links transaction), security_position (its key is its link to holding: a unique link column), fx_rate. Repositories move onto the pack's tables; on open the old tables are dropped with whatever they hold, nothing copied. quantity_micro, price_micro and rate_micro become fixed-point columns (D-257); fee_bp and expected_return_bp ratios. The latest price is declared since E3 (security.price, D-272): decide whether the pack's code reads it — a price per source per day ties, and the declared figure is then unchecked where the code picks by source; a `where` on source may be the answer. FX by date stays pack code. Proof: read a real broker statement (the real-documents folder/investments) into a fresh document through the moved code; M16's unproven broker-statement fix is still owed and is not this unit's.
undone_by: D-283

