### M28 F5
milestone: M28
scope: ledger
title: a salary comes in once, and the slip and the deposit must agree
landed: 2026-10-02
state: built
brief: 2026-10-02, on Home's Month view: a paycheck was counted twice and asserted nowhere — shouldn't it be? What came in showed both the slip's net pay ('Salary net pay, off the slip') and the employer's bank deposit categorised Salary, and Came in summed the two. (1) A salary is counted once: the slip and the bank deposit it paid are one income, not two. Find how came-in is built today (slip net pay plus Salary-category deposits) and decide which side is the figure and which explains it; record a decision. (2) It must be asserted: a slip and the deposit that paid it are matched (by employer and pay date, a month's lag allowed — a slip for one month is often paid early the next), and the slip's net pay must equal the deposit. A match that agrees is proved quietly; one that disagrees is shown, with the gap, where it will be seen (Home's What came in and the paycheck); a slip with no deposit, or a Salary deposit with no slip, says so too. Never silently pick one. The two figures differed — find out with a synthetic reproduction whether that is a month lag, a second deposit, or a real gap, and make each case read right. The engine's way is an identity that is proved, failed or unchecked; use it if it fits the ledger here, otherwise the ledger's own reconciliation, and say which. Parity: a conversation must see the same match and the same gap (the paychecks / month_summary tools).
