### M29 F11
milestone: M29
scope: engine
title: A found-by rule can be applied to the rows already there
landed: 2026-10-04
decision: D-353
state: built
brief: "Author, 2026-10-04: \"yes add the apply button\".\n\n**The gap.** M29 F8 (45fe5a3, D-350) added a link column's `found_by` rule, for example work_day.paycheck found by date in period. It is a write-time rule that fills an empty link when a row is saved, so declaring it changes no existing rows. A sheet of work days already entered stays unlinked until each day is saved again, and the paycheck's total misses them.\n\n**What to add.** An \"Apply to existing rows\" action, the way rule sets already have Apply (find how `apply_rules` and the rule-set Apply on screen work, and mirror them). It runs the found_by rule over every row whose link is empty:\n- It dry-runs first and shows what it will do: \"12 work days get a paycheck\", plus the rows it cannot place, each named with its reason (\"no paycheck for March\", \"two paychecks for November\").\n- On confirm it writes the ones it can place.\n- It never touches a link that is already set, including one a person set by hand.\n- Rows it cannot place stay empty and are listed.\n\n**Where it appears.** In the connection window next to the \"Found by\" control: a line like \"8 work days have no paycheck yet — Apply\", shown only when there are some. It is also offered right after the rule is first set.\n\n**Parity (D-227/D-234).** A conversation can do it too: extend `apply_rules` or add the smallest tool shape that fits the existing surface (check `docs/plugin.md` and the plugin-tool-surface memory note: shaped tools only, take lists). `src/parity.test.ts` must stay green."
