### M33 U3a
milestone: M33
scope: engine
title: Computed and Claims: two names for what has to add up
landed: 2026-10-07
decision: D-417
state: built
brief: Author, 2026-10-07: 'what has to add up' is too descriptive as the on-screen name for a declared identity. He considered Enforcements (rejected: an identity never refuses a row, it reports; enforcing is what a type does) and Checks (rejected: reads as cheques, money-ish). The name is CLAIMS: a claim is proved, failed or unchecked, which is exactly the three verdicts, and it says what Emerald is for (a person or a model asserts something about the data; Emerald proves it or says it does not hold). Rename every on-screen and model-facing occurrence (about 50 in src/ including the declare screen's 'Has to add up? Yes', the Formats page, the format artefact's wording and its error messages, the MCP tool descriptions and instructions, the receipt tour's captions and storyboard) and the docs that describe the screen; the engine's internal name 'identity' in code may stay where only code reads it, the rule being one name per thing for a person and a model. Keep the .format.md artefact readable for files already written (no migration of old documents, author 2026-09-26) only if it costs nothing; otherwise the new word alone. The development pack's 'claimed_by' column on unit is a pack column, not an engine word; leave it, and note it for U6 if the developer tour would show both on one screen. A decision row records the name and the two rejected. Done-test: no on-screen or tool-facing 'has to add up' remains (grep), the tours pass check-tours, and a declared identity reads as a claim everywhere a person or a model meets it. Author, same day: worked-out columns (a derived column, an aggregate such as a sum over linked rows) and identities sit under one heading today, which is confusing; SEPARATE THEM. Two headings, nouns: COMPUTED for the columns whose value is worked out (they produce a value, are read-only, never fail, go blank when a row cannot answer) and CLAIMS for the identities (they produce a verdict). A claim that names a computed column refers to a column the person can already see. The model-facing 'worked-out figure' wording moves to Computed too. On a tool's input keys a model fills (e.g. identities in a declaration): rename what a model reads; rename a key only if it is cheap and the old key keeps working or nothing outside the repo depends on it; say which was done.
