### M33 U7
milestone: M33
scope: engine
title: On top of a pack: the timesheet tour
landed: 2026-10-07
decision: D-429
state: built
brief: The example (2026-10-07): a timesheet declared on top of the ledger and compared to the paycheck. The rule for it, same day: it is a NEW format with a connection to the ledger, never an extension of the paycheck's columns. STOP first: ask the maintainer before declaring anything, because a timesheet format already exists in a real document; the session asks for its declaration and builds the tour on that shape, with synthetic figures only and nothing from that document. The tour declares the format, connects it (a link to the paycheck or to its month), sums hours over the month, and holds an identity against the paycheck in whatever terms that format already states; one line in the storyboard turns the identity failed, and the visitor takes over to fix it. A storyboard the maintainer approves before it is ported. Done-test: the tour runs to its last caption under the check, the identity shows all three verdicts along the way, and the paycheck format is unchanged. 2026-10-07, THE TIMESHEET'S SHAPE (the STOP is answered): one row per day: date, start hour, end hour, hours (computed from start and end), hour rate (money), and the day's gross earning (computed: hours x rate). The connection: the sum of the days' gross earnings in one month must equal the gross of that month's paycheck (a claim). Build the tour on exactly that shape, synthetic figures only. If the engine cannot state 'the days of the same month as the paycheck' without a link from each day to its paycheck (one hop, D-224), use the link and say so; if the claim can only sit on the paycheck's side, declare it without changing the paycheck format's columns, or say why it cannot and propose the honest alternative. Driver, 2026-10-07: the maintainer tests after (no storyboard STOP; ported unapproved, noted at the storyboard's top). Apply what the other tours taught: open on the question the person has and end on its answer; few, short captions; each box the eye should be on outlined; nothing named but Emerald; generic synthetic names (no employer that reads as real).
