### M33 U8a
milestone: M33
scope: engine
title: Emerald's page as a mockup: the story, the four tours, the AI half and the packs, in the maintainer's words
landed: 2026-10-12
state: built
brief: "STOP at the end: the maintainer approves the mockup or says what to change; nothing is ported until then (M33 U8 ports it, and keeps the blockers this unit does not need: the deploy and the README). A mockup of Emerald's page, the launch page at getemerald.dev (bought 2026-10-08), as a static HTML page in docs/mockups/m33-u8/, in the app's look (src/ui/theme/tokens.css; the rule rows with scope ui), light and dark, readable at 375 and 1440 wide.\n\nWhat it holds, from U8's brief: a hero with the maintainer's story in the maintainer's words (a notes app was clunky and held the data on its servers, a markdown-notes app had no structure, a spreadsheet did not stack up once things connected) and the line 'Try to make it wrong'; the four tours stacked (custom, ledger, development, timesheet), each with one sentence above it and nothing explaining the engine, embedded as the real frame from a local dist-demo/ build by M33 U4's contract (?tour=<name>&autoplay=1, referrerpolicy=no-referrer, allow=\"\"), playing as watched crops below 690; the AI half as a placeholder for the 60-90 second recording (a model reads the custom tour's grocery receipt PNG through the MCP server, writes the receipt and its lines, and the identity against the card charge catches the 12.00), with the recording's script drafted beside the mockup; at the foot an email field and 'What would you use it for?', one line on what the address is for, and where a privacy-respecting count would go. The mockup sends nothing: the form and the count are drawn, not wired (their requests, PRIVACY.md and the decision row are U8's).\n\nNew since U8 was written: longer copy for this site (inbox row from a second project, 2026-10-11): 'Integrating known concepts into new ideas', 'Infinite possibilities', and two sections started and left empty, The ledger and The development pack, each pack described on its own, which is the depth this site is for. Use the words as written; a typo fixed or a sentence finished (the development-pack one stops mid-list) is a proposal shown to the maintainer, never silent. Draft the two empty pack sections as proposals marked as such. The privacy line follows the embedding site's correction: 'runs on your machine, with no account and no server, and sends nothing anywhere itself', never 'your data never leaves it' (what a model reads in a conversation goes to its provider). The two sites agree: the problem, what it is, and local-and-yours as the embedding site now says them (the inbox row quotes them). The README is being rewritten (M43 U1), so the page quotes nothing of the current README but line 1, the tagline. Naming: the story names two products, which D-450's exception for the maintainer's own story allows; ask the maintainer whether a public page keeps the names.\n\n2026-10-12, on the first pass (3231ff9): the names stay in the story; proposals 1 to 10 accepted as written, so the marks go and the words are the page's. The look: too basic — a font change (unsure), animations that guide the reader through the map, and reuse of the app's own elements: the page could be the roadmap element on the left that a reader walks through. Second pass (3e48ade): the app's type shipped beside the page (the first pass fell back to the system face), the page laid out as a roadmap — the timeline rail on the left, solid to the section you are at and dashed after, its dot live in accent, advancing as you scroll, a dot a link to its section — and the sections as the rows. APPROVED 2026-10-12: the second pass is right. The mockup's text and layout are what M33 U8 ports.\n\nDone-test: the mockup opens from a launcher in .claude/launch.json; the four tours play in it from a local build; it reads at 375 and 1440 in both schemes; nothing leaves the origin; every word that is a proposal rather than the maintainer's is marked; and the maintainer has approved it or said what to change, written into this row."
