state: built
brief: Author, 2026-10-08: the prose notes are mostly lists with prose around them, and the project's notes should be read in Emerald, as the engine is for. This reverses CLAUDE.md's 'the prose notes never move' (D-240 era); a decision row and log entry record the reversal and why (the reader of these notes is someone who opens them in Emerald; one place per rule; the read bill). Declare a project-specific `rule` format in docs/memory.emd, NOT in the development pack (src/data/packs/development stays as it is; this is the person's own format beside the pack's). Columns to start from, the session's to refine: statement (the bold line, verbatim), why, kind (law | non_negotiable | trap | style), scope (engine | shell | a pack's name), decided_by -> decision (a list of links if a rule has several decisions behind it, which is one of the things this pilot finds out), enforced_by (the test file that fails when it breaks, empty when it is kept by hand), state (live | retired). Move into rows: conventions.md's 'Non-negotiable rules', 'Things that will bite you' (with 'The shell's') and 'The findings that outlive the milestone that found them', and CLAUDE.md's 'Rules that are easy to break by accident', merged where they say the same thing. CLAUDE.md's list becomes a few lines and the format_rows query that lists the rest (CLAUDE.md is a file Claude Code loads, so it stays a file; it points, it does not copy). conventions.md keeps only what is not item-shaped. Measure and report the unknowns, each one either fixed or written down as a finding: (1) how a multi-line text cell is written in a text .emd and what its git diff looks like; (2) how the sheet shows a long text column; (3) whether a view can list rules whose decided_by decision is superseded (claims are about figures, so this is a view or a filter, and if the engine cannot say it that is an open_question); (4) whether the 'Code facts' open question is answered by this format, and answer or narrow it. Done-test: every rule from those sections is a row or is named in the report as deliberately left in prose; CLAUDE.md and conventions.md point to the query instead of copying; docs:check passes; the report lists what did not fit.
