### M41 F1
milestone: M41
scope: engine
title: The frame wears the host's colours and type, and keeps Emerald's meaning
landed: 2026-10-08
decision: D-445
state: built
brief: Author, 2026-10-08, after seeing the frame drawn in VS Code's Copilot Chat: can the frame's background be transparent and its text and components fit whatever app draws it? Yes: MCP Apps hands a view the host's theme in ui/initialize's hostContext (theme light|dark, styles.variables: an optional subset of background, text, border, ring, typography and radius tokens; host fonts as @font-face css, whose origin the view must allow in _meta.ui.csp), and again on ui/notifications/host-context-changed. Check the field names against the ext-apps spec and McpUiHostContext on the day. Do it, without a dependency if the protocol is small (as src/frame/host.ts already is): (1) the frame's page background is transparent, so it sits on the host's surface; (2) the host's variables, when given, map onto Emerald's own tokens for surfaces, ink, hairlines, radii and the body font, so text and quiet controls look native in each host; every one falls back to Emerald's token when the host gives none (the stand-in shows the fallback); (3) the host's fonts are used when it offers them, which also answers the dropped-font finding of M41 U1/U2 (the frame's CSP refuses IBM Plex); (4) WHAT CARRIES MEANING STAYS EMERALD'S: the accent marks what can be acted on (D-125, the rule rows of scope ui), and the verdict colours (proved, failed, unchecked) and the negative figure stay Emerald's in every host, checked for contrast against the host's background in light and dark; a host's accent never replaces them. (5) Re-apply on host-context-changed (a theme switch while the view is open). Teach the stand-in (scripts/try-frame.mjs) to send a host theme (e.g. --host-theme=vscode-dark with a plausible variable set) so both a themed and an unthemed host can be checked. Done-test: on the stand-in, with host variables the frame is transparent and takes the host's text, surface and font, with none it looks as today; accent and verdicts are Emerald's in both, and pass contrast in light and dark; in VS Code's Copilot Chat (the first real host, which draws it) the frame reads as part of the chat; a theme switch in VS Code restyles an open view.
