### M41 F4
milestone: M41
scope: engine
title: The frame is transparent in every host, not only one that names its background
landed: 2026-10-08
decision: D-448
state: built
brief: 2026-10-08, VS Code Copilot Chat (dark theme, Auto routed), on b79f973 (M41 F3, D-447): the roadmap drew (F2/F3 work, through Auto too) but sat on its own darker panel, visibly a box on the chat's surface, while text and font fitted. Cause: M41 F1 (D-445, src/frame/hostStyle.ts) makes the page transparent only when the host names its background in hostContext.styles.variables, and VS Code evidently sends no background variable (or no styles). Change the rule: the frame's page background is transparent in every host, because the host's own surface is what it sits on; Emerald's surfaces stay only where they mean something inside the view (a card, a popover, the elevated row, a selected row), and only where the app draws them opaque today on purpose; ink and hairlines come from the host's variables when given, else from Emerald's tokens for the theme the host names (hostContext.theme), else from prefers-color-scheme. Contrast: where the host gives no background, the frame cannot measure it; say what it assumes (the theme's usual surface) and keep the 4.5:1 guard where it can measure. Also log, to the host's log (the frame's existing log line), exactly what the host handed in hostContext (theme, the style variable names it sent, fonts present or not), so VS Code's real palette is known, and say how the maintainer can read it (VS Code shows a webview's console in Developer: Open Webview Developer Tools, if that reaches an MCP App frame; find out). Teach the stand-in a host theme with a theme but no style variables (as VS Code appears to send) and verify the frame is transparent there in light and dark. Note that Copilot still 'reviewed skill closing-pass' after F3 narrowed its description: say whether VS Code reads .claude/skills on every request regardless, and whether anything in this repo can stop it (do not move or delete the skill). Rebuild dist-plugin at the end. Done-test: on the stand-in with a theme and no style variables, the frame shows no panel of its 