### M41 U1
milestone: M41
scope: engine
title: The spike: one format's rows drawn in the conversation, and where it shows
landed: 2026-10-08
decision: D-431
state: built
brief: Prove the mechanism before building views. MCP Apps (SEP-1865, an official MCP extension since 2026-01-26): a tool carries _meta.ui.resourceUri pointing at a ui:// resource whose content is HTML (text/html;profile=mcp-app); a host that supports it fetches it and draws it in a sandboxed frame in the conversation, and a host that does not shows the tool's text result. Add one read-only tool to the server (e.g. `show`, taking path and format; name it with the one-name rule) whose text result is what format_rows answers today and whose UI draws that format's rows. NO DUPLICATE CODE (2026-10-08): the frame must run the app's own components, not views rewritten for it. So the expected shape is the M33 U1 demo entry's: the frame gets the document's bytes (from the tool's result) and opens them with documentStore.openBytes on the same engine (sql.js), so the same store, components and engine draw it. The risk to measure first: whether the host's sandbox lets sql.js's wasm run inline in the frame (CSP, wasm-unsafe-eval, the bundle and document size it can be handed). Only if it cannot does the server hand the frame rows instead, and then the session says which components would need a second data path and stops for the maintainer before writing any, because that is the duplication ruled out. Whether to depend on the official @modelcontextprotocol/ext-apps package or write the small protocol by hand is the session's call, said; no runtime request either way: the HTML, its script and the wasm are inline in the resource, and the frame makes no request (D-124's spirit). Measure where it draws: the Claude desktop app's Code tab (reported to draw MCP Apps since 2026-05-08), a Claude chat on desktop and web, and the claude CLI in a terminal (expected: text only); say which the maintainer can see without setup. Parity (D-227/D-234): a read, not a write, so no new capability; say so if parity.test.ts asks. Done-test: in the desktop Code tab, asking a session to show docs/memory.emd's milestone format draws it inline with the app's own sheet component on the real engine; the CLI gets the same rows as text; the server's tests pass and build:plugin still writes one file (or says why the UI resource is a second one).
files: src/plugin/tools/sheet.ts, src/plugin/server.ts, src/plugin/tools/tool.ts, src/plugin/catalog.ts, src/plugin/server.test.ts, src/frame/contract.ts, src/frame/host.ts, src/frame/host.test.ts, src/frame/main.tsx, frame.html, vite.config.ts, package.json, src/types/assets.d.ts, .gitignore, docs/plugin.md
taken_by: run-milestone driver
started: 2026-10-08

### M41 U2
milestone: M41
scope: engine
title: Emerald's own views in the frame, every control leading somewhere
landed: 2026-10-08
decision: D-432
state: built
brief: U1 (e966e1a, D-431) drew the sheet; only the sheet, one entry and the board are routed in the frame, so switching to Roadmap snaps back to the sheet (src/frame/main.tsx's catch-all), which failed on 2026-10-08. Draw the engine's real view kinds: the sheet, the board, the roadmap (the development pack's views), and one entry (M35's entry page), chosen by the format's own views as the app chooses them, so a pack's views show as they do in the app and no pack carries UI (M32's line). Reuse the app's components rather than redrawing them, the way M33 U1's demo entry mounts the app's screens without Open and Save. NO CONTROL LEADS NOWHERE (2026-10-08): every switch, button and link the app's components show in the frame either works there or is hidden there; check:frame or a test fails on a route the frame does not draw. Emerald's design language (docs/11-ui-design-language.md): the accent marks what can be acted on, Bidi on every name from a document, light and dark following the host. Claims show their verdicts. Read-only in this unit (typing into a cell changes only the in-memory copy today; make the frame read-only and say so, since U3 decides edits). start_here's answer is the first picture: asking 'what's next' draws the development pack's roadmap of the open milestone and what is ready, because this milestone's reason is seeing the plan live instead of a session's summary. From U1 and the stand-in: (1) the frame's CSP has no font-src, so Emerald's fonts are dropped; hold to the design language within the sandbox (an inline font the CSP admits, or the closest system stack, said); (2) every format_sheet answer carries the whole document copy (about 1.25 MB of base64 for memory.emd) even to text-only clients; send it only to a client that advertises MCP Apps (or let the frame fetch it through an app-only tool call), and measure whether that copy ever reaches the model's context; (3) the frame ignores ui/notifications/tool-cancelled and stays at 'Opening…': handle it. VERIFY ON THE STAND-IN (2026-10-08: continue M41 on the demo frame rather than hold it): `npm run frame:try -- <document> <format>`, served by the launch entry frame-try in the browser pane, is where every view is checked; it proves our side (the frame, the spec's messages, the strict CSP), not a real host's quirks. Claude Desktop on Windows shows local servers' views as text only (anthropics/claude-ai-mcp#1069); when a real host draws on a development machine, one check there closes the gap, and until then the unit says it is unproven in a real host. Live updating is M44 U4, not this unit. Size budget: say what the resource weighs and keep the server one file. Done-test: on the stand-in, docs/memory.emd draws the development pack's roadmap and board, a ledger format from a synthetic document draws its sheet, and an entry opens, matching the app's look in light and dark; no control in the frame leads nowhere; the CLI still gets text.
