### M41 U3
milestone: Later
scope: engine
title: Acting from the view: a change in the frame goes through the same tools
state: planned
brief: PARKED IN LATER, 2026-10-08: the frame stays read-only; revisit once M44's live updates exist and a real host draws the frame on a development machine. The design agreed if it comes back: no edit adapter per action (that would duplicate the app's write code); the frame lets the app's own components edit its copy as they do in the app, then hands the whole document back through one app-only tool hidden from the model ('save this document if it is unchanged since <hash>'), which the server writes with its usual copy-first and refuses when the file moved underneath. That is a new write, so D-234 needs its halves named (or a gap entry) and a decision row; hosts may confirm each frame-initiated tool call, which is friction to measure. Original brief: STOP first: ask the maintainer whether edits in the frame are wanted at all, or read-only is enough; read-only is a complete milestone. If yes: a change made in the frame (move a unit's state on the board, correct a cell) goes through the server, shows the dry run, and writes only on the person's confirm, the same commit:true rule a model follows. Concurrent writers: a text .emd is rewritten whole, so a frame open while a session writes must re-read before it writes. Done-test: a state moved on the board in the frame lands in the document, and a stale frame is refused rather than overwriting.
