report: "Built at 328ec3c; docs:check green (0 errors, 2 budget warnings, both pre-existing); typecheck, build, build:plugin green; full suite 4767/4767 in two full runs in a row (runs 4 and 5). check:tours skipped: no tour's harness or ground changed.\n\nVERSIONS. vitest ^3.2.7 -> ^4.1.12 (the current 4.x; 5.0.5 is out and was not taken, the brief says 4.x). npm ls: vitest 4.1.12, @vitest/mocker 4.1.12 (>= 4.1.11). No other @vitest/* package is a direct dependency. tinypool's override from M51 F5 is dropped: Vitest 4 removed tinypool, and no package in the lock needs it. vite stays 6.4.4 (Vitest 4 asks >= 6). npm audit: 8 moderate, all the electron-builder -> app-builder-lib -> @electron/get -> global-agent -> roarr -> sprintf-js chain (#10); no vitest advisory.\n\nMIGRATION (Vitest's own 3-to-4 guide, v4.vitest.dev/guide/migration, fetched 2026-10-18). This config used none of what it renames or removes: no poolOptions, maxThreads/singleThread, workspace, coverage, environmentMatchGlobs, deps.inline, basic reporter, or test options as a third argument. The include globs are src/electron/hosts, so the narrower default exclude changes nothing. Mocks: no vi.fn constructed with new from an arrow (seen.test.ts's IntersectionObserver is a class), no getMockName or invocationCallOrder, and server.test.ts's restoreAllMocks only restores vi.spyOn spies, which is all it had. What did break, and how:\n(1) sql.js's wasm. Vitest 4's module runner hands files under node_modules to Node, and Node 24 imports a .wasm as a WebAssembly module, so `sql.js/dist/sql-wasm.wasm?bytes` reached Node rather than wasmAsBytes and failed on emscripten's import namespace ('Cannot find package a'). Fix: test.server.deps.inline: [/\\.wasm\\?bytes$/] in vite.config.ts.\n(2) vite-node is gone (Vitest 4 replaced it with Vite's module runner). Three tests started a real process through node_modules/vite-node/vite-node.mjs: subscriptions.test.ts (the stdio server), formatWatch.test.ts (the watch command), hooks/commands.test.ts (a hook from stdin). New scripts/run-source.mjs does the same over Vite's createServer + createServerModuleRunner, closing the server after the entry's import as vite-node did; same argv shape. Re-adding vite-node was the alternative: 3.2.4 is the last on Vite 6, 6.0 needs Vite 8.\n(3) Not a Vitest 4 change but found in its full runs: parity.test.ts's two ledger tests took ~8.3 s each alone (8.9 s under vitest 3.2.7 too, measured with npx vitest@3.2.7) and timed out at 15 s under load in two runs. reachesDirectly re-split every file's text for every write; namesIn caches each file's names once (WeakMap). 1.15 s each, same answers, docs/parity.md snapshot unchanged.\n\nNOT FIXED, WATCH: subscriptions.test.ts's 'within a second' measured 1112 ms once under full load (run 3; 204-502 ms in the others). The span includes starting a second bare node process for the rename-save, which is load, not the server; it passed in runs 4 and 5. If it recurs it wants the M51 F5 treatment (measure from the rename, or CPU time).\n\nDependabot: see the landing commit; #6 and #7 checked after the push."

### M42 F4
milestone: M52
scope: engine
title: The Mac app opens: ad-hoc signed after packing, so macOS offers Open Anyway instead of calling it damaged
landed: 2026-10-19
decision: D-505
state: built
brief: Maintainer, 2026-10-19: Emerald-arm64.dmg on a work Mac, dragged to Applications, would not open: "Emerald is damaged and can't be opened", with Move to Trash. CAUSE: electron-builder.yml sets `mac.identity: null`, so electron-builder skips signing entirely (MacTargetHelper.handleNullIdentity) and not even an ad-hoc signature is applied. Packing renames Electron's app and rewrites Info.plist, which breaks the signature Electron shipped with; a quarantined bundle whose signature does not match gets 'damaged', which has no Open Anyway. So the README's Mac line (System Settings, Privacy & Security, Open Anyway; D-124's path) cannot work for anyone on any release so far. electron-builder 26.17 has no ad-hoc setting: `identity: "-"` is looked up in the keychain like a name and not found (checked in app-builder-lib/out/codeSign/macCodeSign.js). (1) THE HOOK: an `afterPack` script (scripts/, CommonJS or whatever electron-builder 26 loads) that, when `electronPlatformName` is darwin, runs `codesign --force --deep --sign - <appOutDir>/Emerald.app` — no hardened runtime, no entitlements, no identity — and fails the build when codesign fails. It runs before the dmg is made, for both arches. `identity: null` stays, so no keychain identity is ever picked up. (2) THE CHECK IN CI: release.yml's mac job verifies what it built, `codesign --verify --deep --strict --verbose=2` on the packed app (release/mac-arm64/Emerald.app and release/mac/Emerald.app), so a broken signature fails the build rather than reaching a person. (3) THE WORDS: README.md's desktop paragraph says what a Mac really shows (Apple could not verify Emerald; then System Settings, Privacy & Security, Open Anyway), and for a copy downloaded before this fix, `xattr -cr /Applications/Emerald.app` once in Terminal. release.yml's notes and src/demo/page/copy/en.ts only point at the README; check they stay true. The Mac updater still needs a real identity, so its 'needs a signed app' line stays. (4) A DECISION amending D-124: ad-hoc signed is still unsigned in the sense D-124 meant (no certificate, no account, no notarization); say so. (5) A TEST in the repo's style: the config names the hook, the hook signs with `-` only on darwin and does nothing elsewhere. Done-test: a hand-run (workflow_dispatch) rehearsal of release.yml is green with the verify step, its log showing the ad-hoc signature for both arches; docs:check is green. THE MAINTAINER'S ACT, and it is the one that matters: open the rehearsal's arm64 dmg on the work Mac; wrong is still seeing 'damaged', right is a warning whose Open Anyway opens Emerald. Cutting a release that carries it is the maintainer's call after that.
files: scripts/sign-mac.mjs (new, the afterPack hook), electron/macSigning.test.ts (new), electron-builder.yml (afterPack, header comment), .github/workflows/release.yml (the Mac verify step, a comment), README.md (the Mac line), docs/09-decision-log.md (D-505). src/demo/page/copy/en.ts and the release notes only point at the README and stay true.
taken_by: claude-code desktop session
started: 2026-10-19
branch: main
commit: f7f69e2
paths: "electron-builder.yml\nscripts/\n.github/workflows/release.yml\nREADME.md\nsrc/demo/page/copy/en.ts"
report: Built on main at f7f69e2. The hook scripts/sign-mac.mjs ad-hoc signs the packed Mac app (afterPack, darwin only); electron-builder.yml keeps identity: null. Loaded through electron-builder's own resolveFunction on Windows: returns afterPack, no call on win32, codesign --force --deep --sign - on darwin. electron/macSigning.test.ts (3 tests) and typecheck pass. Release rehearsal (workflow_dispatch) run 39231019265 green on both runners, release job skipped as a rehearsal: the Mac log shows release/mac-arm64/Emerald.app and release/mac/Emerald.app 'valid on disk', 'satisfies its Designated Requirement', Signature=adhoc, arm64 and x86_64. README now says what a Mac shows and gives xattr -cr for copies of 0.1.2 or earlier. PROVED ON A REAL MAC, 2026-10-19: the maintainer opened the rehearsal's arm64 dmg on the work Mac that had called 0.1.2 damaged; Open Anyway opened Emerald. The fix reaches people only with the next release, which is the maintainer's to cut.

