brief: What a directory review checks, done before anything is published. (1) Tool annotations: Anthropic's directory requires every tool to carry a title and the applicable readOnlyHint or destructiveHint (OpenAI's scan fails a tool without them too). src/plugin/tools/* already carries many, tested in catalog.test.ts; make it every tool on both servers (the full one and the engine-only one), make the test fail on a tool without them, and check each hint is true (a dry-run-by-default write is still not read-only; delete_format_rows is destructive). (2) A 'Privacy Policy' section in the README, short because the truth is short: the server opens the file it is given and makes no network call; nothing leaves the machine except what the person puts in the conversation; the shell's network rule (D-124) for the app. It must be reachable at an HTTPS link (the public repo's README or the Pages site). (3) The server's instructions and tool descriptions read by a stranger: no project-internal references (D-numbers, milestone names) in what a model or reviewer reads, unless they explain themselves. Done-test: the annotation test fails on an unannotated tool and passes; the README has the privacy section; a grep of tool descriptions and instructions for D-\d+ and M\d+ finds only what is meant to be there.
