commit: 0a64e1c
report: "M42 U7 built at 0a64e1c (pushed). Full suite 4767/4767 green and typecheck clean before the commit; docs:check's only error was D-504's missing row, which this landing writes. No check:tours (only hosts/, scripts/, docs/).\n\nWhat landed: generic run-milestone (sequential default, parallel via dispatch_plan with one worktree per At-once unit, host sessions when offered, a subagent otherwise; take-over of a stalled unit; the report column; one writer per checkout; effort by the unit), closing-pass (one order: checks once, commit the work, land_units with hash and report, commit the landing; worktree children propose an inbox row) and review-phase (open review_entry rows one at a time, verdicts on the row, faults become units, the maintainer closes) in hosts/claude/skills. They find the document as the hooks do and name the project's own checks; skills.test.ts fails if one cites this repo's decisions, units, docs/, scripts/, npm run, memory.emd or a decision log. This repo's .claude/skills copies stay.\n\nDoes it reach people who installed the plugin? Per Claude Code's plugin docs (Plugin loading reference, Versions and updates): it replaces an installed copy only when the computed version (plugin.json's version first) differs, as a string; auto-update is off by default for third-party marketplaces. So plugin.json now carries its own release: <pin>+<n> (this cuts 0.1.2+1, pins still emerald-mcp@0.1.2, no npm publish), cut by `node scripts/pin-plugin.mjs --plugin`; a pin move makes it the bare package version. A digest of the shipped files (hosts/claude/release.ts, recorded in hosts/claude-plugin-release.json) makes plugin.test.ts fail when they change under an unchanged version. claude plugin validate accepts 0.1.2+1. D-504, amending D-500.\n\nProved on a clean CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR: installed emerald@emerald-emd from GitHub at 0.1.2 (declare-a-format only) before the push; after pushing 0a64e1c, `claude plugin marketplace update emerald-emd` + `claude plugin update emerald@emerald-emd` printed 'updated from 0.1.2 to 0.1.2+1', and the new cache folder (0.1.2-1, '+' sanitized) holds closing-pass, declare-a-format, review-phase, run-milestone; a second update says already latest. Not proved: a signed-in session listing the skills (review entry).\n\nSynthetic project (development pack, git repo in temp): two units with disjoint paths; dispatch_plan put both under At once; claimed together; two worktrees committed, merged with git merge --no-edit; two inbox proposals; one land_units inbox dry run. It found a real gap: the proposals name their worktree branch while the claim names the driver's, so land_units refuses without other_branch. Fixed in both run-milestone skills (inbox:[{id, other_branch:true}]); with it the dry run lands both units, two decisions and a review entry.\n\nFor the maintainer: users must update by hand or enable auto-update (README 'Updating'). Found along the way: the shared declare-a-format skill still cites D-numbers of this repo (not touched; it is byte-identical by test)."

### M42 U8
milestone: M42
scope: engine
title: Updates, if you turn them on: one setting off by default, a check on both platforms, and Check for updates now
landed: 2026-10-18
decision: D-502
state: built
brief: Maintainer, 2026-10-18: the desktop app gets updates as an opt-in, off by default. Today there is no update check by design (README: 'Updates are download the new one'; D-124 and docs/engine.md name auto-update as an outbound call the offline rule exists to stop; electron/network.ts blocks every request that is not app://, blob: or data:). This unit changes that rule on the record, and builds the half both platforms share. (1) A DECISION amending D-124: the app makes no network call unless the person turned updates on, and then only to GitHub's release addresses for kfireliyahu/emerald-emd (name the exact hosts the check and, in M42 U9, the download use); a press of Check for updates now is consent for that one check. (2) ONE SETTING, 'Automatic updates', off by default, its label saying exactly what it does on this platform (Windows: checks, downloads, and installs when you press Install and restart, M42 U9; macOS: checks and tells you). Where settings live in the shell is the session's to find; the setting is the app's, never the document's. (3) THE CHECK, in the main process: at start and once a day while on, read the latest release (GitHub's releases/latest, or the latest.yml M42 U9 publishes; pick one and say why), compare with app.getVersion(), and on macOS (and on Windows until U9 lands) show the quiet notice below, with a button that opens the release page in the default browser (shell.openExternal); never download on macOS. (4) HELP > CHECK FOR UPDATES NOW on both platforms, working with the setting off, saying 'You have the latest' or the same notice. (5) THE NETWORK EXCEPTION: electron/network.ts allows exactly those hosts, only while the setting is on or for a check now pressed, and nothing from the renderer; electron/network.test.ts proves the block holds with it off and holds for every other host with it on. (6) WORDS: README's 'No internet, no login' and its update line, PRIVACY.md first (what GitHub sees: IP address and version, when), the page's 'No internet, no login' copy in src/demo/page/copy/en.ts, and the About panel, all say the same rule. Done-test: with the setting off, a full session makes no request (the network test and a run of the shell over the debugging port, the verify-in-shell skill); with it on, a check against a stubbed release reports correctly, on Windows and macOS code paths; Check for updates now works with it off; the decision row is written; docs:check, typecheck and the suite pass. || Maintainer, 2026-10-18: never intrusive, even when on — like the Claude desktop app: no modal, no dialog that steals focus, nothing installed unasked. A newer version shows as a quiet notice inside the window (a small banner or a button in the title bar or rail, the session’s call in the app’s design language) that stays until dismissed or acted on; on Windows, once downloaded, it reads <version> is ready with Install and restart and Later; on macOS, <version> is out with Download. Later hides it until the next start or the next day’s check. Check for updates now answers in the same notice, not a dialog.
