report: M42 U9 built on main in fd3042d; D-503. electron-updater 6.8.9 (dependency, bundled into dist-electron/main.cjs via ssr.noExternal, since the app ships no node_modules). One check: U8's release-API check decides; a newer version on Windows with the setting on goes to electron/windowsUpdater.ts, which sets a generic feed at releases/download/v<version>/ and reads that release's latest.yml (refuses another or a not-newer version), then downloads Emerald-Setup.exe; electron-updater checks its sha512 and deletes a mismatch. Code-signature check is skipped (unsigned, no publisherName): the release's own latest.yml is the guarantee. autoDownload/autoInstallOnAppQuit/differential off; QuietNsisUpdater makes no staging id and main drops the header. Network: isAllowedDownloadRequest on the 'electron-updater' partition allows that release's latest.yml (?noCache) and Emerald-Setup.exe and the two asset hosts (https, default port) only while Updates.downloading() names that version; UA Emerald/<v>, accept-language en. Notices: scheduled download silent until 'Emerald X is ready…' [Install and restart][Later]; a press shows 'Downloading…'; a failure falls back to U8's Download notice with the reason (a checksum mismatch in words); setting off = only told, and turning it off cancels a download. Install and restart: page passes its document path; main writes reopen.json (only a granted path), closes windows through the unsaved question (Cancel clears it), and on will-quit runs quitAndInstall(/S, --force-run); next start opens reopen.json's document. About says the Windows behaviour (state.downloads). Names: Emerald-Setup.exe, Emerald-arm64.dmg, Emerald-x64.dmg; release.yml uploads/sums/attests latest.yml and the blockmap; notes say updates from 0.1.2. Page: RELEASE gone; INSTALLERS + installerLink; NAMED_INSTALLERS_RELEASED=false links releases/latest (the release page) until a release has the new names, then releases/latest/download/<name>; verified in the built page. Tests: windowsUpdater.test.ts drives the real NsisUpdater against a local feed (newer downloaded, same/older refused, bad sha512 refused and nothing kept, other version refused, no staging id); updates.test.ts +12 with a stub downloader; network.test.ts +5; rail.test.ts. Full suite green, typecheck clean. Shell (verify-in-shell, EMERALD_UPDATE_FEED local feed, net log): v0.2.0 downloaded with exactly two requests, ready notice; Install and restart closed the shell, wrote reopen.json, document byte-identical; relaunch reopened it; v0.3.0 bad checksum gave the fallback notice; setting off + Check now made no request; net logs held only the local feed. Rehearsal run 39151952273 green: windows artifact = Emerald-Setup.exe, .blockmap, latest.yml (sha512 matches the file); mac = Emerald-arm64.dmg, Emerald-x64.dmg. app-update.yml: electron-builder writes it for an NSIS target with a publish config (PublishManager onAfterPack), not extracted (no 7-Zip here). Follow-ups: the maintainer's real update (review entry), then flip NAMED_INSTALLERS_RELEASED; the matrix job name now shows the multi-line files list (cosmetic, add a name:); U8's wpad lookup still open.

### M43 U1
milestone: M43
scope: engine
title: The README in the maintainer's words, and a session confirms every claim in it
landed: 2026-10-18
state: built
brief: STOP first: the maintainer writes or edits README.md; no session drafts it unless asked. A session may hand the maintainer, beforehand, a short list of what the README must still carry (the licence lines of D-430, the privacy section M42 U1 will need, how to run it, the demo and embedding section M33 U4 wrote) as facts, not prose. When the maintainer says it is done, the session CONFIRMS rather than rewrites: (1) every claim about what Emerald does is true of the code today (a feature named exists, a command runs, 'no network' holds, a platform listed builds), each false claim listed with where the truth is; (2) every link and image resolves; (3) no competitor names (the project's rule), no real document's names or figures (src/privacy.test.ts and the denylist), and the names match what the app shows (one name per thing); (4) nothing promised that a later milestone has not built (the conversation views of M41, the listing of M42) unless it is worded as coming. The session proposes fixes as a list for the maintainer to accept, and edits only what is accepted; the maintainer's wording stays. A decision row only if something decided changes a rule. Done-test: the maintainer's README is committed; the session's check is written down (claims, links, names) with each finding accepted or declined by the maintainer; docs:check and the privacy test pass. || Driver, 2026-10-09 (M43 U4, facts for the README, not prose): (a) 'Seeing it in the conversation' is stale. Reported 2026-10-09: the frame draws, and works well, in Claude Code sessions in the Claude desktop app's Code tab on Windows; VS Code's Copilot Chat drew it on 2026-10-08. The 'Known gap' line (Claude Desktop on Windows shows views as text only, anthropics/claude-ai-mcp#1069) holds at most for Desktop's own chat: #1069 was still open on 2026-10-09 and that chat was not re-tried. docs/plugin.md 'Which hosts draw it' says what is true now. (b) 'holding a copy of the document as it was when shown' is false since D-442: the drawn view fetches its document with frame_copy (D-446, D-447) and is redrawn when the file changes (push where the host forwards resources/updated, else a check on focus and every 15 s in view); only a host that forwards neither keeps the copy as it was, and the frame says so. (c) Stale claims M43 U4 found: 'Thirty-seven tools' and its finance list (the engine-only server has about twenty); 'CSV export is in the hardening pass'; the coming 'buy me a coffee' link; 'What makes it different' is the ledger's alone; 'Origin and scope' names two card issuers (the names ruling, D-450: competitors by category; whether a reader's institutions are named there is the maintainer's call). (d) The 'Privacy Policy' section is a draft marked for this unit; Contributing already agrees with D-450 (no outside code for now, issues and discussion welcome, a CLA if code is ever taken). || Driver, 2026-10-09 (U9): the install section, verified on fresh clones (Windows 10, Node 24.15, npm 12.2, core.autocrlf true and false, empty npm and Electron caches; D-453). (a) VERIFIED, each passing, with its time: npm ci 18 s; npm run typecheck 23 s; npm run test 103 s (4,454 tests); npm run build 35 s; npm run build:plugin 46 s; npm run build:electron 24 s; npm run build:demo 65 s; npm run check:tours 236 s (it rebuilds the demo, then 9 runs); npm run docs:check 1 s; node dist-plugin/server.mjs answers initialize and tools/list over stdio (23 tools with --engine-only, 80 without). About ten minutes in all. THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt lands in dist/, dist-plugin/ and dist-demo/. (b) The README's `npm install` works too and leaves package-lock.json untouched; `npm ci` is the reproducible form. (c) No install-script approval is needed on npm 12: package.json declines the three (allowScripts, D-453), so the install ends without that warning. (d) Electron 44 downloads its binary on the first `electron` run (npm run desktop, check:tours, icons), not during install: the first run needs the network once. (e) `npm run icons` is NOT a step before `npm run desktop` or dist:win: build/ holds the icons, committed; it only re-cuts them after src/ui/emerald.svg changes. (f) check:tours opens Electron windows, so it needs a display; it is not run in CI. (g) The privacy test skips on a machine without ~/.emerald/denylist.txt, by design. (h) npm prints audit advisories on install (27; 8 in production dependencies, the MCP SDK and React Router); a stranger will see them. (i) Tested on Node 24, which CI uses; package.json declares no engines. (j) Not run here: npm run desktop's window, dist:win, dist:mac (CI's release workflow builds those).
files: README.md (the maintainer's), docs/plugin.md and LICENSING.md only where an accepted fix lands
taken_by: the maintainer, with the 2026-10-16 session guiding and checking
started: 2026-10-16

### M43 U10
milestone: M43
scope: engine
title: No known advisory in what ships: the MCP SDK's chain and React Router upgraded until npm audit is clean for production dependencies
landed: 2026-10-09
decision: D-454
state: built
brief: Found by M43 U9 (2026-10-09) on a fresh clone: `npm audit` prints 27 advisories on install, 8 of them in production dependencies (the MCP SDK and what it brings in, and React Router). A stranger sees them on their first `npm ci`, and the plugin server and the app ship that code. Run `npm audit --omit=dev --json`, and for each advisory say whether Emerald reaches the vulnerable path (the MCP server runs stdio only, no HTTP transport; the app makes no network request; React Router is HashRouter in a local page) — but upgrade regardless where a non-breaking version exists, because 'not reachable' is an argument a stranger will not read. Upgrade the MCP SDK to its latest compatible release and React Router within v6 (or say why v7 is a unit of its own); re-run the plugin's tool-surface tests, the frame (M41) build and the network tests. Then the dev-only advisories: fix what is a minor bump, list the rest with why they do not ship. Done-test: `npm audit --omit=dev` reports 0, or each remaining item has a reason recorded on this unit; the full suite, typecheck, build, build:plugin, build:demo, check:tours and docs:check pass; the tool count over stdio is unchanged (23 engine-only, 80 full).
