### M43 U4
milestone: M43
scope: engine
title: Everything a stranger reads, swept before public: names, privacy, policies, cleanup
landed: 2026-10-09
decision: D-450
state: built
brief: 2026-10-08: before the repository is public, a sweep of everything a stranger can read, beyond the README (U1) and the on-screen terms (U2-U3). The session prepares and does what needs no ruling; what needs one is a list for the maintainer, and nothing the maintainer must decide is decided by a session. (1) PRODUCT NAMES: the rule (rows of scope practice/product; the 'no competitor names' rule: describe by category, keep the points as our own questions). Sweep tracked text (docs/, README, LICENSING, comments, tour data, fixtures' visible text, skills); M40 U5 (D-438) found engine.md and product.md still name products. Hold the distinction for the maintainer to settle per case: competitors are described by category; HOSTS Emerald runs inside (Claude, VS Code, ChatGPT in M41 U4's README draft and docs/plugin.md) may be named as where it works; THE MAINTAINER'S OWN STORY (a notes workspace clunky and holding data on its servers, a notes app with no structure, a spreadsheet not holding up, planned for M33 U8's page) names products in that voice by design. Write the ruling as a rule row. (2) PRIVACY: a 'Privacy Policy' section, short because the truth is short (the server opens the file it is given and makes no network call; nothing leaves the machine but what the person puts in the conversation; the shell's network rule; the page of M33 U8 may send a form post and a count, D-124 unchanged), reachable at an HTTPS link once public; this is M42 U1's privacy part, done here so M42 U1 only checks it is there. The privacy test and the denylist (src/privacy.test.ts, ~/.emerald/denylist.txt) pass over every tracked file; nothing of real documents, names or figures (D-390). (3) POLICIES the public repo needs, drafted for the maintainer's yes: SECURITY.md (how to report, given there is no server); a contributions stance, which is THE MAINTAINER'S DECISION because Emerald is AGPL-3.0 with a commercial licence on request (D-430), so accepting outside code without a CLA or copyright assignment would limit relicensing (options: no outside contributions for now, a CLA, or DCO with the consequence spelled out); a CODE_OF_CONDUCT only if the maintainer wants one; issue templates optional. (4) CLEANUP: stale counts and claims (the README's 'Thirty-seven tools', D-438's notes), dead or internal-only files a stranger would trip on (scratch, .frame-try is gitignored, mockups that name real things), gitignored backups, any secret or token anywhere in history (a scan), and the history-rewrite gate on M33 U4 (arriving as an inbox row) checked against what is in the repo. STOP for the maintainer with the rulings list (names per case, contributions stance, code of conduct yes/no); carry out the answers. Done-test: every tracked file a stranger reads passes the names ruling and the privacy test; the privacy section and SECURITY.md exist; the contributions stance is the maintainer's and written (a decision row); the cleanup list is empty or each item is the maintainer's to keep. HISTORY-REWRITE GATE (an inbox row triaged in M44 U5, 2026-10-08; D-430), checked here before anything is public: on the repository to be made public, git log --format='%an <%ae>%n%cn <%ce>' | sort -u prints only Kfir Raphael Eliyahu <me@kfireliyahu.com>, and the privacy test passes on that clone. The rewrite itself (git filter-repo with a mailmap on a fresh clone, pushed to a fresh, empty github.com/kfireliyahu/emerald-emd rather than a transfer, the pre-rewrite clone deleted or moved offline) is the maintainer's act and M33 U4's deploy waits on it; until it is done this item is the maintainer's and open. || DONE WITHOUT A RULING (driver, 2026-10-08): README 'Privacy Policy' section, marked a draft for U1's rewrite; SECURITY.md drafted with a placeholder address; the closing-pass skill no longer names the old GitHub account; the ledger roadmap's dead link into the archive fixed; secret scan of all 614 commits (tokens, keys, private keys, JWTs, assigned secrets, key files): nothing, every hit a CSS or parser 'token'; privacy test passes on HEAD. || Waiting on the maintainer's rulings (driver, 2026-10-08): (A) NAMES, proposed wording in brackets. Competitors in live text: engine.md 103-104, 140-143 (the comparison table), 203, 379-380, 425-426, 453, 499-518 (the notes-app plugins analogy); product.md 38-39; 02-architecture.md 26 (a sync service [a sync folder]); src/domain/format/identity.ts 7; packs/ledger/12-market-reality.md 13, 35, 50, 55, 134, 138 (a budgeting app, an aggregator, two spreadsheet-databases); plugin.md 428 and the decision index 74 (D-102's title, the budgeting app); src/plugin/instructions.ts 128 and 308 (two other products named Emerald, to steer a model [another app or service that shares the name]); docs/memory.emd rows (56 mentions of eleven products, hosts among them). Wording: each becomes its category — a spreadsheet; an all-in-one notes-and-database workspace; a notes app over plain Markdown files; typed, relational spreadsheet-databases; a bank-connected budgeting subscription; a licensed account aggregator; a design tool. The spreadsheet line as the pitch: CLAUDE.md 3, conventions.md 13, 00-index.md 8, engine.md 28, product.md 15, the decision index 163 (D-238), mockups/m33-u2/host.html 131 and 167; recommend the maintainer's voice stays, and product.md stops leading with it when the repo opens (the launch plan). Hosts and runtimes (recommend keep, named as where it works and what it speaks): Claude, Claude Code, Claude Desktop (~180 mentions), README 236-237 and plugin.md 222, 227, 311 (ChatGPT, VS Code with GitHub Copilot, Goose), Ollama, LM Studio, llama.cpp and 'OpenAI-compatible' in electron/runtimes.ts, electron/generate.ts, scripts/local-host.mjs and plugin.md. The maintainer's own story (recommend keep): engine.md 142 and 226, the origins account 107, src/plugin/backup.ts 92 (a named trial). The archive (about 20 lines naming competitors, two naming the other Emeralds): past entries are never edited;