### M43 U7
milestone: M43
scope: engine
title: What a downloader is owed: an About with the licence and source, the not-advice line, checksummed releases built in CI, and the privacy policy at its own address
landed: 2026-10-09
decision: D-452
state: built
brief: The rest of the 2026-10-09 legal/security read, after M43 U6. (1) AGPL section 6: whoever gets an installer can get its Corresponding Source — an About (the native about panel via app.setAboutPanelOptions, and a Help menu item) showing the version, 'AGPL-3.0-only, no warranty', the source repository's URL and THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt; the README and each release page link the tagged source. (2) A plain line, in the README and the About: Emerald computes, it does not advise; figures read from a payslip, pension report or statement are to be checked against the source (the ledger pack stays computing, never recommending, D-308). (3) A release workflow on GitHub Actions (windows-latest, macos-latest) that builds the unsigned installers from a tag, publishes them with SHA-256 checksums and artifact attestations, so where a download came from is checkable without a signature (D-124 stands). (4) The README's install text for macOS says the current path past Gatekeeper for an unsigned app (System Settings, Privacy & Security, Open Anyway), since the right-click bypass is gone on recent macOS. (5) The privacy policy moves from the README into PRIVACY.md, a stable HTTPS address for registry and directory listings; the README links it; M33 U8's page, if it ever takes a form post or a count, updates it first (who processes it, retention, deletion, no cookies). (6) SECURITY.md: model-read document text is untrusted input (prompt injection) and is in scope where a tool lets it do more than the tool says; GitHub private vulnerability reporting on the public repo is the maintainer's switch. Waits on the public repository's URL for (1) and (3), which is the maintainer's act (M43 U4's history-rewrite gate).
