### M44 U1
milestone: M44
scope: development
title: An inbox format: another project's session hands its facts to this document
landed: 2026-10-08
decision: D-439
state: built
brief: Proposed 2026-10-08 by a second project's session, accepted with corrections. The case: that session had facts and findings for this project and the only channel was the clipboard; three of six hand-offs never arrived; on 2026-10-08 it sent them as a message to another session, which lasts only while that session exists. This closes two open M38 T1 review entries: 'Exchange facts between two documents in two projects' and 'Answer an open question with a fact rather than a decision'. Add a sixth format to the development pack (src/data/packs/development/): `inbox`, with from (text: the SENDING DOCUMENT'S PATH, not a session title, since a title stops meaning anything when the session ends; the title may go in subject or body), sent_on (date), subject (text), body (text, markdown), refs (text: the sender's own refs, e.g. 'other.emd D-10, D-18'), state (enum: new, triaged, applied, dropped), and what it became: answered_by as a link to a decision, plus whatever lets a hand-off become an open_question, review_entry or unit (a link to each, or a free `became` text column if the engine has no link to one of several formats; the session's call, said). Nothing adds up. start_here lists inbox rows whose state is new, from the pack's views (src/data/packs/development/views.md); pack.test.ts prints the new table and CLAUDE.md's start_here table gets the row. The format's notes state the rules AS A CONVENTION, not a guarantee (D-138: the engine does not know which session writes): a sender writes a row once and never edits it; the owner moves state and fills what it became; a foreign session writes to no other format. Say in the notes that a text .emd is rewritten whole on every write, so a write from another project during a driver run is the concurrent-writers case (batches apply whole or not at all, and a copy is taken first). No new engine write: rows go through record_format_rows, so D-234 parity is untouched. Existing documents that keep the pack get the format when the pack is updated, as D-386 and D-405 already handle. A decision row records the format and its convention. Prove it with the real case: once the format is in docs/memory.emd, the second project's session writes its 2026-10-08 hand-off as inbox rows itself (four facts, the history-rewrite gate on M33 U4, eight engine findings), start_here shows them, and this project triages them. No reply goes to the second project's session from the planning session; telling it the format exists is the maintainer's or the building session's to do, with the maintainer's yes. Done-test: the pack declares inbox; start_here lists new inbox rows; pack.test.ts and docs:check pass; the real hand-off arrives as rows and is triaged.
