### M44 U4
milestone: M44
scope: engine
title: A view drawn in the conversation updates in place
landed: 2026-10-08
decision: D-442
state: built
brief: 2026-10-08: a view M41 draws in the conversation updates in place when the document changes, as Emerald's own window does through its watcher (D-127), instead of staying a snapshot of the moment the tool was called (moved here from M41 U2). The frame subscribes to the document through the host (M44 U2's resources/subscribe) and refetches it only on resources/updated. FIRST, measure whether hosts forward a frame's subscription and the updated notification (the spec defines a frame calling tools and reading resources through the host; forwarding notifications is unconfirmed); the stand-in (`npm run frame:try`) can be taught to forward them, but say plainly that the stand-in proves only our side and a real host has to be checked when one draws on a development machine. ONLY where a host does not forward the push does the frame fall back to a cheap change check (the document's modified time or a content hash, through an app-only tool hidden from the model), on regaining visibility or focus and at a modest interval while visible, never while hidden and never a tight poll. A redraw keeps the person's scroll and selection. This is also M41 U3's groundwork: a frame that knows the document moved can refuse a stale write. Done-test: in the stand-in, a row written by a session while a view is drawn appears in it without a new tool call; where the fallback runs, a check costs one small call, not a document copy; what each real host does is written down.
