### M45 U4
milestone: M45
scope: engine
title: Safety copies live in Emerald's own folder, not beside the document, and the app lists them to restore
landed: 2026-10-09
decision: D-455
state: built
brief: Author, 2026-10-09: the timestamped `.bak` copies beside a document 'spam the folder' and do not feel normal. Amend D-098 with a new decision: the automatic copy the MCP server takes before a mutating batch goes to Emerald's own per-user folder, the way desktop apps keep recovery copies: Windows %LOCALAPPDATA%\Emerald\Backups, macOS ~/Library/Application Support/Emerald/Backups, Linux $XDG_STATE_HOME/emerald/backups (else ~/.local/state/emerald/backups). Computed in plain Node, because the plugin runs without Electron, and the shell computes the same path (local, not roaming; do not use Electron's userData if it differs). One subfolder per document, named so a person can tell which document it is (the document's basename plus a short hash of its absolute path). Still timestamped, still the last five kept per document, and the tool's reply still says where the copy went (full path). D-098's reason, 'a backup the user cannot find is not a backup', is now answered by the app: in the desktop shell, File gets 'Earlier copies of this document', listing those copies (time, size) with Restore. Restore takes a safety copy of the current file first, then replaces it, then reopens. Through the bridge only (src/ imports bridge.ts and nothing else from electron/). The static-site build shows no such item. Named copies (keep_copy, D-399) stay beside the document: a person asks for those by name. No migration of `.bak` files already beside documents (no old-file migration): they are left where they are. Parity (D-227): restoring an earlier copy is a person's action on a screen, so either a tool does it too, or the decision says why restore stays screen-only (the model must not overwrite a document from a backup unasked). Grep the docs and the server's instructions for 'beside the document', '.bak' and D-098, and fix every claim this makes false; the .gitignore `*.emd.*.bak` line can stay for old copies. Done-test: a write through the plugin leaves nothing new beside the document; the copy is in the per-user folder; the tool's reply names it; the shell lists it and Restore works (verify-in-shell); the full suite, typecheck, build, build:plugin, build:electron, docs:check pass; the network tests stay green.
