brief: 2026-10-10: the context menu (Ctrl+K) should save some UI, opened with a button click and a tooltip saying Ctrl+K opens it; wanted next. Help could not be found either: the desktop window hides its menu bar until Alt is pressed (autoHideMenuBar, electron/main.ts), so Help > About and File > Earlier copies of this document (M45 U4, F3) are invisible unless you know. Build: (1) A button always on screen, in the app's chrome where it costs least room (sidebar or top bar; follow the ui rule rows, format_rows rule match:{scope:ui, state:live}), that opens the command palette. Its tooltip names it and teaches the shortcut ('Commands, Ctrl+K'; on macOS '⌘K'), and the same button works in the web build. The palette exists so the screen can carry fewer controls; this button is the one way in that needs no knowledge. (2) Every item in the shell's native menu (electron/menu.ts) is also a palette command, so nothing is reachable only behind Alt: About Emerald, Earlier copies of this document, and whatever else the menu holds that the palette lacks. A test fails when a menu item has no palette command (the way parity.test guards tools). (3) Decide, in the decision row, whether the hidden menu bar stays hidden now that the palette is findable; recommend keeping it hidden (it is the native fallback, and Alt still shows it) unless the button makes that wrong. The make-it-in-place rule applies: count the clicks to reach About and Earlier copies before and after. Hebrew labels go through Bidi. Done-test: the button is visible on every route, at desktop and narrow widths, and its tooltip shows the shortcut; clicking it opens the palette, and About and Earlier copies are found by typing; the menu-to-palette test passes; typecheck, the full suite, build, build:electron, check:tours and docs:check pass; verified in the browser (read_page, not screenshots) and in the shell (verify-in-shell, killing only electron processes this session started, never the maintainer's own open window). Then the maintainer tries it: the button's place and look are the maintainer's to judge.
