report: M50 U3 built at 9fe7f94 (D-522). A spreadsheet keyed by a unique column (sourceKeyOf, unchanged) is now matched against every row of its format, whichever file or person wrote it: next month's export under another name changes the rows last month's wrote and adds what is new, where before it was refused line by line as repeating a unique value (only a file of the same name was read again, D-408). Gone stays per file. A row an export updates moves to that reading, so superseding the older reading keeps it. A read fills only the columns it maps (readingFills), so a value typed in another column survives. Found along the way and fixed: after a read had changed a row, the commit kept the old mark, so a third read called that row edited by a person and left it; a read that writes over a row now marks it afresh (a test fails without the fix). Both halves: read_onto_format's dry run heads it 'Already in <format> - what this read changes' with changed / added / as it was and each changed row's columns; the Read screen says 'Already here, each found by <col>, which is unique: 1 changed · 1 added · 1 as before', with 'Changed: T-1 (state)'. A read with no key and no header row says why in one sentence on both (no unique column, or the unique one not filled). The written summaries no longer say 'read again' for a file not read before. The M24 U4 test that expected a value from another file to be refused now expects it found; a second unique column the read is not keyed by still refuses. Tests: the full suite once (one failure, that M24 U4 test, updated), then related (177 files) green, plus privacy and typecheck. Checked in the browser on a synthetic text document: November's csv onto October's three rows gave 4 rows and the lines above; a format with no unique column shows the sentence. check:tours skipped: no tour drives the Read screen. The read_onto_format description gained a clause, build:plugin rebuilt; ask-cold.mjs could not run (the claude CLI here is not logged in), so nobody did a cold read. No schema change.

