### M51 F3
milestone: M51
scope: plugin
title: Drawing is a flow the person never notices: the model is told whether it drew, and says nothing about it when it did
landed: 2026-10-18
decision: D-493
state: built
brief: "2026-10-18, after M51 F2: it worked, but the session said it called start_here on emerald-ledger and added \"The server says this client can draw views, but it can't check whether one actually appeared. If you see a view next to this message, that's the board. If not, nothing was drawn, and the list below is the whole answer.\" Too much for users new to it: it should be a flow its users do not notice. The board was drawn in the Code tab after F2. What is wrong is that the session relayed F1/F2's machinery to the person: which server it called, and D-489's first line (drawnLine in src/plugin/tools/sheet.ts) paraphrased as a disclaimer. That line is for the model, not the person. Make it read as an instruction to the model, never as text to repeat: when the client says it draws, the line tells the model the view is beside the answer and to say nothing about drawing, servers or tools, and add only what the view does not show; when nothing could be drawn, the model gives the answer as text without a word about why (at most a short plain sentence if the person asked to see a board). The same for the hook's first line (F2) and the instruction blocks and CLAUDE.md's sentence: none of it may lead a model to name a server, a tool or the drawing mechanism to the person. Keep F2's protection: a model must still never claim a view was drawn that was not. Done-test: in the maintainer's Code tab, 'show me what's ready in docs/memory.emd' draws the board and the reply says nothing about servers, tools or whether it drew; a client that cannot draw gets the list as text with no explanation of drawing; tests cover both wordings.\n\n2026-10-18: another possible problem: even when the frame is drawn, the session gives the details as text. When the client draws, the model must not retype the view's contents in its reply: the view is the answer, and the reply is at most a line adding what the view does not show. The tool's text still carries the data (the model reasons over it, and a client that cannot draw needs it), so this is how that text is framed to the model, not removing it."
